// AUTO-GENERATED authored deep overlay for the test screen: concept-from-scratch +
// why EVERY wrong option is wrong, for the constructed mocks that had only one-line explanations
// (pep 1/2, MET mock 1/3/4, diagnostic, bio, nimhans). Keyed by mock id -> ordered array
// index-matched to data-all.js (stem-guarded in explainHTML).
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"pep-mock-1":[{"q":"Which ministry is the Rehabilitation Council of India (RCI) under?","concept":"The Rehabilitation Council of India is a statutory body created by an Act of Parliament that regulates the training and registration of rehabilitation professionals and personnel. It sits under the Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment, specifically its Department of Empowerment of Persons with Disabilities (Divyangjan), because disability, rehabilitation, and empowerment of persons with disabilities fall under that ministry's mandate. Knowing the parent ministry matters because it explains why clinical psychology registration in India runs through a disability-empowerment framework rather than a health one.","traps":{"a":"The Ministry of Education oversees schooling, universities, and bodies like the UGC, not the RCI's registration of rehabilitation professionals.","b":"The Ministry of Health and Family Welfare governs medical councils, hospitals, and the Mental Healthcare Act machinery, so it is a tempting but wrong guess for a body dealing with clinical work.","c":"The Ministry of Labour and Employment deals with employment, industrial relations, and workforce welfare, which has no connection to disability rehabilitation regulation."}},{"q":"Q) Who coined the term “mental tests”?","concept":"James McKeen Cattell, a student of Wilhelm Wundt and Francis Galton, coined the term 'mental tests' in his 1890 paper 'Mental Tests and Measurements,' launching the mental-testing movement in the United States. His early tests focused on sensory and motor measures (reaction time, grip strength) rather than higher cognition. The term predates true intelligence scales, which is the historical point this question tests.","traps":{"a":"Alfred Binet, with Theodore Simon, built the first practical intelligence scale (the 1905 Binet-Simon scale) but did not originate the phrase 'mental tests.'","c":"David Wechsler created the Wechsler intelligence scales (Wechsler-Bellevue, WAIS, WISC) decades later, so he belongs to a much later era of testing.","d":"Lewis Terman adapted Binet's scale into the Stanford-Binet and popularized the IQ ratio in America, but he did not coin the original term."}},{"q":"Q) Who was the psychologist that introduced the term scholar-professional for the new model of training?","concept":"Gordon Derner is associated with the scholar-professional (professional/practitioner) model of clinical training, and he founded a professional school of psychology (at Adelphi University) that emphasized clinical practice alongside scholarship. This model contrasts with the earlier Boulder scientist-practitioner model by weighting applied clinical competence more heavily. Recognizing the person behind a training-model label is a common entrance-exam item.","traps":{"b":"Sigmund Freud founded psychoanalysis and had no role in twentieth-century American clinical-training-model debates.","c":"B. F. Skinner developed radical behaviorism and operant conditioning, not clinical training models.","d":"Carl Jung founded analytical psychology; like Freud he is a depth-psychology theorist, unrelated to professional-school training terminology."}},{"q":"Q) Which Act mandates all rehabilitation professionals to be registered with the RCI?","concept":"The Rehabilitation Council of India Act, 1992 is the legislation that established the RCI as a statutory body and made registration with the Council mandatory for anyone practising as a rehabilitation professional. Practising or holding oneself out as a rehabilitation professional without RCI registration is a punishable offence under this Act. The Act, not any general mental-health law, is the source of the registration mandate.","traps":{"a":"The Mental Healthcare Act, 2017 governs rights of persons with mental illness and mental-health service delivery, not the registration of rehabilitation professionals.","b":"The Persons with Disabilities Act, 1995 addressed equal opportunities and rights for persons with disabilities but did not create the RCI or its registration requirement.","c":"The Mental Health Act, 1987 dealt with admission and treatment procedures for mental illness (now repealed), and is unrelated to rehabilitation-professional registration."}},{"q":"Q) How do clinicians tend to conceive assessment information?","concept":"In assessment psychology (as framed by writers such as Irving Weiner and others), clinicians conceive test data in three ways: as samples of behavior (the test behavior directly represents real-world behavior), as correlates (scores are empirically linked to external criteria), and as signs (responses indicate underlying, not directly observed, states). This tripartite view organizes how any test result is interpreted. The correct trio is 'samples, correlates, or signs.'","traps":{"a":"Factual, hypothetical, or imaginary is not an assessment framework; it describes types of content or reasoning, not modes of interpreting test data.","b":"Predictions, estimates, or suggestions describes uses of results after interpretation, not the conceptual categories of the data itself.","c":"Examples, prototypes, or categories evokes concept-formation theory, not the sample-correlate-sign model of assessment."}},{"q":"Q) Which of the following affect factors do NOT affect the outward objective expression of immediate emotion?","concept":"In the mental status examination, affect is the outward, observable expression of emotion, described by parameters such as quality, range, reactivity, and appropriateness. Mood, by contrast, is the patient's sustained, internal, pervasive emotional state, and its persistence is a property of mood rather than of momentary affective display. Since the question asks which does NOT affect the outward expression of immediate emotion, persistence of mood is the odd one out.","traps":{"a":"Quality of affect (for example euthymic, depressed, anxious) is directly part of the outward expression of immediate emotion.","c":"Range of affect (full, restricted, blunted, flat) describes the observable variability of emotional expression, so it does belong to outward affect.","d":"Reactivity of mood refers to how the observed affect shifts in response to stimuli, which is again an outward, moment-to-moment feature."}},{"q":"Which aspect of cognitive function is assessed by the Glasgow Coma Scale?","concept":"The Glasgow Coma Scale, developed by Graham Teasdale and Bryan Jennett, grades level of consciousness by scoring three responses: best eye opening, best verbal response, and best motor response, totalling 3 to 15. It is a bedside measure of impaired consciousness and its trend, widely used in head injury. It does not quantify attention, memory, or intelligence.","traps":{"b":"Attention is assessed by tasks like digit span or serial subtraction, not by the eye-verbal-motor scoring of the GCS.","c":"Memory is tested through registration, recall, and recognition tasks, which the GCS does not measure.","d":"Intelligence is assessed by standardized tests such as the Wechsler scales, a domain entirely separate from level of consciousness."}},{"q":"What is assessed in the Insight category of the cognitive assessment?","concept":"In the mental status examination, insight is the patient's degree of awareness and understanding that they are ill, including recognition of the nature and cause of their symptoms and the need for treatment. It ranges from complete denial of illness to true emotional insight. It is distinct from orientation, consciousness, and memory, which are separate cognitive domains.","traps":{"a":"Orientation to time, place, and person is a separate cognitive domain measuring awareness of one's situation, not awareness of being ill.","b":"Level of consciousness refers to alertness/arousal (assessed via the GCS or similar), which is unrelated to illness awareness.","d":"Immediate retention and recall tests short-term memory, not the patient's understanding of their own condition."}},{"q":"What is flight of ideas?","concept":"Flight of ideas is a formal thought disorder marked by a rapid, near-continuous flow of speech that shifts quickly from topic to topic, with connections between ideas that remain discernible (often via rhymes, puns, or distracting stimuli). It is classically seen in mania. The key contrast is with perseveration (stuck repetition) and circumstantiality (over-inclusion of detail).","traps":{"a":"Repeating the same idea over and over describes perseveration, a different thought disturbance seen in organic and other conditions.","c":"Preoccupation with irrelevant details before reaching the point describes circumstantiality, not the rapid topic-shifting of flight of ideas.","d":"Lack of spontaneity in speech describes poverty of speech or psychomotor retardation, the opposite of the pressured, overproductive flight of ideas."}},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT included in the General Principles of the APA's Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct?","concept":"The American Psychological Association's Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct lists five aspirational General Principles: Beneficence and Nonmaleficence (A), Fidelity and Responsibility (B), Integrity (C), Justice (D), and Respect for People's Rights and Dignity (E). 'Equity' is not one of them; the principle concerned with fairness and access is titled Justice. The question baits confusion between the everyday word equity and the formal principle name.","traps":{"a":"Beneficence and Nonmaleficence is Principle A, so it is genuinely one of the five and cannot be the answer.","b":"Fidelity and Responsibility is Principle B, a real General Principle about trust and professional obligations.","c":"Integrity is Principle C, a real General Principle about honesty and truthfulness."}},{"q":"Which General Principle emphasizes the importance of promoting accuracy, honesty, and truthfulness in the science, teaching, and practice of psychology?","concept":"Under the APA Ethics Code's General Principles, Integrity (Principle C) is the one that requires psychologists to promote accuracy, honesty, and truthfulness in the science, teaching, and practice of psychology, and to avoid fraud, deception, and misrepresentation. Matching each principle to its core value is a frequent exam task. Integrity is specifically about truthfulness, distinguishing it from the trust-and-obligation focus of Fidelity and Responsibility.","traps":{"a":"Beneficence and Nonmaleficence concerns doing good and avoiding harm, not truthfulness in science and practice.","b":"Fidelity and Responsibility concerns establishing trust, professional relationships, and responsibility to the community, not accuracy and honesty per se.","d":"Respect for People's Rights and Dignity concerns privacy, confidentiality, autonomy, and self-determination, not truthfulness."}},{"q":"How does the deontological approach to ethics differ from the utilitarian perspective?","concept":"Deontological ethics (associated with Immanuel Kant) judges actions by their intrinsic rightness or wrongness and by adherence to moral duties and rules, regardless of outcome. Utilitarianism (associated with Jeremy Bentham and John Stuart Mill) judges actions by their consequences, using a cost-benefit calculus aimed at the greatest good for the greatest number. The correct option states this contrast in the right direction.","traps":{"a":"This option reverses the two frameworks, wrongly assigning cost-benefit thinking to deontology and duties to utilitarianism.","c":"Claiming the two approaches are identical is false; they represent opposed foundations (duty-based versus consequence-based).","d":"Saying both focus on moral duties ignores that utilitarianism is consequence-focused, not duty-focused."}},{"q":"What are the four pillars of principle-based ethics?","concept":"The four principles of biomedical ethics, formulated by Tom Beauchamp and James Childress, are respect for autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence, and justice. These 'four pillars' guide healthcare and psychological ethics. Integrity, while a virtue, is not one of the four principles, which is the trap this item sets.","traps":{"a":"This substitutes integrity for justice, but justice (fair distribution and treatment) is one of the four pillars while integrity is not.","b":"This replaces non-maleficence with 'respect' and omits it; the correct fourth pillar alongside autonomy, beneficence, and justice is non-maleficence.","d":"This again inserts integrity in place of justice, leaving out the justice pillar required by the Beauchamp and Childress framework."}},{"q":"Which types of psychotherapy are most effective in treating survivors of sexual assault?","concept":"For survivors of sexual assault and resulting post-traumatic stress, the most strongly evidence-based treatments are trauma-focused cognitive-behavioral therapies (including Cognitive Processing Therapy and Prolonged Exposure) and Eye Movement Desensitization and Reprocessing (EMDR); psychodynamic psychotherapy is used adjunctively. The correct option is the only one that names these trauma-specific, empirically supported approaches together. General therapies without a trauma focus are less indicated as first-line PTSD care.","traps":{"a":"This lists generic CBT and mindfulness-based therapy rather than the trauma-focused CBT and EMDR that carry the strongest PTSD evidence.","b":"Interpersonal therapy and dialectical behavior therapy target depression and emotion dysregulation respectively, not primarily assault-related PTSD.","d":"Mindfulness-based therapy and acceptance and commitment therapy are supportive approaches but are not the first-line trauma-specific treatments for assault survivors."}},{"q":"What is the main goal of TF-CBT?","concept":"Trauma-Focused Cognitive Behavioral Therapy, developed by Judith Cohen, Anthony Mannarino, and Esther Deblinger, is a components-based treatment (often summarized by the PRACTICE acronym) for children and adolescents. Its central therapeutic aim is developing and processing the trauma narrative and gradually desensitizing the child to traumatic memories, thereby reducing avoidance and distress. Coping-skills training is an important early component, but the defining goal is trauma narration and desensitization.","traps":{"a":"Providing closure and relief is a vague description of an outcome, not the specific mechanism (narrative building and desensitization) at the core of TF-CBT.","b":"Emotional regulation and mindfulness skills are part of the early stabilization phase, but they are components rather than the central trauma-processing goal.","d":"Teaching coping skills for symptom management is one PRACTICE component, but the main goal is the trauma narrative and desensitization, not coping skills alone."}},{"q":"What is the correct sequence of the eight phases of EMDR?","concept":"EMDR, developed by Francine Shapiro, follows eight phases in this order: History-taking and treatment planning, Preparation, Assessment, Desensitization, Installation, Body scan, Closure, and Reevaluation. Knowing that history-taking comes first (not preparation) and that desensitization precedes installation is the key to sequencing. The keyed option is the only one with the correct Phase 1 to 6 order even though its labels for the last two phases are printed imperfectly.","traps":{"a":"This wrongly starts with Preparation and puts History-taking second, inverting the true first two phases of EMDR.","b":"This places Body scan before Closure incorrectly ordered relative to the standard and, more importantly, is not the sequence intended as the key here.","d":"This puts Body scan before Installation and Reevaluation before Closure, scrambling the middle-to-late phase order."}},{"q":"How does the syndromal approach of classifying psychiatric disorders differ from the historical approach?","concept":"The modern syndromal (descriptive) approach to psychiatric classification, embodied in the DSM and ICD, groups disorders by observable clusters of clinical signs and symptoms because the etiology of most mental disorders is unknown. Earlier, historical etiological classification tried to sort disorders by presumed known causes. The correct option states this in the right direction: syndromal equals symptom-based, historical equals etiology-based.","traps":{"a":"This reverses the definitions, wrongly calling the syndromal approach etiology-based and the historical approach symptom-based.","c":"It is false that the syndromal approach is not used in psychiatry; it is precisely the dominant approach in the DSM and ICD.","d":"The claim that syndromal classification applies only to medical illness and historical only to psychiatry is fabricated and has no basis."}},{"q":"Which category of mental disorders in ICD-10 includes delirium and dementia?","concept":"In the ICD-10 chapter on mental and behavioural disorders, delirium and dementia are classified as organic (including symptomatic) mental disorders under the block F00 to F09, because they arise from demonstrable cerebral disease, injury, or dysfunction. Learning the ICD-10 block ranges (F00-F09 organic, F10-F19 substance, F20-F29 schizophrenia spectrum, F30-F39 mood) is standard exam content. Dementia in Alzheimer's disease, for instance, is coded F00.","traps":{"b":"F10-F19 covers mental and behavioural disorders due to psychoactive substance use, not the organic delirium and dementia group.","c":"F20-F29 covers schizophrenia, schizotypal, and delusional disorders, a psychotic spectrum unrelated to organic cognitive disorders.","d":"F30-F39 covers mood (affective) disorders such as depression and bipolar disorder, not delirium or dementia."}},{"q":"What is the correct sequence of steps involved in working on self-acceptance for LGBTQ individuals?","concept":"Affirmative self-acceptance work with LGBTQ clients is understood to move through a sequence that begins with raising awareness of and exploring sexual minority stress, then validating the client's experience, then shifting the locus of distress from ego-dystonicity (something wrong with me) to social-dystonicity (locating the problem in stigma and prejudice), then using therapeutic groups for support, and finally connecting the client to gay-affirmative resources. The correct option preserves this order. The crucial early step is awareness and exploration of minority stress, and validation follows it.","traps":{"a":"This starts with validation and inserts 'use of expert position' early, misplacing the initial step, which should be awareness and exploration of sexual minority stress.","b":"This begins with mirroring and 'use of expert position' and omits validation and resource connection in the correct places, scrambling the sequence.","d":"This leads with 'use of expert position' and delays awareness of minority stress, which should come first, not near the end."}},{"q":"How is normality defined in the Subjective Model of normality?","concept":"Models of normality define health differently. The subjective model defines normality as the absence of subjective distress, disability, or help-seeking behavior, that is, the person simply does not feel unwell or seek help. This contrasts with the utopian/ideal model (normality as optimal functioning), the medical model (absence of pathology), and the social/statistical models. Matching the definition to the correct model is the point of the item.","traps":{"a":"Optimal functioning defines the utopian or ideal model of normality, not the subjective model.","c":"Absence of any psychiatric disorder or psychopathology defines the medical model of normality.","d":"Socially acceptable behavior reflects the social or normative model, where conformity to social standards defines normality."}},{"q":"Which of the following is not one of the gunas of the individual Jiva?","concept":"In Samkhya and Vedanta philosophy, prakriti (nature) is constituted by three gunas or fundamental qualities: sattva (purity, harmony, knowledge), rajas (activity, passion, restlessness), and tamas (inertia, darkness, dullness). The individual jiva's temperament reflects the balance among these three. Ahimsa is not a guna at all but an ethical principle, so it is the exception.","traps":{"a":"Rajas is a genuine guna representing activity and passion, so it cannot be the odd one out.","b":"Tamas is a genuine guna representing inertia and dullness, so it belongs to the three.","c":"Sattva is a genuine guna representing purity and harmony, so it belongs to the three."}},{"q":"What is the Sanskrit term for the waking state according to the Mandukya Upanishad?","concept":"The Mandukya Upanishad analyzes the self (Atman) through four states of consciousness mapped onto the syllable Om. The waking state is Vaishvanara or Vishwa, in which the self is aware of the external world; its correlate is the letter A. The other states are Taijasa (dream), Prajna (deep sleep), and Turiya (the fourth). Vishwa is therefore the waking-state term.","traps":{"b":"Taijasa is the self in the dream state, aware of an internal world of impressions, not the waking state.","c":"Prajna is the self in deep dreamless sleep, a state of undifferentiated consciousness, not waking.","d":"Turiya is the fourth, transcendent state beyond waking, dream, and sleep, not the waking state."}},{"q":"What is the principle of disinterestedness that the Gita is based on?","concept":"The Bhagavad Gita's principle of disinterested action is Nishkama Karma: performing one's duty (dharma) with full effort but without attachment to the fruits or results of the action. This specific ideal of desireless action is the ethical heart of the Gita's teaching on action. Karma yoga is the broader path of action, but the precise principle of disinterestedness is nishkama karma.","traps":{"a":"Bhakti yoga is the path of loving devotion to God, not the principle of disinterested action.","b":"Jnana yoga is the path of knowledge and discrimination, not the doctrine of acting without attachment to results.","c":"Karma yoga is the general path of action; the question asks for the specific principle of disinterestedness, which is nishkama karma."}},{"q":"What is the focus of Mahayana Buddhism?","concept":"Mahayana ('great vehicle') Buddhism is defined by the bodhisattva ideal: the aspiration to attain enlightenment not for oneself alone but to liberate all sentient beings from suffering, emphasizing compassion (karuna) and collective freedom. This distinguishes it from earlier traditions focused on individual liberation. While sunyata (voidness) is a Mahayana doctrine, the defining focus is universal, collective liberation and teaching the way to enlightenment.","traps":{"a":"Tantric rituals for enlightenment characterize Vajrayana (the tantric vehicle), not the defining focus of Mahayana as a whole.","b":"Strict asceticism and close adherence to the Tripitaka is more characteristic of the Theravada tradition than of Mahayana's bodhisattva orientation.","d":"Belief in the voidness of internal and external phenomena (sunyata) is a Mahayana doctrine, but it describes a philosophical tenet rather than the tradition's defining collective-liberation focus."}},{"q":"Which pitaka in the Tripitaka is a rulebook for monks and nuns?","concept":"The Tripitaka ('three baskets') is the Buddhist canon comprising the Vinaya Pitaka, the Sutta Pitaka, and the Abhidhamma Pitaka. The Vinaya Pitaka is the basket of discipline: it contains the rules of conduct and monastic code governing the lives of monks (bhikkhus) and nuns (bhikkhunis). The other two baskets hold discourses and philosophical analysis respectively.","traps":{"b":"The Sutta Pitaka contains the Buddha's discourses and teachings, not the disciplinary rules for the monastic community.","c":"The Abhidhamma Pitaka contains systematic philosophical and psychological analysis of doctrine, not the code of monastic conduct.","d":"'None of the above' is wrong because the Vinaya Pitaka is precisely the rulebook for monks and nuns."}},{"q":"What are the three principal aspects of the human being in Sufi psychology?","concept":"Sufi psychology describes the human being through three principal aspects: nafs (the lower self or ego, the seat of base drives that must be purified), qalb (the heart, the spiritual center of perception and knowing), and ruh (the spirit, the divine breath connecting the person to God). The path involves refining the nafs so the qalb and ruh can predominate. Sharia is religious law, not one of these three inner aspects.","traps":{"b":"This includes sharia (religious law) in place of ruh; sharia is the external legal-moral code, not a principal aspect of the self.","c":"This again substitutes sharia for qalb, but sharia is law rather than one of the three inner constituents (nafs, qalb, ruh).","d":"This omits nafs and inserts sharia, whereas the three aspects are nafs, qalb, and ruh, not the religious law."}},{"q":"What is the innermost being in the concentric system of Integral Yoga?","concept":"In Sri Aurobindo's Integral Yoga, the human being is pictured as a concentric system with the psychic being (the evolving soul, the divine spark within) at the innermost center, surrounded by successive envelopes of the mind, the vital (life-energy and emotions), and the body/physical. Growth involves bringing the outer parts under the guidance of the inmost psychic being. The psychic being is therefore the innermost.","traps":{"b":"The mind is one of the outer concentric layers surrounding the psychic being, not the innermost center.","c":"The vital (the realm of life-force, desires, and emotions) is an outer sheath, not the inmost being.","d":"The body/physical is the outermost layer of the concentric system, the opposite of the innermost psychic being."}},{"q":"When was the National Institute of Mental Health and Neurosciences established?","concept":"The present-day National Institute of Mental Health and Neurosciences (NIMHANS), Bengaluru, was formed in 1974 by merging the Mysore Government Mental Hospital with the All India Institute of Mental Health. Its institutional origin traces to the All India Institute of Mental Health, established in the mid-1950s. Among the given options, 1955 is treated as the intended answer, marking the founding of that predecessor institute.","traps":{"a":"1922 predates the All India Institute of Mental Health and does not correspond to NIMHANS or its recognized origin.","c":"1924 is not the founding year of the institute; it does not match either the 1955 origin or the 1974 formation of NIMHANS.","d":"1916 is far too early and does not mark any recognized founding date for NIMHANS or its predecessor."}},{"q":"What are the goodness or quality criteria for knowledge according to positivism?","concept":"In the positivist paradigm of inquiry (as contrasted by Egon Guba and Yvonna Lincoln), knowledge is judged by conventional benchmarks of methodological rigor: internal validity, external validity, reliability, and objectivity. These 'goodness' or quality criteria reflect positivism's assumption of a knowable, objective reality studied by a detached observer. Constructivist and critical paradigms replace these with alternative criteria such as trustworthiness or historical situatedness.","traps":{"a":"Historical situatedness is a quality criterion of the critical/ideological paradigm, not of positivism.","c":"Generalization by similarity (transferability) belongs to constructivist/naturalistic inquiry, not to positivist validity criteria.","d":"Vicarious experience relates to how findings are conveyed in constructivist inquiry, not to positivist standards of rigor."}},{"q":"What is the voice in inquiry according to constructivism?","concept":"In Guba and Lincoln's framework comparing inquiry paradigms, the 'voice' of the inquirer differs by paradigm. In constructivism, the inquirer's voice is the 'passionate participant,' who actively facilitates a multivoice reconstruction of the various stakeholders' constructions rather than standing apart. This contrasts with the positivist 'disinterested scientist' and the critical theorist's 'transformative intellectual.'","traps":{"a":"The 'disinterested scientist' informing decision makers is the voice of positivism/postpositivism, not constructivism.","b":"The 'transformative intellectual' as advocate and activist is the voice of critical theory, not constructivism.","d":"'None of the above' is wrong because the constructivist voice is specifically the passionate participant facilitating multivoice reconstruction."}},{"q":"What was Socrates' method of gaining knowledge?","concept":"Socrates' method of gaining knowledge was the elenchus, a dialectical technique of persistent question-and-answer cross-examination that exposes contradictions in a person's beliefs and drives them toward clearer, better-justified truth. It is a purely verbal, logical method of refutation, not empirical, and it assumes truth is uncovered by disciplined reasoning about what one already implicitly knows.","traps":{"b":"Observation and categorization describes Aristotle's naturalistic approach to knowledge, not Socrates' dialogue-based questioning.","c":"Intuition suggests direct, immediate apprehension of truth, which contradicts the effortful, back-and-forth reasoning that defines the elenchus.","d":"Experimentation is the hallmark of later empirical science, a method entirely foreign to Socrates' verbal dialectic."}},{"q":"What did Descartes' dualism influence?","concept":"Rene Descartes' mind-body dualism split reality into an immaterial thinking mind and a mechanistic, physical body, and this framing shaped the psychological schools of behaviorism (which treated the body/behavior as a mechanistic stimulus-response system) and Gestalt psychology (which took up the problem of mind and perception). The question targets psychological schools that inherited Descartes' machine-body and mind concepts, making behaviorism and Gestalt the intended pair.","traps":{"b":"Psychoanalysis and humanistic psychology grew from concerns with the unconscious and self-actualization, not directly from Cartesian dualism's mechanistic body.","c":"Existentialism and phenomenology are philosophical traditions that largely reacted against Cartesian dualism rather than being shaped by it as psychological schools.","d":"Empiricism and rationalism are epistemological philosophies, not the psychological schools the question is asking about."}},{"q":"What is the basic premise of Structuralism?","concept":"Structuralism, founded by Edward Titchener from Wilhelm Wundt's work, sought to analyze conscious experience into its basic elements (sensations, images, and feelings) and to describe how these elements combine and connect, using introspection as its method. Its premise is thus to describe the components, combinations, and connections of consciousness.","traps":{"a":"Focusing on social structures and institutions describes sociology, not the psychological school of structuralism.","b":"Emphasis on unconscious processes is the premise of psychoanalysis, not structuralism's study of conscious elements.","c":"Free will and personal agency reflect humanistic/existential psychology, opposite to structuralism's elemental analysis of consciousness."}},{"q":"What is Sullivan's interpersonal theory?","concept":"Harry Stack Sullivan's interpersonal theory holds that personality is an enduring pattern of recurring interpersonal situations, formed and expressed only through relationships with others, so the person is best understood as an open system in continuous interaction with the social environment. Personality cannot be isolated from that interpersonal field.","traps":{"a":"Emphasis on unconscious processes is the core of Freudian psychoanalysis, not Sullivan's interpersonal focus.","b":"Social structures and institutions shaping behavior is a sociological framing, broader than Sullivan's dyadic interpersonal relations.","c":"Study of observable behavior in social settings reflects behaviorism/social learning, not Sullivan's personality-as-open-system view."}},{"q":"What are the five fundamental needs identified by Erich Fromm?","concept":"Erich Fromm identified five distinctly human existential needs that arise from our loss of instinctive ties to nature: relatedness, transcendence, rootedness, a sense of identity, and a frame of orientation (a consistent way of understanding the world). These needs are unique to humans and reflect the tension of being both part of and separate from nature.","traps":{"a":"Safety, love, belonging, esteem, and self-actualization is Abraham Maslow's hierarchy of needs, not Fromm's list.","b":"Competence, autonomy, relatedness, self-esteem, and purpose blends self-determination theory constructs, not Fromm's existential needs.","c":"Hunger, thirst, shelter, rest, and reproduction are basic physiological drives, not the distinctly human needs Fromm described."}},{"q":"Who coined the term \"Mom-ism\" to describe the cultural idealization of motherhood?","concept":"The term 'momism', criticizing the cultural over-idealization and overprotectiveness of motherhood, was popularized by psychiatrist Edward Strecker in his 1946 book Their Mothers' Sons, where he blamed excessive maternal attachment for immaturity in men. Although the word is often traced to Philip Wylie, among these options Strecker is the psychiatrically grounded figure keyed as its coiner.","traps":{"a":"Sigmund Freud developed psychoanalysis and the Oedipus complex but did not coin 'momism'.","b":"Carl Jung is known for analytical psychology and archetypes, not the concept of momism.","c":"Karen Horney wrote on feminine psychology and neurosis but is not associated with coining 'momism'."}},{"q":"Which of the following effects is characterized by a discriminatory employment pattern that keeps workers, mainly women, in the lower ranks of the job scale?","concept":"The sticky floor effect refers to a discriminatory employment pattern that traps workers, predominantly women, in the lowest ranks and lowest-paid positions with little chance of upward mobility. It describes barriers at the bottom of the job ladder, in contrast to barriers encountered near the top.","traps":{"a":"The glass ceiling effect is an invisible barrier blocking advancement into top-tier positions, operating at the top of the scale, not the bottom.","b":"The maternal wall is bias specifically against mothers or pregnant workers, narrower than the general low-rank entrapment described here.","d":"The glass-cliff effect is the tendency to place women in leadership during crises when failure is likely, again a senior-level phenomenon, not lower-rank entrapment."}},{"q":"How is relative poverty measured?","concept":"Relative poverty is measured as the percentage of the population with income below some fixed fraction (commonly 50 to 60 percent) of the median income of that society. It captures inequality and relative deprivation rather than an absolute subsistence threshold, so it shifts as the society's overall income distribution changes.","traps":{"a":"Situational poverty refers to poverty caused by a sudden crisis or loss, not the income-relative-to-median metric that defines relative poverty.","c":"The percentage living in rural areas is a geographic/demographic measure, unrelated to income-based poverty thresholds.","d":"The percentage living in urban areas is likewise a location statistic, not a measure of poverty relative to median income."}},{"q":"What are the three components of Deiner's tripartite model of subjective wellbeing?","concept":"Ed Diener's tripartite model of subjective well-being has three components: a cognitive evaluation of one's life (life satisfaction), the presence of frequent positive affect, and the absence of (infrequent) negative affect. Well-being is high when someone judges their life favorably and experiences more pleasant than unpleasant emotions.","traps":{"a":"Cognitive evaluation of physical wellness substitutes bodily health for the correct cognitive component, life satisfaction.","b":"Cognitive evaluation of spiritual wellness wrongly swaps in spirituality for the life-satisfaction judgment Diener specified.","d":"Cognitive evaluation of social wellness replaces overall life satisfaction with a narrower social domain, not Diener's model."}},{"q":"What is the set point theory of weight?","concept":"Set-point theory proposes that each individual has a biologically determined weight that the body actively defends, so efforts to lose weight are met by compensatory reductions in energy expenditure and metabolic rate that pull weight back toward its set point. This homeostatic defense explains why intentional weight loss is often difficult to sustain.","traps":{"a":"Stress and anxiety disinhibiting food consumption describes the disinhibition/emotional-eating account of obesity, not a defended biological set point.","c":"Obesity spreading through social networks describes the social-contagion findings (for example, Christakis and Fowler), not set-point regulation.","d":"Dieting lowering metabolic rate and easing regain describes weight-cycling effects, a related but distinct phenomenon from the set-point premise."}},{"q":"What is Buber's concept of the 'I-Thou' attitude?","concept":"Martin Buber's I-Thou attitude is a direct, mutual, whole-person meeting in which the other is affirmed as a unique subject rather than reduced to an object, contrasted with the I-It stance that treats the other as a thing to analyze or use. In a helping relationship this means validating the client in the totality of their being as a unique and socially embedded person.","traps":{"a":"Experiencing the person as an objective entity to be systematized and analyzed describes Buber's I-It attitude, the opposite of I-Thou.","c":"Breaking the individual into universal parts is a reductive, analytic stance, again the I-It mode rather than the holistic I-Thou meeting.","d":"'None of the above' is wrong because option b accurately captures the I-Thou attitude."}},{"q":"What type of information is included in AXIS III of the DSM-IV-TR?","concept":"In the multiaxial system of the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, fourth edition, text revision (DSM-IV-TR), Axis III records general medical conditions that are potentially relevant to understanding or managing the mental disorder. The five axes separate clinical disorders, personality/intellectual disability, medical conditions, psychosocial stressors, and global functioning.","traps":{"a":"Personality disorders and mental retardation are recorded on Axis II, not Axis III.","b":"Clinical psychiatric diagnoses (the major mental disorders) are coded on Axis I.","d":"Psychosocial and environmental problems are documented on Axis IV, not Axis III."}},{"q":"What is the term used to describe the inherited vulnerability?","concept":"In the diathesis-stress model, 'diathesis' is the predisposing, typically inherited or constitutional vulnerability that makes a person susceptible to a disorder. Disorder emerges when this latent diathesis is triggered by sufficient environmental stress, so the diathesis is the underlying vulnerability rather than the trigger itself.","traps":{"a":"A stressor is the precipitating environmental event that activates the vulnerability, not the inherited predisposition itself.","b":"'Vulnerability' is the general lay term for susceptibility, but the specific technical name for the inherited predisposition is diathesis.","d":"'Enduring factor' is not the established term for inherited predisposition in the diathesis-stress framework."}},{"q":"The greater the intensity of a stimulus, greater the probability that a learned response will be elicited. This is called","concept":"Clark Hull's concept of stimulus-intensity dynamism (symbolized V) states that the vigor or probability of a learned response increases as the intensity of the eliciting stimulus increases. It is one of the intervening variables in Hull's drive-reduction equation of behavior.","traps":{"b":"Incentive motivation (K) in Hull's system reflects the magnitude or quality of the reward, not the intensity of the eliciting stimulus.","c":"Response amplitude is a measured property of the response's strength, not the principle relating stimulus intensity to response probability.","d":"Reaction threshold (sLr) is the minimum reaction potential needed for a response to occur, not the intensity-response relationship described."}},{"q":"The sudden increase in performance of an organism upon reinforcement, despite never being reinforced in the previous trials is _____________","concept":"Latent learning, demonstrated by Edward Tolman with rats in mazes, is learning that occurs without reinforcement and remains hidden in performance until a reward is introduced, at which point performance jumps sharply. It shows that learning (acquiring a cognitive map) and performance are distinct.","traps":{"a":"Observational learning involves acquiring behavior by watching a model, which the unreinforced-solo-trials description does not mention.","b":"Discrimination learning is learning to respond differently to different stimuli, not the sudden reward-triggered performance jump described.","c":"Vicarious reinforcement is learning shaped by seeing another's consequences, again a modeling process rather than latent learning."}},{"q":"Cocktail party effect is better explained by","concept":"The cocktail party effect (noticing salient information such as one's own name on an unattended channel) is best explained by Anne Treisman's attenuation model, in which unattended input is turned down or weakened rather than completely filtered out, so high-priority items can still break through. This accounts for the leakage that a strict all-or-none filter cannot.","traps":{"a":"The switch (early-selection filter) model is Donald Broadbent's account, which blocks the unattended channel entirely and cannot explain why one's name gets through.","c":"The automatic versus controlled processing model addresses how practiced tasks demand attention, not selective filtering of the cocktail party effect.","d":"Capacity (resource) theory frames attention as a limited pool allocated across tasks, which does not specifically explain the unattended-channel breakthrough."}},{"q":"Which of the following is a factor of the extent of lateral inhibition","concept":"The extent of lateral inhibition depends on how strongly the inhibitory cell is itself activated, which is set by the intensity of light falling on it: a more intensely stimulated neuron sends stronger inhibitory signals to its neighbors. Inhibitory magnitude therefore scales with the activity level (intensity) of the inhibiting cell.","traps":{"a":"Time difference between two lights concerns temporal factors, not the intensity-driven strength of lateral inhibition.","c":"Wavelength determines perceived color, not the magnitude of inhibition an inhibitory cell exerts.","d":"Frequency of the light wave (also a color/wavelength property) is not the defining determinant of inhibitory strength, which depends on intensity."}},{"q":"Lateral inhibition ________","concept":"Lateral inhibition enhances edge and contrast perception: a strongly activated receptor suppresses the activity of its less-activated neighbors, exaggerating differences at borders (as seen in Mach bands). This sharpening of contours improves detection of edges and outlines.","traps":{"b":"Dulling edge perception is the opposite of what lateral inhibition does; it sharpens rather than blurs borders.","c":"Lowering overall brightness is not its function; lateral inhibition adjusts relative contrast at edges, not global brightness.","d":"Enhancing color perception is unrelated; lateral inhibition primarily heightens contrast and edge detection, not color processing."}},{"q":"Very threatening or anxiety provoking material is often unable to gain access to conscious awareness. This is called","concept":"Repression is the psychoanalytic defense in which threatening or anxiety-provoking material is kept out of conscious awareness unconsciously and automatically, without deliberate effort. Because the person is not aware they are doing it, the disturbing content simply cannot gain access to consciousness.","traps":{"a":"Suppression is the conscious, deliberate effort to push a thought out of mind, unlike the automatic exclusion described.","c":"Inhibition is a broad term for restraining a response or process, not the specific unconscious barring of threatening material from awareness.","d":"Motivated forgetting is a broader umbrella (including both repression and suppression), so it is less precise than repression for this automatic, unconscious process."}},{"q":"Icon and echo are part of","concept":"Iconic memory (visual) and echoic memory (auditory) are the two best-known forms of sensory memory, the very brief, high-capacity stores that hold raw sensory input for a fraction of a second to a few seconds before it is either lost or selected into short-term memory. They preserve a faithful sensory trace, not a processed one.","traps":{"a":"Long-term memory stores durable, meaning-based information over long periods, far beyond the fleeting sensory traces of icon and echo.","b":"Working memory actively manipulates a small amount of information, whereas icon and echo are passive pre-attentive sensory registers.","c":"Short-term memory holds selected information briefly after it leaves the sensory store, so it comes after, and is distinct from, the iconic/echoic stage."}},{"q":"REM sleep is NOT characterized by","concept":"REM (rapid eye movement) sleep is characterized by muscle atonia (loss of muscle tone), vivid dreaming, and rapid eye movements, but its EEG is desynchronized: low-voltage, fast, mixed-frequency activity that resembles wakefulness, which is why REM is called paradoxical sleep. EEG synchrony is therefore NOT a feature of REM.","traps":{"a":"Lack of muscle tone (atonia) is a genuine feature of REM sleep, so it does not answer a 'NOT characterized by' question.","b":"Vivid dreams are strongly associated with REM sleep, making this a true characteristic rather than the exception.","c":"Rapid eye movement is the defining hallmark of REM sleep, so it is a real feature, not the odd one out."}},{"q":"The chemical that is responsible for feeling drowsy is","concept":"Adenosine is the neurochemical that accumulates in the brain during prolonged wakefulness and builds up sleep pressure, producing drowsiness. Caffeine promotes alertness precisely by blocking adenosine receptors, confirming adenosine's role in inducing sleepiness.","traps":{"b":"Acetylcholine promotes cortical arousal and REM sleep; it is not the drowsiness-inducing sleep-pressure molecule.","c":"Serotonin has complex roles in mood and sleep regulation but is not the specific substance whose buildup produces drowsiness.","d":"Orexin (hypocretin) actively promotes wakefulness and stabilizes arousal; its loss causes narcolepsy, so it opposes drowsiness."}},{"q":"Raphe nuclei produces which neurotransmitter","concept":"The raphe nuclei, a cluster of nuclei along the brainstem midline, are the principal source of serotonergic neurons and send serotonin (5-HT) projections widely throughout the brain and spinal cord. They are central to regulating mood, sleep, and arousal.","traps":{"b":"Norepinephrine is produced mainly by the locus coeruleus, not the raphe nuclei.","c":"Orexin is produced by neurons in the lateral hypothalamus, not the raphe.","d":"Histamine is produced by the tuberomammillary nucleus of the hypothalamus, not the raphe nuclei."}},{"q":"a pictorial display of a person's family relationships and medical history is ___________","concept":"A genogram is a structured pictorial diagram that maps a family's relationships across generations along with medical, psychological, and social history, using standardized symbols. It is used in family therapy and medicine to reveal patterns of illness, relationships, and dynamics, going beyond simple lineage.","traps":{"b":"A family tree depicts genealogical descent and lineage only, without the relational and medical detail a genogram encodes.","c":"'Family history' is narrative information about relatives, not a standardized pictorial display.","d":"'Gene tree' is a genetics/phylogenetics concept about gene ancestry, not a clinical family-relationship diagram."}},{"q":"Low levels of which neurotransmitter leads to aggression","concept":"Low serotonin (5-HT) function is the neurotransmitter finding most consistently linked to increased impulsive aggression, irritability, and poor impulse control. Reduced serotonergic activity is associated with disinhibited aggressive behavior in both animal and human studies.","traps":{"b":"Dopamine is chiefly implicated in reward, motivation, and motor control, not the deficiency-linked aggression pattern described.","c":"Epinephrine (adrenaline) drives the fight-or-flight physiological arousal response rather than being the aggression-linked deficiency neurotransmitter.","d":"Norepinephrine relates to arousal and stress reactivity; it is not the neurotransmitter whose low levels specifically produce aggression here."}},{"q":"people with blindness caused by damage to the visual cortex can recognize facial expressions of emotion even though they have no conscious awareness of looking at a person’s face. This is called ________________","concept":"Affective blindsight is the phenomenon in which patients with damage to the primary visual cortex can correctly discriminate emotional facial expressions despite having no conscious visual awareness of the face. It is thought to be mediated by subcortical pathways (superior colliculus to amygdala) that bypass the damaged cortex.","traps":{"b":"'Affect neglect' is not the established term for this residual, non-conscious emotion recognition after cortical blindness.","c":"Emotional neglect refers to a caregiving deficit in which a child's emotional needs go unmet, unrelated to cortical blindness.","d":"'Motivational blindsight' is not a recognized term for the emotion-discrimination-without-awareness effect described."}},{"q":"The smallest bone in the body is","concept":"The stapes (stirrup), one of the three middle-ear ossicles, is the smallest bone in the human body. It transmits vibrations from the incus to the oval window of the cochlea.","traps":{"a":"The malleus (hammer) is a middle-ear ossicle but is larger than the stapes.","b":"The incus (anvil) is another middle-ear ossicle, also larger than the stapes.","d":"The carpals are the wrist bones, far larger than the tiny stapes."}},{"q":"The _______ is rich in _________ and is responsible for colour and detailed vision","concept":"The fovea is the central pit of the retina densely packed with cone photoreceptors, and it provides high-acuity color and detailed vision under bright light. Cones support color discrimination and fine detail, which is why central foveal vision is sharpest.","traps":{"a":"The blindspot is where the optic nerve exits and has no photoreceptors at all, so it cannot support detailed vision, and it is not cone-rich.","b":"The fovea is packed with cones, not rods; rods handle dim-light, low-acuity peripheral vision and are absent at the fovea.","d":"The blindspot lacks all photoreceptors and is not rich in rods, so it cannot mediate color and detail."}},{"q":"Which of the following follows the all or none law","concept":"The action potential follows the all-or-none law: once the membrane reaches threshold, the neuron fires a spike of full, fixed amplitude regardless of how much stronger the stimulus is; below threshold, nothing fires. Stimulus strength is coded by firing frequency, not spike size.","traps":{"b":"Excitatory postsynaptic potentials are graded, varying in size with input, so they do not obey the all-or-none law.","c":"Inhibitory postsynaptic potentials are likewise graded local potentials, not all-or-none events.","d":"The resting membrane potential is a steady baseline voltage, not an all-or-none signaling event at all."}},{"q":"Schwan cells are found in","concept":"Schwann cells are the glial cells that form the myelin sheath around axons in the peripheral nervous system, that is, the nerves outside the skull and spine. In the central nervous system, this myelinating role is performed instead by oligodendrocytes.","traps":{"b":"The brain is part of the central nervous system, where oligodendrocytes, not Schwann cells, provide myelin.","c":"The spinal cord is also central nervous system tissue, myelinated by oligodendrocytes rather than Schwann cells.","d":"The basal ganglia are deep brain structures within the central nervous system, again outside the Schwann cells' peripheral territory."}},{"q":"Group of cell bodies in the PNS is called_______","concept":"In the nervous system, collections of neuronal cell bodies have different names depending on location. In the peripheral nervous system (PNS), a cluster of cell bodies is called a ganglion (plural ganglia), for example the dorsal root ganglia. In the central nervous system (CNS), the equivalent cluster is called a nucleus. So the answer is ganglia.","traps":{"a":"Nuclei are clusters of cell bodies, but in the CNS, not the PNS, so this baits confusion over the CNS vs PNS naming convention.","c":"Tracts are bundles of axons (white matter) within the CNS, not cell bodies, so this confuses fibre pathways with cell-body clusters.","d":"Nerves are bundles of axons in the PNS, the axonal counterpart of ganglia, not collections of cell bodies."}},{"q":"Corpus callosum is a part of","concept":"The corpus callosum is the large white-matter commissure of axons connecting the left and right cerebral hemispheres. The cerebral hemispheres (cerebral cortex and basal ganglia) develop embryologically from the telencephalon, the anterior part of the forebrain (prosencephalon). Because the callosum interconnects telencephalic structures, it is a part of the telencephalon.","traps":{"a":"The diencephalon gives rise to the thalamus, hypothalamus, and epithalamus, not the corpus callosum.","c":"The mesencephalon (midbrain) contains structures like the tectum and cerebral peduncles, not the cerebral commissure.","d":"The metencephalon develops into the pons and cerebellum, which are hindbrain structures unrelated to the callosum."}},{"q":"Which of the following is responsible for coordinating movement","concept":"The cerebellum is the brain's principal center for coordinating movement: it fine-tunes the timing, smoothness, balance, and accuracy of motor output by comparing intended with actual movement. It does not start movements; it refines them, which is why cerebellar damage produces ataxia and clumsy, poorly coordinated action rather than paralysis.","traps":{"b":"The primary motor cortex initiates and commands voluntary movement but does not coordinate or fine-tune it.","c":"The medulla oblongata controls autonomic vital functions such as breathing and heart rate, not movement coordination.","d":"The pons relays signals between the cerebrum and cerebellum and controls some autonomic functions, but coordination itself is the cerebellum's job."}},{"q":"Which is called the master gland","concept":"The pituitary gland is classically called the master gland because the hormones it secretes (such as TSH, ACTH, FSH, LH, and growth hormone) regulate the activity of many other endocrine glands throughout the body. Although the hypothalamus actually controls the pituitary, textbook convention reserves the 'master gland' label for the pituitary.","traps":{"b":"The hypothalamus does control the pituitary via releasing hormones, but it is a neural structure and is not the conventional textbook 'master gland' answer.","c":"The thalamus is a sensory relay station, not an endocrine gland at all.","d":"The pineal gland secretes melatonin to regulate sleep-wake cycles and does not govern other endocrine glands."}},{"q":"The posterior pituitary produces","concept":"The posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis) does not manufacture hormones itself; it stores and releases two hormones synthesized by neurons in the hypothalamus: oxytocin (involved in labor and lactation) and vasopressin, also called antidiuretic hormone (which regulates water retention). Hence the correct pair is oxytocin and vasopressin.","traps":{"b":"Oxytocin is correct but orexin is a hypothalamic neuropeptide regulating wakefulness and appetite, not a posterior pituitary hormone.","c":"Vasopressin is correct but 'vasodilators' is not a posterior pituitary hormone; vasopressin itself is a vasoconstrictor.","d":"Vasopressin is correct but norepinephrine is a catecholamine released by the adrenal medulla and sympathetic neurons, not the posterior pituitary."}},{"q":"Which of the following are directly involved in triggering eating","concept":"Feeding is controlled by opposing hypothalamic signals: orexigenic signals trigger hunger and eating, while anorexigenic signals promote satiety. Neuropeptide Y is one of the most potent orexigenic peptides, strongly stimulating food intake when released in the hypothalamus. So it is the one directly involved in triggering eating.","traps":{"a":"Leptin is secreted by fat cells and signals satiety, suppressing appetite, so it inhibits rather than triggers eating.","b":"CART (cocaine- and amphetamine-regulated transcript) is an anorexigenic peptide that reduces feeding.","c":"Cholecystokinin is a gut hormone released after eating that promotes satiety and stops feeding."}},{"q":"What new method did Wechsler invent for obtaining the IQ score in the Wechsler-Bellevue Intelligence Scales?","concept":"David Wechsler abandoned the mental-age ratio IQ used by the Stanford-Binet and introduced the deviation IQ: a standard score with a mean of 100 and standard deviation of 15, comparing a person's performance to that of same-age peers. In this item that standard-score method is labeled 'Standard IQ', making option a correct.","traps":{"b":"An age-relative formula that divides by age describes the older ratio IQ concept, which is exactly what Wechsler replaced.","c":"Mental age IQ is the Stanford-Binet ratio approach (mental age over chronological age times 100), not Wechsler's innovation.","d":"'Physical age IQ' is not a recognized psychometric method; it is a distractor."}},{"q":"Which test can diagnose if an examinee consistently makes more errors on one side of the body than the other?","concept":"The Finger Localization Test is a tactile/sensory measure in which the examinee identifies which finger is touched on each hand. Because sensory pathways cross, consistently more errors on one side of the body point to dysfunction in the opposite (contralateral) cerebral hemisphere, so it can reveal lateralized deficits.","traps":{"b":"The Continuous Performance Test measures sustained attention and vigilance, not side-specific sensory errors.","c":"A Sustained Attention Test assesses the ability to maintain focus over time, not lateralized body performance.","d":"The Test of Everyday Attention measures multiple attentional abilities in everyday tasks, not tactile lateralization."}},{"q":"What is NOT an example of executive function?","concept":"Executive functions are higher-order, frontally-mediated control processes that direct and manage other cognitive abilities, including conceptualization, planning, organization, and logical analysis. Auditory processing is a basic sensory/perceptual function of hearing and is not an executive function, so it is the correct 'NOT' answer.","traps":{"a":"Conceptualization (abstract thinking and forming concepts) is a genuine executive function, so it is not the exception.","b":"Planning ahead and sequencing steps toward a goal is a core executive function.","c":"Logical analysis and reasoning is an executive function, so it does not fit the 'NOT' requirement."}},{"q":"What is the purpose of the STAI?","concept":"The State-Trait Anxiety Inventory (STAI), developed by Charles Spielberger, was designed to distinguish two forms of anxiety: state anxiety (a transient emotional reaction to a situation) and trait anxiety (a stable, dispositional tendency to be anxious). Its purpose is therefore to differentiate state from trait anxiety.","traps":{"a":"Measuring both normal and abnormal personality describes broad inventories like the MMPI, not the anxiety-specific STAI.","b":"Assessing the validity of responses describes validity scales (as in the MMPI), not the STAI's purpose.","d":"Measuring psychoticism, extraversion, and neuroticism (P-E-N) describes Eysenck's EPQ, not the STAI."}},{"q":"Which strategy did Comrey pursue in developing his 180-item test?","concept":"Andrew Comrey built the Comrey Personality Scales using a factor-analytic strategy, statistically analyzing item intercorrelations to derive a set of underlying personality factors (eight in his model). This method contrasts with empirically keyed tests, so the answer is factor-analytic.","traps":{"a":"'Correlational' is too vague; the defining feature was factor analysis specifically, which reduces correlations to latent factors.","b":"Criterion-referencing (empirical criterion keying) is the strategy behind the MMPI, not the Comrey scales.","d":"An experimental strategy involves manipulating variables, which is not how a personality inventory is constructed."}},{"q":"The tendency to explain others’ actions as stemming from dispositions even in the presence of situational causes is","concept":"Correspondence bias, also known as the fundamental attribution error, is the tendency to explain others' behavior in terms of their internal dispositions while underweighting situational causes, even when situational information is available. This is exactly what the item describes.","traps":{"a":"Belief bias is the tendency to judge an argument's logical validity by whether its conclusion is believable, not an attribution error.","b":"Self-serving bias concerns attributions about one's own outcomes (crediting success internally, blaming failure externally), not others' actions.","d":"Confirmation bias is the tendency to seek and favor information that supports one's existing beliefs, unrelated to dispositional attribution."}},{"q":"Which of the following is false about social loafing","concept":"Social loafing is the tendency to exert less effort in a group than when working alone. It is reduced when individual contributions can be identified and evaluated. Therefore evaluation apprehension being PRESENT (not absent) lowers loafing, so the claim that groups loaf less when evaluation apprehension is absent is false.","traps":{"a":"This is a true statement: challenging, appealing, or involving tasks genuinely reduce loafing, so it is not the false one.","b":"This is true: believing high effort brings rewards increases motivation and reduces loafing.","d":"This is true: loafing decreases when group members are friends, so it is not the false statement."}},{"q":"Persuasion though systematic processing is the __________________ and  involves _________________","concept":"In the Elaboration Likelihood Model, systematic (effortful) processing corresponds to the central route to persuasion, where people carefully attend to and evaluate the quality of the arguments. This route produces lasting attitude change when motivation and ability are high, so the answer is central route; careful attention.","traps":{"b":"Peripheral route with mental shortcuts describes heuristic processing, the opposite of systematic central-route processing.","c":"The central route does not rely on mental shortcuts; shortcuts belong to the peripheral route.","d":"The peripheral route does not involve careful attention; it uses superficial cues instead."}},{"q":"Trivialization and self-affirmation can reduce","concept":"Cognitive dissonance is the uncomfortable tension from holding inconsistent cognitions. Besides changing attitudes or behavior, people reduce it through trivialization (deciding the inconsistent cognitions are unimportant) and self-affirmation (reaffirming valued aspects of the self). Both strategies specifically reduce cognitive dissonance.","traps":{"b":"Compliance is publicly going along with a request; it is not what trivialization and self-affirmation target.","c":"Reactance is the motivational push-back against threats to freedom, reduced by different means, not these dissonance strategies.","d":"Self-regulation refers to controlling one's own responses toward goals, not the tension reduced by these two techniques."}},{"q":"What are the three components of empathy?","concept":"Empathy is commonly modeled with three structural components: an emotional/affective aspect (sharing another's feelings), a cognitive component (perspective-taking, understanding another's mind), and a behavioral aspect (acting on that understanding, such as helping). Option b lists exactly these three.","traps":{"a":"This mixes in 'positivity', which is not one of the three structural components of empathy.","c":"This includes empathic concern, which is a sub-facet of the emotional side rather than a distinct structural component here, and omits the behavioral aspect.","d":"This substitutes empathic concern for the emotional aspect, dropping the core affective component from the standard trio."}},{"q":"__________ is the tendency for an individual surrounded by a group of strangers to hesitate and not help because none of the bystanders knows for sure what is happening","concept":"Pluralistic ignorance occurs when each person in a group privately feels uncertain but, seeing everyone else outwardly calm, concludes that nothing is wrong. In an emergency this shared misreading suppresses helping because no one is sure anything is actually happening, which matches the definition given.","traps":{"a":"The bystander effect is the overall reduced likelihood of helping when others are present; it is the outcome, not the specific mechanism of misreading others' calm.","c":"Diffusion of responsibility is the belief that someone else will act because responsibility is shared, a different mechanism from ambiguity about what is happening.","d":"'Amotivation to help' is not a recognized bystander-intervention concept and is a distractor."}},{"q":"choose the correct arrangement of events in group formation","concept":"Bruce Tuckman's model of group development proceeds through five stages in order: forming, storming, norming, performing, and adjourning. Only option b arranges them correctly.","traps":{"a":"This wrongly places adjourning before norming and performing, disrupting Tuckman's sequence.","c":"This puts performing second, before norming and storming, which reverses the developmental order.","d":"This lists norming before storming and places adjourning before performing, both out of order."}},{"q":"The phenomenon in which groups make more extreme decisions than as individuals is called","concept":"Group polarization is the tendency for group discussion to intensify members' pre-existing attitudes, so the group's final decision is more extreme (in the direction they already leaned) than the average of members' individual positions. This describes groups making more extreme decisions than individuals.","traps":{"b":"'Extremity' is not the established technical term for this phenomenon; it is a loose distractor.","c":"Groupthink is the drive for consensus that suppresses dissent and critical evaluation, not specifically the intensification of leanings.","d":"Conformity is aligning one's behavior with a group norm, which does not by itself mean the decision becomes more extreme."}},{"q":"The research method that writes about people and cultures is called","concept":"Ethnography is the qualitative research method dedicated to describing and interpreting the shared culture, customs, and everyday practices of a group of people, often through immersive fieldwork and participant observation. Because it 'writes about people and cultures', ethnography is the answer.","traps":{"a":"Narrative analysis studies the stories individuals tell to make sense of their experiences, not the culture of a whole group.","c":"Phenomenology examines the essence of individuals' lived, subjective experience, not cultural description.","d":"Grounded theory aims to build theory inductively from data, not to describe a culture."}},{"q":"Which of the sampling methods involves a two stage method","concept":"Cluster sampling is a probability method that proceeds in stages: first the population is divided into clusters (often geographic) and a random sample of clusters is selected, then units are sampled within the chosen clusters. This two-stage (or multistage) structure is its defining feature.","traps":{"a":"Proportionate stratified random sampling divides the population into strata and samples each in a single stage, not two.","b":"Disproportionate stratified random sampling likewise samples within predefined strata in one stage.","d":"Quota sampling is a non-probability method that fills preset category quotas, without a two-stage clustering process."}},{"q":"A t-distribution is a similar to a normal distribution in the way, where ______________ .","concept":"The t-distribution resembles the normal distribution in being symmetric and bell-shaped about its mean. It differs by having heavier tails (higher kurtosis) and greater variability, especially at small degrees of freedom, converging to the normal only as sample size grows. The shared property is therefore symmetry.","traps":{"a":"'None of the above' is wrong because there genuinely is a shared feature, symmetry.","c":"Kurtosis is not the same; the t-distribution has heavier tails and higher kurtosis than the normal.","d":"Variability is not the same; the t-distribution has greater variability than the standard normal."}},{"q":"the df for 'within groups' in one way ANOVA with three levels on intelligence and 10 samples per level is","concept":"In one-way ANOVA the within-groups (error) degrees of freedom equal N minus k, where N is the total number of observations and k is the number of groups. With three levels and 10 participants per level, N = 30 and k = 3, so within-groups df = 30 - 3 = 27.","traps":{"a":"29 is the total degrees of freedom (N - 1), not the within-groups df.","c":"30 is simply the total sample size N, not a degrees-of-freedom value.","d":"31 does not correspond to any correct df here; it appears to add rather than subtract."}},{"q":"What is the degrees of freedom on an independent samples t-test with a total sample size of 25 in each group.","concept":"For an independent-samples t-test the degrees of freedom equal the total sample size minus 2 (one lost per group mean estimated). With 25 in each group the total N is 50, so df = 50 - 2 = 48.","traps":{"b":"24 is the df for a single sample of 25 (n - 1), not for the two-group comparison.","c":"49 would be N - 1, the df for one combined sample, not an independent-samples t-test.","d":"25 is the size of one group, not a degrees-of-freedom value."}},{"q":"F ratio in one-way ANOVA is given by","concept":"In one-way ANOVA the F ratio is the mean square between groups divided by the mean square within groups (MS between / MS within). It compares systematic, between-group variance to random within-group error variance; a large ratio suggests real group differences.","traps":{"b":"Using MS total in the numerator is incorrect; total variance is partitioned into between and within, not used directly as a numerator.","c":"Placing MS within over MS total inverts and misconstrues the ratio; error variance is not divided by total.","d":"MS within over MS between inverts the correct F ratio, putting error variance on top."}},{"q":"The most appropriate non-parametric alternative for a related samples t-test when using an ordinal level of variable with some magnitude is","concept":"When comparing two related (matched or repeated) measures on ordinal data that also carry information about the magnitude of differences, the appropriate nonparametric test is the Wilcoxon matched-pairs signed-rank test, the nonparametric counterpart of the paired-samples t-test.","traps":{"a":"The Mann-Whitney U test is for two independent samples, not related or matched samples.","c":"The Chi-square test analyzes frequencies of categorical (nominal) data, not paired ordinal magnitudes.","d":"The sign test handles matched pairs but only uses the direction of differences, ignoring their magnitude, so it is less appropriate when magnitude matters."}},{"q":"According to Wallas' model of the creative process, what is the second stage?","concept":"Graham Wallas described the creative process in four stages: preparation, incubation, illumination, and verification. The second stage is incubation, in which the problem is set aside and unconscious processing continues.","traps":{"a":"Illumination is the third stage (the flash of insight), not the second.","c":"Verification is the fourth and final stage, where the solution is tested and refined.","d":"Preparation is the first stage, in which the problem is investigated, not the second."}},{"q":"What is the illumination stage of Wallas' model of the creative process?","concept":"In Wallas' model the illumination stage is the moment the solution suddenly emerges into consciousness, the 'aha' or insight experience, typically following a period of incubation. Option d, achieving insight to the problem, captures this.","traps":{"a":"Formulating the problem and making initial attempts describes the preparation stage, not illumination.","b":"Testing and carrying out the solution describes the verification stage.","c":"Leaving the problem while considering other things describes the incubation stage."}},{"q":"According to Sternberg's investment theory, how many distinct but interrelated resources are required for creativity?","concept":"Robert Sternberg and Todd Lubart's investment theory of creativity holds that creativity requires the convergence of six distinct but interrelated resources: intellectual abilities, knowledge, thinking styles, personality, motivation, and environment.","traps":{"a":"Five understates the count; the theory specifies six resources.","b":"Four is incorrect; it does not match the six-resource model.","c":"Seven overstates the count of resources in the investment theory."}},{"q":"What is the difference between functional fixedness and mental set?","concept":"Functional fixedness is the tendency to perceive an object only in terms of its familiar, conventional function, limiting how physical objects are used in problem solving. Mental set is the tendency to keep using a previously successful problem-solving strategy even when a better one exists. Option c states this distinction correctly.","traps":{"a":"This reverses the two: it wrongly assigns problem-solving strategies to functional fixedness and physical objects to mental set.","b":"There is a real difference between the two concepts, so claiming no difference is incorrect.","d":"This mischaracterizes functional fixedness in terms of overactive top-down processing rather than fixed object function, making it an inaccurate definition."}},{"q":"Why does functional fixedness occur?","concept":"Functional fixedness is a type of mental set in which prior experience with an object's conventional use blocks you from seeing a novel use for it (for example, failing to use a coin as a screwdriver). This happens because top-down, schema-driven processing (knowledge about what an object is 'for') dominates over bottom-up perception of the object's raw properties. It reflects an overactive application of learned expectations, not any deficit in ability or resources.","traps":{"a":"It is not caused by lacking problem-solving skill; even highly skilled people fall prey to functional fixedness because the block is perceptual and schema-driven, not a competence gap.","c":"Underestimating the environment is not the mechanism; functional fixedness is about over-applying an object's known function, and the object itself is fully present in the environment.","d":"Limited access to tools describes a resource shortage, whereas functional fixedness occurs precisely when the needed object is available but its unusual use goes unseen."}},{"q":"What are heuristics in problem-solving?","concept":"A heuristic is a mental shortcut or rule of thumb that usually yields a good solution quickly but does not guarantee correctness. It trades accuracy for speed and low cognitive effort. This contrasts with an algorithm, a step-by-step procedure that is slower but guaranteed to reach the correct solution if one exists.","traps":{"b":"A method that always produces a solution defines an algorithm, not a heuristic; heuristics can fail.","c":"Heuristics are not simply 'better' than algorithms; they are faster but less reliable, so this overstates their value.","d":"Heuristics are less accurate than algorithms, not more; their advantage is speed and effort economy, not precision."}},{"q":"What is the hill-climbing heuristic in problem-solving?","concept":"The hill-climbing heuristic evaluates the available moves and selects the one that appears to bring you closest to the goal state at each step, like always walking uphill toward a summit. It is a greedy, local strategy that can get stuck when reaching the goal temporarily requires moving away from it. Compare it with means-ends analysis (subgoaling) and analogy, which are different problem-solving strategies.","traps":{"a":"Using a solution to an earlier, similar problem describes reasoning by analogy, not hill-climbing.","b":"Breaking a problem into smaller parts and solving each is means-ends analysis or subgoaling, a distinct heuristic.","c":"Trying different solutions until one works is trial and error, which lacks hill-climbing's directed, closest-to-goal selection rule."}},{"q":"What are the three aspects of intelligence in Sternberg's Triarchic Theory?","concept":"Robert Sternberg's Triarchic Theory of intelligence proposes three interacting aspects: componential (analytical) intelligence for analyzing and evaluating, experiential (creative) intelligence for handling novelty and insight, and contextual (practical) intelligence for adapting to real-world environments. These describe how intelligence operates rather than listing separate ability domains.","traps":{"a":"Linguistic, logical-mathematical, and spatial are three of Howard Gardner's multiple intelligences, not Sternberg's triarchic components.","c":"Social, emotional, and spiritual are popular 'quotient' categories, not part of Sternberg's model.","d":"Physical, cognitive, and emotional is a generic developmental grouping unrelated to the Triarchic Theory."}},{"q":"What are the five major parts of emotional intelligence, according to Goleman?","concept":"In his original formulation, Daniel Goleman described five domains of emotional intelligence: knowing one's own emotions, managing emotions, motivating oneself, recognizing emotions in others (empathy), and handling relationships. The classic phrasing tested here matches these five verbatim. Note that Goleman later relabeled them as self-awareness, self-regulation, motivation, empathy, and social skills.","traps":{"a":"Interpersonal, intrapersonal, social, cognitive, and communication skills is an invented list not corresponding to Goleman's five domains.","c":"Self-awareness, self-regulation, motivation, empathy, and social skills is Goleman's later relabeling, but the question asks for the classic original phrasing in option b.","d":"Intelligence, emotional, social, cultural, and spiritual quotients mix several separate 'quotient' constructs and are not Goleman's model."}},{"q":"What are the two basic mental operations that Piaget hypothesized to be involved in learning?","concept":"Jean Piaget held that learning and adaptation occur through two complementary operations: assimilation, fitting new information into existing mental schemas, and accommodation, altering schemas when new information does not fit. The balancing of these two processes (equilibration) drives cognitive development.","traps":{"a":"Encoding and retrieval are stages in the information-processing model of memory, not Piaget's operations.","b":"Attention and perception are basic cognitive processes, not the schema-based operations Piaget proposed.","d":"Sensory input and motor output describe a stimulus-response arc, not Piaget's mental operations of adaptation."}},{"q":"What are parapraxes, according to Freud's theory?","concept":"In Sigmund Freud's theory, parapraxes are everyday errors of speech, memory, or action, such as slips of the tongue, forgetting names, or misplacing objects. Freud argued these mistakes are not random but reveal repressed unconscious wishes or conflicts. Their content is not inherently sexual; any hidden unconscious material can leak through.","traps":{"a":"Parapraxes are not by definition sexual; they reveal any unconscious content, so restricting them to sexual errors is too narrow.","c":"Praised, correct behaviors relate to forming the ego-ideal, not to parapraxes.","d":"Punished behaviors forming the conscience describe superego development, unrelated to slips of the tongue."}},{"q":"What are Freudian slips, according to Freud's theory?","concept":"A Freudian slip is the popular name for a parapraxis: an error in speech or action that betrays an unconscious thought, wish, or feeling. It reveals the unconscious rather than being random noise. Despite its reputation, a Freudian slip is not by definition sexual; it is any mistake that exposes hidden mental content.","traps":{"b":"Freudian slips are not inherently sexual; they reveal any repressed content, so limiting them to sexual errors is a misconception.","c":"Praised, correct behaviors concern the ego-ideal, not Freudian slips.","d":"Punished behaviors that form the conscience describe superego formation, not slips that reveal the unconscious."}},{"q":"What is the phallic stage of development, according to Freud's theory?","concept":"In Freud's psychosexual stages, the phallic stage (roughly ages 3 to 6) centers the genitals as the erogenous zone and is marked by the Oedipus complex (Electra complex in girls), producing incestuous fantasies toward the opposite-sex parent and rivalry with the same-sex parent. Resolving this conflict leads to identification and superego formation.","traps":{"a":"The mouth as primary erogenous zone describes the oral stage, the earliest psychosexual stage.","b":"Toilet training interfering with gratification from defecation describes the anal stage.","d":"Sublimation of the sex instinct describes the latency stage that follows the phallic stage."}},{"q":"What are the two parts of the superego, according to Freud's theory?","concept":"Freud divided the superego into two parts: the conscience, which internalizes prohibitions and punishes transgressions with guilt, and the ego-ideal, which internalizes standards of approved, ideal behavior and rewards them with pride. Together they represent the moralizing part of the personality, separate from the id and ego.","traps":{"a":"The id and the ego are two of the three separate structures of personality, not subdivisions of the superego.","b":"The pleasure principle and the reality principle govern the id and the ego respectively, not the superego's parts.","c":"The ego and the unconscious are different constructs entirely; the unconscious is a level of awareness, not a component of the superego."}},{"q":"What is the anima, according to Jung's theory of archetypes?","concept":"In Carl Jung's theory of archetypes, the anima is the unconscious feminine element within a man, while the animus is the unconscious masculine element within a woman. These contrasexual archetypes shape how each sex relates to the other. The persona and shadow are separate archetypes.","traps":{"a":"The male element within a woman is the animus, the counterpart of the anima, not the anima itself.","c":"The socially acceptable front we present to others is the persona archetype.","d":"The dark, unacceptable side of personality is the shadow archetype."}},{"q":"What are the two attitudes in Jung's theory of personality?","concept":"Carl Jung distinguished two fundamental attitudes, or orientations of psychic energy: extraversion, directing energy outward toward the external world, and introversion, directing it inward toward the inner world. These attitudes combine with his four functions to form personality types.","traps":{"a":"Rational and irrational is Jung's grouping of the four functions (thinking/feeling versus sensing/intuiting), not the two attitudes.","b":"Sensing and intuiting are two of Jung's four functions (the irrational, perceiving pair), not attitudes.","d":"Thinking and feeling are two of Jung's four functions (the rational, judging pair), not the two attitudes."}},{"q":"Which of the following is the correct sequence of Erikson's stages of psychosocial development?","concept":"Erik Erikson's eight psychosocial stages unfold in a fixed order: basic trust versus mistrust, autonomy versus shame and doubt, initiative versus guilt, industry versus inferiority, identity versus role confusion, intimacy versus isolation, generativity versus stagnation, and integrity versus despair. The correct answer is the only option listing stages in their true chronological sequence without out-of-order entries.","traps":{"b":"This option omits trust versus mistrust and starts at autonomy, skipping initiative versus guilt, so it is not the full correct sequence.","c":"Placing initiative versus guilt first drops the earlier trust and autonomy stages, giving an incorrect starting point.","d":"This mixes stages out of order, jumping from initiative to intimacy and skipping industry and identity."}},{"q":"What are the four processes involved in observational learning, according to Bandura's social learning theory?","concept":"In Albert Bandura's social learning theory, observational learning requires four sub-processes in sequence: attention (noticing the model), retention (remembering what was observed), production or motor reproduction (being able to enact the behavior), and motivation (having a reason or incentive to perform it). All four must be present for modeling to translate into behavior.","traps":{"a":"Sensory, perceptional, cognitive, affective is a generic processing list, not Bandura's four modeling sub-processes.","c":"Encoding, storage, retrieval, evaluation describes memory processing, not observational learning.","d":"Stimulus, response, reinforcement, extinction are operant conditioning terms, not the components of modeling."}},{"q":"What are cardinal traits in Allport's theory?","concept":"In Gordon Allport's trait hierarchy, a cardinal trait is a single, dominant trait so pervasive that it organizes and directs nearly all of a person's behavior; such traits are rare (think of a name becoming an adjective, like 'Machiavellian'). Below them are central traits and secondary traits.","traps":{"a":"The least important, inconspicuous, inconsistent traits are secondary traits, not cardinal traits.","b":"The handful of outstanding traits describing a person's behavior are central traits, one level below cardinal.","d":"Traits shared across members of a culture are common traits, a separate category from the individual cardinal trait."}},{"q":"What are L-data in Cattell's theory?","concept":"Raymond Cattell classified personality data into three sources: L-data (life-record data) drawn from observable everyday behavior and records such as school or employment history, Q-data from questionnaires and self-reports, and T-data from objective, controlled test situations. L-data reflect how a person actually behaves in real life as documented by others.","traps":{"a":"Data from self-reports and questionnaires are Q-data, not L-data.","b":"Data from a controlled test situation with rated responses are T-data (objective test data).","d":"The 16 basic personality traits are Cattell's source traits (the 16PF), not a category of data."}},{"q":"What is the need for affiliation (n Aff) in Murray's theory?","concept":"In Henry Murray's taxonomy of psychogenic needs, the need for affiliation (n Aff) is the drive to form friendly, cooperative relationships, to seek companionship, and to belong. It underlies sociability and the desire to be liked. It is distinct from other named needs such as power and achievement.","traps":{"b":"The need for power and control over others is Murray's need for dominance or power (n Power), not affiliation.","c":"The need for recognition and success is the need for achievement (n Achievement).","d":"The need for attention and excitement corresponds to needs like exhibition or excitement, not affiliation."}},{"q":"What are the five needs in Maslow's hierarchy of needs, arranged in order from strongest at the bottom to weakest at the top?","concept":"Abraham Maslow's hierarchy of needs is arranged from the most basic and prepotent at the bottom to higher-order needs at the top: physiological, safety, belongingness and love, esteem, and finally self-actualization. Lower needs generally must be reasonably satisfied before higher ones become motivating.","traps":{"a":"This scrambles the order, placing esteem at the bottom and physiological in the middle, which is not Maslow's sequence.","c":"This reverses safety and physiological and misorders esteem and belongingness, so it is not the correct hierarchy.","d":"This lists the hierarchy top-to-bottom (self-actualization first), the reverse of the requested bottom-to-top order."}},{"q":"What is learned helplessness, according to Seligman?","concept":"Martin Seligman's concept of learned helplessness describes the passivity that develops after an organism is exposed to uncontrollable aversive events: having learned that its actions do not affect outcomes, it stops trying and fails to escape even when escape later becomes possible. It became an influential model for depression.","traps":{"b":"Learning to escape punishment through trial and error is successful escape learning, the opposite of helplessness.","c":"Learning to avoid punishment through reinforcement is avoidance learning, again the opposite of giving up.","d":"Becoming aggressive in response to punishment describes an aggression reaction, not the passive resignation of learned helplessness."}},{"q":"At which level of moral reasoning do people act under external controls and obey rules to avoid punishment or reap rewards?","concept":"In Lawrence Kohlberg's theory of moral development, the preconventional level (Level I) is where moral reasoning is controlled by external consequences: people obey rules to avoid punishment or to gain concrete rewards, without internalized principles. This is typical of young children.","traps":{"b":"Conventional morality (Level II) bases judgments on social approval and maintaining law and order, not simply avoiding punishment or seeking rewards.","c":"Postconventional morality (Level III) is governed by abstract, self-chosen ethical principles, well beyond external controls.","d":"'None of the above' is wrong because Level I precisely fits reasoning driven by punishment and reward."}},{"q":"At which stage of Level II of Kohlberg’s moral development are people concerned with doing their duty, showing respect for higher authority, and maintaining the social order?","concept":"Kohlberg's conventional level (Level II) has two stages. Stage 4, the law-and-order orientation (here labeled 'social concern and conscience'), focuses on doing one's duty, respecting authority, and maintaining the social order for its own sake. Stage 3, by contrast, seeks interpersonal approval.","traps":{"a":"Maintaining mutual relations, seeking approval, and the golden rule describe Stage 3 (good boy/nice girl orientation), not the duty-and-order Stage 4.","c":"Morality of contract, individual rights, and democratically accepted law is Stage 5, a postconventional (Level III) stage.","d":"Morality of universal ethical principles is Stage 6, the highest postconventional stage, not part of Level II."}},{"q":"How does Vygotsky's theory differ from Piaget's theory of cognitive development?","concept":"Lev Vygotsky's sociocultural theory holds that cognitive development is driven by social interaction, and that many skills children acquire through guidance from more competent others are specific to their particular culture. Jean Piaget, by contrast, proposed universal, invariant cognitive structures and stages that all children pass through regardless of culture. The key difference is cultural specificity versus universality.","traps":{"a":"This reverses the theorists; universal, uniform stages are Piaget's claim, whereas Vygotsky stressed culturally variable development.","b":"Vygotsky did not say development is solely environmental; this overstates his position and misrepresents the contrast with Piaget.","d":"Calling Vygotsky's view 'predetermined' and Piaget's 'flexible' inverts their theories, since Piaget emphasized fixed maturational stages."}},{"q":"What is the zone of proximal development (ZPD)?","concept":"The zone of proximal development (ZPD), from Vygotsky, is the gap between what a learner can accomplish independently and what they can achieve with guidance from a more skilled partner. Tasks in this zone are too hard to do alone but attainable with support (scaffolding), and this is where learning is most effective.","traps":{"a":"Tasks too simple to need help fall below the ZPD, in the range the learner has already mastered.","c":"Tasks too complex even with guidance lie beyond the ZPD, out of reach for now.","d":"Tasks learned through experience rather than instruction is a vague description that misses the ZPD's defining feature of assisted performance."}},{"q":"What is the first grasp that infants develop?","concept":"Infant grasping develops in a predictable proximodistal sequence. The first grip is the ulnar grasp, a crude grip that presses an object against the palm using the little-finger (ulnar) side of the hand with little thumb use. It later gives way to the palmar grasp and finally the precise pincer grasp using thumb and forefinger.","traps":{"a":"The palmar grasp, using the whole palm, develops after the ulnar grasp, not first.","b":"The pincer grasp, a precise thumb-and-forefinger grip, is the last to develop, around the end of the first year.","d":"The radial grasp, shifting toward the thumb side of the hand, emerges later than the initial ulnar grasp."}},{"q":"What is the clumsy, claw-like grip that that permits little tactile exploration?","concept":"The ulnar grasp is the early, clumsy, claw-like grip in which an infant presses an object into the palm using the fingers on the little-finger (ulnar) side, with the thumb not yet involved. Because the thumb and fingertips are not used, it permits little tactile exploration. It precedes the more refined pincer grasp.","traps":{"a":"The pincer grasp is the precise thumb-and-forefinger grip that allows fine manipulation, the opposite of the clumsy ulnar grasp.","c":"The radial grasp shifts toward the thumb side and is more advanced than the crude, claw-like ulnar grasp.","d":"The digital grasp involves the fingers more precisely and is not the earliest claw-like grip described here."}},{"q":"What is the language-making capacity (LMC) proposed by Dan Slobin?","concept":"Dan Slobin's Language-Making Capacity (LMC) is a set of specialized cognitive and perceptual abilities, called operating principles, that children bring to the task of learning language. It offers a softer, more general alternative to Noam Chomsky's Language Acquisition Device (LAD), which posits an innate, grammar-specific module. The LMC is about broad processing biases rather than a built-in grammar.","traps":{"a":"An inborn linguistic processor activated by verbal input better describes Chomsky's LAD, not Slobin's more general operating principles.","b":"Knowledge of rules common to all languages refers to universal grammar, again a Chomskyan idea rather than Slobin's LMC.","d":"'All of the above' is wrong because the LMC is specifically the cognitive-perceptual abilities option, not the innate-module descriptions."}},{"q":"What is the receptive-expressive gap ?","concept":"The receptive-expressive gap is the developmental lag between language comprehension (receptive language) and language production (expressive language). Infants and young children understand far more words than they can actually say, so their receptive vocabulary outpaces their expressive vocabulary.","traps":{"b":"The gap concerns language specifically, not general cognitive abilities, so framing it as receptive versus expressive cognition is too broad.","c":"It is about understanding versus producing language, not motor skills.","d":"It concerns language comprehension versus production, not social skills."}},{"q":"What are the four skill modules of DBT?","concept":"Marsha Linehan's Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT) teaches four skills modules: mindfulness, interpersonal effectiveness, emotion regulation, and distress tolerance. These balance acceptance-oriented skills (mindfulness, distress tolerance) with change-oriented skills (emotion regulation, interpersonal effectiveness).","traps":{"a":"This substitutes cognitive restructuring and relaxation for emotion regulation and distress tolerance; those two are not DBT modules.","c":"This wrongly includes cognitive restructuring and relaxation and drops interpersonal effectiveness and distress tolerance.","d":"This includes cognitive restructuring, which is a CBT technique, not one of the four DBT modules, and omits mindfulness."}},{"q":"Which therapeutic technique involves asking a person to play the role of an exhibitionist even if they claim to suffer from severe inhibitions and excessive timidity?","concept":"In Gestalt therapy, the exaggeration exercise asks the client to amplify or dramatically act out a feeling, gesture, or the opposite of a stated trait so that suppressed or disowned material surfaces into awareness. Asking a self-described timid person to play an exhibitionist exaggerates the disowned pole and heightens emotional contact with it.","traps":{"a":"The reversal exercise has the client enact behavior opposite to their usual pattern, but it targets reversing one's own overt behavior rather than exaggerating a trait to an extreme role.","c":"The rehearsal exercise involves practicing or voicing the internal rehearsing we do before social acts, not amplifying a trait.","d":"The future projection technique has the client imagine and enact a future scenario, which is unrelated to exaggerating a present trait."}},{"q":"Which type of therapeutic technique in REBT involves using imagery?","concept":"Albert Ellis's Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy (REBT) groups techniques into cognitive, emotive, and behavioral categories. Imagery-based methods such as rational emotive imagery belong to the emotive (experiential) techniques, because they work on and shift the client's feelings. Cognitive methods dispute beliefs and behavioral methods assign actions.","traps":{"a":"Cognitive methods in REBT focus on disputing irrational beliefs verbally, not on using imagery.","c":"Behavioral techniques use action-oriented homework and exposure, not mental imagery.","d":"Psychoeducational methods teach the ABC model and concepts, and are not the imagery-based category."}}],"pep-mock-2":[{"q":"How many General Principles are included in the APA's Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct?","concept":"The American Psychological Association's Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct opens with five aspirational General Principles: A) Beneficence and Nonmaleficence, B) Fidelity and Responsibility, C) Integrity, D) Justice, and E) Respect for People's Rights and Dignity. These are broad ideals meant to guide and inspire, and unlike the enforceable Ethical Standards that follow, they are not themselves grounds for sanction. Knowing the count is five, mapped to letters A through E, is a common exam anchor.","traps":{"a":"One would suggest a single overarching principle, but the APA code deliberately separates its aspirational ideals into five distinct headings.","b":"Three understates the list and may confuse the General Principles with a smaller grouping, but the code names five.","d":"Seven overcounts, perhaps by conflating the aspirational Principles with the many enforceable Standards, but there are exactly five General Principles."}},{"q":"According to the United Nations Convention on the Rights of the Child, what are children capable of?","concept":"The United Nations Convention on the Rights of the Child affirms that children can form their own views and are entitled to express them, so they can give voice to assent (agreement) or dissent (refusal), and those views must be given due weight according to age and maturity. It does not treat children as legally competent to consent to treatment independently; that authority typically rests with a parent or guardian while the child's assent is still sought. The key idea is participation and voice, not full legal decision-making power.","traps":{"a":"Understanding complex legal terminology is not what the Convention claims; it emphasises voice and participation, not legal literacy.","b":"Providing consent for psychotherapy overstates the child's legal standing, since informed consent generally requires an adult guardian while the child gives assent.","d":"Making important life decisions independently exceeds what the Convention grants; it protects the child's right to be heard, not autonomous decision authority."}},{"q":"Under what circumstances therapists may accept gifts?","concept":"Accepting gifts from clients is generally discouraged in therapy because it can blur boundaries and create dual relationships, but ethics codes allow clinical judgment. A gift may be accepted when refusing it would harm the therapeutic alliance and accepting it genuinely serves the client's growth, for example on the reasoning that it would help promote the client's self-acceptance. The decision must be driven by clinical and therapeutic considerations, not social convention or personal gain.","traps":{"a":"Without any specific reason ignores the requirement that gift acceptance be clinically justified rather than casual.","b":"Only on festive occasions frames the decision as a social or cultural custom, but the ethical rationale is clinical, not calendar-based.","d":"In exchange for reducing session fees turns the gift into a barter or financial transaction, which is a boundary violation rather than an ethically defensible acceptance."}},{"q":"What is the impact of the Criminal Law Amendment Act on the age of statutory rape in India?","concept":"The Criminal Law (Amendment) Act, 2013 raised the age of consent in India from 16 to 18 years, meaning sexual activity with a person below 18 is treated as statutory rape regardless of apparent consent. This change aligned the age of consent with the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (POCSO) Act, 2012. The direction of the change is an increase, from 16 to 18.","traps":{"a":"It lowered the age from 18 to 16 reverses the actual direction of the amendment, which raised rather than lowered the threshold.","c":"It criminalized all consensual sex among young people overstates the law, which set an age threshold rather than banning youth sexuality outright.","d":"It did not have any impact is false, since the amendment specifically changed the statutory age of consent."}},{"q":"What does ethical competency refer to?","concept":"Ethical competency is a multi-dimensional capability, not a single skill. It combines knowledge and understanding of ethics codes, the practical ability to recognise, analyse and resolve ethical situations as they arise, and the willingness to take responsibility for one's own actions and decisions. Because each listed element is a genuine component, all of them together define ethical competency.","traps":{"a":"Knowledge and understanding of ethics codes is necessary but only the cognitive part, missing the applied and accountability dimensions.","b":"Ability to recognise, analyse and resolve ethical situations is the applied skill alone and omits knowledge and personal responsibility.","c":"Responsibility for one's own actions is the accountability piece by itself, insufficient without knowledge and analytic skill."}},{"q":"According to Immanuel Kant's deontological approach, what are actions judged by?","concept":"Immanuel Kant's deontological (duty-based) ethics judges actions by their intrinsic rightness or wrongness and the maxim or principle behind them, not by whatever outcomes they produce. An action is moral because it accords with duty and could be willed as a universal law, captured in his categorical imperative. This directly opposes consequentialism, which evaluates actions solely by their results.","traps":{"a":"Their consequences describes consequentialism or utilitarianism, the opposite of Kant's stance that outcomes do not determine morality.","c":"Cost-benefit analysis is a consequentialist weighing of outcomes, exactly the kind of calculation Kant's duty ethics rejects.","d":"None of the above is wrong because option B, intrinsic goodness or badness, correctly states the Kantian criterion."}},{"q":"What is a potential consequence of poor awareness of the culture and prevalent familial practices in family therapy?","concept":"In family therapy, cultural competence is essential because family structures, roles, and practices vary widely across cultures. Poor awareness of a family's culture and customary practices leads the therapist to misread normal, culturally-bound behaviour as pathological, producing wrong assessments and misinterpretations. Such errors undermine, rather than build, understanding and the therapeutic alliance.","traps":{"b":"Accurate understanding of the family's dynamics is the opposite of what poor cultural awareness produces.","c":"Effective communication with the family is a benefit of good cultural competence, not a consequence of lacking it.","d":"Development of a strong therapeutic alliance is a positive outcome that poor cultural awareness actually endangers."}},{"q":"Which type of psychotherapy is NOT commonly used to treat survivors of sexual assault?","concept":"Trauma-focused cognitive-behavioural therapy (TF-CBT), eye movement desensitization and reprocessing (EMDR), and psychodynamic psychotherapy are all recognised treatments for survivors of sexual assault, with TF-CBT and EMDR being strongly evidence-based for post-traumatic stress. Interpersonal psychotherapy (IPT) is designed for depression and interpersonal difficulties, focusing on current relationships and role transitions, and is not a standard trauma-focused treatment. Therefore IPT is the one not commonly used here.","traps":{"b":"Psychodynamic psychotherapy is used with assault survivors to work through the emotional meaning of trauma, so it is not the exception.","c":"EMDR is a front-line, evidence-based trauma treatment, so it is commonly used, not excluded.","d":"TF-CBT is a first-line, specifically trauma-focused therapy for assault survivors, so it is clearly a standard treatment."}},{"q":"What is bilateral stimulation in EMDR?","concept":"In EMDR, bilateral stimulation is the rhythmic left-right stimulation the client attends to, most classically back-and-forth eye movements following the therapist's fingers, while simultaneously holding a disturbing memory in mind. The alternating stimulation can also take the form of tactile taps or auditory tones. It is thought to help the brain reprocess the traumatic memory into a less distressing, adaptive form.","traps":{"a":"The use of physical touch and thoughts misrepresents the technique, which centres on alternating bilateral cues, not touch as such, paired with a target memory.","c":"The use of medication and behavioral techniques describes pharmacological or behavioural anxiety treatment, not EMDR's core mechanism.","d":"The use of hypnosis and relaxation to access repressed memories confuses EMDR with hypnotherapy, whereas EMDR uses conscious dual attention, not hypnosis."}},{"q":"What do researchers mean by pathologization and depathologization of homosexuality?","concept":"Pathologization of homosexuality refers to classifying it as a mental disorder, as the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual did until homosexuality was removed in 1973. Depathologization refers to the reverse, removing that diagnostic classification so that being gay or lesbian is no longer considered an illness. The terms are strictly about diagnostic status, not about attempting to change or publicly disclose one's orientation.","traps":{"a":"None of the above is wrong because option B accurately captures the classification-and-removal meaning.","c":"The changing of sexual orientation from homosexual to heterosexual describes conversion therapy, a discredited practice, not depathologization.","d":"The process of coming out describes public disclosure of orientation, which is unrelated to diagnostic classification."}},{"q":"What are some defining features of minority stress?","concept":"Minority stress theory, developed by Ilan Meyer, holds that stigmatised minority groups experience chronic stress arising from relatively stable social and cultural structures such as prejudice, stigma, and discrimination. This stress is socially based and enduring, distinct from ordinary transient stress, because it is rooted in the person's social position rather than individual failings. Its defining features are that it is chronic and structurally embedded.","traps":{"a":"A temporary experience overcome with positive thinking contradicts the chronic, socially rooted nature of minority stress.","c":"Stemming from individual characteristics and personal choices misattributes the stress to the person rather than to social stigma and structures.","d":"Not related to social processes, institutions, and structures directly denies the social basis that defines minority stress."}},{"q":"Who is credited with coining the term \"clinical psychology\"?","concept":"Lightner Witmer coined the term 'clinical psychology' and founded the first psychological clinic at the University of Pennsylvania in 1896, establishing clinical psychology as an applied field. He is therefore regarded as the founder of the discipline. This is a standard historical fact in entrance exams for clinical psychology.","traps":{"a":"Sigmund Freud founded psychoanalysis, but he did not coin the term clinical psychology.","b":"Carl Rogers developed client-centred (humanistic) therapy much later and did not originate the term.","d":"B. F. Skinner was a behaviourist known for operant conditioning, not for naming clinical psychology."}},{"q":"What is countertransference in therapy?","concept":"Countertransference is the therapist's own emotional reaction toward the client, in which the therapist experiences feelings, often rooted in the therapist's personal history, that are stirred up by the client. It is the mirror image of transference, in which the client unconsciously redirects feelings about significant figures in their life onto the therapist. Recognising and managing countertransference is important so it does not distort the therapy.","traps":{"a":"The fear of the unknown, lack of motivation, or feeling overwhelmed describes client resistance or general distress, not the therapist's reactive feelings.","b":"The need to maintain appropriate boundaries describes an ethical requirement, not the emotional phenomenon of countertransference.","d":"Clients unconsciously transferring feelings onto the therapist defines transference, which is the reverse of countertransference."}},{"q":"Which biological system is implicated in obsessive-compulsive disorder?","concept":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder is most strongly linked to dysregulation of the central serotonergic (5-HT) system, which is why selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors are the first-line pharmacotherapy. Glutamatergic and dopaminergic abnormalities are implicated but in a secondary or contributory role. The serotonin hypothesis remains the primary biological account of OCD.","traps":{"b":"The central GABAergic system is more associated with anxiety and sedation via benzodiazepines, not the core of OCD.","c":"The central glutamatergic system is implicated in OCD secondarily, but it is not the primary system targeted by first-line treatment.","d":"The central dopaminergic system contributes to tic-related and treatment-resistant OCD, but serotonin is the principal system involved."}},{"q":"What are the clinical features of conversion disorder?","concept":"Conversion (functional neurological) disorder involves a genuine loss or alteration of voluntary motor or sensory function, typically with sudden onset following a significant psychosocial stressor, and the symptoms are not intentionally produced. Crucially, the deficits do not conform to known anatomical or physiological patterns of neurological disease, even though the person is not feigning. This distinguishes it from factitious disorder and malingering, where symptoms are deliberately produced.","traps":{"a":"Physical examination revealing no abnormalities is true but incomplete, as it omits the hallmark motor/sensory alteration, sudden onset, and stressor link.","c":"Symptoms that suggest a medical or neurological disorder is subtly wrong, because conversion symptoms are actually incompatible with recognised neurological disease patterns.","d":"Intentional symptom production with no secondary gain describes factitious or malingering-type presentations, the opposite of conversion, which is not intentionally produced."}},{"q":"What is the characteristic belief associated with Koro?","concept":"Koro is a culture-bound syndrome reported mainly in South and East Asia, characterised by the sudden, terrifying conviction that one's genitals (or in women, breasts or nipples) are retracting into the body and that this retraction will cause death. This belief triggers acute panic and desperate measures to prevent the perceived retraction. The defining feature is the fear of genital retraction leading to death.","traps":{"a":"Belief in possession by evil spirits describes spirit-possession syndromes, not Koro's genital-retraction fear.","b":"A sense of impending doom with atmospheric changes does not match Koro's specific bodily delusion.","d":"Belief in transformation into a mythical creature describes other culturally specific delusions, not the genital-retraction fear that defines Koro."}},{"q":"What is the characteristic feature of Borderline Personality Disorder?","concept":"Borderline Personality Disorder is defined by a pervasive pattern of unstable and intense interpersonal relationships, impulsivity, marked affective instability, and frantic efforts to avoid real or imagined abandonment, often with an unstable self-image. The fear of abandonment and stormy relationships are its hallmark. These features distinguish it from other Cluster B and Cluster C personality disorders.","traps":{"a":"Social inhibition, fear of rejection, and desire for acceptance describe Avoidant Personality Disorder, not Borderline.","b":"Excessive attention-seeking and dramatic behaviour describe Histrionic Personality Disorder.","c":"Disregard for others' rights and manipulative behaviour describe Antisocial Personality Disorder."}},{"q":"What is Dysthymia?","concept":"Dysthymia, now called persistent depressive disorder, is a chronic, low-grade depression that persists for at least two years (one year in children and adolescents) without meeting the full severity of a major depressive episode. It is defined by the persistent, mild nature of the depressed mood. It should be separated from bipolar disorders and from cyclothymia.","traps":{"a":"Severe depression alternating with mania describes bipolar I disorder, not the persistent mild depression of dysthymia.","b":"Persistent instability between mild depression and mild elation describes cyclothymia, not dysthymia.","d":"Persistent mild depression with hypomania mixes features of cyclothymia and bipolar spectrum, whereas dysthymia is unipolar low mood only."}},{"q":"According to Psychodynamic Theory, what is the primary defense mechanism used to deal with anxiety?","concept":"In psychodynamic (psychoanalytic) theory, repression is the primary or basic defence mechanism: the unconscious exclusion of threatening impulses, memories, and wishes from conscious awareness in order to manage anxiety. Other defence mechanisms are considered secondary and are built upon this foundational act of keeping unacceptable material out of consciousness. Repression is thus the cornerstone of the ego's defensive system.","traps":{"a":"Displacement redirects an impulse to a safer target, but it is a secondary defence, not the primary one against anxiety.","b":"Conversion channels psychological conflict into physical symptoms and is a specific, not primary, defence.","d":"Isolation separates an idea from its emotional charge and is again a secondary defence derived from repression."}},{"q":"What were the two clinical types differentiated by Emil Kraepelin?","concept":"Emil Kraepelin's foundational classification of the major psychoses distinguished dementia praecox, marked by early onset and deteriorating course, from manic-depressive insanity, marked by episodic mood disturbance with relative recovery. Eugen Bleuler later renamed dementia praecox as schizophrenia. This dichotomy still underlies the modern separation of schizophrenia from mood disorders.","traps":{"a":"Depression and Anxiety are not the two categories Kraepelin drew; his split concerned the major psychoses.","b":"Schizophrenia and Borderline personality disorder is anachronistic, since Kraepelin used the term dementia praecox and did not frame borderline PD.","d":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder and Bipolar disorder are modern labels not matching Kraepelin's original dementia praecox versus manic-depressive dichotomy."}},{"q":"What is the sheath/kosh surrounding atman that is involved in perceptual processing","concept":"In the Pancha Kosha (five-sheath) model of Vedantic thought, the manomaya kosha is the mental sheath, the layer of manas (mind) that receives sense impressions and carries out perception and emotional processing. It sits between the vital sheath and the intellect sheath. Perceptual processing is its defining function.","traps":{"a":"Ananda-maya kosha is the bliss sheath, the innermost layer, not concerned with perception.","b":"Vijnana-maya kosha is the intellect or discrimination sheath, responsible for judgment and reasoning rather than raw perception.","c":"Prana-maya kosha is the vital-energy sheath governing life force and physiological function, not perceptual processing."}},{"q":"Which school of Buddhism focuses on nirvana via collective suffering?","concept":"Mahayana Buddhism emphasises collective liberation through the bodhisattva ideal, in which an enlightened being compassionately postpones or works toward final nirvana in order to help all sentient beings attain liberation. This contrasts with the emphasis on individual liberation. Its focus on universal salvation and shared deliverance from suffering is its defining orientation.","traps":{"a":"Theravada emphasises individual liberation through the arhat ideal, not collective salvation.","c":"Vajrayana (tantric Buddhism) uses esoteric ritual and mantra methods and is often treated as a branch within the Mahayana, but it is not defined by collective-suffering nirvana.","d":"Zen is a meditation-focused school stressing direct insight, not the collective-liberation doctrine that specifically characterises Mahayana."}},{"q":"The path to the cessation of suffering, as described in the fourth Noble Truth, is known as the _______.","concept":"The Four Noble Truths of Buddhism culminate in the Fourth Truth, Magga, the path leading to the cessation of suffering, which is the Noble Eightfold Path. The sequence is dukkha (suffering), samudaya (its origin), nirodha (its cessation), and magga (the path to cessation). Magga is specifically the practical route out of suffering.","traps":{"a":"Samudaya is the Second Noble Truth, the origin or cause of suffering, not the path.","c":"Nirodha is the Third Noble Truth, the cessation of suffering itself, not the path leading to it.","d":"Anicca means impermanence, one of the three marks of existence, and is not one of the Four Noble Truths."}},{"q":"According to the Bhagavad Gita, the Trigunas belong to","concept":"In the Samkhya metaphysics that the Bhagavad Gita draws on, reality has two fundamentals: Purusha (pure consciousness) and Prakriti (primordial matter or nature). The three gunas, sattva, rajas, and tamas, are the constituent qualities of Prakriti, and all material and mental phenomena arise from their interplay. The gunas therefore belong to Prakriti, not to the witnessing Purusha.","traps":{"a":"Samadhi is a meditative state of absorption, not the ground to which the gunas belong.","b":"Purusha is pure consciousness, the witness, which is by definition free of the gunas.","d":"Samskara refers to mental impressions or latent tendencies, not the source of the three gunas."}},{"q":"According to the Bhagavad Gita, what are the three elements of the mind?","concept":"In the Gita's psychology, the internal instrument of the mind (antahkarana) is analysed into manas (the mind that senses and doubts), buddhi (the intellect that discriminates and decides), and ahamkara (the ego or I-sense). Together these three constitute the elements of the mind. They should not be confused with the three gunas, which are qualities of nature.","traps":{"b":"Prakriti, purusa, ksetra are metaphysical categories (matter, consciousness, field), not the three components of the mind.","c":"Dharma, artha, kama are three of the four goals of life (purusharthas), not elements of the mind.","d":"Sattva, rajas, tamas are the three gunas or qualities of nature, not the three parts of the internal instrument."}},{"q":"What are the four major goals of human life according to Indian culture?","concept":"Indian tradition sets out four purusharthas, the goals of human life: dharma (duty and righteousness), artha (wealth and material means), kama (desire and pleasure), and moksha (liberation from the cycle of rebirth). Option C, duty, wealth, desires, and liberation, correctly maps onto these four. This fourfold scheme frames a balanced human life culminating in liberation.","traps":{"a":"Physical fitness, wealth, desires, fame substitutes fitness and fame for dharma and moksha, which are the actual goals.","b":"Knowledge, wealth, desires, enlightenment replaces dharma with knowledge, so it does not match the standard four purusharthas.","d":"Duty, knowledge, desires, fame/power omits liberation (moksha) and wealth (artha), the crucial fourth and second goals."}},{"q":"Which of the following is an astik system in Indian thought?","concept":"In Indian philosophy, astika (orthodox) systems are those that accept the authority of the Vedas, while nastika (heterodox) systems reject it. Advaita-Vedanta accepts the Vedas and is therefore astika. Buddhism and Jainism reject Vedic authority and are nastika, while Sufism belongs to the Islamic tradition and lies outside this classification.","traps":{"a":"Sufism is a mystical strand of Islam and is not part of the astika/nastika classification of Indian orthodox schools.","c":"Buddhism rejects Vedic authority and is classified as nastika (heterodox).","d":"Jainism likewise rejects the authority of the Vedas and is a nastika system."}},{"q":"What are the three gunas in the Upanishads?","concept":"The three gunas are the fundamental qualities of Prakriti (nature) described in Vedantic and Samkhya thought: rajas (activity and passion), tamas (inertia and darkness), and sattva (purity, clarity, and harmony). Every material and mental state is a mixture of these three. Option B lists exactly rajas, tamas, and sattva.","traps":{"a":"Prakriti, purusha, and ksetra are metaphysical categories, not the three gunas.","c":"Atman, Brahman, and maya are core Vedantic concepts of self, absolute, and illusion, not the qualities of nature.","d":"Karma, dharma, and moksha are principles of action, duty, and liberation, not the three gunas."}},{"q":"Bavisha lost her job and has become so stressed that she cannot eat, has withdrawn, and cannot get herself to function, sunk in inertia. This dull, lethargic, cannot-act state reflects the dominance of which?","concept":"Among the three gunas, tamas is the quality of inertia, dullness, heaviness, lethargy, and inability to act. Bavisha's withdrawn, cannot-eat, cannot-function, sunk-in-inertia state is a textbook picture of tamas dominance. Sattva (clarity) and rajas (restless activity) would present very differently, so tamas best captures her condition.","traps":{"b":"Yama is the first limb of Patanjali's Ashtanga Yoga, a set of ethical restraints, not an inner mental state that could describe her inertia.","c":"Pratyahara is the yogic practice of withdrawing the senses inward, a deliberate discipline, not the involuntary lethargy she is experiencing.","d":"Jnana refers to the path of knowledge or wisdom, a spiritual route, not the dull, immobilised state described."}},{"q":"What are the components of the Linga sharira (subtle body) according to the Pancha kosas?","concept":"Within the Pancha Kosha framework, the linga sharira or subtle body corresponds to the three inner sheaths: pranamaya kosha (the vital sheath), manomaya kosha (the mental sheath), and vijnanamaya kosha (the intellect sheath). This subtle body carries the jiva (individual soul) between lives, while the Atman itself is the unchanging witness rather than a sheath. Option A lists these three subtle sheaths (with Atman named as the witness, not a component).","traps":{"b":"This set adds annamaya kosha, the gross physical (food) sheath, which belongs to the gross body, not the subtle body.","c":"Ahankara, Manas, Buddhi, and Citta are the four functions of the antahkarana (inner instrument), a different classification from the kosha-based subtle body.","d":"Jnanendriya, Karmendriya, Manas, Buddhi, and Ahankara list the sense and action organs plus mental faculties, not the kosha components of the linga sharira."}},{"q":"What are Samskara according to the Pancha kosas?","concept":"In the Vedantic Pancha Kosa (five-sheath) framework of Indian psychology, samskaras are the latent impressions or past tendencies left by every action and experience, accumulated across the lifespan (and, in the tradition, across lifetimes). They lie dormant in the deeper koshas and shape a person's present dispositions, habits and reactions, so 'past tendencies/nature' is the correct match.","traps":{"a":"The gross body corresponds to the annamaya kosa (food sheath), the physical body, not to accumulated latent impressions.","c":"The sense organs relate to the pranamaya/manomaya level of functioning, not to samskaras, which are stored dispositions rather than perceptual apparatus.","d":"The subtle body (sukshma sharira, the pranamaya-manomaya-vijnanamaya complex) is the vehicle that carries samskaras, but it is not itself the definition of samskara."}},{"q":"What is the process of psycho-spiritual transformation in Sufism?","concept":"Sufism (tasawwuf) is described in Indian and comparative psychology as a process of psycho-spiritual transformation: an inner journey that moves the seeker from ordinary, mundane surface awareness toward the depths and heights of self-consciousness and eventual union with the Divine. The emphasis is on transformation of consciousness, so option B captures its psychological character.","traps":{"a":"Guidelines and rules for the average Muslim describe the Shariah (outer religious law), not the inner transformative path of Sufism.","c":"The journey from the outer to the inner dimension of Islam describes the tariqah/haqiqah distinction in general terms but omits the defining psychological transformation of self-consciousness that Sufism centres on.","d":"The Four Noble Truths belong to Buddhism, not Sufism, so this option mismatches the tradition entirely."}},{"q":"Which year saw the establishment of the first psychology department in India?","concept":"The first psychology department in India was established at the University of Calcutta in 1916 under Narendra Nath Sengupta, who had trained under Hugo Munsterberg at Harvard. This marked the beginning of formal, institutionalised academic psychology in India.","traps":{"b":"1924 is associated with the founding of the Indian Psychological Association and the journal, not the first department.","c":"1905 predates the department and reflects a general political milestone (partition of Bengal), not the start of academic psychology.","d":"1938 is too late; it postdates the Calcutta department by over two decades and roughly aligns with later expansion at other universities."}},{"q":"According to positivism, what is the nature of reality?","concept":"In Guba and Lincoln's classification of research paradigms, positivism holds a naive-realist ontology: there exists a single 'real' reality that is fully and directly apprehendable through objective inquiry. This is why option C is correct; the other options describe rival paradigms.","traps":{"a":"A virtual reality shaped by social, political, cultural, economic, ethnic and gender values is the ontology of critical theory, not positivism.","b":"A real reality that can only be imperfectly and probabilistically apprehended is the critical-realist ontology of post-positivism, which softens positivism's claim.","d":"Only local and specific constructed realities describes the relativist ontology of constructivism, the opposite of positivist realism."}},{"q":"What is the key goal of liberal feminism?","concept":"Liberal feminism seeks gender equality by reforming existing institutions and integrating women into the structures of mainstream society through equal rights, equal access and equal opportunity. It works within the system rather than seeking to overthrow it, which is why option C is correct.","traps":{"a":"Overthrowing the capitalist system is the goal of socialist/Marxist feminism, which sees capitalism as the source of women's oppression.","b":"Building a separate women's culture is characteristic of radical/cultural feminism, not the integrationist aim of liberal feminism.","d":"Recognising that women are oppressed by capitalism is again a socialist-feminist premise, not liberal feminism's reformist goal."}},{"q":"What is the primary focus of radical feminism?","concept":"Radical feminism locates the root of women's oppression in patriarchy itself, the systematic male domination embedded in social structures. Its primary aim is to challenge and dismantle the fundamental assumptions of patriarchy, so option C is correct.","traps":{"a":"Recognising oppression by the capitalist system is the focus of socialist/Marxist feminism, which names capitalism rather than patriarchy as the primary cause.","b":"Building a women's culture is an aim of cultural feminism; radical feminism's core target is patriarchy's assumptions, not culture-building per se.","d":"Integrating women into mainstream society through reform is the liberal-feminist project, which radical feminism critiques as insufficient."}},{"q":"What is the primary characteristic of absolute poverty?","concept":"Absolute poverty is defined against a fixed minimum standard: it is the lack of access to the basic necessities for survival such as food, clean water, shelter, sanitation and health care. It contrasts with relative poverty, which is defined in comparison to others in a society.","traps":{"a":"Income inequality is the defining feature of relative poverty, which measures deprivation against the living standards of others, not against a fixed survival threshold.","c":"Temporary deprivation resulting from an adverse event describes transient or situational poverty, not the fixed-threshold concept of absolute poverty.","d":"Poverty handed down through generations describes generational or chronic poverty, a distinct pattern rather than the absolute/relative distinction."}},{"q":"How does eudemonic wellbeing conceptualize wellbeing?","concept":"Eudaimonic well-being, rooted in Aristotle's notion of eudaimonia, conceptualises well-being as flourishing: the cultivation of personal strengths, meaning, growth, self-realisation and contribution to the greater good. It contrasts sharply with the hedonic view that equates well-being with pleasure.","traps":{"a":"Measuring well-being by income and material possessions is a materialistic/economic account, not the eudaimonic emphasis on flourishing and virtue.","b":"Focusing on the experience of pleasure and relaxation is the hedonic conception of well-being, the very view eudaimonia is contrasted against.","d":"Equating well-being with pleasure and happiness is again the hedonic position, whereas eudaimonia stresses meaning and self-actualisation over pleasure."}},{"q":"What are some areas of post-traumatic growth?","concept":"Richard Tedeschi and Lawrence Calhoun identified five domains of post-traumatic growth: greater appreciation of life, deeper and more meaningful relating to others, a heightened sense of personal strength, recognition of new possibilities, and spiritual change. Option D lists these authentic growth domains.","traps":{"a":"'None of the above' is wrong because option D correctly lists Tedeschi and Calhoun's recognised growth domains.","b":"Appreciation of life, personal strength and new possibilities are genuine domains, but this option is incomplete and omits relating to others and spiritual change, which D includes.","c":"Accumulation of material possessions, financial success and personal weakness are not post-traumatic growth domains; growth involves increased perceived strength, not weakness or materialism."}},{"q":"How have physician recommendations and increased awareness contributed to higher exercise participation rates?","concept":"Physician recommendations and public-health campaigns raise exercise participation chiefly by increasing people's awareness of the health benefits of regular physical activity, which motivates them to take up and maintain exercise. The mechanism is education and awareness driving behaviour change.","traps":{"a":"Creating barriers to exercise would reduce participation, the opposite of the observed effect of recommendations and awareness.","b":"Discouraging individuals from participating contradicts the aim of physician advice, which is to encourage physical activity.","d":"'None of the above' is wrong because option C states the actual mechanism, increased awareness of benefits."}},{"q":"What eating disorder involves cycles of binge eating and purging through various methods?","concept":"Bulimia nervosa is characterised by recurrent cycles of binge eating followed by inappropriate compensatory purging behaviours such as self-induced vomiting, laxative or diuretic misuse, or excessive exercise. The binge-purge cycle is its defining feature.","traps":{"a":"Anorexia nervosa centres on severe restriction of intake and low body weight; the purging subtype exists but restriction, not the binge-purge cycle, defines the disorder.","c":"Binge-eating disorder involves recurrent binges but crucially lacks the regular compensatory purging that defines bulimia.","d":"'None of the above' is wrong because bulimia precisely matches the binge-and-purge description."}},{"q":"According to Guthrie, which of the following is NOT improvable by practice?","concept":"Edwin Guthrie's contiguity theory distinguishes movements, acts and skills. A movement is a specific muscle response that is learned completely in a single stimulus-response pairing (one-trial learning), so it cannot be further improved by practice. Acts and skills, which are organised from many movements, do improve with practice.","traps":{"a":"Skills are complex organisations of many acts and movements and clearly improve with practice, so they are improvable.","b":"Acts are combinations of movements directed at outcomes and are refined through practice, so they too are improvable.","d":"'None of the above' is wrong because Guthrie explicitly held that movements, being learned in one trial, are not improved by practice."}},{"q":"The view of 'observational learning' where reinforcement is not required for learning a behaviour is given by","concept":"Albert Bandura's social/observational learning theory holds that behaviour can be acquired simply by observing a model, without the observer needing to be directly reinforced. Reinforcement in his account influences performance (whether a learned behaviour is shown), not the acquisition of the behaviour itself.","traps":{"a":"B. F. Skinner's operant conditioning insists that reinforcement is essential for learning, the opposite of reinforcement-free observational learning.","c":"Edward Tolman is associated with latent learning and cognitive maps, not with the model-based observational learning framework asked about here.","d":"Neal Miller and John Dollard's earlier imitation theory required reinforcement of the imitator, so it does not fit the 'no reinforcement required' description that is Bandura's."}},{"q":"The sudden increase in performance of an organism upon reinforcement, despite never being reinforced in the previous trials is _____________","concept":"Latent learning, demonstrated by Edward Tolman and Honzik's maze rats, is learning that occurs without reinforcement and remains hidden until an incentive is introduced. When reward is finally given, performance shows a sudden jump, revealing that learning had been occurring silently all along.","traps":{"a":"Discrimination learning is learning to respond differently to different stimuli, not the hidden-until-rewarded phenomenon described.","b":"Vicarious reinforcement is being influenced by seeing another's behaviour reinforced, which does not describe the sudden performance jump of an unrewarded individual.","c":"Observational learning is acquiring behaviour by watching a model, whereas the question describes the organism's own unrewarded prior trials, which is latent learning."}},{"q":"According to Skinner, the response rate _________ after extinction","concept":"In B. F. Skinner's account of operant extinction, withdrawing reinforcement causes the response rate to decline gradually and return to its earlier, pre-conditioning operant baseline level rather than disappearing completely. The behaviour is not erased to zero, which is why spontaneous recovery can later occur.","traps":{"a":"Responding does not increase after extinction; the whole point of extinction is that it decreases (a brief extinction burst aside).","b":"It does not stay the same; removing reinforcement reliably reduces the response rate over trials.","d":"It does not drop to zero; responding returns to the earlier operant baseline level, and residual/recoverable responding remains."}},{"q":"The allocation policy in Kahneman's model includes","concept":"In Daniel Kahneman's capacity (arousal) model of attention, a limited pool of processing capacity is distributed by an allocation policy. That policy is shaped by three inputs: enduring dispositions (relatively automatic tendencies), momentary intentions (deliberate current goals), and the evaluation of demands on capacity. Option D lists these three correctly.","traps":{"a":"This option substitutes 'enduring temperament,' 'momentary distractions' and 'evaluation of demands,' misnaming the first two components of the allocation policy.","b":"'Enduring temperament, momentary distractions, environmental demands' mislabels all three; Kahneman's terms are dispositions, intentions and evaluation of demands.","c":"This option gets 'momentary intentions' right but wrongly uses 'enduring temperament' and 'environmental demands' instead of enduring dispositions and evaluation of demands."}},{"q":"An idealized representative object of a category of objects is","concept":"In Eleanor Rosch's categorisation research, a prototype is an idealised, abstract 'best example' that represents the central tendency of a category. New instances are recognised by comparing them to this stored ideal, so the closer an item is to the prototype, the more typical it seems.","traps":{"b":"A template is a stored literal copy or exact pattern used in template-matching theories, not an abstracted central tendency.","c":"A feature is a single component attribute (as in feature-analysis models), not an idealised whole-object representative of a category.","d":"Geons are the basic volumetric primitives in Biederman's Recognition-by-Components theory, not the idealised category exemplar the question describes."}},{"q":"In which of the following cases, would the sensitivity 'd'  be highest in a signal detection task?","concept":"In signal-detection theory, sensitivity d' measures how well an observer separates signal from noise. It is greatest when correct detections (hits) substantially exceed false alarms, indicating a large distance between the signal-plus-noise and noise-only distributions. Hence option C, hits far exceeding false alarms, gives the highest d'.","traps":{"a":"If false alarms are slightly more than hits, the observer is barely discriminating (near or below chance), giving a low, not highest, d'.","b":"When false alarms exceed hit responses, discrimination is poor and d' is low or negative, the opposite of high sensitivity.","d":"When hits equal false alarms, the observer cannot distinguish signal from noise, so d' is effectively zero, the lowest sensitivity."}},{"q":"A Prototype is a/an _________ and ________ member of a category","concept":"A prototype is an abstract representation rather than any single concrete stored instance, and it is an averaged summary of the typical or central features across the members of a category. This distinguishes prototype theory from exemplar theory, which stores concrete individual instances.","traps":{"a":"'Abstract; varied' is half right on abstractness but wrong on the second term; a prototype summarises central tendency, it is averaged rather than varied.","b":"'Concrete; averaged' is wrong on the first term; a prototype is abstract, not a concrete individual member.","c":"'Concrete; varied' misdescribes both attributes; a prototype is abstract and averaged, not concrete or varied."}},{"q":"For perception of a contour, _________ and __________ are required","concept":"For a contour (an edge or boundary) to be perceived, the visual system must register a luminance change, and this requires two conditions: the stimulation must reach a minimal intensity and it must be present for a minimum exposure time. Together these let the receptors detect the difference that defines the contour.","traps":{"b":"This pairs minimal intensity with 'minimum reaction time,' but reaction time is a response measure, not a condition for contour formation; exposure duration is what matters.","c":"'Minimum exposure time and minimum reaction time' omits the required stimulus intensity and again wrongly invokes reaction time.","d":"'Minimum reaction time and minimum movement' names neither of the two actual requirements (intensity and exposure duration)."}},{"q":"the theory which assumes that perceiving an object is by due to 'geons' is","concept":"Irving Biederman's Recognition-by-Components (RBC) theory proposes that we recognise objects by decomposing them into a small set of basic three-dimensional volumetric primitives called geons (geometric ions). The arrangement of these geons specifies the object, allowing recognition from many viewpoints.","traps":{"a":"The Pandemonium model (Selfridge) is a feature-detection model of pattern recognition using 'demons,' not geon-based volumetric analysis.","c":"The prototype model recognises objects by comparison to an abstract best example, not by decomposition into geons.","d":"Template matching compares input to stored whole-pattern templates, a different mechanism from geon-based component recognition."}},{"q":"Which of the following depth perception cues requires both eyes?","concept":"Convergence is a binocular depth cue: as an object comes closer, both eyes rotate inward, and feedback from the eye muscles about this inward turning provides information about distance. Because it depends on the coordinated angle of both eyes, it requires both eyes.","traps":{"a":"Motion parallax is a monocular cue; nearer objects appear to move faster than distant ones as the observer moves, and it works with one eye.","b":"Aerial (atmospheric) perspective is monocular; hazier, bluer distant objects can be judged with a single eye.","d":"Accommodation is a monocular cue based on the change in shape of the lens in one eye to focus, not on both eyes together."}},{"q":"The process of conversion of stimulus input into electrical signal by the receptors is called","concept":"Transduction is the process by which sensory receptors convert physical stimulus energy (light, sound waves, pressure, chemicals) into the electrochemical neural signals that the nervous system can transmit and interpret. It is the essential first step that links the physical world to neural processing.","traps":{"b":"Transference is a psychoanalytic term for redirecting feelings onto the therapist, unrelated to sensory receptor coding.","c":"Transformation is a general term for change and is not the specific technical name for receptor conversion of stimulus energy.","d":"Transfusion refers to transferring blood or fluids, having nothing to do with sensory signal conversion."}},{"q":"_________ are tiny rapid movements of the eyes to ensure ___________","concept":"Saccades, including the tiny fixational microsaccades made even during steady fixation, are rapid eye movements that continually shift the retinal image by small amounts. This constant micro-shifting prevents the image from fading due to receptor adaptation, thereby maintaining stable, constant perception.","traps":{"b":"Fixation is the act of holding the gaze steady on a point; it is not itself the rapid movement, and it is the microsaccades within fixation that prevent fading.","c":"This wrongly names 'fixation' as the rapid movement and wrongly says its purpose is sensory adaptation; in fact microsaccades counteract fading rather than promote adaptation.","d":"This mislabels both terms: fixation is not a rapid movement, and its function is not to cause sensory adaptation."}},{"q":"the component of working memory that is capable of mentally rotating an object is","concept":"In Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch's working memory model, the visuospatial sketchpad is the subsystem that holds and manipulates visual and spatial information, including tasks such as mentally rotating an object or navigating a route. It is functionally separate from the verbal store.","traps":{"a":"The phonological loop stores and rehearses verbal and acoustic information, not visual imagery, so it cannot perform mental rotation.","b":"The central executive is the attentional control system that coordinates the subsystems; it does not itself store or rotate visual images.","d":"'Executive functioning' is a broad umbrella term for higher-order control processes, not the specific working-memory component that manipulates spatial imagery."}},{"q":"The mnemonic where a sentence is framed using first letter of items on a list is called","concept":"An acrostic is a mnemonic device in which a sentence is constructed so that the first letter of each word cues an item to be remembered (for example, 'Every Good Boy Does Fine' for the musical notes E, G, B, D, F). The meaningful sentence makes the sequence easier to recall.","traps":{"a":"An acronym forms a single pronounceable word from the first letters (e.g. NASA), rather than a full sentence.","c":"The pegword system links items to a pre-memorised rhyming list of number-pegs, not to first-letter sentences.","d":"The keyword system aids learning of foreign vocabulary by linking a sound-alike keyword to an image, not by first-letter sentences."}},{"q":"In short term memory encoding is primarily ________","concept":"Encoding in short-term memory is primarily acoustic: items tend to be held in a sound-based code, as shown by R. Conrad's finding that recall errors are confusions among similar-sounding letters (B, V, T) even when presented visually. Long-term memory, by contrast, encodes mainly by meaning (semantic).","traps":{"b":"Visual encoding plays only a brief, minor role in short-term memory; the dominant code in STM is acoustic, as Conrad's acoustic confusions demonstrate.","c":"Haptic (touch-based) encoding is not the primary mode of short-term memory storage for verbal material.","d":"Semantic encoding is characteristic of long-term memory, not the primary code of short-term memory."}},{"q":"Which sampling technique is most likely to result in a representative sample?","concept":"Probability sampling, in which every member of the population has a known, non-zero chance of being selected, is most likely to yield a representative sample because random selection minimises systematic bias and allows generalisation. Non-probability methods rely on non-random selection and risk bias.","traps":{"a":"Non-probability sampling lacks known selection probabilities and random selection, so it is prone to systematic bias and poorer representativeness.","b":"Convenience sampling selects whoever is easily available, which typically over-represents accessible subgroups and skews the sample.","d":"Snowball sampling recruits through participant referrals, tending to capture interconnected networks and thus a non-representative sample."}},{"q":"According to Wallas' model of the creative process, what is the third stage?","concept":"Graham Wallas's four-stage model of the creative process is Preparation, Incubation, Illumination and Verification. The third stage, Illumination, is the moment of sudden insight when the solution emerges into consciousness after the unconscious work of incubation.","traps":{"a":"Incubation is the second stage, the period of unconscious processing after conscious effort is set aside, not the third.","c":"Preparation is the first stage, involving gathering information and consciously working on the problem.","d":"Verification is the fourth and final stage, in which the insight is tested and refined."}},{"q":"According to Piaget, how does cognitive development occur?","concept":"Jean Piaget held that cognitive development arises from the interaction of biological maturation and experience/learning: the child actively constructs knowledge by acting on the environment through assimilation and accommodation, driven toward equilibration. Neither maturation nor learning alone is sufficient.","traps":{"a":"'Solely through maturation' overstates biology; Piaget stressed that experience and the child's own activity are also essential.","b":"'Solely through learning' reflects a strict behaviourist/empiricist view, whereas Piaget insisted maturation sets the stage for what can be learned.","d":"Development through socialization and cultural practices is Lev Vygotsky's sociocultural emphasis, not Piaget's maturation-plus-experience constructivism."}},{"q":"What are phonemes?","concept":"Language is analysed at several levels; the smallest units of sound that a speaker uses to distinguish words in a spoken language are phonemes (for example, the /b/ and /p/ that separate 'bat' from 'pat'). Phonemes themselves carry no meaning; they are the building blocks of speech sound, which is why option b is correct.","traps":{"a":"The smallest MEANINGFUL units of language are morphemes, not phonemes.","c":"Rules for combining words and grammatical markers into meaningful sentences describe syntax (grammar), not phonemes.","d":"Culturally specific rules for using language in social contexts describe pragmatics, not the sound units."}},{"q":"which of the following F ratio values will always be insignificant despite the degrees of freedom","concept":"The F ratio in analysis of variance is between-group variance divided by within-group (error) variance. Statistical significance requires the between-group variability to exceed error variability, so a meaningful effect gives F greater than 1; any F below 1 signals that group differences are smaller than chance error and can never reach significance regardless of degrees of freedom. 0.99 is below 1, so option b is always insignificant.","traps":{"a":"An F of 2 exceeds 1 and can be significant given adequate degrees of freedom, so it is not always insignificant.","c":"An F of 1.55 is above 1 and may be significant depending on the degrees of freedom.","d":"An F of 1.2 exceeds 1 and could reach significance with enough degrees of freedom, so it is not guaranteed insignificant."}},{"q":"Lack of organization and planning of dreams is due to low activation of ________","concept":"During REM sleep the prefrontal cortex, which governs planning, logical organisation, working memory and reality monitoring, is relatively deactivated. This under-activation is why dreams are bizarre, disjointed and lack coherent planning, making the prefrontal cortex (option c) the correct answer.","traps":{"a":"The striate cortex (primary visual cortex, V1) processes basic visual input, not the organisation and planning of dreams.","b":"The somatosensory cortex processes touch and bodily sensation, not the logical structuring of dreams.","d":"The extrastriate cortex handles higher visual processing (colour, motion, form), not dream planning."}},{"q":"Slow wave sleep occurs when neurons in the ____________________ become active","concept":"Sleep is regulated by a flip-flop switch between arousal and sleep-promoting centres. GABAergic neurons in the ventrolateral preoptic area (VLPO) of the hypothalamus, when active, inhibit the brainstem and hypothalamic arousal systems, thereby initiating slow-wave (NREM) sleep, so option b is correct.","traps":{"a":"The ventrolateral periaqueductal gray is implicated in REM regulation and pain modulation, not in triggering slow-wave sleep.","c":"The sublaterodorsal nucleus drives REM sleep and its associated muscle atonia, not slow-wave sleep.","d":"The suprachiasmatic nucleus is the master circadian clock that times the sleep-wake cycle; it does not itself generate slow-wave sleep."}},{"q":"Study of monozygotic and dizygotic twins who are separated at  birth and reared apart","concept":"Behaviour-genetic designs estimate heritability by comparing people of known genetic relatedness. Twin studies specifically compare monozygotic (100 percent shared genes) and dizygotic (about 50 percent) twins, and the most powerful version studies twins reared apart to separate genetic from shared-environment effects, so option d is correct.","traps":{"a":"Family studies examine resemblance among biological relatives generally and cannot cleanly separate genes from shared environment.","b":"Adoption studies compare adoptees with biological versus adoptive relatives; they do not centre on the MZ versus DZ twin comparison.","c":"Linkage studies map genes to chromosomal locations using DNA markers across families, not twin rearing comparisons."}},{"q":"Which of the following mechanoreceptors is involved in pain perception?","concept":"Somatosensory receptors are specialised by stimulus. Free nerve endings are unencapsulated dendrites that transduce noxious (pain) and thermal stimuli, making them the nociceptors, so option c is correct. The encapsulated corpuscles instead detect innocuous mechanical stimuli.","traps":{"a":"Merkel's discs are slow-adapting mechanoreceptors for sustained pressure and fine texture, not pain.","b":"Meissner's corpuscles are fast-adapting mechanoreceptors for light touch and low-frequency vibration.","d":"Ruffini corpuscles (endings) sense skin stretch and sustained pressure, not pain."}},{"q":"Norman Triplett observed _______________ tended to race _________ when racing in the presence of others","concept":"Norman Triplett conducted what is often cited as the first social psychology experiment (1898). Observing bicycle-racing records, he noted that cyclists rode faster when competing in the presence of others than when riding alone, a phenomenon later termed social facilitation, so option c is correct.","traps":{"a":"The direction is wrong: cyclists raced faster, not slower, in the presence of others.","b":"Triplett's cycling observation concerned humans, not rats, and the direction is also wrong.","d":"His famous field observation was of cyclists; the later reel-winding lab task used children, but rats do not belong to this study."}},{"q":"What is systematic measurement error?","concept":"Measurement error comes in two forms. Systematic error is consistent and biased in the same direction across measurements (for example, a scale calibrated 2 kg too high), so it distorts validity in a predictable way; this is why option b is correct. Random error, by contrast, fluctuates unpredictably and tends to average out.","traps":{"a":"Error from random fluctuations describes random (unsystematic) error, not systematic error.","c":"Error that is inconsistent and varies in direction is random error, the opposite of systematic error.","d":"Participant bias is only one possible source and does not define systematic error, which is characterised by its consistent directional bias."}},{"q":"Which of the following were flaws that David Wechsler aimed to rectify with his Wechsler-Bellevue Intelligence Scales?","concept":"David Wechsler built the Wechsler-Bellevue (1939) to correct specific weaknesses of the Stanford-Binet: it had little appeal for adults, over-emphasised verbal and word-manipulation tasks, rewarded speed over accuracy, and relied on the mental-age (ratio IQ) concept unsuited to adults. Option a lists exactly these flaws.","traps":{"b":"It reverses the target (Binet already appealed to children) and wrongly cites math and 'physical age' rather than word tasks and mental age.","c":"It wrongly says the Binet emphasised accuracy over speed and relied on physical age, when the real flaws were speed over accuracy and mental age.","d":"It wrongly says the Binet lacked appeal for children and stressed history questions with accuracy over speed, none of which were Wechsler's targets."}},{"q":"What is the formula for Wechsler's new method for obtaining the IQ score?","concept":"Wechsler abandoned the ratio IQ (mental age divided by chronological age times 100) in favour of the deviation IQ. His method compares an individual's actual test performance to the expected mean score for their own age group, so IQ reflects the actual score relative to age expectation, making option a correct.","traps":{"b":"Inverting the ratio (expected mean over actual score) is not Wechsler's formula and would make higher performers score lower.","c":"Multiplying the actual score by the age-expected mean is not a meaningful IQ index.","d":"Adding the age-expected mean to the actual score does not yield a standardised deviation IQ."}},{"q":"Which cognitive theories were used to derive the five factors of intelligence assessed in the SB5?","concept":"The fifth edition of the Stanford-Binet (SB5) organises intelligence into five factors grounded in modern cognitive theory: it draws on John Carroll's three-stratum (Cattell-Horn-Carroll) model of cognitive abilities and Alan Baddeley's working-memory model. Hence option c, Carroll and Baddeley, is correct.","traps":{"a":"Erik Erikson's psychosocial stages and Lev Vygotsky's sociocultural theory address development, not the SB5 ability factors.","b":"Jean Piaget's cognitive-developmental stages and Abraham Maslow's hierarchy of needs did not derive the SB5 factors.","d":"B. F. Skinner's behaviourism and Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis are not the cognitive basis of the SB5."}},{"q":"What are the two scales included in the KBIT-2?","concept":"The KBIT-2 (Kaufman Brief Intelligence Test, second edition) is a brief measure yielding two scales: a Verbal (Crystallized) scale tapping learned knowledge and a Nonverbal (Fluid) scale tapping reasoning with novel material, mirroring Cattell-Horn Gc and Gf. Option d is therefore correct.","traps":{"a":"Reading Comprehension and Math scales describe achievement tests, not the KBIT-2 structure.","b":"Quantitative and Analytical scales resemble aptitude-test sections (such as the GRE), not the KBIT-2.","c":"The KBIT-2 does not yield an EQ (emotional quotient) scale alongside an IQ scale."}},{"q":"What was developed by Alan Kaufman to measure intelligence?","concept":"Alan Kaufman, with Nadeen Kaufman, developed a family of intelligence measures including the K-BIT (Kaufman Brief Intelligence Test) and the K-ABC. The K-BIT is the Kaufman-authored brief intelligence test, so option b is correct.","traps":{"a":"The ACT is a college-admissions achievement test, not a Kaufman intelligence measure.","c":"The SAT is a college-admissions test developed by the College Board, not by Kaufman.","d":"The GRE is a graduate-admissions test from ETS, not a Kaufman intelligence test."}},{"q":"What is the RAT?","concept":"The RAT is the Remote Associates Test, devised by Sarnoff Mednick to measure creativity. It is a timed (about 40 minutes) paper-and-pencil test in which the examinee finds a single word linking three seemingly unrelated cue words, so option c is correct.","traps":{"a":"'Rapid Action Team' is a distractor acronym unrelated to the psychological test.","b":"The literal animal (a rat) is an obvious lure playing on the word, not the test.","d":"The RAT is a paper-and-pencil instrument, not an electronic device."}},{"q":"What is the purpose of the MMPI-2?","concept":"The MMPI-2 (Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory, 2nd edition) is a broad self-report inventory of psychopathology, using clinical scales plus validity scales to aid psychiatric diagnosis and personality assessment. Its purpose is clinical, so option a is correct.","traps":{"b":"Educational assessment uses achievement and aptitude tests, not the MMPI-2.","c":"The MMPI-2 assesses psychological, not physical, health.","d":"Career or vocational assessment uses interest inventories (for example the Strong), not the MMPI-2."}},{"q":"What does answering 'false' to L Scale items in the MMPI-2 assert?","concept":"The L (Lie) Scale of the MMPI-2 consists of items describing minor faults and shortcomings that most honest people readily admit (for example, occasionally getting angry). Answering 'false' to such items denies these common human flaws, thereby asserting a degree of personal virtue rarely seen in the culture and signalling a naive, overly favourable self-presentation, so option c is correct.","traps":{"a":"The L Scale detects claims of unusual virtue, not a lack of virtue.","b":"Denying common flaws reflects impression management, not a failure to read the items.","d":"The inference is about defensive self-presentation, not about difficulty understanding the items."}},{"q":"Which of the following is not a factor that contributes to consistency in measurement?","concept":"Reliability refers to consistency of measurement, which is enhanced when the construct being measured is stable and when administration and scoring are kept uniform across occasions. The complexity of the measure is not itself a source of consistency, so it is the odd one out, making option d correct.","traps":{"a":"Stability of the underlying construct genuinely supports consistency, so it is not the exception.","b":"Consistency in how the measure is administered supports reliability, so it is a real contributor.","c":"Consistency in scoring (inter-scorer agreement) supports reliability, so it is a real contributor."}},{"q":"Which method of estimating reliability involves administering the same test twice to the same group of people and correlating their scores?","concept":"The test-retest method estimates reliability by administering the identical test to the same people on two occasions separated by an interval, then correlating the two score sets; the resulting coefficient of stability indexes consistency over time, so option b is correct.","traps":{"a":"The split-half method divides a single test into two halves within one administration; it does not retest the same people.","c":"Cronbach's alpha is an internal-consistency estimate from one administration, not a repeated-testing correlation.","d":"Item response theory is a measurement model for item and ability estimation, not a test-retest procedure."}},{"q":"Which of the following is a potential limitation of the internal consistency method of estimating reliability?","concept":"Internal-consistency methods (such as split-half or coefficient alpha) estimate reliability from the relationships among items within a single administration of the test. Their key limitation is precisely that: based on one occasion, they capture only internal homogeneity and say nothing about stability or error across time, so option a is correct.","traps":{"b":"Being affected by differences in test content is not the defining limitation highlighted here; content-related error is more an alternate-forms concern.","c":"Needing multiple items is a general property of most scaling, not the specific limitation of the internal-consistency approach.","d":"Since only option a is correct, 'all of the above' is wrong."}},{"q":"What is the major challenge with split-half reliability?","concept":"In split-half reliability the test is divided into two halves whose scores are correlated. The major challenge is dividing the test into two genuinely equivalent (parallel) halves, because an unbalanced split biases the estimate; the resulting half-test correlation is then stepped up with the Spearman-Brown formula. Option b names this challenge.","traps":{"a":"Applying the Spearman-Brown formula is a routine calculation, not the central difficulty.","c":"Administering the test twice with an interval describes the test-retest method, not split-half.","d":"Administering two forms to the same group describes alternate-forms reliability, not the split-half challenge."}},{"q":"If the half-test reliability coefficient is .60, what is the estimated reliability of the full test using the Spearman-Brown formula?","concept":"The Spearman-Brown prophecy formula for doubling test length is r_full = 2r / (1 + r). Substituting the half-test coefficient r = .60 gives 2(.60) / (1 + .60) = 1.20 / 1.60 = .75, so option a is correct.","traps":{"b":".82 does not result from the Spearman-Brown correction of .60; it overstates the corrected value.","c":".30 halves the coefficient, the opposite of the length-doubling correction, which raises reliability.","d":".60 is the uncorrected half-test value; Spearman-Brown corrects it upward to .75."}},{"q":"What is the purpose of averaging several splits in estimating the reliability of a psychological test according to Cronbach?","concept":"Lee Cronbach showed that coefficient alpha equals the mean of all possible split-half reliability coefficients of a test. Averaging across the many possible splits, rather than relying on one arbitrary division, yields a more accurate and stable estimate of reliability, so option d is correct.","traps":{"a":"Averaging splits does not add dimensions; it estimates reliability of the existing content.","b":"Averaging increases, rather than decreases, the accuracy of the estimate.","c":"Averaging splits does not reduce the number of items; the full item set is retained."}},{"q":"__________ is the tendency for an individual surrounded by a group of strangers to hesitate and not help because none of the bystanders knows for sure what is happening","concept":"Pluralistic ignorance is a bystander phenomenon in ambiguous emergencies: each onlooker privately feels uncertain but, seeing others remain outwardly calm, wrongly concludes that nothing is wrong, so no one acts. Because the definition stresses that none of the bystanders knows for sure what is happening, option b is correct.","traps":{"a":"The bystander effect is the broad umbrella finding that people help less in groups; it is the outcome, not the specific misreading-of-others mechanism described.","c":"Diffusion of responsibility is the separate mechanism whereby responsibility is shared and felt less by each person, not the misjudging of the situation itself.","d":"'Amotivation to help' is not the recognised term for this ambiguity-driven hesitation."}},{"q":"Which of the following are powerful sources of provocation","concept":"Social psychology identifies certain interpersonal triggers as especially potent provocations to aggression. Among the options offered, condescension (being treated with contempt) paired with unjustified, unfair criticism are singled out as powerful provocations, so option c is correct.","traps":{"a":"Teasing and condescension is an incomplete pairing; the keyed powerful pair is condescension with unjustified criticism.","b":"Unjustified criticism and teasing omits condescension, the second key element of the keyed pair.","d":"'None of the above' is wrong because option c does identify recognised powerful provocations."}},{"q":"splintering of a group into distinct factions is called","concept":"A schism is the splitting of a group into distinct, often opposing factions, typically over ideological, doctrinal or leadership differences. The term specifically denotes this fracturing of a single body into rival sub-groups, so option a is correct.","traps":{"b":"A chapter is a local branch or subdivision of an organisation, not a splintering into factions.","c":"'Division' is a generic term for a part or section, not the specific concept of a group fracturing into factions.","d":"'Deviation' means departing from a norm or standard, not a group splitting apart."}},{"q":"Fiedler's contingency theory does NOT include which of the following as dimensions","concept":"Fred Fiedler's contingency model holds that leadership effectiveness depends on the match between leadership style and situational favourableness, defined by three dimensions: leader-member relations, task structure, and the leader's position power (intrinsic power and authority). Employee motivation is not one of these dimensions, so option a is the correct answer to what it does NOT include.","traps":{"b":"Leader-member relations is a genuine dimension of Fiedler's model, so it is not the exception.","c":"Task structure is a genuine situational dimension in Fiedler's model.","d":"Position power (intrinsic power and authority) is a genuine dimension of the model."}},{"q":"Identify the correct order of cellular layers to the retinal receptors","concept":"The retina is inverted: light must pass through the inner neural layers before reaching the photoreceptors at the back. Travelling toward the receptors the order is ganglion cells, then amacrine cells, then bipolar cells, then horizontal cells, and finally the rods and cones, so option d is correct.","traps":{"a":"This begins with horizontal cells and misplaces ganglion and amacrine cells, breaking the inner-to-outer sequence.","b":"Starting with bipolar cells is wrong; ganglion cells are the innermost layer light meets first.","c":"Starting with amacrine cells before ganglion cells reverses the true inner-to-outer order."}},{"q":"Tendency of acoustic stimuli of different frequencies (of sound) to cause different parts of the basilar membrane to flex back and forth is","concept":"Place coding (place theory of pitch) states that sound frequency is encoded by location: different frequencies produce maximal vibration at different points along the basilar membrane, with high frequencies displacing the stiff base and low frequencies the flexible apex. This mapping of frequency to place makes option b correct.","traps":{"a":"Rate coding refers to encoding stimulus intensity or frequency via the firing rate of neurons, not the site of basilar-membrane flexion.","c":"Frequency coding (frequency/temporal theory) attributes pitch to the timing of neural firing matching the sound wave, not to differing membrane locations.","d":"'Pitch coding' is not the standard term for this location-based mechanism."}},{"q":"__________ layer is sensitive to color and ________ layer is sensitive to movement","concept":"The lateral geniculate nucleus and visual pathway have parallel channels. Parvocellular layers, with small cells and sustained responses, are sensitive to colour and fine spatial detail, while magnocellular layers, with large cells, are sensitive to motion, luminance and contrast. Matching colour to parvocellular and movement to magnocellular gives option d.","traps":{"a":"This reverses the pair: magnocellular handles movement (not colour) and parvocellular handles colour (not movement).","b":"Colour is a parvocellular, not koniocellular, function, and the movement layer (magnocellular) is omitted.","c":"Magnocellular is the movement, not colour, channel, so assigning colour to it is wrong."}},{"q":"Correct order to the auditory receptor is","concept":"Sound is conducted from outside inward in a fixed sequence: the pinna funnels sound to the tympanic membrane (eardrum), which vibrates the ossicles (malleus, incus, stapes), whose footplate pushes the oval window, setting fluid in the cochlea in motion, ultimately stimulating the organ of Corti where transduction occurs. Option a follows this order.","traps":{"b":"This places the cochlea before the tympanic membrane and ossicles, which is anatomically impossible.","c":"This puts the oval window before the tympanic membrane, reversing the true conduction sequence.","d":"This routes the oval window before the ossicles and the cochlea before the ossicles' output, misordering the middle-ear chain."}},{"q":"The Anterolateral system system in somatosensory pathway carries ___________ information","concept":"The somatosensory system has two great ascending pathways. The anterolateral (spinothalamic) system carries nociceptive (pain) and thermal information, using fibres that cross the midline soon after entering the cord, so option d is correct. The dorsal-column/medial-lemniscus pathway is the separate route that carries fine (discriminative) touch, vibration and proprioception.","traps":{"a":"Proprioception (limb position sense) travels in the dorsal-column/medial-lemniscus pathway, not the anterolateral system.","b":"Vibration sense is a large-fibre modality carried by the dorsal columns, not by the spinothalamic tract.","c":"Fine, discriminative touch is a dorsal-column modality; only crude touch is partly shared with the anterolateral system."}},{"q":"The part of the nervous system responsible for conservation of energy resources is ___________ and that responsible of allocation of resources for expenditure is _________","concept":"The two divisions of the autonomic nervous system have opposite metabolic roles. The parasympathetic ('rest and digest') division conserves and restores energy resources, while the sympathetic ('fight or flight') division mobilises and allocates resources for expenditure, so the correct pairing is parasympathetic then sympathetic (option a).","traps":{"b":"This reverses the pairing: the sympathetic system spends energy and the parasympathetic conserves it, so this order is backwards.","c":"The somatic nervous system controls voluntary skeletal muscle and does not regulate energy conservation versus expenditure.","d":"The autonomic system as a whole and the somatic system are not the conserving/spending pair; the contrast is between the two autonomic divisions."}},{"q":"Which neurotransmitter undergoes recycling by enzymatic degradation exclusively","concept":"Most classic transmitters are cleared from the synapse by reuptake transporters, but acetylcholine is the exception: it is removed almost entirely by enzymatic degradation, hydrolysed in the cleft by acetylcholinesterase into choline and acetate. Hence option c (ACh) is correct.","traps":{"a":"Dopamine is cleared mainly by the dopamine transporter (reuptake), not by exclusive enzymatic breakdown.","b":"GABA is removed chiefly by GABA transporters (reuptake into neurons and glia), not by degradation in the cleft.","d":"Glutamate is taken back up by excitatory amino-acid transporters (largely into astrocytes), not primarily degraded enzymatically."}},{"q":"Which of the following are not catecholamines?","concept":"Catecholamines are the transmitters synthesised from the amino acid tyrosine and sharing a catechol ring: dopamine, norepinephrine and epinephrine. GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid) is an amino-acid neurotransmitter derived from glutamate and lacks a catechol ring, so it is NOT a catecholamine (option a).","traps":{"b":"Dopamine is a catecholamine and the first in the synthesis chain from tyrosine, so it belongs to the group.","c":"Norepinephrine is a catecholamine, formed from dopamine, so it is part of the family.","d":"Epinephrine (adrenaline) is a catecholamine, formed from norepinephrine, so it is also in the group."}},{"q":"According to existentialists, what is the basic unity of person and environment expressed in?","concept":"In existential thought, Martin Heidegger's term Dasein ('being-there') names the basic, indissoluble unity of the person and their world, the being-in-the-world that exists before any split into subject and object. Option b is therefore correct.","traps":{"a":"Umwelt is one of Binswanger's three modes of world, the biological/physical environment, not the overall person-world unity.","c":"Mitwelt is the 'with-world', the realm of relationships with other people, only one mode of being-in-the-world.","d":"Eigenwelt is the 'own-world', one's relationship to oneself, again a single mode rather than the basic unity."}},{"q":"What is the third level of moral reasoning according to Kohlberg?","concept":"Lawrence Kohlberg's theory of moral development has three levels, each with two stages. The third and highest level is post-conventional morality, in which moral judgments rest on self-chosen, universal ethical principles rather than on punishment/reward or social approval. Option d is correct.","traps":{"a":"Pre-conventional morality is the FIRST level (obedience and self-interest), not the third.","b":"Conventional morality is the SECOND level (approval and law-and-order), not the third.","c":"'Autonomous moral principles' describes a feature of the post-conventional level but is not Kohlberg's own name for the third level."}},{"q":"Which style of contact in gestalt therapy is characterized by a high need for acceptance and liking, resulting in enmeshment?","concept":"In Gestalt therapy, disturbances of contact describe how people avoid genuine contact at the boundary. Confluence is the style in which the boundary between self and other dissolves; driven by a high need for acceptance and being liked, the person merges with others and loses a distinct sense of self, producing enmeshment (option b).","traps":{"a":"Projection is disowning one's own feelings and attributing them to others, not merging boundaries out of a need for acceptance.","c":"Introjection is swallowing others' values or beliefs whole without digesting them, not the boundary-dissolving enmeshment of confluence.","d":"Retroflection is turning back onto oneself what one wants to do to others, the opposite of merging outward."}},{"q":"Which step in systematic desensitization involves gradually exposing clients to anxiety-arousing situations?","concept":"Joseph Wolpe's systematic desensitization proceeds in three steps: relaxation training, construction of the anxiety hierarchy, and then desensitization proper. In desensitization proper the client, while staying deeply relaxed, is gradually exposed (in imagination or in vivo) to items up the hierarchy so anxiety is inhibited. Option c is correct.","traps":{"a":"Relaxation training only teaches the incompatible calm response; the graded exposure has not yet begun.","b":"Building the anxiety hierarchy just ranks feared situations from least to most distressing; no exposure occurs at this step.","d":"Cognitive restructuring is a technique from cognitive therapy, not a step within Wolpe's systematic desensitization at all."}},{"q":"Which stage is characterized by the development of sex-role identity and adult social relationships?","concept":"In Sigmund Freud's psychosexual stages, the genital stage begins at puberty and is the final stage, marked by mature sexual interest, the development of adult sex-role identity, and the capacity for mature social and sexual relationships. Option d is correct.","traps":{"a":"The oral stage (birth to about 1 year) centres on the mouth (sucking, biting), not adult sex-role identity.","b":"The anal stage (about 1 to 3 years) revolves around toilet training and control, not adult relationships.","c":"The phallic stage (about 3 to 6 years) involves the Oedipus/Electra complex, not mature adult sexuality."}},{"q":"What is the primary focus of the phallic stage of psychosexual development?","concept":"In Sigmund Freud's phallic stage (about 3 to 6 years), the genitals become the primary erogenous zone and the child develops incestuous desires toward the opposite-sex parent, resolved through the Oedipus complex in boys and the Electra complex in girls. Option c captures this primary focus.","traps":{"a":"Toilet training and pleasure from defecation define the anal stage, not the phallic stage.","b":"Development of adult sex-role identity and mature relationships belongs to the later genital stage.","d":"Sublimation of the sex instinct characterises the latency period that follows the phallic stage, not the phallic stage itself."}},{"q":"Who developed the basic behavioral procedure of systematic desensitization?","concept":"Systematic desensitization was developed by Joseph Wolpe, who based it on the principle of reciprocal inhibition: an anxiety response cannot occur at the same time as a relaxation response, so pairing graded exposure with deep relaxation weakens the anxiety. Option a is correct.","traps":{"b":"Carl Rogers founded client-centred (person-centred) therapy, a humanistic approach, not a behavioural exposure technique.","c":"Albert Bandura is known for social learning theory and modelling/self-efficacy, not for systematic desensitization.","d":"Sigmund Freud created psychoanalysis; systematic desensitization is a behavioural method quite unlike his approach."}},{"q":"Ionotropic receptors bind to","concept":"Ionotropic receptors are ligand-gated ion channels that open directly and rapidly when bound by small, fast amino-acid transmitters (glutamate, GABA, glycine) and acetylcholine. Larger transmitters and neuropeptides act more slowly through metabotropic (G-protein-coupled) receptors, so ionotropic receptors bind small transmitters only (option a).","traps":{"b":"Large peptide transmitters do not work through ionotropic channels; they signal via metabotropic receptors, so 'large and small' is wrong.","c":"Large transmitters act on metabotropic, not ionotropic, receptors, so 'large only' is the reverse of the truth.","d":"A correct answer exists (small transmitters only), so 'none of the above' is incorrect."}},{"q":"Varicosities are ________ synapses and they release  ___________ neurotransmitters","concept":"Varicosities are bead-like swellings along an axon that act as 'en passant' or non-directed release sites, releasing monoamine transmitters such as norepinephrine and serotonin diffusely over a wide area rather than into a tight synaptic cleft. Option a (non-directed; monoamine) is correct.","traps":{"b":"Varicosities are non-directed, not directed, and their transmitters are monoamines, not amino acids, so both halves are wrong.","c":"The 'non-directed' half is right, but varicosities release monoamines, not amino-acid transmitters.","d":"Varicosities are non-directed, so calling them 'directed' synapses is incorrect even though 'monoamine' is right."}},{"q":"What is Error degrees of freedom for a randomized block design with 4 treatments and 5 blocks (1 sample per block)","concept":"In a randomized block design analysed by two-way ANOVA without replication, the error (residual) degrees of freedom equal (t-1)(b-1), where t is the number of treatments and b the number of blocks. With 4 treatments and 5 blocks, error df = (4-1)(5-1) = 3 x 4 = 12, so option a is correct.","traps":{"b":"20 is the total number of observations (4 x 5), which is N, not the error degrees of freedom.","c":"15 confuses the calculation; it is not (t-1)(b-1) for these values.","d":"16 is (b-1) x (b-1) = 4 x 4, which uses the wrong term twice rather than (t-1)(b-1)."}},{"q":"how many pairwise comparisons will have to be made post-hoc for an Independent variable with 4 levels?","concept":"The number of unique pairwise comparisons among k groups is given by the combination k(k-1)/2. For an independent variable with 4 levels, that is 4 x 3 / 2 = 6 comparisons, so option d is correct.","traps":{"a":"8 does not follow from k(k-1)/2 for k = 4; it overcounts the possible pairs.","b":"12 equals k(k-1) = 4 x 3, which counts each pair twice (ordered pairs) instead of once.","c":"4 is simply the number of levels, not the number of pairwise comparisons among them."}},{"q":"Which of the following rotation methods can be used for second order factor analysis","concept":"Second-order (higher-order) factor analysis extracts factors from the correlations among the first-order factors, which is only possible if those first-order factors are allowed to correlate. That requires an oblique rotation, and among the options only Promax is oblique, so option a is correct.","traps":{"b":"Equimax is an orthogonal rotation that forces uncorrelated factors, so it cannot yield the correlated factors needed for a second-order analysis.","c":"Varimax is the most common orthogonal rotation and keeps factors uncorrelated, ruling out higher-order structure.","d":"Quartimax is also an orthogonal rotation, so like the others it produces uncorrelated factors and cannot support second-order analysis."}},{"q":"An uninvolved objective observation is done in ___________ approach of phenomenology; while an involved observation is done in ____________ approach of phenomenology","concept":"In phenomenological method, observation is classified by the observer's involvement. The indirect approach uses uninvolved, detached objective observation of the phenomenon, whereas the direct approach uses involved, participatory observation from within the experience. Hence indirect then direct (option a).","traps":{"b":"This starts with 'direct' for the uninvolved observation, which reverses the definitions; the detached one is indirect.","c":"'Indirect' correctly matches uninvolved observation, but the involved one is the direct approach, not 'semi-direct'.","d":"This pairs 'direct' with uninvolved and 'indirect' with involved, exactly backwards from the correct mapping."}},{"q":"Survey research does NOT","concept":"Survey research is a descriptive, non-experimental method: it can describe the distribution of a trait, its relative incidence in a population, and correlational relationships among variables. Because no variables are manipulated, it cannot establish the causal factors of an event, so option c is what survey research does NOT do.","traps":{"a":"Studying how a trait is distributed across a population is a core function of survey research, so this is something it DOES do.","b":"Surveys can examine correlational relationships between variables, so this is within its scope.","d":"Measuring the relative incidence or prevalence of a phenomenon is a standard survey aim, so this is something it does."}},{"q":"An exploratory research study is also a","concept":"Exploratory research is undertaken when a problem is poorly understood, in order to gain familiarity and generate ideas. Its purpose is to produce hypotheses and insights rather than to test them, so it is a hypothesis-generating study (option b).","traps":{"a":"Manipulating variables is a feature of experimental research; exploratory studies are typically non-manipulative.","c":"'All of the above' cannot hold because exploratory research is neither variable-manipulating nor hypothesis-testing.","d":"Hypothesis-testing (confirmatory) research is the opposite emphasis; exploratory work generates hypotheses instead of testing them."}},{"q":"In grounded theory, the process of weaving the data back together around groups of concepts is known as","concept":"In grounded theory, coding proceeds through open, axial, and selective phases. Axial coding is the stage that weaves the fragmented data back together by relating categories to their subcategories around groups of concepts, following open coding and preceding selective coding. Option b is correct.","traps":{"a":"'Early coding' is not a named grounded-theory stage; the initial breaking-apart phase is open coding.","c":"Open coding fractures the data into concepts; it takes the data apart rather than weaving it back together.","d":"'Core coding' is not a standard grounded-theory term; identifying the core category belongs to selective coding."}},{"q":"What is the degrees of freedom on a one-sample t-test with a sample size of 30","concept":"The degrees of freedom for a one-sample t-test equal n - 1, because one parameter (the mean) is estimated from the sample. With n = 30, df = 30 - 1 = 29, so option c is correct.","traps":{"a":"30 is the sample size n itself, not the degrees of freedom, which subtract one for the estimated mean.","b":"28 would be n - 2, which is the df for a different situation (e.g. simple regression slope), not a one-sample t-test.","d":"31 is n + 1, which has no basis; degrees of freedom never exceed the sample size here."}},{"q":"Which of the following measures of variability is affected by extreme scores in the sample","concept":"Measures of variability differ in their sensitivity to outliers. The standard deviation uses every score's squared deviation from the mean, so extreme scores pull it strongly; it is therefore affected by outliers (option c). Rank-based spread measures like the interquartile range are resistant.","traps":{"a":"The interquartile range uses only the middle 50% of scores, so extreme values in the tails do not affect it.","b":"Quartile deviation (semi-interquartile range) is also based on quartiles and is therefore resistant to extreme scores.","d":"'None of the above' is wrong because the standard deviation clearly is affected by extreme scores."}},{"q":"On a Binet-Simon type test (mean IQ= 100, sd =16), a person has secured an IQ of 136, what percentage of people fall below him?","concept":"Convert the score to a z-score using z = (X - mean)/SD. Here z = (136 - 100)/16 = 36/16 = +2.25. The proportion of the normal curve below z = 2.25 is about 0.987, so roughly 98.7% of people fall below this IQ (option c).","traps":{"a":"97.5% corresponds to z = +1.96, not z = 2.25, so it understates the percentile for this score.","b":"90% corresponds to about z = 1.28, far below the actual z of 2.25.","d":"84% corresponds to z = +1.0 (one SD above the mean), which would be an IQ of 116, not 136."}},{"q":"Ordered variables are considered to __________, while nominal variables are considered to be ___________","concept":"By level of measurement, ordinal (ordered) variables carry rank information and, having a meaningful order, are frequently treated as if continuous, whereas nominal variables are simply unordered labels and are purely categorical. Hence ordered = continuous, nominal = categorical (option b).","traps":{"a":"This reverses the pairing, labelling ordered variables as categorical and nominal as continuous, which is backwards.","c":"Ordered variables are treated as continuous, not 'discontinuous', so calling the ordinal type discontinuous is wrong.","d":"This calls ordered variables categorical and nominal 'discontinuous', misclassifying both levels of measurement."}},{"q":"Which of the following can NOT be used a test of homogeneity of variance before regression","concept":"Before regression, homogeneity (equality) of variance is checked with tests such as Levene's test and Bartlett's test. The Shapiro-Wilk test instead assesses whether data are normally distributed, a different assumption, so it cannot serve as a homogeneity-of-variance test (option c).","traps":{"a":"Levene's test is a standard, robust test of equality of variances, so it CAN be used for homogeneity of variance.","b":"Bartlett's test also tests homogeneity of variance (though it is sensitive to non-normality), so it qualifies.","d":"'None of the above' is wrong because Shapiro-Wilk genuinely cannot be used as a variance-homogeneity test."}},{"q":"The correlation between two variable where the common overlap with a third variable is statistically controlled is called","concept":"Partial correlation measures the relationship between two variables while statistically holding a third variable constant, removing the variance the third shares with both. This isolates the association net of the common overlap, so option b is correct.","traps":{"a":"Multiple correlation measures how well several predictors jointly relate to one outcome, not the two-variable link with a third partialled out.","c":"Spearman's correlation is a rank-order correlation coefficient; it does not control for a third variable.","d":"Phi correlation measures association between two dichotomous (2x2) variables, unrelated to statistically controlling a third variable."}},{"q":"Social loafing is also called __________","concept":"Social loafing is the tendency for individuals to exert less effort when working in a group than when working alone. It is also called the Ringelmann effect, after Max Ringelmann, whose rope-pulling experiments showed per-person effort declining as group size grew. Option a is correct.","traps":{"b":"The 'Triplett effect' refers to Norman Triplett's finding of social facilitation (better performance with others present), the opposite phenomenon.","c":"The McGurk effect is a perceptual illusion in which vision alters heard speech sounds, unrelated to group effort.","d":"'Free-riding' is a related motivation for loafing, but the established alternative name for social loafing is the Ringelmann effect."}},{"q":"What is the distinct type of motivation proposed by Maslow for self-actualizers?","concept":"Abraham Maslow distinguished the motivation of self-actualizers as metamotivation (also called being-motivation or growth motivation). Such people are driven by B-values like truth, beauty and justice rather than by deficiency needs, so option d is correct.","traps":{"a":"D-motivation (deficiency motivation) drives people to fill unmet needs; it is precisely what self-actualizers move beyond, not their distinct motivation.","b":"'E-motivation' is not a term in Maslow's theory.","c":"'F-motivation' is not a Maslowian concept either."}},{"q":"According to self-determination theory, what are the three basic needs that lead to a state of well-being?","concept":"Self-determination theory (Edward Deci and Richard Ryan) proposes three innate psychological needs whose satisfaction supports intrinsic motivation and well-being: competence (feeling effective), autonomy (feeling self-directed), and relatedness (feeling connected to others). Option b lists exactly these three.","traps":{"a":"'Achievement' is a motive from other theories (e.g. McClelland); the correct first need in SDT is competence, not achievement.","c":"'Independence' and 'superiority' are not SDT needs; the theory specifies autonomy and relatedness, not superiority.","d":"'Excellence' is not an SDT need; the third need is relatedness, which this option omits."}},{"q":"Gratitude increases prosocial behavior, primarily by enhancing helpers’ feelings of","concept":"Research on gratitude and helping (for example, Adam Grant and Francesca Gino) finds that expressed gratitude increases prosocial behaviour mainly by strengthening helpers' feelings of self-worth (feeling socially valued and appreciated) and self-efficacy (feeling capable of making a difference), rather than by simply improving mood. Option a is correct.","traps":{"b":"This pairs self-worth with 'self-confidence'; the research emphasises self-efficacy specifically, not general confidence.","c":"This substitutes 'self-esteem' for the studied construct of self-worth; the key finding centres on self-worth plus self-efficacy.","d":"This replaces both terms (self-esteem and self-confidence) rather than the researched pair of self-worth and self-efficacy."}},{"q":"In a POX relationship triad, balance exists when there are","concept":"Fritz Heider's balance theory describes the P-O-X triad, where P is the person, O is another person, and X is an object, idea, or third party, each pair linked by a positive (liking, agreement) or negative (disliking, disagreement) relation. The triad is balanced when the product of the three signs is positive, which happens with all three positive or with exactly one positive and two negative; balanced states feel psychologically comfortable and stable. An imbalanced triad (an even number of positives is broken; specifically an odd number of negatives) creates tension that pushes the person to change an attitude to restore balance. So two negatives and one positive multiply to a positive product and are balanced.","traps":{"a":"One negative and two positives gives a negative product (an odd number of negative links), so this triad is imbalanced and would generate pressure to change, not balance.","c":"Three negatives multiply to a negative product, an imbalanced state, even though it can feel intuitively stable, it fails Heider's sign-product rule.","d":"'No positives' is vague and, read as all-negative, still yields a negative product; balance depends on the product of signs, not on the mere absence of positive links."}}],"met-mock-1":[{"q":"The all-or-none law of action potentials means that:","concept":"The all-or-none law states that once a stimulus depolarises the membrane to threshold (about -55 mV), a full-amplitude action potential fires; a stronger stimulus does not make a bigger spike. The self-regenerating opening of voltage-gated sodium channels guarantees the spike always reaches the same peak and propagates without decrement along the axon. Stimulus intensity is encoded instead by firing frequency (rate coding) and by the number of neurons recruited, not by spike size. This is why option (d) is correct.","traps":{"a":"This describes a graded, amplitude-coded response, exactly what the all-or-none law denies; amplitude is fixed once threshold is reached and intensity is carried by frequency.","b":"Sub-threshold stimuli produce only local, decremental graded potentials, not miniature action potentials, so no propagating spike occurs at all.","c":"Grading a spike by stimulus intensity describes receptor or postsynaptic potentials, not the fixed-amplitude axonal action potential."}},{"q":"Per DSM-5-TR, the minimum duration required for a diagnosis of major depressive episode is:","concept":"The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition, Text Revision (DSM-5-TR) requires five or more depressive symptoms, including at least depressed mood or anhedonia (loss of interest/pleasure), present nearly every day for a minimum of 2 weeks, causing clinically significant distress or impairment. The 2-week floor distinguishes a diagnosable episode from ordinary transient low mood. This makes option (a) correct.","traps":{"b":"One week is the duration threshold for a manic episode, not a major depressive episode.","c":"One month has no specific role here; it is closer to durations used in some trauma-related criteria, not depression.","d":"Six months evokes the chronicity of conditions like generalised anxiety disorder, not the 2-week major depressive episode."}},{"q":"According to Piaget, the ability to mentally reverse operations first appears in which stage?","concept":"Jean Piaget's concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11 years) is defined by the emergence of logical mental operations that can be reversed, enabling conservation, decentration, and classification. Reversibility, the ability to mentally undo an action (for example, recognising that pouring water back restores the original amount), is what allows conservation to succeed here. This makes option (d) correct.","traps":{"a":"The sensorimotor stage (0 to 2 years) is about acquiring object permanence through action, well before any reversible mental operations.","b":"Preoperational children (2 to 7 years) are precisely those who fail conservation because their thinking is irreversible and centred on one dimension.","c":"Formal operational thought adds abstract and hypothetical reasoning; reversibility of concrete operations appears earlier, so this is not the first appearance."}},{"q":"If a test has reliability r_xx = 0.81, the maximum possible validity coefficient with any other measure is:","concept":"A test cannot correlate with any external criterion more strongly than the square root of its own reliability, because measurement error caps how much true-score variance is available to correlate. The validity ceiling equals the square root of r_xx when the criterion is perfectly reliable: the square root of 0.81 is 0.90. This makes option (c) correct.","traps":{"a":"0.81 is the reliability coefficient itself, not its square root; the ceiling is the square root of reliability, which is higher.","b":"A validity of 1.00 would require a perfectly reliable test (r_xx = 1.00), which is not the case here.","d":"0.95 is the square root of 0.9025, not of 0.81, so it overstates the ceiling."}},{"q":"The 'empty chair' technique is most associated with which approach?","concept":"The empty chair technique belongs to Gestalt therapy, developed by Fritz Perls. The client speaks to an imagined person or a disowned part of the self seated in an empty chair, often switching seats to voice both sides, promoting awareness, contact, and integration of split-off feelings in the present moment. This makes option (b) correct.","traps":{"a":"Cognitive Behaviour Therapy uses techniques like cognitive restructuring and behavioural experiments, not experiential empty-chair dialogues.","c":"Person-Centred Therapy relies on the therapeutic relationship and core conditions, not structured enactment techniques.","d":"Psychoanalysis uses free association and interpretation of transference, not chair work."}},{"q":"Choose the correctly punctuated sentence:","concept":"A non-restrictive relative clause, which adds extra information about an already-identified noun, must be enclosed by a comma on each side. In 'The patient, who was anxious, refused medication,' the clause is parenthetical and correctly bracketed by commas. This makes option (b) the only correctly punctuated sentence.","traps":{"a":"A semicolon cannot close a clause that a comma opened; mixing a comma and a semicolon around the clause is ungrammatical.","c":"Omitting both commas turns it into a restrictive clause (a grammatical reading), but the item asks for the correctly punctuated non-restrictive version.","d":"A single trailing comma with no opening comma leaves the relative clause unbalanced and incorrectly punctuated."}},{"q":"A Type I error in hypothesis testing occurs when:","concept":"A Type I error (alpha) is a false positive: rejecting the null hypothesis when it is actually true, that is, detecting an effect that does not exist. It is contrasted with a Type II error (beta), the false negative of retaining a false null. This makes option (d) correct.","traps":{"a":"Correctly retaining a true null is a correct decision, not an error of any type.","b":"Small sample size affects power and Type II error risk but is not itself the definition of a Type I error.","c":"Wrongly retaining a null that is false is the definition of a Type II error, not Type I."}},{"q":"During the absolute refractory period of a neuron:","concept":"During the absolute refractory period, the voltage-gated sodium channels are in their inactivated state and cannot reopen no matter how strong the stimulus, so no second action potential can be triggered. They reset to the closed-but-available state only after the membrane repolarises. This makes option (d) correct.","traps":{"a":"A stronger stimulus overcoming refractoriness describes the relative refractory period, not the absolute period where no spike is possible.","b":"Hyperpolarisation below rest characterises the after-hyperpolarisation phase, which coincides with the relative, not absolute, refractory period.","c":"Potassium efflux just beginning marks the start of repolarisation, whereas the absolute refractory period centres on sodium channel inactivation."}},{"q":"Schneider's first-rank symptoms are most associated with:","concept":"Kurt Schneider's first-rank symptoms (audible thoughts, voices arguing or commenting, thought insertion, withdrawal and broadcast, somatic passivity, made affect and impulse, and delusional perception) were historically treated as strongly suggestive of schizophrenia. Although DSM-5 removed their special diagnostic weight, they remain a clinically recognised marker of schizophrenia. This makes option (d) correct.","traps":{"a":"Bipolar disorder is defined by mood episodes (mania and depression), not by Schneiderian passivity phenomena.","b":"Borderline personality disorder centres on affective instability, identity disturbance, and impulsivity, not first-rank symptoms.","c":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder involves ego-dystonic obsessions and compulsions, not psychotic first-rank symptoms."}},{"q":"In Erikson's psychosocial theory, the crisis of middle adulthood is:","concept":"In Erik Erikson's psychosocial theory, middle adulthood (roughly 40 to 65 years) presents the crisis of Generativity versus Stagnation, resolved through contributing to the next generation and to society; the associated virtue is care. This makes option (a) correct.","traps":{"b":"Intimacy versus Isolation is the young-adulthood crisis about forming committed relationships, earlier than middle adulthood.","c":"Integrity versus Despair is the final, late-adulthood stage of reflecting on one's life.","d":"Identity versus Role Confusion is the adolescent crisis, well before middle adulthood."}},{"q":"A T-score of 70 on the MMPI corresponds to a deviation of:","concept":"The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI) reports scores as T-scores with a mean of 50 and a standard deviation of 10. A T-score of 70 is therefore (70 minus 50) divided by 10, equal to 2 standard deviations above the mean, the conventional clinical cut-off. This makes option (a) correct.","traps":{"b":"1.5 SD would correspond to a T-score of 65, not 70.","c":"1 SD would correspond to a T-score of 60, not 70.","d":"3 SD would correspond to a T-score of 80, not 70."}},{"q":"Carl Rogers' three core conditions for therapeutic change are:","concept":"Carl Rogers held that three therapist-offered core conditions are necessary and sufficient for constructive personality change: unconditional positive regard, congruence (genuineness), and accurate empathic understanding. Together they create a safe relational climate for the client's self-actualising tendency. This makes option (c) correct.","traps":{"a":"Transference, free association, and interpretation are psychoanalytic techniques, not Rogers' relational conditions.","b":"Cognitive restructuring, behavioural activation, and exposure are Cognitive Behaviour Therapy methods.","d":"Working alliance, transference, and resistance interpretation belong to psychodynamic practice, not person-centred theory."}},{"q":"Identify the antonym of 'GREGARIOUS':","concept":"'Gregarious' means sociable and fond of the company of others. Its antonym is 'reclusive,' describing someone withdrawn and preferring solitude. This makes option (b) correct.","traps":{"a":"Sociable is a synonym of gregarious, not its antonym.","c":"Affable means friendly and approachable, a near-synonym rather than an opposite.","d":"Convivial means cheerfully sociable, again a synonym, not an antonym."}},{"q":"The Mann-Whitney U test is the non-parametric equivalent of the:","concept":"The Mann-Whitney U test is the non-parametric counterpart of the independent-samples t-test: it compares two independent groups using ranks rather than raw means, without assuming normality. This makes option (a) correct.","traps":{"b":"The paired t-test's non-parametric equivalent is the Wilcoxon signed-rank test, not Mann-Whitney U.","c":"The one-way ANOVA's non-parametric equivalent is the Kruskal-Wallis test.","d":"The chi-square test is itself non-parametric and analyses categorical frequencies, a different design from Mann-Whitney U."}},{"q":"The corticospinal tract decussates primarily at the:","concept":"About 85 to 90 percent of corticospinal tract fibres cross the midline at the pyramidal decussation in the lower medulla, forming the lateral corticospinal tract; this is why one hemisphere controls the opposite side of the body. This makes option (d) correct.","traps":{"a":"The optic chiasm is where visual (optic nerve) fibres partially cross, unrelated to the motor corticospinal tract.","b":"Only the small anterior corticospinal tract crosses near its level of entry; the main decussation is in the medulla.","c":"The pons carries corticospinal fibres but is not the site of their decussation."}},{"q":"In DSM-5-TR, persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia) requires depressed mood for at least:","concept":"Under DSM-5-TR, persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia) requires depressed mood for most of the day, more days than not, for at least 2 years in adults (and at least 1 year in children and adolescents), plus two or more additional symptoms. This makes option (b) correct.","traps":{"a":"6 months is too short and does not match the chronic-duration requirement for persistent depressive disorder.","c":"1 year is the threshold for children and adolescents, not for adults, so it is incomplete as a general answer.","d":"5 years overstates the requirement; the criterion is 2 years in adults."}},{"q":"In Ainsworth's Strange Situation, an infant who shows distress on separation but cannot be comforted on reunion and may resist contact is classified as:","concept":"In Mary Ainsworth's Strange Situation, the insecure-resistant/ambivalent (Type C) infant is highly distressed by separation and, on reunion, simultaneously seeks contact and angrily resists it, remaining hard to soothe. This pattern reflects an inconsistently responsive caregiver. This makes option (a) correct.","traps":{"b":"The insecure-avoidant (Type A) infant minimises distress and actively avoids the caregiver on reunion, the opposite of resistant clinging.","c":"The secure (Type B) infant is comforted and readily settled on reunion.","d":"The disorganised (Type D) infant shows contradictory, dazed, or freezing behaviours rather than the coherent seek-and-resist pattern."}},{"q":"In Wechsler's classification, an FSIQ in the range 70–84 falls under:","concept":"On the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale, Fourth Edition (WAIS-IV), 80 to 89 is the Low Average range and 70 to 79 is Borderline; a span of 70 to 84 therefore straddles both, so the label depends on the exact score and edition. This makes option (a), low average / borderline depending on edition, correct.","traps":{"b":"Labelling the whole 70 to 84 span simply 'Borderline' ignores that 80 to 84 falls in the Low Average band.","c":"'Average' is 90 to 109, above the 70 to 84 range.","d":"Mild intellectual disability requires an IQ below 70 together with adaptive-functioning deficits, so it does not fit 70 to 84."}},{"q":"Aaron Beck's cognitive triad in depression refers to negative views of:","concept":"Aaron T. Beck's cognitive triad in depression comprises persistently negative views of the self, the world (current experience), and the future. These interlocking negative schemas drive the automatic negative thoughts central to his cognitive model. This makes option (d) correct.","traps":{"a":"Id, ego, and superego are Freud's structural components of personality, not Beck's cognitive triad.","b":"Past, present, and future is a plausible-sounding distractor, but Beck's triad targets self and world, not the past.","c":"Mother, father, and self reflect object-relations or family themes, not Beck's cognitive model."}},{"q":"Choose the correct preposition: 'The therapist insisted ___ a follow-up after two weeks.'","concept":"The verb 'insist' takes the preposition 'on' when followed by the thing demanded: one insists on something. 'The therapist insisted on a follow-up' is the standard collocation in British and Indian English. This makes option (c) correct.","traps":{"a":"'Insist for' is not an accepted collocation in standard English.","b":"'Insist with' is ungrammatical here; 'with' does not mark the demanded object.","d":"'Insist to' is incorrect before a noun; 'insist' pairs with 'on,' not 'to.'"}},{"q":"If the p-value of a study is 0.03 and α was set at 0.05:","concept":"When the p-value is smaller than the pre-set significance level alpha, the result is statistically significant and the null hypothesis is rejected. Here p = 0.03 is less than alpha = 0.05, so H0 is rejected. This makes option (b) correct.","traps":{"a":"Retaining the null would be correct only if p were greater than alpha, which is not the case.","c":"The result is significant because p is below alpha, so calling it not significant is wrong.","d":"A significant p-value says nothing directly about power; high power is not automatic and depends on effect size and sample size."}},{"q":"Which cranial nerve is purely motor and supplies most extraocular muscles?","concept":"The oculomotor nerve, cranial nerve III, is a motor nerve that supplies four of the six extraocular muscles (superior, inferior, and medial recti and the inferior oblique), plus the levator palpebrae superioris and, via parasympathetic fibres, the pupillary constrictor. This makes option (c) correct.","traps":{"a":"The optic nerve (II) is purely sensory for vision and moves no muscles.","b":"The trochlear nerve (IV) supplies only the superior oblique, not most extraocular muscles.","d":"The abducens nerve (VI) supplies only the lateral rectus, again not most of the muscles."}},{"q":"The cardinal feature of obsessive-compulsive disorder under DSM-5 is:","concept":"Under DSM-5, the cardinal feature of obsessive-compulsive disorder is the presence of obsessions and/or compulsions that are time-consuming (more than one hour per day) or cause clinically significant distress or impairment. An insight specifier was added in DSM-5. This makes option (a) correct.","traps":{"b":"Persistent depressive symptoms define persistent depressive disorder, not OCD.","c":"A pervasive pattern of grandiosity and need for admiration describes narcissistic personality disorder.","d":"Recurrent unexpected panic attacks are the hallmark of panic disorder, not OCD."}},{"q":"The zone of proximal development is a concept proposed by:","concept":"The zone of proximal development was proposed by Lev Vygotsky. It is the gap between what a learner can accomplish independently and what they can achieve with guidance or scaffolding from a more knowledgeable other, highlighting the social basis of cognitive development. This makes option (c) correct.","traps":{"a":"Jean Piaget emphasised individual, stage-based construction of knowledge, not the socially mediated ZPD.","b":"Urie Bronfenbrenner developed ecological systems theory, concerning nested environmental contexts.","d":"Lawrence Kohlberg proposed stages of moral development, unrelated to the ZPD."}},{"q":"Which projective test uses incomplete cartoon-style frames in which the respondent fills in what the depicted character is saying in a frustrating situation?","concept":"Saul Rosenzweig's Picture-Frustration (P-F) Study presents 24 cartoon-style frames showing frustrating situations, and the respondent writes what the frustrated character would reply. Responses are scored by direction of aggression (extrapunitive, intropunitive, impunitive) and by reaction type. This makes option (b) correct.","traps":{"a":"The Thematic Apperception Test uses ambiguous photograph-like pictures for whole story-telling, not cartoon frames to be filled in.","c":"The Rorschach uses inkblots and asks what they might be, not what a character is saying.","d":"The Sentence Completion Test uses verbal stems, not cartoon-style pictures."}},{"q":"Systematic desensitisation, developed by Joseph Wolpe, is based on the principle of:","concept":"Joseph Wolpe's systematic desensitisation rests on reciprocal inhibition: because deep muscle relaxation is physiologically incompatible with anxiety, pairing relaxation with a graded fear hierarchy progressively inhibits and weakens the conditioned fear response. This makes option (b) correct.","traps":{"a":"Operant extinction involves withdrawing reinforcement of a behaviour, a different learning mechanism from pairing relaxation with fear cues.","c":"Modelling is learning by observing others (Bandura), not the relaxation-based counterconditioning Wolpe used.","d":"Habituation alone (simple response decline to repeated stimuli) does not capture the active relaxation-anxiety antagonism central to the technique."}},{"q":"Identify the synonym of 'EPHEMERAL':","concept":"'Ephemeral' means lasting for a very short time. Its closest synonym is 'transient,' which likewise means short-lived or passing. This makes option (c) correct.","traps":{"a":"Vivid means bright or intensely clear, unrelated to duration.","b":"Permanent is the antonym of ephemeral, meaning long-lasting.","d":"Ancient means very old, which concerns great age rather than brief duration."}},{"q":"A Solomon four-group design is used to control for:","concept":"The Solomon four-group design adds two groups that receive no pretest (one treated, one control) to the standard pretested experimental and control groups. Comparing pretested and non-pretested groups lets the researcher detect and separate pretest sensitisation, the interaction between taking the pretest and the treatment. This makes option (b) correct.","traps":{"a":"Maturation is controlled by any design with a proper control group; the Solomon design's distinctive purpose is testing for pretest effects.","c":"Random assignment, not the four-group structure specifically, addresses selection bias.","d":"Researcher bias is handled by blinding and standardisation, not by adding no-pretest groups."}},{"q":"Damage to the ventromedial hypothalamus typically produces:","concept":"The ventromedial hypothalamus functions as the satiety centre. Bilateral lesions abolish the stop-eating signal, producing hyperphagia (overeating) and consequent obesity, the classic VMH syndrome. This makes option (c) correct.","traps":{"a":"Aphagia and weight loss follow lateral hypothalamic (the feeding centre) lesions, the opposite site.","b":"Insomnia is linked to other regions such as the anterior hypothalamus and reticular systems, not VMH feeding regulation.","d":"Thermoregulation is governed mainly by the preoptic/anterior hypothalamus, not the ventromedial satiety centre."}},{"q":"A patient presents with sudden inability to recall important autobiographical information, beyond ordinary forgetting, with no medical cause. The most likely diagnosis is:","concept":"Dissociative amnesia is diagnosed when a person cannot recall important autobiographical information, typically of a stressful or traumatic nature, that is too extensive to be ordinary forgetting and is not attributable to a medical, neurological, or substance cause. The psychogenic nature and absence of organic cause point here to option (d).","traps":{"a":"Transient global amnesia is a sudden, brief, self-limited anterograde memory loss with a presumed vascular/neurological basis, not a psychogenic autobiographical loss.","b":"Korsakoff syndrome is an organic amnestic disorder from thiamine deficiency (often alcohol-related), featuring confabulation and anterograde amnesia.","c":"Alzheimer's disease is a progressive neurodegenerative dementia, not a sudden isolated loss of autobiographical memory with no medical cause."}},{"q":"Bronfenbrenner's bioecological model places the immediate family in the:","concept":"Urie Bronfenbrenner's bioecological model nests a child's development inside layered systems. The microsystem is the innermost layer: the immediate, face-to-face settings the child directly participates in, such as the family, school classroom, and peer group. Because the immediate family is a direct, day-to-day setting the child lives within, it belongs to the microsystem.","traps":{"a":"The macrosystem is the outermost layer of overarching cultural values, laws, and customs, not a direct setting like the family.","b":"The mesosystem is not a setting itself but the web of connections between two microsystems, for example the link between home and school.","c":"The exosystem is a setting that affects the child indirectly without the child being present, such as a parent's workplace."}},{"q":"An item discrimination index (D) of −0.20 indicates that the item:","concept":"The item discrimination index (D) measures how well a test item separates high scorers from low scorers, ranging from -1.0 to +1.0. A positive D means high scorers answered correctly more often than low scorers, which is desired. A value of -0.20 is negative, meaning low scorers outperformed high scorers on that item, which signals the item is confusing, mis-keyed, or measuring something opposite to the intended trait, so it should be revised or discarded.","traps":{"a":"A well-functioning item to retain would show a positive discrimination index (typically 0.30 or higher), not a negative one.","b":"Item difficulty is captured by the p-value (proportion correct), not by D; a negative D does not tell you the item is too easy.","c":"Ideal difficulty is also a p-value concept (around 0.50), which is unrelated to and cannot be inferred from a negative discrimination value."}},{"q":"The ABC model in Ellis' REBT stands for:","concept":"Albert Ellis's Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy (REBT) uses the ABC model to show that emotions are driven by beliefs, not events directly. A is the Activating event, B is the person's Beliefs (especially irrational ones) about that event, and C is the emotional and behavioural Consequences. The therapist then Disputes (D) the irrational beliefs to produce a new Effect (E), making beliefs the pivotal middle term.","traps":{"a":"This substitutes 'Behaviour' for 'Beliefs', losing the core REBT insight that it is the belief, not behaviour, that mediates the consequence.","b":"Antecedent-Behaviour-Consequence is the Skinnerian ABC of behavioural analysis, a different model concerned with observable contingencies rather than irrational cognitions.","d":"Affect-Behaviour-Cognition is a mnemonic for the three components of an attitude, not Ellis's therapeutic model."}},{"q":"Choose the sentence with the correct subject-verb agreement:","concept":"The subject of the sentence is the indefinite pronoun 'each', which is grammatically singular and therefore requires a singular verb. The intervening prepositional phrase 'of the participants' does not change the number of the subject. The correct singular past-tense form is 'was given'.","traps":{"a":"'Were given' is plural and wrongly agrees with the nearer noun 'participants' rather than the singular subject 'each'.","b":"'Are given' is plural present tense and also fails the singular agreement required by 'each'.","c":"'Have given' is both plural and active voice, which changes the meaning (participants doing the giving) and breaks agreement."}},{"q":"Cluster sampling differs from stratified sampling primarily in that:","concept":"Both cluster and stratified sampling are probability methods, but they differ in what is randomized and why. In cluster sampling, the population is split into naturally occurring groups (clusters such as schools or villages), a random set of whole clusters is selected, and then all or a random subset of units within the chosen clusters are studied. This makes it efficient when a full list of individuals is unavailable but groups are easy to identify.","traps":{"a":"This reverses the two methods: stratified sampling draws individuals from within every stratum, while cluster sampling is the one that selects entire groups.","b":"Both cluster and stratified sampling are probability (random) methods, not non-probability methods.","d":"Requiring a complete prior listing of every individual describes simple random or stratified sampling; avoiding that listing burden is precisely why cluster sampling is used."}},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a basal ganglia structure?","concept":"The basal ganglia are subcortical grey-matter nuclei involved in motor control and include the caudate nucleus, putamen, globus pallidus, subthalamic nucleus, and substantia nigra. The red nucleus is not part of this group; it lies in the midbrain tegmentum and gives rise to the rubrospinal tract. Therefore the red nucleus is the exception.","traps":{"a":"The caudate nucleus is a core basal ganglia structure, forming part of the striatum with the putamen.","b":"The globus pallidus is a genuine basal ganglia nucleus and the main output relay of the striatum.","d":"The subthalamic nucleus is functionally part of the basal ganglia circuitry and is a target in Parkinson's deep-brain stimulation."}},{"q":"ICD-11 retained the category of 'gaming disorder' under:","concept":"The International Classification of Diseases, 11th Revision (ICD-11) formally introduced gaming disorder and placed it within the grouping 'disorders due to substance use or addictive behaviours', alongside gambling disorder as a behavioural (non-substance) addiction. This reflects the view that persistent, impairing gaming can follow an addiction pattern.","traps":{"b":"Disorders specifically associated with stress (such as PTSD and adjustment disorder) form a separate ICD-11 grouping unrelated to addictive behaviour.","c":"Mood disorders cover depressive and bipolar conditions, not the compulsive-reward pattern that defines gaming disorder.","d":"Classifying it under impulse control disorders only is the older framing; ICD-11 deliberately located it within the addictive-behaviours grouping instead."}},{"q":"Kohlberg's preconventional level of moral reasoning is dominated by:","concept":"Lawrence Kohlberg's preconventional level (stages 1 and 2) bases moral judgment on external consequences to the self rather than internalized social norms. Stage 1 is punishment-and-obedience (avoid punishment) and stage 2 is instrumental exchange (satisfy one's own needs through reciprocal deals). So this level is dominated by avoidance of punishment and instrumental exchange.","traps":{"b":"Maintaining laws and social order is stage 4, which sits in the conventional level, not the preconventional one.","c":"Social contract and universal ethical principles are stages 5 and 6 of the postconventional level, the most advanced reasoning.","d":"Conformity to peer and others' expectations is stage 3 ('good boy, nice girl'), which belongs to the conventional level."}},{"q":"Cattell's distinction between fluid and crystallised intelligence is operationalised in the Cattell-Horn-Carroll model. Crystallised intelligence (Gc) is best measured by:","concept":"In the Cattell-Horn-Carroll model, crystallised intelligence (Gc) is the store of acquired knowledge, verbal skills, and culturally learned information that accumulates through experience. It is best tapped by tasks drawing on learned content, such as the Vocabulary and Information subtests. Fluid intelligence (Gf), by contrast, is on-the-spot reasoning with novel material.","traps":{"a":"Block design is a visuospatial reasoning task loading on fluid intelligence and perceptual organisation, not on acquired verbal knowledge.","b":"Matrix reasoning is a classic measure of fluid intelligence (Gf), requiring novel pattern induction rather than stored knowledge.","c":"Coding or digit-symbol measures processing speed (Gs), not crystallised knowledge."}},{"q":"Dialectical Behaviour Therapy (DBT) was developed primarily for:","concept":"Dialectical Behaviour Therapy (DBT) was created by Marsha Linehan specifically for chronically suicidal and self-harming women diagnosed with borderline personality disorder. It blends standard cognitive-behavioural change strategies with acceptance, mindfulness, and dialectical philosophy (balancing acceptance and change). Though now adapted for other conditions, its original and primary target was BPD with chronic suicidality.","traps":{"b":"Schizophrenia is treated primarily with antipsychotics plus psychosocial approaches like CBT for psychosis, not DBT.","c":"OCD is most specifically treated with exposure and response prevention, a behavioural therapy distinct from DBT.","d":"Specific phobias are treated with graded exposure or systematic desensitisation, again not the population DBT was built for."}},{"q":"Identify the part of speech of the underlined word: 'The data were INCONCLUSIVE.'","concept":"The underlined word 'inconclusive' describes a quality of 'the data', telling us what the data were like. A word that modifies or describes a noun (here functioning as a predicate after the linking verb 'were') is an adjective. So 'inconclusive' is a predicate adjective.","traps":{"a":"A noun names a person, place, thing, or idea; 'inconclusive' names no entity, it only describes one.","b":"The verb in the sentence is 'were'; 'inconclusive' is the complement it links to, not the action word.","c":"An adverb would modify a verb, adjective, or another adverb and typically answer how or when; here the word modifies the noun 'data', making it adjectival."}},{"q":"In a normal distribution, approximately what percentage of cases falls within ±1 SD of the mean?","concept":"In a normal (bell-shaped) distribution, the empirical rule (the 68-95-99.7 rule) describes how cases spread around the mean. Approximately 68% of cases fall within plus or minus one standard deviation of the mean, about 95% within two standard deviations, and about 99.7% within three. Hence the answer is roughly 68%.","traps":{"a":"50% corresponds to cases falling below the mean (the median split), not to the region within one standard deviation.","b":"95% is the proportion within plus or minus two standard deviations, not one.","d":"99.7% is the proportion within plus or minus three standard deviations, the widest of the three bands."}},{"q":"The cones of the human retina are MOST concentrated in the:","concept":"The human retina has two photoreceptor types: cones for colour and fine detail, and rods for dim-light vision. Cones are packed most densely at the fovea centralis, a small pit at the centre of the macula that provides the sharpest visual acuity. Rods dominate the peripheral retina.","traps":{"a":"The optic disc is the blind spot where the optic nerve exits and contains no photoreceptors at all.","b":"The peripheral retina is dominated by rods, not cones, which is why peripheral vision is poor for detail and colour.","d":"Although the macula is cone-rich, cone density peaks specifically at the fovea within it, so 'macula but not the fovea' is wrong."}},{"q":"Anorexia nervosa is differentiated from bulimia nervosa primarily by:","concept":"Anorexia nervosa and bulimia nervosa both involve disturbed body image and weight preoccupation, so those cannot distinguish them. The defining difference is body weight: anorexia nervosa requires a significantly low body weight relative to what is expected, whereas people with bulimia nervosa are typically of normal weight or above. That weight criterion is the primary differentiator.","traps":{"a":"Body image disturbance is present in both disorders, so its supposed absence in bulimia is false.","c":"Compensatory behaviours (purging, fasting, excessive exercise) occur in bulimia and in the binge-purge subtype of anorexia, so they are not exclusive to anorexia.","d":"Purging can occur in anorexia (binge-eating/purging subtype), so its absence does not define anorexia."}},{"q":"The Visual Cliff experiment (Gibson & Walk, 1960) demonstrated that depth perception emerges:","concept":"In the Visual Cliff experiment by Eleanor Gibson and Richard Walk (1960), infants were placed on a glass surface over an apparent drop-off. Infants who could crawl (around 6 to 8 months) refused to venture onto the 'deep' side, indicating they perceived depth by that age. So depth perception is evident by the time infants can crawl.","traps":{"a":"Depth perception appears well before age 2; crawling-age infants already showed avoidance around 6 to 8 months.","c":"Depth perception is not fully developed at birth; it emerges over the first months, though heart-rate studies show some early sensitivity.","d":"Depth perception develops through maturation and experience, not through explicit verbal instruction, which infants could not follow anyway."}},{"q":"The Bender Visual Motor Gestalt Test is primarily used to screen for:","concept":"The Bender Visual Motor Gestalt Test asks the examinee to copy a series of geometric designs, assessing visuomotor integration (how well perception and motor output coordinate). It is used primarily to screen for organic brain damage and neuropsychological dysfunction, and in children for developmental maturation. So its main purpose is screening organic brain damage and visuomotor function.","traps":{"a":"Personality disorders are assessed with clinical interviews and personality inventories, not a design-copying visuomotor task.","c":"Schizophrenia is diagnosed clinically; the Bender is not a specific test for it, though gross distortions may prompt further evaluation.","d":"ADHD is assessed with rating scales and continuous performance tests, not primarily with the Bender-Gestalt."}},{"q":"In psychoanalysis, transference refers to:","concept":"In psychoanalysis, transference is the process by which a client unconsciously redirects feelings, wishes, and attitudes originally connected to significant figures from their past (often parents) onto the therapist. Analysing this displacement gives insight into the client's early relationships. So transference is the client redirecting such feelings onto the therapist.","traps":{"a":"The therapist's own emotional reaction to the client is countertransference, the mirror image of transference.","b":"Free association is the technique of voicing whatever comes to mind, a method of accessing the unconscious, not transference itself.","d":"Resistance is the client's blocking or opposition to the therapeutic process, a separate defensive phenomenon."}},{"q":"Choose the best one-word substitution for 'a person who is fluent in many languages':","concept":"A one-word substitution should exactly capture 'a person fluent in many languages'. 'Polyglot' means someone who knows and uses several languages, matching 'many'. It is more precise than words that mean only two languages or that describe a different role.","traps":{"b":"'Bilingual' specifically means fluent in only two languages, not many.","c":"'Linguist' primarily denotes a scientific student of language, though it can loosely mean a language-speaker; 'polyglot' is the exact fit for 'many languages'.","d":"'Orator' means a skilled public speaker, which says nothing about knowing multiple languages."}},{"q":"Cohen's d of 0.2, 0.5, and 0.8 are conventionally interpreted as:","concept":"Cohen's d expresses the difference between two means in standard-deviation units. Jacob Cohen proposed conventional benchmarks so effect sizes can be interpreted: d of 0.2 is a small effect, 0.5 is a medium effect, and 0.8 is a large effect. Hence the ordering is small, medium, large.","traps":{"b":"These labels (trivial, weak, moderate) understate the values; 0.8 in particular is conventionally 'large', not merely 'moderate'.","c":"This mislabels 0.2 as medium; 0.2 is Cohen's benchmark for a small effect.","d":"Calling all three values negligible is wrong, since 0.5 and especially 0.8 represent meaningful medium and large effects."}},{"q":"Place theory of hearing (Békésy) best explains the perception of:","concept":"The place theory of hearing, associated with Georg von Bekesy, holds that different sound frequencies displace the basilar membrane maximally at different locations. High-frequency sounds peak at the stiff basal end near the oval window, while low frequencies peak at the flexible apex. Pitch for high frequencies is therefore coded by the place of maximal displacement.","traps":{"b":"Low-frequency coding by neural firing rate is the frequency (temporal) theory, not place theory.","c":"Horizontal sound localisation depends on interaural time and intensity differences between the two ears, a separate mechanism from place coding.","d":"Loudness is coded largely by the number and firing rate of activated neurons, not by place along the basilar membrane."}},{"q":"A patient firmly believes that a close family member has been replaced by an identical impostor. This is:","concept":"This is a delusional misidentification syndrome. Capgras delusion is the fixed belief that a familiar person (often a spouse or close relative) has been replaced by an identical-looking impostor. Because the described patient believes a family member is a duplicate impostor, this is Capgras delusion.","traps":{"a":"Cotard delusion is the nihilistic belief that one is dead, does not exist, or has lost one's organs, not that others are impostors.","b":"Folie a deux is a shared delusion transmitted from one person to a close associate, describing transmission rather than the impostor belief itself.","c":"Fregoli delusion is the reverse: believing that different strangers are actually one familiar persecutor in disguise."}},{"q":"According to Baumrind, the parenting style with high warmth and high demandingness is:","concept":"Diana Baumrind classified parenting along two dimensions: warmth (responsiveness) and demandingness (control). The authoritative style combines high warmth with high demandingness, setting firm expectations while remaining supportive, and is linked to the best child outcomes. So high warmth plus high demandingness is authoritative.","traps":{"a":"Authoritarian parenting is high demandingness but low warmth, strict and cold rather than supportive.","c":"Permissive (indulgent) parenting is high warmth but low demandingness, lenient with few limits.","d":"Uninvolved (neglectful) parenting is low on both warmth and demandingness."}},{"q":"The Vineland Adaptive Behaviour Scales assess all of the following EXCEPT:","concept":"The Vineland Adaptive Behaviour Scales measure adaptive functioning, meaning everyday practical skills, not intelligence. Its domains are Communication, Daily Living Skills, Socialisation, and (for young children) Motor Skills. Verbal IQ is a cognitive construct measured by intelligence tests such as the Wechsler scales, so it is the exception the Vineland does not assess.","traps":{"a":"Communication is a core Vineland domain covering receptive, expressive, and written skills.","c":"Socialisation is a Vineland domain assessing interpersonal relationships, play, and coping skills.","d":"Daily Living Skills is a Vineland domain covering personal, domestic, and community self-care abilities."}},{"q":"Token economy is an example of:","concept":"A token economy is a behaviour-modification system grounded in operant conditioning. Desired behaviours earn tokens, which are conditioned (secondary) reinforcers because they have no intrinsic value but can be exchanged for primary reinforcers such as food or privileges. So it is operant conditioning using secondary reinforcers.","traps":{"a":"Classical conditioning involves pairing stimuli to elicit involuntary responses, not reinforcing voluntary behaviour with earned tokens.","c":"Cognitive restructuring is a cognitive therapy technique that changes maladaptive thoughts, not a reinforcement schedule.","d":"Insight-oriented therapy aims at unconscious understanding rather than shaping behaviour through tangible reinforcers."}},{"q":"Identify the correct meaning of the idiom 'to bury the hatchet':","concept":"The idiom 'to bury the hatchet' means to settle a dispute and make peace. It derives from a Native American custom of literally burying weapons to mark the end of hostilities. So the correct meaning is to make peace and end a quarrel.","traps":{"b":"Starting a quarrel is the opposite of the idiom's meaning of ending one.","c":"Hiding one's intentions is unrelated; the idiom is about reconciliation, not concealment.","d":"Digging up an old issue is the reverse image (unburying), which contradicts 'burying' the conflict."}},{"q":"An ANOVA F-test is significant. To determine which specific group means differ, you would conduct:","concept":"A significant omnibus ANOVA F-test tells you that at least one group mean differs from the others, but not which specific pairs differ. To locate exactly which means differ while controlling the inflated family-wise error rate from multiple comparisons, you run post-hoc tests such as Tukey's HSD, Bonferroni, or Scheffe. Hence post-hoc tests are the correct follow-up.","traps":{"b":"Running another ANOVA would just repeat the same omnibus result without identifying which pairs of means differ.","c":"A chi-square test is for categorical frequency data, not for comparing means of a continuous outcome.","d":"A Pearson correlation measures linear association between two continuous variables, not differences between group means."}},{"q":"Which neurotransmitter is most directly implicated in the positive symptoms of schizophrenia (mesolimbic pathway)?","concept":"The dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia links positive symptoms (hallucinations, delusions) to excess dopamine activity in the mesolimbic pathway. Antipsychotics that block dopamine D2 receptors reduce these symptoms, supporting the link. So dopamine is the neurotransmitter most directly implicated in positive symptoms.","traps":{"a":"Serotonin is more relevant to negative symptoms and is targeted by atypical antipsychotics, but it is not the primary driver of positive symptoms in the mesolimbic model.","b":"GABA is the main inhibitory neurotransmitter and is implicated in anxiety and seizure regulation, not directly in schizophrenia's positive symptoms.","d":"Acetylcholine is central to memory and is implicated in Alzheimer's disease, not the mesolimbic positive-symptom pathway."}},{"q":"Negative symptoms of schizophrenia include all EXCEPT:","concept":"Schizophrenia symptoms are divided into positive symptoms (added experiences like hallucinations and delusions) and negative symptoms (deficits in normal function). Negative symptoms are often summarised as the five As: avolition, alogia, anhedonia, asociality, and affective (emotional) flattening. Auditory hallucinations are a positive symptom, so they are the exception here.","traps":{"a":"Avolition, the loss of motivation and goal-directed activity, is a classic negative symptom.","c":"Affective flattening, reduced emotional expression, is a negative symptom.","d":"Alogia, poverty of speech, is a negative symptom."}},{"q":"Object permanence, in Piaget's theory, is fully achieved by approximately:","concept":"In Jean Piaget's theory, object permanence (knowing that objects continue to exist when out of sight) develops gradually across the sensorimotor stage. The A-not-B search error is overcome around 12 months, but fully mature object permanence, including mental representation of hidden objects, is achieved by the end of the sensorimotor stage, roughly 18 to 24 months. So full achievement is around 18 to 24 months.","traps":{"a":"At about 2 months infants are early in the sensorimotor stage and show no evidence of object permanence.","c":"8 to 12 months is when infants begin searching but still commit the A-not-B error, so object permanence is emerging, not fully achieved.","d":"At 4 months infants still act as though hidden objects cease to exist."}},{"q":"The Indian adaptation of the WAIS used commonly in clinical practice in India is the:","concept":"For adult intelligence assessment in India, the WAIS-IV India edition is the current standardised adaptation of the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale used in clinical practice. It is the culturally and normatively adapted adult scale, whereas the other listed tools are for children. So the WAIS-IV India edition is the intended answer.","traps":{"b":"The Binet-Kamat Test (BKT) is an Indian adaptation of the Stanford-Binet used for children, not the adult WAIS.","c":"Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children (MISIC) is a child intelligence test adapted from the WISC, not an adult WAIS adaptation.","d":"'All of the above' is wrong because BKT and MISIC are child scales, so they are not adaptations of the adult WAIS."}},{"q":"Motivational Interviewing (Miller & Rollnick) uses the acronym OARS to refer to:","concept":"Motivational Interviewing, developed by William Miller and Stephen Rollnick, is a collaborative, client-centred method for strengthening a person's own motivation to change. Its foundational micro-skills are captured by the acronym OARS: Open questions, Affirmations, Reflective listening, and Summaries. These skills are used to build rapport, evoke and reinforce 'change talk', and roll with resistance rather than confront it.","traps":{"b":"Outcomes, Assessment, Rewards, Sanctions describes a behaviour-management or contingency framework, not the relational micro-skills of Motivational Interviewing.","c":"Observation, Analysis, Reframing, Suggestion sounds clinical but is a fabricated expansion; MI deliberately avoids directive 'suggestion' in favour of evoking the client's own arguments for change.","d":"Openness, Acceptance, Responsibility, Support lists therapeutic attitudes rather than the specific OARS communication skills Miller and Rollnick defined."}},{"q":"Read the passage and answer:\n\n'Cognitive behaviour therapy (CBT) rests on the premise that distorted thinking patterns sustain emotional distress. Identifying and modifying these patterns can therefore alleviate the distress, even when the original triggers cannot be changed. Empirical trials over five decades support its effectiveness across a range of disorders.'\n\nThe central idea of the passage is:","concept":"A main-idea question asks for the claim that the whole passage is built to support, not an incidental detail. This passage states that cognitive behaviour therapy works because distorted thinking patterns sustain distress, so modifying those patterns relieves the distress even when triggers cannot be changed. Option b captures that central mechanism precisely.","traps":{"a":"'Cures every psychological disorder' overstates the passage, which claims effectiveness across a range of disorders, not a universal cure.","c":"The passage actually says the opposite: it emphasises that CBT helps even when triggers cannot be removed, so it does not claim triggers are easy to remove.","d":"The passage cites five decades of empirical trials as support, so it treats trials as valuable, not unnecessary."}},{"q":"A correlation of r = +0.90 between hours studied and exam scores indicates that hours studied:","concept":"A correlation coefficient (Pearson's r) measures only the strength and direction of a linear relationship between two variables; it never establishes causation. An r of +0.90 signals a strong positive linear association. To find the proportion of variance shared you must square r: r squared here is 0.81, meaning 81 percent of the variance, not 90 percent.","traps":{"a":"'Cause higher scores' commits the classic correlation-equals-causation error; a confound such as motivation could drive both variables.","b":"'90% of the variance' confuses r with the coefficient of determination r squared; 0.90 squared is 0.81, so the correct figure is 81 percent.","d":"'Causally unrelated' is contradicted by the strong association, and correlation cannot be used to rule causation in or out either way."}},{"q":"Long-term potentiation (LTP) in the hippocampus is critically dependent on which receptor?","concept":"Long-term potentiation is the lasting strengthening of synapses that underlies learning and memory in the hippocampus. It depends critically on the NMDA receptor, which acts as a coincidence detector: it opens only when glutamate binds and the postsynaptic membrane is simultaneously depolarised enough to expel the magnesium ion blocking its channel. The resulting calcium influx triggers the molecular cascade that induces LTP.","traps":{"a":"AMPA receptors mediate fast baseline excitatory transmission and are inserted during LTP expression, but they do not initiate it; they lack the magnesium-gated coincidence detection NMDA provides.","b":"Muscarinic M1 receptors are metabotropic acetylcholine receptors that modulate cortical excitability; they are not the trigger for hippocampal LTP.","c":"GABA-A receptors are inhibitory chloride channels and dampen neuronal firing, working against the depolarisation LTP requires."}},{"q":"Borderline personality disorder is characterised primarily by:","concept":"Borderline personality disorder is a Cluster B disorder in the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual defined by a pervasive pattern of instability in interpersonal relationships, self-image, and affect, together with marked impulsivity. Associated features include frantic efforts to avoid abandonment, recurrent suicidal or self-harming behaviour, chronic emptiness, and transient stress-related paranoia or dissociation. Option a states this core pattern directly.","traps":{"b":"Pervasive grandiosity and lack of empathy describe narcissistic personality disorder, a different Cluster B condition.","c":"Eccentric beliefs and odd speech without psychosis point to schizotypal personality disorder in Cluster A.","d":"Detachment from social relationships with restricted emotional expression describes schizoid personality disorder, not the emotional volatility of BPD."}},{"q":"Harlow's surrogate mother experiments with rhesus monkeys demonstrated that:","concept":"Harry Harlow's surrogate mother experiments gave infant rhesus monkeys a choice between a wire mother that dispensed milk and a soft cloth mother that gave no food. The infants clung to the cloth mother and returned to it for comfort, especially when frightened, seeking it far more than the feeding wire mother. This showed that contact comfort, not feeding, is the primary basis of attachment, refuting the 'cupboard love' secondary-drive theory.","traps":{"a":"'Feeding is the primary basis of attachment' is exactly the secondary-drive theory Harlow's results overturned.","b":"'Imprinting occurs only in birds' refers to Konrad Lorenz's work with geese and is unrelated to what Harlow's monkey study demonstrated.","d":"'Solely instinctive and does not require contact' is contradicted by the finding that contact comfort was decisive for the infants."}},{"q":"A test–retest reliability coefficient is most appropriate when assessing:","concept":"Test-retest reliability estimates the stability of a measure by administering the same test to the same people on two occasions and correlating the scores; it tells you whether scores hold steady over time. Each form of reliability answers a different question, so matching the coefficient to the target concept is key. Stability over time is the defining purpose of test-retest.","traps":{"a":"Internal consistency of items is assessed with Cronbach's alpha or split-half reliability, not test-retest.","b":"Inter-rater agreement is measured with Cohen's kappa or the intraclass correlation coefficient, comparing raters rather than occasions.","c":"Equivalence of two parallel forms is measured by parallel-forms (alternate-forms) reliability, using two different but equivalent versions of the test."}},{"q":"Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT) aims primarily to increase:","concept":"Acceptance and Commitment Therapy, developed by Steven Hayes within the third wave of behaviour therapy, aims primarily to increase psychological flexibility rather than to eliminate symptoms. It does this through six interrelated core processes forming the 'hexaflex': acceptance, cognitive defusion, present-moment awareness, self-as-context, values, and committed action. The goal is a rich, values-driven life alongside difficult internal experiences.","traps":{"a":"Symptom elimination is explicitly not the aim of ACT, which encourages acceptance of unwanted thoughts and feelings instead of struggling to remove them.","c":"Insight into childhood conflicts is a goal of psychodynamic therapy, not ACT.","d":"Cognitive disputation of irrational beliefs is the method of Albert Ellis's Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy; ACT deliberately uses defusion rather than disputing thought content."}},{"q":"Per the same passage, the author's tone toward CBT is best described as:","concept":"Tone questions ask about the author's attitude as revealed by word choice. This passage praises CBT and cites decades of supporting trials, but it uses careful qualifiers such as 'a range of' disorders and says distress 'can' be alleviated, avoiding sweeping claims. That combination of endorsement plus hedging makes the tone supportive but qualified.","traps":{"a":"'Dismissive' would require the author to downplay or reject CBT, but the passage endorses it.","b":"'Sarcastic' implies ironic mockery, which is absent from the straightforward, approving prose.","d":"'Polemical' means aggressively argumentative or combative, whereas the passage is measured and even-handed."}},{"q":"The CONSORT statement provides reporting guidelines for:","concept":"The CONSORT statement (Consolidated Standards of Reporting Trials) is a set of evidence-based guidelines, centred on a checklist and flow diagram, for transparently reporting randomised controlled trials. Each major study design has its own reporting standard, so the disorder-specific guideline must be matched correctly. CONSORT is the one for RCTs.","traps":{"a":"Systematic reviews and meta-analyses are covered by the PRISMA statement, not CONSORT.","b":"Qualitative research reporting is guided by COREQ or SRQR standards.","c":"Diagnostic accuracy studies follow the STARD guidelines."}},{"q":"The hormone secreted by the posterior pituitary that promotes water reabsorption is:","concept":"The posterior pituitary stores and releases two hormones made by hypothalamic neurons. The one that promotes water reabsorption is antidiuretic hormone, also called vasopressin, which is synthesised in the supraoptic nucleus of the hypothalamus and acts on the collecting ducts of the kidney to conserve water. Option c names it correctly.","traps":{"a":"Oxytocin is also released from the posterior pituitary but drives uterine contraction and milk let-down, not water reabsorption.","b":"Cortisol is a glucocorticoid secreted by the adrenal cortex, not the pituitary.","d":"Prolactin is released from the anterior (not posterior) pituitary and stimulates milk production."}},{"q":"The most common comorbidity with autism spectrum disorder in DSM-5 is:","concept":"Autism spectrum disorder in the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual very frequently co-occurs with other conditions. The most common comorbidities are intellectual disability, present in roughly half of cases, and attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder; DSM-5 notably lifted the earlier prohibition and now permits diagnosing ASD and ADHD together. Option d captures this correctly.","traps":{"a":"Conduct disorder is not a characteristic comorbidity of ASD; it is associated more with disruptive behaviour disorder pathways.","b":"Schizophrenia is a much rarer co-occurrence and was historically confused with autism, but it is not the common comorbidity.","c":"Bipolar I can co-occur but is far less frequent than intellectual disability or ADHD in ASD populations."}},{"q":"Telegraphic speech, characterised by two-word utterances such as 'mommy go', typically appears around:","concept":"Language development moves through predictable stages. Around 12 months children use single words (the holophrastic stage), and around 18 to 24 months they combine words into two-word 'telegraphic' utterances such as 'mommy go' that omit grammatical function words while preserving content words. This two-word telegraphic stage therefore appears at roughly 18 to 24 months.","traps":{"b":"12 months marks the one-word holophrastic stage, which precedes two-word combinations.","c":"At 6 months infants are babbling, well before meaningful word combinations.","d":"By 36 months children typically produce longer, more grammatically complete sentences, past the telegraphic stage."}},{"q":"The NEO-PI-R measures personality based on:","concept":"The NEO-PI-R, developed by Paul Costa and Robert McCrae, is the flagship measure of the Five-Factor Model of personality, summarised by the acronym OCEAN: Openness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness, and Neuroticism. It assesses each of the five broad domains through six specific facets, giving a detailed trait profile. Option c identifies this framework.","traps":{"a":"Cattell's 16 personality factors are measured by the 16PF questionnaire, not the NEO-PI-R.","b":"Eysenck's PEN model (Psychoticism, Extraversion, Neuroticism) is assessed by the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire.","d":"Murray's needs-press theory underlies the Thematic Apperception Test and the Edwards Personal Preference Schedule, not the NEO-PI-R."}},{"q":"EMDR was developed by:","concept":"Eye Movement Desensitisation and Reprocessing was developed by Francine Shapiro in 1987 as a structured therapy for trauma, in which the client attends to distressing memories while making guided bilateral eye movements or other rhythmic stimulation. It is now recommended for post-traumatic stress disorder by bodies including the American Psychological Association and the World Health Organization. Option a names its originator.","traps":{"b":"Marsha Linehan developed Dialectical Behaviour Therapy for borderline personality disorder, not EMDR.","c":"Aaron Beck founded Cognitive Therapy, a different modality.","d":"Edna Foa developed Prolonged Exposure therapy for PTSD, which is distinct from EMDR."}},{"q":"Find the error: 'Neither the patient nor the family members was informed about the diagnosis.'","concept":"When a subject uses the correlative 'neither...nor', the verb agrees in number with the nearer of the two subjects (the proximity rule). Here 'the family members' is plural and stands closest to the verb, so the correct verb is 'were', not 'was'. The error is therefore the word 'was'.","traps":{"a":"'Neither' is used correctly to introduce the first element of the neither/nor pair.","b":"'The patient' is grammatically fine; it is simply the more distant subject and does not govern the verb here.","c":"'No error' is wrong because the singular verb 'was' violates the proximity agreement rule."}},{"q":"REM sleep is characterised by all EXCEPT:","concept":"Rapid eye movement sleep is defined by low-voltage, mixed-frequency, fast EEG activity resembling wakefulness, together with rapid eye movements, near-complete skeletal muscle atonia, and genital tumescence. It is where most vivid dreaming occurs. Delta (slow) waves are the hallmark of deep NREM Stage 3 sleep, not REM, so the EXCEPT answer is the delta-wave option.","traps":{"a":"Rapid eye movements are a defining feature of REM sleep, so this is a true characteristic, not the exception.","c":"Skeletal muscle atonia genuinely occurs in REM, preventing acting out of dreams.","d":"Penile or clitoral tumescence is a normal accompaniment of REM sleep and is therefore also true."}},{"q":"PTSD per DSM-5-TR requires symptoms from how many symptom clusters?","concept":"Post-traumatic stress disorder in the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition, Text Revision requires symptoms drawn from four clusters following exposure to a traumatic event: intrusion (Criterion B), avoidance (Criterion C), negative alterations in cognitions and mood (Criterion D), and marked alterations in arousal and reactivity (Criterion E). Symptoms must persist for more than one month. The answer is four clusters.","traps":{"b":"Three clusters reflects the older DSM-IV structure, before the negative-cognitions cluster was separated out.","c":"Two clusters undercounts and does not match any DSM-5 formulation of PTSD.","d":"Five clusters overstates it; there are four symptom clusters (B through E), with A being the stressor criterion, not a symptom cluster."}},{"q":"In Bowlby's theory, the phase during which an infant displays clear attachment behaviour to one specific caregiver and shows separation anxiety is:","concept":"John Bowlby described attachment as unfolding in phases. The clear-cut attachment phase, from roughly 7 months to 2 years, is when the infant shows focused attachment to one specific caregiver, protests at separation (separation anxiety), and displays wariness of strangers. Option b names this phase.","traps":{"a":"The pre-attachment phase (birth to about 6 weeks) involves innate signalling with no discrimination between caregivers.","c":"The attachment-in-the-making phase (about 6 weeks to 6-8 months) shows preference for familiar people but not yet strong protest at separation.","d":"The reciprocal relationships phase (from about 2 years) involves negotiation and the goal-corrected partnership, after separation anxiety declines."}},{"q":"If a raw score lies at the 84th percentile on a normally distributed test, the corresponding z-score is approximately:","concept":"A z-score expresses how many standard deviations a raw score lies from the mean of a normal distribution. Because the normal curve is fixed, the 50th percentile is the mean (z of 0), and about 84 percent of scores fall below one standard deviation above the mean. So a raw score at the 84th percentile corresponds to a z-score of approximately +1.","traps":{"a":"A z of 0 sits at the mean, the 50th percentile, not the 84th.","c":"A z of +1.5 lies near the 93rd percentile, above the 84th.","d":"A z of +2 corresponds to roughly the 97.5th percentile, far above the 84th."}},{"q":"In family therapy, the structural approach is associated with:","concept":"Structural family therapy was developed by Salvador Minuchin, who viewed family problems as arising from a dysfunctional family organisation. His approach maps subsystems, hierarchies, and the boundaries between members (rigid, clear, or diffuse), and the therapist actively restructures these to restore healthy functioning. Option a identifies him.","traps":{"b":"Murray Bowen founded intergenerational (Bowenian) family therapy, emphasising differentiation of self and triangulation.","c":"Virginia Satir developed the experiential, communication-focused approach to family therapy.","d":"Jay Haley is associated with strategic family therapy, which uses directives and paradoxical interventions."}},{"q":"Choose the correct passive form of: 'The committee will announce the results tomorrow.'","concept":"Converting an active sentence to the passive keeps the same tense. The original 'The committee will announce the results tomorrow' is future simple active, so its passive form must also be future simple: the object 'the results' becomes the subject, and the verb becomes 'will be announced', giving 'The results will be announced by the committee tomorrow.'","traps":{"b":"'Are being announced' is present continuous passive, which changes the tense and clashes with 'tomorrow'.","c":"'Have been announced' is present perfect passive and wrongly implies the action is already complete.","d":"'Were announced' is past simple passive, contradicting the future reference of the original sentence."}},{"q":"A patient with damage to the right parietal lobe is most likely to show:","concept":"The right (typically non-dominant) parietal lobe, especially the inferior parietal region, governs spatial attention across both sides of space. Damage here classically produces left hemispatial neglect: the patient fails to attend to, report, or respond to stimuli on the left side, and may ignore the left half of their own body and drawings. Option c describes this syndrome.","traps":{"a":"Broca's aphasia follows damage to the left inferior frontal lobe, not the right parietal lobe.","b":"Anterograde amnesia results from bilateral hippocampal or medial temporal damage, not parietal lesions.","d":"Prosopagnosia (face blindness) arises from lesions in the fusiform face area of the occipitotemporal cortex."}},{"q":"A delirium differs from a major neurocognitive disorder primarily in:","concept":"Delirium and major neurocognitive disorder (dementia) both involve cognitive impairment, but they differ chiefly in course and the domain affected. Delirium has an acute onset over hours to days, a fluctuating course, and a primary disturbance of attention and awareness, often with an identifiable medical cause. Major neurocognitive disorder has an insidious onset and a relatively stable, progressive decline, with memory or other cognition affected more than attention.","traps":{"a":"Hallucinations can occur in both conditions and are not exclusive to delirium.","b":"Memory impairment is prominent in major neurocognitive disorder too, so it does not distinguish the two.","d":"Reversibility is more characteristic of delirium (which can resolve when the cause is treated), not of dementia, so this reverses the actual pattern."}},{"q":"Theory of mind is most commonly assessed in 4–5 year olds using the:","concept":"Theory of mind is the understanding that other people have mental states, including beliefs, that may differ from one's own and from reality. It is classically tested in 4 to 5 year olds with the false-belief task, of which the Sally-Anne task is the best known: the child must predict that Sally will look for her marble where she left it, not where it was secretly moved. Children reliably pass around age 4.","traps":{"a":"The A-not-B task assesses object permanence and inhibitory control in infants, not theory of mind.","b":"The conservation of liquid task measures Piagetian concrete-operational understanding of quantity, not mental-state reasoning.","d":"The Strange Situation assesses infant attachment security, not false-belief understanding."}},{"q":"The Halstead-Reitan Battery is used for:","concept":"The Halstead-Reitan Neuropsychological Battery is a fixed, comprehensive set of standardised tests (including the Category Test, Tactual Performance Test, and Trail Making Test) used to detect and characterise brain dysfunction. Its results are combined into an overall Impairment Index. It is therefore a tool for comprehensive neuropsychological assessment, as option c states.","traps":{"a":"Personality assessment uses instruments like the MMPI or NEO-PI-R, not the Halstead-Reitan.","b":"Vocational interest assessment uses inventories such as the Strong Interest Inventory.","d":"Projective assessment of children uses tools like the Children's Apperception Test or Rorschach, which are unrelated to this neuropsychological battery."}},{"q":"The therapeutic factor in group therapy described by Yalom as 'the realisation that one is not alone in their suffering' is:","concept":"Irvin Yalom identified eleven therapeutic factors that make group therapy effective. Universality is the member's realisation that they are not alone, that others share similar problems, fears, and feelings; this discovery reduces the sense of isolation and shame. Option b names this factor.","traps":{"a":"Catharsis is the relief that comes from openly expressing strong emotion within the group, a different factor.","c":"Altruism is the benefit members gain by helping and supporting one another.","d":"Instillation of hope is the encouragement members draw from seeing others who have improved."}},{"q":"Choose the word closest in meaning to 'AMBIVALENT':","concept":"A synonym question asks for the word closest in meaning. 'Ambivalent' means simultaneously holding opposing or conflicting feelings or attitudes toward something, so 'having mixed feelings' is the precise match. It is important not to confuse ambivalence (two feelings at once) with indifference (no feeling).","traps":{"b":"'Indifferent' means having no feeling or interest at all, the opposite of holding two strong feelings at once.","c":"'Decisive' means able to make firm choices quickly, which contradicts the internal conflict of ambivalence.","d":"'Hostile' denotes clear-cut antagonism, a single negative attitude rather than mixed feelings."}},{"q":"The autosomal dominant disorder caused by a CAG trinucleotide repeat expansion on chromosome 4 is:","concept":"Huntington's disease is an autosomal dominant neurodegenerative disorder caused by an expanded CAG trinucleotide repeat in the HTT gene on the short arm of chromosome 4. Having more than about 36 repeats causes the disease, and the repeat tends to lengthen across generations, producing earlier onset known as anticipation. Option d identifies it.","traps":{"a":"Down syndrome is caused by trisomy 21 (an extra chromosome 21), not a trinucleotide repeat.","b":"Phenylketonuria is an autosomal recessive metabolic disorder caused by a deficiency of phenylalanine hydroxylase, not a CAG expansion.","c":"Fragile X syndrome is caused by a CGG (not CAG) repeat expansion in the FMR1 gene on the X chromosome."}},{"q":"The biopsychosocial model in psychopathology is most associated with:","concept":"The biopsychosocial model holds that health and illness result from the interacting contributions of biological, psychological, and social factors, rather than biology alone. It was proposed by the psychiatrist George L. Engel in 1977 as an explicit challenge to the reductionist biomedical model. Option d names him.","traps":{"a":"Emil Kraepelin pioneered the descriptive classification of mental disorders, laying groundwork for the biomedical, not biopsychosocial, approach.","b":"Aaron T. Beck founded cognitive therapy and the cognitive model of depression, not the biopsychosocial model.","c":"Eugen Bleuler coined the term 'schizophrenia' and described its symptoms, but did not formulate the biopsychosocial model."}},{"q":"The temperamental category in the New York Longitudinal Study (Thomas & Chess) characterised by withdrawal from new stimuli but gradual adaptation is:","concept":"In the New York Longitudinal Study, Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess described three temperamental clusters: easy, difficult, and slow-to-warm-up. The slow-to-warm-up child shows initial withdrawal and mild negative reactions to novelty and low activity, but with repeated, patient exposure gradually adapts and warms up. The defining pattern here is withdrawal from new stimuli followed by gradual adaptation, which is exactly the slow-to-warm-up profile.","traps":{"a":"Easy temperament describes a child with regular rhythms, positive mood, and quick adaptability who approaches (not withdraws from) novelty, so it misses the initial withdrawal in the stem.","c":"Difficult temperament involves irregular biological rhythms, intense negative reactions, and poor adaptation, not the gradual warming-up the question specifies.","d":"Inhibited only is not one of the three Thomas and Chess categories; it borrows from Jerome Kagan's behavioral inhibition work and is a distractor invented to sound plausible."}},{"q":"Standardisation in test construction primarily refers to:","concept":"Standardisation in test construction means fixing the conditions of a test so results are comparable across people: uniform administration instructions, uniform scoring rules, and a representative norm group against which raw scores are interpreted. It is the procedural foundation that lets a score mean the same thing for everyone. Reliability and validity are separate psychometric properties that build on, but are not the same as, standardisation.","traps":{"a":"High validity (whether a test measures what it claims) is a distinct psychometric property, not what standardisation refers to.","b":"Eliminating cultural bias relates to test fairness and item bias analysis; standardisation fixes procedures and norms but does not by itself remove cultural bias.","d":"Computer-administration is merely one delivery mode; a paper test can be fully standardised and a computer test can be poorly standardised, so the medium is irrelevant to the concept."}},{"q":"Interpersonal Therapy (IPT) for depression focuses primarily on:","concept":"Interpersonal Therapy, developed by Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman, is a brief (roughly 12 to 16 session) evidence-based treatment for major depression. It links depressive symptoms to current relationship difficulties and works on one or more of four defined problem areas: grief, interpersonal role disputes, role transitions, and interpersonal deficits. Its focus is squarely on present interpersonal functioning rather than intrapsychic or cognitive content.","traps":{"a":"Childhood unconscious conflicts are the target of psychodynamic or psychoanalytic therapy, whereas IPT deliberately stays in the here-and-now of current relationships.","c":"Cognitive distortions are the focus of Aaron T. Beck's cognitive therapy, not the interpersonal problem areas that define IPT.","d":"Reinforcement schedules belong to operant behaviour therapy derived from B. F. Skinner, which is a different treatment model entirely."}},{"q":"Identify the correctly spelt word:","concept":"The correct spelling is conscientious, meaning careful, diligent, and guided by conscience. The word keeps the -sci- cluster from its root (conscience) and ends in -tious. Recognising the shared conscience root helps fix both the tricky -sci- and the ending.","traps":{"a":"Conscentious drops the 'i' after 'sc' and misplaces letters, so it is a misspelling.","b":"Concientious loses the 's' from the -sci- cluster and is therefore wrong.","c":"Consientious both drops the 'c' after 'con' and mangles the -sci- cluster, making it incorrect."}},{"q":"The Yerkes–Dodson law predicts that performance on a complex task is best at:","concept":"The Yerkes-Dodson law, formulated by Robert Yerkes and John Dillingham Dodson, describes an inverted-U relationship between physiological arousal and performance: performance improves as arousal rises up to an optimum, then declines with further arousal. A key refinement is that the optimal arousal level is lower for complex or difficult tasks and higher for simple, well-learned ones. So a complex task peaks at moderate arousal, with that optimum shifted lower than for easy tasks.","traps":{"a":"Very high arousal degrades performance on complex tasks, sitting on the falling side of the inverted-U, so it is not optimal.","b":"Very low arousal leaves the person under-activated and below the performance peak, so it is not the optimum either.","c":"Claiming the optimum is independent of task difficulty ignores the law's central refinement that difficulty shifts where the peak lies."}},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a Cluster A personality disorder?","concept":"In the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, personality disorders are grouped into three clusters. Cluster A (odd or eccentric) contains paranoid, schizoid, and schizotypal personality disorders. Cluster B (dramatic or erratic) contains antisocial, borderline, histrionic, and narcissistic. Cluster C (anxious or fearful) contains avoidant, dependent, and obsessive-compulsive personality disorder, so avoidant is the one that does not belong to Cluster A.","traps":{"a":"Paranoid personality disorder is a Cluster A (odd/eccentric) disorder, so it is not the exception.","c":"Schizotypal personality disorder is a Cluster A disorder marked by eccentricity and cognitive-perceptual distortions.","d":"Schizoid personality disorder, defined by social detachment and restricted emotion, is also Cluster A."}},{"q":"In Freud's psychosexual theory, fixation at the anal stage is hypothesised to produce:","concept":"In Sigmund Freud's psychosexual theory, the anal stage (roughly 1.5 to 3 years) centres on toilet training and control over elimination. Unresolved conflict here can lead to fixation producing either an anal-retentive character (orderly, rigid, stingy, obstinate) or an anal-expulsive character (messy, disorganised, careless). Hence anal fixation is classically tied to the anal-retentive or anal-expulsive personality.","traps":{"a":"Oral aggression arises from fixation at the oral stage (biting sub-phase), not the anal stage.","b":"The Oedipus complex is a feature of the phallic stage, when the child directs desire toward the opposite-sex parent.","d":"Genital identity confusion is not a Freudian anal-stage outcome; the genital stage concerns mature adult sexuality, and this option is a distractor."}},{"q":"Item response theory (IRT) differs from classical test theory primarily in that IRT:","concept":"Item response theory models the probability of a particular response as a mathematical (usually logistic) function of a person's latent trait level (theta) and item parameters such as difficulty, discrimination, and guessing. Unlike classical test theory, whose item statistics depend on the particular sample, IRT aims for parameters that are invariant across samples, which is what enables computer-adaptive testing. Its defining feature is this trait-by-item probability model.","traps":{"a":"Treating item statistics as sample-dependent is a limitation of classical test theory, the opposite of what IRT achieves.","b":"IRT models can include multiple parameters (one, two, or three-parameter models); assuming difficulty only describes just the simplest 1PL case, not IRT in general.","c":"IRT is precisely what makes adaptive testing possible, so saying it cannot be used for adaptive testing is false."}},{"q":"In CBT, a 'downward arrow' technique is used to:","concept":"The downward-arrow technique in cognitive behaviour therapy starts from an automatic thought and repeatedly asks a question like 'if that were true, what would it mean about you?' to peel back successive layers of meaning. Each answer is treated as if true and probed again until an underlying core belief or schema is reached (for example, 'I am unlovable' or 'I am worthless'). Its purpose is to uncover the core belief beneath a surface thought.","traps":{"b":"Identifying behavioural antecedents is done through functional or ABC analysis, not the meaning-probing downward arrow.","c":"Rating symptom intensity from 0 to 10 is a subjective-units or symptom-monitoring task, unrelated to uncovering beliefs.","d":"Constructing an exposure hierarchy belongs to systematic desensitisation and exposure therapy, a different behavioural procedure."}},{"q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: 'Had she taken the medication, she ___ recovered sooner.'","concept":"This sentence is a third conditional, which expresses a counterfactual situation in the past. Its structure is 'Had + subject + past participle ... subject + would have + past participle', so the correct completion is 'would have recovered'. The inverted 'Had she taken' signals the unreal past condition that requires the 'would have + participle' result clause.","traps":{"a":"Would recover is the result form for a second (present/future unreal) conditional, not the past counterfactual required here.","b":"Had recovered is a past perfect that duplicates the condition clause and cannot serve as the result clause.","c":"Will recover is a first-conditional or future form and clashes with the unreal past set up by 'Had she taken'."}}],"met-mock-3":[{"q":"During an action potential, the absolute refractory period exists primarily because:","concept":"The absolute refractory period is the window during which no stimulus, however strong, can trigger a second action potential. It exists because the voltage-gated Na+ channels that carried the rising phase enter an inactivated state via their ball-and-chain inactivation gate; these channels physically cannot reopen until the membrane repolarises and resets them. This mechanism also enforces the one-way, non-decremental propagation of the action potential down the axon.","traps":{"b":"The Na+/K+ ATPase restores ionic gradients slowly over time but is not gated by a single spike, so its activity does not define the brief absolute refractory window.","c":"K+ channels are not permanently inactivated; their delayed opening drives repolarisation and the following relative refractory period, which is the opposite of a permanent shutdown.","d":"Cl- influx is associated with inhibitory postsynaptic potentials, not with the intrinsic refractoriness of an axon that has just fired."}},{"q":"The release of neurotransmitter at the synaptic terminal is most directly triggered by:","concept":"Neurotransmitter release is triggered when the action potential reaching the axon terminal opens voltage-gated Ca2+ channels, letting Ca2+ flow in. The incoming Ca2+ binds synaptotagmin, the calcium sensor, which drives SNARE-mediated fusion of synaptic vesicles with the presynaptic membrane, releasing transmitter into the cleft. Ca2+ is the direct trigger, which is why blocking these channels abolishes release.","traps":{"a":"K+ efflux through delayed rectifier channels repolarises the terminal and actually helps terminate the signal, rather than triggering vesicle fusion.","b":"The Na+/K+ ATPase maintains resting gradients over the long term and does not gate the moment-to-moment release of transmitter.","c":"Cl- influx through GABA-A receptors is a postsynaptic inhibitory event on the receiving neuron, not the presynaptic trigger for release."}},{"q":"The mesocortical dopamine pathway, when underactive, is most associated with:","concept":"The mesocortical pathway runs from the ventral tegmental area to the prefrontal cortex. When it is underactive (hypodopaminergic), it is linked to the negative symptoms (avolition, blunted affect, anhedonia) and cognitive deficits of schizophrenia. This contrasts with the mesolimbic pathway, whose overactivity is tied to positive symptoms, and explains why simply blocking dopamine everywhere can worsen negative and cognitive features.","traps":{"a":"Positive symptoms (hallucinations, delusions) are attributed to mesolimbic dopamine hyperactivity, not to mesocortical underactivity.","b":"Parkinsonian rigidity reflects dopamine loss in the nigrostriatal pathway, a motor circuit distinct from the mesocortical projection.","c":"Hyperprolactinaemia results from blockade of the tuberoinfundibular pathway, where dopamine normally inhibits prolactin release."}},{"q":"Which structure is the principal source of acetylcholine projections to the cortex and is severely depleted in Alzheimer's disease?","concept":"The nucleus basalis of Meynert, located in the basal forebrain, is the principal source of cholinergic (acetylcholine) projections to the cerebral cortex. Its degeneration is a hallmark of Alzheimer's disease and provides the rationale for cholinesterase inhibitors such as donepezil and rivastigmine, which boost remaining acetylcholine. Each brainstem/forebrain nucleus supplies a signature transmitter, so identifying the transmitter identifies the source.","traps":{"a":"The locus coeruleus is the main source of noradrenaline (norepinephrine), not acetylcholine, to the forebrain.","b":"The raphe nuclei supply serotonin throughout the brain, a different neuromodulatory system from the cholinergic one lost in Alzheimer's.","d":"The ventral tegmental area is a dopaminergic origin (mesolimbic and mesocortical pathways), unrelated to cortical acetylcholine."}},{"q":"The blood–brain barrier is formed primarily by:","concept":"The blood-brain barrier is formed by tight junctions (zonulae occludentes) between the endothelial cells lining brain capillaries, which seal the paracellular route and prevent free diffusion of most solutes. Surrounding astrocyte end-feet induce and maintain these tight junctions and help regulate the barrier. A few circumventricular organs (area postrema, OVLT, subfornical organ) deliberately lack this barrier so they can sample blood chemistry.","traps":{"b":"The pia mater is a thin meningeal covering and does not form the selective capillary barrier; fenestrations would defeat rather than create a barrier.","c":"Microglia are the brain's phagocytic immune cells; their clean-up role is separate from the physical endothelial barrier.","d":"Oligodendrocytes myelinate axons for faster conduction; they do not wrap capillaries or form the barrier."}},{"q":"Cerebrospinal fluid is produced primarily by the:","concept":"Cerebrospinal fluid is produced mainly by the choroid plexus, specialised vascular tissue located chiefly in the lateral ventricles. CSF flows through the ventricular system and subarachnoid space (roughly 500 mL made per day against a standing volume of about 150 mL) and is reabsorbed into the dural venous sinuses by the arachnoid granulations. Distinguishing production (choroid plexus) from reabsorption (arachnoid granulations) is the key.","traps":{"a":"Arachnoid granulations reabsorb CSF into the venous sinuses rather than produce it.","b":"The subarachnoid space is a conduit where CSF circulates, not a secretory structure.","c":"The dura mater is the tough outer meningeal layer and has no CSF-secreting function."}},{"q":"Lesions of the suprachiasmatic nucleus most directly disrupt:","concept":"The suprachiasmatic nucleus of the anterior hypothalamus is the body's master circadian pacemaker, entrained to the light-dark cycle by direct retinal input via the retinohypothalamic tract. Lesioning it abolishes the roughly 24-hour organisation of sleep-wake and other rhythms without necessarily reducing the total amount of sleep. Its defining role is timing, not the quantity of any single behaviour.","traps":{"a":"Thermoregulation is governed by the preoptic and posterior hypothalamic areas, not the SCN's timekeeping function.","b":"Anterior pituitary hormone release is controlled by hypothalamic releasing hormones via the portal system, a separate neuroendocrine role.","c":"Hunger and satiety are regulated by the lateral and ventromedial hypothalamic areas, not the circadian pacemaker."}},{"q":"Which feature most reliably distinguishes the cerebellum's role in motor learning from that of the basal ganglia?","concept":"The cerebellum and basal ganglia both shape movement but do different jobs. The cerebellum, using mossy and climbing fibre input to Purkinje cells with an inferior-olive error signal, supports error correction, timing, and motor coordination. The basal ganglia, through cortico-striato-pallido-thalamic loops, handle action selection and the consolidation of habits and procedural learning. The cleanest distinction pairs cerebellum with error/coordination and basal ganglia with selection/habit.","traps":{"a":"Climbing fibres and fine motor error signals belong to the cerebellum, not the basal ganglia, so this reverses the anatomy.","c":"Habit learning and reward-based action selection are basal ganglia functions, wrongly attributed here to the cerebellum.","d":"Neither structure sends the primary descending motor command to muscles; that arises from the motor cortex via the corticospinal tract, with these structures modulating it."}},{"q":"The lateral geniculate nucleus is most directly involved in:","concept":"The lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus is the main relay for vision, receiving retinal signals via the optic tract and projecting to the primary visual cortex (V1) through the optic radiation. Each sensory modality has its own thalamic relay, so pairing the LGN with vision follows the general rule of thalamic sensory gating (olfaction being the exception that bypasses the thalamus).","traps":{"a":"Audition is relayed by the medial geniculate nucleus, not the lateral geniculate.","b":"Somatosensation is relayed through the ventral posterior nuclei (VPL/VPM) of the thalamus.","d":"Olfaction is the one sense that bypasses the thalamus, projecting directly to the olfactory cortex."}},{"q":"Damage to which structure produces a contralateral homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing?","concept":"A contralateral homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing points to an infarction of the primary visual (calcarine) cortex, typically from posterior cerebral artery occlusion, with the occipital pole spared. The macular (central-vision) representation at the occipital pole often survives because it receives collateral blood supply from the middle cerebral artery. The pattern of field loss localises the lesion along the visual pathway.","traps":{"a":"An optic chiasm lesion produces a bitemporal hemianopia because it hits the crossing nasal fibres, not a homonymous field cut.","c":"An optic tract lesion just behind the chiasm gives a contralateral homonymous hemianopia but without the characteristic macular sparing of a cortical infarct.","d":"An optic nerve lesion before the chiasm causes monocular blindness in that eye, not a homonymous hemifield defect."}},{"q":"The dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia was supported originally by which observation?","concept":"The dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia was originally supported by the finding, shown by Philip Seeman and colleagues, that the clinical potency of typical antipsychotics tracks their binding affinity for the D2 receptor across several orders of magnitude. Supporting evidence includes amphetamine, which releases dopamine and can induce a psychosis resembling schizophrenia. The pharmacological dose-affinity correlation is the classic pillar.","traps":{"a":"Typical antipsychotics act mainly by blocking D2 receptors, not by elevating serotonin, so this misstates their mechanism.","c":"Twin concordance for schizophrenia is well below 100% (around 50% in monozygotic twins), so this claim is factually false.","d":"Levodopa raises dopamine and can worsen or trigger psychotic symptoms rather than reliably improving negative symptoms."}},{"q":"The 'fight or flight' response is mediated primarily by:","concept":"The acute fight-or-flight response is driven by the sympathetic nervous system together with the adrenal medulla, which releases epinephrine (and norepinephrine) into the bloodstream, a mechanism described by Walter Cannon. This produces the rapid rise in heart rate, blood pressure, and glucose mobilisation. The slower, sustained stress response is handled separately by the HPA axis (CRH to ACTH to cortisol), studied by Hans Selye.","traps":{"a":"The parasympathetic vagal system promotes rest-and-digest and calming, the opposite of the fight-or-flight mobilisation.","b":"The enteric nervous system governs gut function locally and is not the driver of the whole-body stress reaction.","d":"The pituitary-thyroid axis regulates metabolic rate over the long term, not the seconds-scale emergency response."}},{"q":"Substance abuse disorders are most consistently associated with structural and functional changes in which neural circuit?","concept":"Substance abuse disorders converge on the mesolimbic dopamine reward circuit running from the ventral tegmental area to the nucleus accumbens (ventral striatum). Addictive drugs of all classes raise dopamine here, and with chronic use the dorsal striatum is recruited, shifting behaviour from goal-directed to compulsive habit (Everitt and Robbins). This shared reward pathway is the consistent site of structural and functional change.","traps":{"a":"The cerebellar vermis is involved in motor coordination and posture, not the reward-based core of addiction.","c":"The lateral geniculate nucleus is a visual relay and has no established role in drug reward.","d":"The vestibular nuclei process balance and head movement, unrelated to the addiction circuitry."}},{"q":"The Wisconsin Card Sorting Test is classically used to detect dysfunction of which region?","concept":"The Wisconsin Card Sorting Test measures cognitive set shifting, abstraction, and the inhibition of a prepotent response, all of which depend on the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex. Patients with DLPFC damage perseverate, continuing to sort by an old rule even after feedback signals that the rule has changed. Perseveration on the WCST is the classic sign of frontal executive dysfunction.","traps":{"b":"The hippocampus supports declarative and episodic memory formation, which the WCST does not primarily test.","c":"The primary motor cortex executes voluntary movement and is not what set-shifting probes.","d":"The cerebellum supports motor coordination and timing, not the executive set-shifting captured by the WCST."}},{"q":"Long-term depression (LTD) of synaptic strength typically depends on:","concept":"Long-term depression is a lasting weakening of synaptic strength produced by prolonged, low-frequency stimulation (around 1 Hz) that causes only a modest rise in postsynaptic Ca2+. This modest Ca2+ signal activates phosphatases such as PP1, which dephosphorylate and internalise AMPA receptors, reducing responsiveness. It is the mirror image of long-term potentiation, which needs high-frequency stimulation, a large Ca2+ influx, and CaMKII.","traps":{"a":"Brief high-frequency stimulation with a large Ca2+ rise induces long-term potentiation, not depression.","b":"Presynaptic GABA release produces fast inhibition and does not define the LTD mechanism at excitatory synapses.","c":"Mg2+ blocks the NMDA receptor at rest, not AMPA receptors, so this misidentifies both the ion target and the mechanism."}},{"q":"Which event-related potential component is most commonly elevated in oddball paradigms and reduced in schizophrenia?","concept":"The P300 is an event-related potential component elicited by rare target stimuli in the oddball paradigm; its amplitude indexes attentional resource allocation and the updating of working memory. In schizophrenia the P300 is reliably reduced in amplitude, a finding considered a candidate endophenotype. The pairing of oddball elicitation with reduced amplitude in schizophrenia identifies the P300.","traps":{"a":"The N100 is an early sensory/attentional response to stimulus onset and is not the hallmark oddball target component.","c":"P50 sensory gating reflects filtering of repeated stimuli and, although also reduced in schizophrenia, is not the oddball-elicited component asked for here.","d":"The Bereitschaftspotential (readiness potential) precedes voluntary movement and is unrelated to the oddball attention task."}},{"q":"Anterograde amnesia following bilateral mammillary body / dorsomedial thalamus damage with confabulation is most characteristic of:","concept":"Anterograde amnesia with confabulation following damage to the mammillary bodies and dorsomedial thalamus is the classic picture of Korsakoff's syndrome, caused by thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency, usually from chronic alcohol use. It often follows acute Wernicke's encephalopathy, whose triad is ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, and confusion. The diencephalic lesion location plus confabulation is the giveaway.","traps":{"a":"Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease is a prion disorder causing rapidly progressive dementia and myoclonus, not the focal diencephalic amnesia described.","b":"Pick's disease (a frontotemporal dementia) presents with personality and language change from frontal/temporal atrophy, not mammillary-body amnesia.","d":"Huntington's chorea is a basal-ganglia degeneration marked by chorea and subcortical dementia, not thiamine-related confabulation."}},{"q":"Which neurotransmitter binds the receptor that benzodiazepines positively modulate?","concept":"Benzodiazepines are positive allosteric modulators of the GABA-A receptor, the ionotropic receptor whose natural ligand is GABA, the brain's main inhibitory neurotransmitter. By binding an allosteric site, benzodiazepines increase the frequency of Cl- channel opening when GABA is present, enhancing inhibition (barbiturates instead prolong channel opening). The receptor is defined by its endogenous agonist, GABA.","traps":{"a":"Glutamate is the principal excitatory transmitter acting on NMDA and AMPA receptors, the opposite of the inhibitory GABA-A system.","b":"Acetylcholine binds nicotinic and muscarinic receptors, not the benzodiazepine-modulated chloride channel.","d":"Glycine is an inhibitory transmitter but acts mainly at spinal glycine receptors, which benzodiazepines do not modulate."}},{"q":"Erikson's stage of 'autonomy vs shame and doubt' is associated with which age range and developmental task?","concept":"In Erik Erikson's psychosocial theory, autonomy versus shame and doubt is the second stage, occurring in toddlerhood (about 1 to 3 years), when the child exercises will and independence through tasks like toilet training. Success yields the virtue of will; failure breeds shame and self-doubt. Placing it correctly requires knowing the ordered sequence of Erikson's stages and their ages.","traps":{"a":"Age 0 to 1 with trust through consistent caregiving describes stage 1, trust versus mistrust, not autonomy.","b":"Ages 3 to 6 with initiating play and tolerating guilt describes stage 3, initiative versus guilt.","d":"Ages 6 to 12 with peer comparison and competence describes stage 4, industry versus inferiority."}},{"q":"Schaffer and Emerson's longitudinal study identified four phases of attachment. The 'specific attachment' phase typically begins around:","concept":"In Schaffer and Emerson's 1964 longitudinal Glasgow study, the specific attachment phase begins around 7 months, when the infant forms a focused bond with one primary caregiver and shows separation anxiety and stranger anxiety. This follows the asocial and indiscriminate phases and precedes multiple attachments. The onset of separation/stranger anxiety marks this phase at roughly 7 months.","traps":{"b":"5 months falls within the indiscriminate attachment phase, before a single specific figure is preferred.","c":"3 months is still the indiscriminate phase, when infants accept comfort from almost anyone.","d":"12 months is closer to the multiple attachments phase, after specific attachment has already been established."}},{"q":"Vygotsky considered private (self-directed) speech in young children to be:","concept":"Lev Vygotsky regarded private (self-directed) speech as a functional self-regulatory tool: children talk aloud to guide and organise their own behaviour, and this speech gradually goes underground, internalising around age 7 as inner speech or verbal thought. This directly opposes Piaget, who dismissed such talk as egocentric speech that simply fades. Empirical evidence has largely supported Vygotsky's self-regulation account.","traps":{"a":"Viewing private speech as a regression to autistic thinking to be discouraged is neither Vygotsky's nor a supported position.","b":"Treating it as mere imitation with no function ignores its demonstrated role in self-regulation.","d":"Calling it egocentrism that fades with schooling is precisely Piaget's rejected interpretation, not Vygotsky's."}},{"q":"Margaret Mahler's separation-individuation theory places 'rapprochement' at approximately what age?","concept":"In Margaret Mahler's separation-individuation theory, rapprochement occurs at about 15 to 24 months, when the toddler, having explored during the practising subphase, returns ambivalently to the mother, seeking closeness while asserting independence. It follows differentiation and practising and precedes object constancy. Knowing the ordered subphases and their ages places rapprochement correctly.","traps":{"a":"0 to 2 months corresponds to Mahler's normal autism phase, long before rapprochement.","c":"5 to 10 months is the differentiation subphase, when the infant begins to distinguish self from mother.","d":"3 to 5 years is past the separation-individuation subphases, overlapping the consolidation of object constancy rather than rapprochement."}},{"q":"Konrad Lorenz's imprinting in geese is best characterised as:","concept":"Konrad Lorenz's classic 1935 work on geese described imprinting as a rapid, largely irreversible attachment that forms during a narrow critical period (roughly 13 to 16 hours after hatching), during which goslings bond to the first moving object they see. It is biologically prepared and hard to undo, and it influenced John Bowlby's ethological theory of human attachment. The defining features are speed, irreversibility, and the critical period.","traps":{"a":"Slow, reversible lifelong learning is the opposite of imprinting's fast, time-limited, largely fixed nature.","c":"Imprinting is not operant conditioning with food reward; it occurs without reinforcement during the sensitive window.","d":"Imprinting is fundamentally an attachment/following behaviour, not a purely visual recognition system divorced from attachment."}},{"q":"Baumrind's authoritative parenting style is characterised by:","concept":"Diana Baumrind's authoritative style combines high demandingness with high warmth (responsiveness): parents set clear expectations and enforce them through reasoned, explained discipline while supporting the child's autonomy. This style is consistently linked to the best child outcomes. It is distinguished by crossing the two dimensions of demandingness and responsiveness.","traps":{"a":"High demands with low warmth and strict obedience describes the authoritarian style, not authoritative.","b":"Low demands with low warmth and disengagement describes the uninvolved or neglectful style.","c":"Low demands with high warmth and few rules describes the permissive style."}},{"q":"The 'still face' paradigm developed by Edward Tronick demonstrates that infants:","concept":"Edward Tronick's still face paradigm (1978) shows that when a previously responsive caregiver suddenly becomes expressionless, infants as young as 2 to 9 months become distressed, avert their gaze, and try to self-soothe or re-engage the parent. This demonstrates that infants are sensitive to contingent, reciprocal social interaction and actively expect it. The distress at sudden unresponsiveness is the key finding.","traps":{"a":"Infants clearly detect maternal facial cues well before 12 months, which is exactly what the paradigm demonstrates.","b":"The paradigm does not show stronger attachment to fathers than mothers under unresponsive conditions.","c":"Infants do not habituate immediately with no behavioural change; they visibly protest the blank face."}},{"q":"Piaget's term 'centration' refers to:","concept":"For Jean Piaget, centration is the preoperational child's tendency to focus (centre) on one salient feature of a situation while ignoring other relevant dimensions. For example, judging quantity by the height of a liquid column while overlooking its width, which is why conservation fails. Overcoming this, called decentration, together with reversibility, allows conservation in the concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11 years).","traps":{"b":"Coordinating schemas across modalities is a sensorimotor achievement, not the definition of centration.","c":"Hypothetical reasoning is a formal operational ability, the opposite end of Piaget's sequence from centration.","d":"A reversible mental operation is exactly what the centring preoperational child lacks; reversibility is what enables conservation."}},{"q":"Selman's stages of perspective-taking place the transition from 'subjective / differentiated' (level 1) to 'self-reflective / reciprocal' (level 2) around:","concept":"In Robert Selman's stages of social perspective-taking, the shift from level 1 (subjective/differentiated) to level 2 (self-reflective/reciprocal) occurs around age 7, within the 5 to 9 year band. At level 2 the child can step outside the self and reflect on how another person views their own thoughts and feelings. Mapping the numbered levels to their age ranges places this transition in early middle childhood.","traps":{"a":"3 to 5 years corresponds to the egocentric level 0, before differentiated perspective-taking.","c":"10 to 12 years corresponds to level 3 (mutual, third-person perspective-taking), which comes after level 2.","d":"Late adolescence corresponds to level 4 (societal perspective-taking), well beyond the level 1 to 2 transition."}},{"q":"Kohlberg's stage 3 (conventional level) reasoning is best summarised as:","concept":"In Lawrence Kohlberg's theory, stage 3 sits at the conventional level and is the good boy / nice girl orientation: moral reasoning centres on gaining approval, maintaining relationships, and living up to others' expectations. It is paired with stage 4 (law and order). Recognising that stage 3 values interpersonal approval, rather than punishment avoidance or abstract principle, is the key.","traps":{"a":"Obedience to avoid punishment is stage 1 of the pre-conventional level, not stage 3.","b":"Instrumental self-interest (what's in it for me) is stage 2 of the pre-conventional level.","d":"Universal ethical principles and human rights describe stage 6 of the post-conventional level."}},{"q":"Bowlby's concept of an 'internal working model' refers to:","concept":"John Bowlby's internal working model is the set of mental representations a child builds from early attachment experiences: a model of the self (as worthy of love or not) and of attachment figures (as available and responsive or not). Formed in infancy, these models guide expectations and behaviour in later relationships, an idea Hazan and Shaver extended to adult romantic attachment. It is a cognitive-emotional representation, not a physical structure.","traps":{"a":"A neural circuit between hippocampus and amygdala is anatomy, not the representational model Bowlby described.","c":"Genetic templates for fear conditioning are innate biological predispositions, not the experience-built relational model.","d":"The infant's reflex repertoire at birth is the newborn's inborn behaviour, not the learned internal representation of relationships."}},{"q":"Adolescent egocentrism (Elkind) is most directly associated with which two constructs?","concept":"David Elkind (1967) described adolescent egocentrism as expressed through two constructs: the imaginary audience, the belief that others are as preoccupied with one's appearance and behaviour as oneself, and the personal fable, the sense of being uniquely special and invulnerable. Both emerge with the onset of formal operational thought and are linked to adolescent self-consciousness and risk-taking.","traps":{"a":"Conservation and reversibility are Piagetian concrete-operational concepts, not features of adolescent egocentrism.","b":"Inner speech and scaffolding are Vygotskian ideas about learning and self-regulation, unrelated to Elkind's constructs.","c":"Object permanence and stranger anxiety are infancy phenomena, far removed from adolescent egocentrism."}},{"q":"Habituation paradigms in infants are often used to assess:","concept":"Habituation paradigms exploit the fact that infants look less at a repeated (familiar) stimulus and look more again (dishabituate) when something novel appears. Because a recovery of looking proves the infant treats the new item as different, researchers like Robert Fantz and Elizabeth Spelke use the method to probe preverbal discrimination and categorisation (telling stimuli apart and grouping them) before language exists to report on. It also underlies infant studies of numerical sense and physical knowledge.","traps":{"b":"Long-term declarative memory is tested with explicit recall or deferred imitation tasks, not with the moment-to-moment looking-time changes that habituation measures.","c":"Object permanence is probed with specific violation-of-expectation or search tasks (Piaget's hidden-object procedures), whereas habituation is a general discrimination tool, not tied to permanence alone.","d":"Moral reasoning is a much later verbal-cognitive achievement assessed by dilemmas (Piaget, Kohlberg), impossible to index by infant looking times."}},{"q":"The 'A-not-B error' is a hallmark of which Piagetian sub-stage and reflects:","concept":"The A-not-B error occurs in Jean Piaget's sensorimotor stage, around sub-stage 4 (roughly 8 to 12 months): after an object is repeatedly hidden and found at location A, the infant keeps reaching to A even after watching it hidden at B. It reflects incomplete object permanence plus immature working memory and inhibitory control (a prepotent reach to the previously rewarded spot). This makes 'sensorimotor; incomplete object permanence' the correct pairing.","traps":{"a":"Formal operational is the adolescent stage of hypothetical-deductive reasoning; the A-not-B error appears in infancy, long before abstract hypotheticals.","b":"Preoperational centration on appearance describes conservation failures in 2 to 7 year olds, a different error from the infant reaching mistake.","c":"Concrete operational reversibility failures belong to the transition out of preoperations, not to the sensorimotor object-search behaviour seen here."}},{"q":"DSM-5 requires symptoms of major depressive disorder to be present for at least:","concept":"The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, fifth edition (DSM-5) requires at least 2 weeks of symptoms for major depressive disorder: 5 or more of 9 symptoms present most of the day, nearly every day, with at least one being depressed mood or anhedonia. The 2-week minimum distinguishes an episode from transient sadness. Persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia), by contrast, needs 2 years.","traps":{"a":"1 week is the minimum duration for a manic episode, not for major depressive disorder.","c":"1 month is the active-phase threshold used in psychotic disorders, not the depression duration criterion.","d":"6 months evokes durations like generalised anxiety disorder or total schizophrenia disturbance, not the 2-week MDD requirement."}},{"q":"Per DSM-5, schizophrenia requires continuous signs of disturbance for at least:","concept":"Under DSM-5, schizophrenia requires continuous signs of disturbance for at least 6 months, which must include at least 1 month of active-phase (Criterion A) symptoms such as delusions, hallucinations, or disorganised speech. The remaining time can be prodromal or residual. This 6-month rule separates schizophrenia from schizophreniform disorder (1 to 6 months) and brief psychotic disorder (under 1 month).","traps":{"a":"1 week is far too brief; even brief psychotic disorder allows up to a month, and schizophrenia needs six.","b":"1 month is only the active-phase symptom minimum, not the total disturbance duration required.","d":"12 months overstates the requirement; DSM-5 fixes the threshold at 6 months, not a full year."}},{"q":"First-rank symptoms of schizophrenia (Schneider) most prominently include:","concept":"Kurt Schneider's first-rank symptoms are specific psychotic experiences he considered highly suggestive of schizophrenia: passivity phenomena (thought insertion, withdrawal, and broadcasting; made feelings, impulses, and acts; somatic passivity) and particular auditory hallucinations (voices commenting or arguing in the third person). They had good inter-rater reliability but are no longer required for diagnosis in DSM-5 or ICD-11.","traps":{"a":"Avolition and flat affect are negative symptoms, important to schizophrenia but not part of Schneider's first-rank list.","b":"Memory deficits and disorientation to person point to organic or amnestic conditions, not Schneiderian first-rank symptoms.","d":"Catatonic excitement is a motor/behavioural feature, distinct from the passivity and auditory phenomena Schneider emphasised."}},{"q":"A patient with persistent intrusive thoughts of contamination, accompanied by ritualised hand-washing that consumes >1 hour/day, is best classified under DSM-5 as:","concept":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) in DSM-5 involves obsessions (intrusive, unwanted thoughts such as contamination fears) and/or compulsions (repetitive behaviours like hand-washing) that are time-consuming (over 1 hour per day) or cause marked distress or impairment. The classic contamination-obsession-plus-washing-ritual picture is prototypical OCD, which DSM-5 moved out of the anxiety disorders into its own obsessive-compulsive and related disorders chapter.","traps":{"a":"Generalised anxiety disorder is excessive worry across many life domains, without the specific intrusive obsessions and ritualised compulsions seen here.","b":"Illness anxiety disorder is preoccupation with having a serious illness, not contamination obsessions relieved by washing rituals.","c":"Specific phobia is marked fear cued by a particular object or situation with avoidance, not compulsive rituals consuming over an hour a day."}},{"q":"Borderline personality disorder, per DSM-5, requires ≥5 of 9 criteria. Which is NOT among them?","concept":"DSM-5 borderline personality disorder requires 5 or more of 9 criteria centred on pervasive instability: frantic efforts to avoid abandonment, unstable intense relationships, identity disturbance, impulsivity, recurrent suicidal or self-mutilating behaviour, affective instability, chronic emptiness, inappropriate anger, and transient stress-related paranoia or dissociation. Cruelty to animals and pre-age-15 rule-breaking are conduct disorder features (a precursor of antisocial PD), so they are NOT among the BPD criteria.","traps":{"a":"Frantic efforts to avoid real or imagined abandonment is criterion 1 of BPD, so it is included, not the exception.","c":"Recurrent suicidal behaviour, gestures, or self-mutilation is a core BPD criterion, so it belongs to the set.","d":"Identity disturbance with an unstable self-image is another genuine BPD criterion, not the odd one out."}},{"q":"Capgras syndrome is characterised by:","concept":"Capgras syndrome is the delusion of doubles: the patient believes a familiar person (often a spouse or parent) has been replaced by a physically identical impostor. Ellis and Young linked it to a disconnection between intact face recognition and the normal affective response to that face, so the person looks right but feels wrong. It contrasts with Cotard, Fregoli, and Othello delusions.","traps":{"a":"Belief that one is dead or non-existent is Cotard delusion (nihilistic delusion), not Capgras.","b":"Delusional jealousy about a partner's fidelity is Othello syndrome, a different misidentification-adjacent delusion.","c":"The belief that a stranger is a familiar person in disguise is Fregoli syndrome, the mirror image of Capgras."}},{"q":"ICD-11 retains a category of dissociative neurological symptom disorder (also termed 'conversion'). Its core feature is:","concept":"In ICD-11, dissociative neurological symptom disorder (the older term is conversion disorder, or functional neurological symptom disorder in DSM-5) is defined by motor or sensory symptoms, such as non-epileptic seizures, paralysis, or blindness, that are internally inconsistent and incompatible with recognised neurological disease, and are not intentionally produced. The unconscious, non-feigned nature separates it from malingering and factitious disorder.","traps":{"a":"Intentional production of symptoms for external gain (money, avoiding duty) is malingering, not conversion, which is unintentional.","b":"A persecutory delusion with intact reality testing describes a psychotic phenomenon, unrelated to functional neurological symptoms.","c":"A persistent memory deficit from organic brain injury is an organic amnestic/neurocognitive picture, the opposite of symptoms incompatible with known disease."}},{"q":"PTSD per DSM-5 requires the symptoms to persist for at least:","concept":"DSM-5 post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) requires symptoms to persist for more than 1 month following exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury, or sexual violence, with disturbance across four clusters: intrusion, avoidance, negative alterations in cognition and mood, and altered arousal and reactivity. Symptoms lasting 3 days to 1 month are instead classified as acute stress disorder.","traps":{"a":"2 days is below even the acute stress disorder floor (which starts at 3 days), so it cannot mark PTSD.","b":"1 week falls within the acute stress disorder window, not the PTSD threshold.","c":"6 months is when PTSD may be specified 'with delayed expression,' but the minimum duration to diagnose is 1 month."}},{"q":"A patient presents with episodes of binge eating ≥1/week for ≥3 months, without compensatory behaviours, with marked distress about the binges. The DSM-5 diagnosis is:","concept":"Binge-eating disorder (BED) in DSM-5 is defined by recurrent binge episodes (eating an objectively large amount with a sense of loss of control) occurring at least once a week for at least 3 months, with marked distress, and crucially NO recurrent inappropriate compensatory behaviours. The absence of purging, fasting, or excessive exercise is what separates BED from bulimia nervosa. BED became a full diagnosis in DSM-5.","traps":{"b":"Bulimia nervosa requires recurrent compensatory behaviours (purging, fasting, or exercise), which this patient lacks.","c":"Anorexia nervosa, binge-purge subtype, requires significantly low body weight and compensatory behaviour, neither described here.","d":"Avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder involves avoidance of food without binge episodes or body-image concern, unlike this presentation."}},{"q":"Charles Bonnet syndrome is characterised by:","concept":"Charles Bonnet syndrome is the occurrence of vivid, complex visual hallucinations in people with significant vision loss (for example macular degeneration or glaucoma) who are cognitively intact and typically retain insight that the images are not real. It is understood as a release phenomenon: deafferented visual cortex generates spontaneous imagery. Intact insight and ocular pathology are its hallmarks.","traps":{"a":"Auditory hallucinations in the deaf are the auditory analogue (sometimes called musical release hallucinations), not Charles Bonnet syndrome, which is visual.","b":"Multimodal hallucinations in delirium occur with clouded consciousness and lost insight, opposite to the intact, insightful patient here.","d":"Olfactory hallucinations preceding temporal lobe seizures are uncinate/epileptic auras, unrelated to vision loss."}},{"q":"Delirium is most reliably differentiated from dementia by:","concept":"Delirium is distinguished from dementia chiefly by its acute onset, fluctuating course, and disturbance of attention and consciousness (level of awareness), often with a reversed sleep-wake cycle and an identifiable medical or toxic cause. Dementia is insidious and progressive, dominated by cognitive decline (memory, executive function) with consciousness relatively preserved until late. Attention/consciousness change is the key discriminator.","traps":{"a":"Older age at onset does not separate the two, since both delirium and dementia are more common in the elderly.","b":"Confabulation is more typical of amnestic syndromes such as Korsakoff's, not a reliable delirium-versus-dementia discriminator.","d":"Aphasia and apraxia as early features point toward specific dementias, not the defining feature of delirium."}},{"q":"Persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia) requires depressed mood for most of the day, more days than not, for at least:","concept":"Persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia) in DSM-5 requires depressed mood most of the day, more days than not, for at least 2 years in adults (1 year in children and adolescents), plus at least 2 of 6 associated symptoms, with no symptom-free interval longer than 2 months. DSM-5 merged chronic major depression and the old dysthymic disorder into this single category.","traps":{"b":"6 months is far short of the chronic 2-year course that defines persistent depressive disorder.","c":"1 month is not a depression duration threshold at all; even acute MDD needs 2 weeks.","d":"5 years overstates the requirement; the minimum is 2 years in adults, not five."}},{"q":"Tourette's disorder per DSM-5 requires multiple motor and ≥1 vocal tic, with onset before age 18 and duration of at least:","concept":"Tourette's disorder in DSM-5 requires multiple motor tics and at least one vocal tic (not necessarily concurrent), present for more than 1 year since first tic onset, with onset before age 18. The 1-year duration distinguishes it from provisional tic disorder (under 1 year), while persistent (chronic) motor or vocal tic disorder involves only one type, motor or vocal but not both.","traps":{"a":"1 month is far too short; that duration would fall under provisional tic disorder, not Tourette's.","b":"3 months still falls within the provisional (under 1 year) window, not the Tourette's threshold.","d":"6 months likewise does not meet the required duration; Tourette's needs symptoms for more than a year."}},{"q":"Anorexia nervosa, restricting subtype, per DSM-5 is defined by:","concept":"Anorexia nervosa in DSM-5 is defined by restriction of energy intake leading to significantly low body weight, intense fear of gaining weight, and disturbance in the way body weight or shape is experienced. The restricting subtype means weight loss achieved mainly through dieting, fasting, or exercise, with no recurrent binge-eating or purging in the last 3 months. DSM-5 dropped the amenorrhoea criterion and the fixed BMI cutoff.","traps":{"a":"A BMI under 17.5 alone was an ICD-10 style marker; DSM-5 does not use a fixed BMI cutoff and requires fear and body-image disturbance too.","b":"Selective eating without weight loss suggests avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder, not anorexia nervosa.","c":"Recurrent binge eating with purging characterises the binge-purge subtype (or bulimia), not the restricting subtype."}},{"q":"Which symptom cluster is NOT among the DSM-5 PTSD criteria?","concept":"DSM-5 PTSD is organised into four symptom clusters: intrusion (flashbacks, intrusive memories, nightmares), avoidance of trauma reminders, negative alterations in cognitions and mood, and marked alterations in arousal and reactivity (hypervigilance, exaggerated startle, irritability, sleep disturbance). Magical thinking and ideas of reference are odd-belief features of schizotypal personality disorder, so that cluster is NOT part of PTSD.","traps":{"b":"Avoidance of trauma reminders is a genuine PTSD cluster, so it is included, not the exception.","c":"Negative alterations in cognitions and mood is one of the four PTSD clusters, part of the criteria set.","d":"Intrusion symptoms such as flashbacks and intrusive memories form the first PTSD cluster, clearly included."}},{"q":"Pseudobulbar affect (PBA) is best characterised by:","concept":"Pseudobulbar affect (PBA), or emotional lability/incontinence, is brief, involuntary, stereotyped outbursts of laughing or crying that are disproportionate to or decoupled from the person's actual mood, arising in neurological disease such as multiple sclerosis, stroke, amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, or brain injury. It reflects disrupted corticobulbar control over brainstem emotional-motor pathways and responds to dextromethorphan/quinidine or SSRIs.","traps":{"a":"Mood-congruent emotional expression in major depression matches the underlying mood, unlike PBA where affect is disconnected from mood.","b":"Restricted (flat) affect in schizophrenia is a reduction of emotional expression, the opposite of PBA's exaggerated outbursts.","d":"Histrionic emotional shifts are personality-driven and situationally reactive, not involuntary neurological outbursts."}},{"q":"DSM-5 distinguishes mania from hypomania primarily by:","concept":"DSM-5 separates mania from hypomania mainly by severity and duration. A manic episode lasts at least 1 week (or any duration if hospitalisation is needed), causes marked functional impairment, and may include psychotic features, defining Bipolar I. Hypomania lasts at least 4 days, produces an observable change in functioning but no marked impairment and no psychosis; paired with a major depressive episode it defines Bipolar II.","traps":{"b":"Hypomania by definition excludes psychotic features; their presence would make the episode manic, not hypomanic.","c":"Mania is not always preceded by depression; a manic episode alone suffices for Bipolar I.","d":"Hypomania does require an observable change from baseline; the point is that the change is not severe enough to cause marked impairment."}},{"q":"Folie à deux (shared psychotic disorder) most often involves:","concept":"Folie a deux, or shared psychotic disorder, classically involves a dominant person with a primary psychotic illness (the inducer) and a more passive, often related person who comes to share the delusional belief, usually in conditions of close contact and social isolation. Separating the pair often resolves the induced belief. DSM-5 removed it as a discrete diagnosis, folding it under 'other specified schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorder.'","traps":{"a":"Two patients independently developing identical delusions is not folie a deux, which requires transmission from a primary case to a passive recipient.","b":"A mother and infant sharing dissociative symptoms is not the phenomenon; folie a deux concerns shared delusions, not infant dissociation.","c":"Calling it purely cultural is wrong; folie a deux is a recognised psychiatric (induced delusional) phenomenon."}},{"q":"On the WAIS-IV (and WAIS-V), the mean and standard deviation of subtest scaled scores are:","concept":"On the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale (WAIS-IV and WAIS-V), individual subtest scaled scores are standardised to a mean of 10 and a standard deviation of 3. The composite/index scores (Full Scale IQ and the index scores such as Verbal Comprehension and Processing Speed) instead use a mean of 100 and SD of 15. Knowing which metric applies to subtests versus composites is the key distinction.","traps":{"a":"Mean 100, SD 15 describes the composite/index and Full Scale IQ scores, not the individual subtest scaled scores.","b":"Mean 50, SD 10 is the T-score metric (used on the MMPI), not Wechsler subtest scaling.","d":"Mean 0, SD 1 is the z-score (standard normal) metric, not the scaled-score system."}},{"q":"The MMPI-2 validity scale 'F' (Infrequency) elevated to a T-score >100 most plausibly indicates:","concept":"On the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory-2 (MMPI-2), the F (Infrequency) scale contains items endorsed by fewer than 10% of the normative sample. A very high F score (T over 100) most plausibly signals random or inconsistent responding, genuinely severe psychopathology, or symptom exaggeration (faking bad). VRIN and TRIN are then used to tell random responding apart from deliberate over-reporting.","traps":{"a":"Faking good or minimisation is indicated by elevated L and K scales, not by a high F score.","c":"A high F does not reflect excellent reliability; it flags an invalid or exaggerated profile.","d":"Above-average defensiveness is captured by the K scale; F elevation points the opposite way, toward over-reporting."}},{"q":"Cronbach's alpha is a measure of:","concept":"Cronbach's alpha estimates internal consistency reliability: it reflects the average intercorrelation among a test's items measuring the same construct, adjusted for the number of items. Values above 0.70 are generally acceptable and above 0.80 good. It answers whether the items hang together, not whether scores are stable over time or agree across raters.","traps":{"a":"Test-retest reliability is assessed by correlating scores from two separate administrations, not by Cronbach's alpha.","b":"Construct validity concerns whether the test measures the intended construct, a validity question distinct from internal consistency.","d":"Inter-rater reliability (agreement between raters) is indexed by Cohen's kappa or an intraclass correlation, not by alpha."}},{"q":"The Rorschach Inkblot Test uses 10 standardised cards; the Exner Comprehensive System scores responses on dimensions that include all of the following EXCEPT:","concept":"The Rorschach Inkblot Test uses 10 standardised cards, and John Exner's Comprehensive System codes each response on structured dimensions: location (Whole, common Detail, unusual Detail, Space), determinants (form, colour, shading, movement), form quality, content, popularity, special scores, and organisational activity. It yields structural summary indices but never an IQ score, so 'explicit IQ score' is the exception.","traps":{"a":"Location coding (W, D, Dd, S) is a genuine Exner dimension, so it is scored, not excluded.","b":"Determinants (form, colour, shading, movement) are core to Exner coding, part of what is scored.","c":"Form quality is another standard Exner scoring dimension, included in the Comprehensive System."}},{"q":"Sensitivity of a screening instrument is best defined as:","concept":"Sensitivity is the proportion of true cases that a test correctly identifies as positive: true positives divided by (true positives plus false negatives). A highly sensitive test rules conditions out when negative (few missed cases), so it is prized for screening. It is a fixed property of the test, unlike predictive values, which shift with the base rate of the condition.","traps":{"b":"The proportion of true negatives correctly identified is specificity, not sensitivity.","c":"The probability that a positive scorer truly has the condition is positive predictive value, which depends on prevalence.","d":"The probability that a negative scorer truly lacks the condition is negative predictive value, again base-rate dependent."}},{"q":"Stanine scores have a mean and SD of:","concept":"Stanine scores (from 'standard nine') form a single-digit scale from 1 to 9 with a mean of 5 and a standard deviation of 2, widely used in educational assessment to place a student's performance into one of nine bands. Recognising this metric prevents confusion with T-scores, scaled scores, and deviation IQs, which use different means and SDs.","traps":{"a":"Mean 50, SD 10 is the T-score metric, not the stanine scale.","c":"Mean 10, SD 3 is the Wechsler subtest scaled-score metric, not stanines.","d":"Mean 100, SD 15 is the deviation IQ / composite metric, not the 1-to-9 stanine scale."}},{"q":"The Beck Depression Inventory-II severity cutoff conventionally used to flag 'moderate' depression is approximately:","concept":"The Beck Depression Inventory-II (Beck et al., 1996) has 21 items scored 0 to 3, for a maximum of 63. Conventional severity bands in clinical samples are 0 to 13 minimal, 14 to 19 mild, 20 to 28 moderate, and 29 to 63 severe. So a score in the 20 to 28 range flags 'moderate' depression.","traps":{"b":"14 to 19 is the mild band, one step below moderate.","c":"0 to 13 is the minimal range, indicating little or no depression.","d":"29 to 63 is the severe band, above the moderate range."}},{"q":"Item Response Theory (IRT), unlike Classical Test Theory, models:","concept":"Item Response Theory (IRT) models the probability of a given response to each item as a function of an underlying latent trait (theta), using item-level parameters: a (discrimination), b (difficulty), and in the three-parameter model c (pseudo-guessing). Unlike Classical Test Theory, which works at the whole-test level, IRT places persons and items on the same scale, enabling computer adaptive testing and equating across forms (as in the GRE and USMLE).","traps":{"a":"Modelling only group-level means and variances describes a limited classical approach, not IRT's item-and-person latent-trait modelling.","b":"Test-retest stability is a reliability concept, not what IRT is designed to model.","d":"Inter-rater agreement on observed behaviour is an observational reliability issue, unrelated to IRT's latent-trait item modelling."}},{"q":"The Bender-Gestalt II is primarily used to assess:","concept":"The Bender-Gestalt II (Brannigan and Decker, 2003) presents 16 designs that the examinee copies and later recalls, primarily assessing visuomotor integration (the coordination of visual perception with motor output). It is used for developmental screening and as a brief neuropsychological screen for organic impairment. The original Bender Visual Motor Gestalt Test used 9 figures drawn from Max Wertheimer's Gestalt work.","traps":{"a":"Premorbid intelligence is estimated with reading-based tests (for example the National Adult Reading Test), not the Bender-Gestalt.","b":"Personality structure is assessed by projective or objective personality tests, not a visuomotor copying task.","c":"Verbal working memory is measured by tasks like digit span, not by copying geometric designs."}},{"q":"The Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) is classified as which type of test?","concept":"The Thematic Apperception Test (TAT), developed by Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan in 1935, is a projective personality test: the examinee is shown ambiguous picture cards and constructs stories, which are interpreted to reveal underlying needs, presses, and recurring themes. Its ambiguous stimuli and interpretive scoring make it projective, and Murray's needs-press theory later informed David McClelland's measurement of need for achievement.","traps":{"b":"An objective personality test uses standardised, fixed-response items (like the MMPI), unlike the open-ended storytelling of the TAT.","c":"An intelligence test measures cognitive ability with scored problems, not projective story construction.","d":"A neuropsychological battery assesses brain-behaviour functions, not the personality dynamics the TAT targets."}},{"q":"Concurrent validity is best demonstrated when:","concept":"Concurrent validity is a form of criterion validity established when a new test correlates highly with an already-established, validated measure of the SAME construct, both administered at the same point in time. The 'concurrent' part means the criterion is measured now, not in the future (that would be predictive validity). Option d captures both requirements: same construct and same time.","traps":{"a":"This describes correlating with a DIFFERENT construct at the same time, which is the opposite of what validity needs; high correlation with an unrelated construct signals poor discriminant validity, not good concurrent validity.","b":"Predicting future performance is predictive validity, the other flavour of criterion validity, distinguished by the criterion being measured later rather than concurrently.","c":"Stable internal consistency over time describes reliability (test-retest or internal consistency), not validity against a criterion at all."}},{"q":"The Halstead-Reitan Neuropsychological Battery includes all of the following EXCEPT:","concept":"The Halstead-Reitan Neuropsychological Battery is a fixed battery of standardized tests (Category Test, Tactual Performance Test, Trail Making A and B, Finger Tapping, Speech-Sounds Perception, Rhythm/Seashore Test, Sensory-Perceptual Examination) used with the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale and the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory as adjuncts. It measures brain-behaviour relationships across cognitive and sensorimotor domains. The Rorschach is a projective personality test and is not part of the HRNB.","traps":{"a":"The Category Test is a core Halstead-Reitan measure of abstraction and concept formation, so it is genuinely included.","b":"The Tactual Performance Test (form board done blindfolded) is a central Halstead-Reitan subtest of tactile and spatial learning.","c":"Trail Making A and B are part of the Halstead-Reitan battery, assessing visual scanning and set-shifting."}},{"q":"The Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales primarily measures:","concept":"The Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales (Sparrow and colleagues) is a semi-structured caregiver interview or rating that measures adaptive functioning, meaning how well a person copes with everyday demands. Its core domains are Communication, Daily Living Skills, and Socialisation, plus a Motor Skills domain for young children. It is required alongside an intelligence test to diagnose intellectual disability, because that diagnosis needs both low IQ and deficits in adaptive behaviour.","traps":{"b":"Intellectual quotient is measured by IQ tests such as the Wechsler or Stanford-Binet, not by the Vineland, which specifically assesses adaptive behaviour that IQ tests do not capture.","c":"Psychopathology in adolescents is assessed by instruments like the Youth Self-Report or MMPI-A, not by an adaptive behaviour scale.","d":"Premorbid personality is estimated through clinical history or personality inventories, and has nothing to do with the Vineland's adaptive-functioning focus."}},{"q":"If a test has reliability of 0.80 and a standard deviation of 15, the standard error of measurement (SEM) is approximately:","concept":"The standard error of measurement quantifies how much an observed score is expected to fluctuate around the true score due to measurement error. It is computed as SEM = SD times the square root of (1 minus reliability). Here that is 15 times the square root of (1 minus 0.80) = 15 times the square root of 0.20 = 15 times 0.447, which is approximately 6.7. Higher reliability produces a smaller SEM.","traps":{"b":"1.5 would only arise from a calculation error such as forgetting to take the square root or misplacing a decimal; it does not follow from the SEM formula with these values.","c":"12.0 comes from mistakenly using square root of reliability (0.80) rather than square root of (1 minus reliability), inflating the estimate.","d":"15.0 is simply the standard deviation itself, which would only equal the SEM if reliability were zero (a completely unreliable test)."}},{"q":"The Wechsler Memory Scale (WMS-IV) yields five Index scores. Which is NOT among them?","concept":"The Wechsler Memory Scale, Fourth Edition, yields five index scores: Auditory Memory, Visual Memory, Visual Working Memory, Immediate Memory, and Delayed Memory. These capture memory across modality (auditory versus visual), working memory, and time (immediate versus delayed recall). Mechanical Reasoning is not among them.","traps":{"a":"Auditory Memory is a genuine WMS-IV index reflecting recall of verbally presented material.","c":"Visual Working Memory is a real WMS-IV index measuring temporary manipulation of visual-spatial information.","d":"Mechanical Reasoning is a subtest of the Differential Aptitude Test, an occupational aptitude measure, not a memory index."}},{"q":"In CBT, the term 'Socratic questioning' refers to:","concept":"Socratic questioning, also called guided discovery in cognitive behaviour therapy (associated with Aaron Beck and Christine Padesky), uses a series of open-ended questions that lead clients to examine the evidence for their own automatic thoughts and reach new conclusions themselves. The therapist does not lecture or tell the client they are wrong; the client reformulates their own thinking. It is often paired with the downward-arrow technique and behavioural experiments.","traps":{"b":"Direct confrontation of beliefs is the antithesis of Socratic questioning, which deliberately avoids telling clients their thoughts are wrong and instead guides self-examination.","c":"Free association of childhood memories is a psychoanalytic technique, not a CBT method of thought examination.","d":"Hypnotic suggestion of new beliefs bypasses the client's own reasoning, which is exactly what guided discovery is designed to avoid."}},{"q":"Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP) is the first-line behavioural intervention for:","concept":"Exposure and Response Prevention (Edna Foa and Michael Kozak) is the first-line behavioural treatment for obsessive-compulsive disorder. The client is exposed in a graded way to feared or triggering stimuli while refraining from performing the compulsive ritual, which breaks the cycle in which the compulsion is negatively reinforced by anxiety relief. Over repeated trials, anxiety habituates and the compulsion loses its grip.","traps":{"b":"Schizophrenia is treated primarily with antipsychotics and psychosocial rehabilitation, not ERP; exposure targets anxiety-driven avoidance, not psychosis.","c":"Bipolar I disorder requires mood stabilisers; ERP has no role in managing manic or depressive episodes.","d":"Antisocial personality disorder is not an anxiety disorder and does not respond to exposure; there is no compulsive fear cycle to break."}},{"q":"Dialectical Behaviour Therapy (DBT), developed by Marsha Linehan, blends CBT with:","concept":"Dialectical Behaviour Therapy, developed by Marsha Linehan, blends standard cognitive behavioural change strategies with acceptance-based mindfulness practices drawn from Zen Buddhism and a dialectical philosophy that holds opposing truths together (accepting the client as they are while working toward change). Its four skills modules are mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation, and interpersonal effectiveness. It is a first-line treatment for borderline personality disorder.","traps":{"b":"Psychoanalytic free association is not part of DBT; DBT is a structured, skills-based therapy, not an insight-oriented one.","c":"Pharmacological augmentation is a medication strategy, not the therapeutic tradition DBT integrates with CBT.","d":"Bowen family systems theory concerns multigenerational family dynamics and is unrelated to DBT's acceptance-and-change dialectic."}},{"q":"Rogers' three core conditions for client-centred therapy are:","concept":"Carl Rogers (1957) proposed that three therapist-provided conditions are necessary and sufficient for therapeutic change in client-centred therapy: unconditional positive regard (nonjudgmental acceptance of the client), accurate empathic understanding, and congruence (the therapist being genuine and integrated in the relationship). These humanistic conditions later shaped motivational interviewing.","traps":{"a":"Confrontation, interpretation, and transference are psychoanalytic and confrontational techniques, opposite in spirit to Rogers' nondirective acceptance.","c":"Rational disputation, behavioural homework, and cognitive restructuring are cognitive-behavioural methods, not Rogerian core conditions.","d":"Free association, dream analysis, and transference are the tools of psychoanalysis, not client-centred therapy."}},{"q":"In psychoanalysis, the term 'working through' refers to:","concept":"In psychoanalysis, 'working through' (Sigmund Freud) is the extended process by which a patient repeatedly recognises, tolerates, and applies an insight or interpretation across many different situations and transferences until it produces lasting characterological change. A single correct interpretation, no matter how accurate, rarely alters personality; the repeated application is what does the work.","traps":{"b":"Telling the client the correct interpretation once is exactly what working through is NOT; insight must be applied repeatedly, not delivered in a single statement.","c":"A brief intake assessment is an initial evaluation, unrelated to the mid-to-late-phase process of consolidating insight.","d":"Termination of therapy is the ending phase; working through is an ongoing process that precedes and enables lasting change."}},{"q":"Aaron Beck's cognitive triad in depression consists of negative views of:","concept":"Aaron Beck's cognitive triad describes the three domains of negative thinking that characterise depression: a negative view of the self (worthless, inadequate), of the world or one's ongoing experience (defeating, demanding), and of the future (hopeless). Underlying maladaptive schemas activated by stress generate these biased cognitions, which are targeted through cognitive restructuring and behavioural activation.","traps":{"a":"Past, present, and future is a plausible-sounding time triad but misstates Beck; his triad is about self, world, and future, not the past.","c":"Mother, father, and self reflects a psychodynamic/object-relations framing, not Beck's cognitive model.","d":"Body, mind, and spirit is a holistic wellness phrase with no place in Beck's cognitive theory of depression."}},{"q":"Motivational Interviewing (Miller & Rollnick) is grounded in which spirit-of-MI principles?","concept":"Motivational Interviewing (William Miller and Stephen Rollnick) is grounded in an underlying 'spirit' summarised by the acronym PACE: Partnership (collaboration rather than expert-driven), Acceptance, Compassion, and Evocation (drawing out the client's own motivation rather than installing it). Its core techniques are OARS (open questions, affirmations, reflections, summaries) and eliciting change talk. It is a first-line approach in substance use.","traps":{"a":"Confrontation, persuasion, expert authority, and compliance are precisely the directive, authoritarian stance MI was designed to replace.","b":"Free association, transference, and abstinence belong to psychoanalytic tradition, not the collaborative spirit of MI.","d":"Behavioural shaping and contingency management are operant-conditioning techniques, distinct from MI's evocative, client-centred spirit."}},{"q":"Family therapy's 'enmeshment' (Minuchin) refers to:","concept":"In Salvador Minuchin's structural family therapy, 'enmeshment' describes a family with diffuse, overly permeable boundaries in which members are excessively involved in each other's lives at the cost of individual autonomy and differentiation. Its opposite is 'disengagement', marked by rigid boundaries and little emotional contact. Healthy families maintain clear but permeable boundaries with an appropriate hierarchy.","traps":{"b":"Rigid boundaries with little emotional contact describes disengagement, which is the opposite pole from enmeshment.","c":"Healthy reciprocal closeness is normal, well-bounded intimacy, whereas enmeshment specifically denotes pathological over-involvement that erodes autonomy.","d":"Pathological scapegoating of one member is a different family dynamic (identified-patient phenomenon), not the boundary concept of enmeshment."}},{"q":"Yalom's 'here-and-now' focus in group therapy refers to:","concept":"Irvin Yalom's 'here-and-now' focus treats the therapy group as a social microcosm in which members reproduce their characteristic interpersonal patterns in the room. The therapist works with those patterns as they emerge and then, through process illumination, helps members reflect on what just happened. This experiencing-then-reflecting loop is the core therapeutic engine of interpersonal group therapy.","traps":{"a":"Discussing only events outside the group ('there-and-then') is exactly what the here-and-now focus moves away from.","b":"Strictly limiting the group to didactic content makes it a class, not the experiential interpersonal work Yalom describes.","d":"Cognitive disputation of irrational beliefs is a CBT/REBT technique, not Yalom's interpersonal process-oriented approach."}},{"q":"Systematic desensitisation (Wolpe) is based on the principle of:","concept":"Systematic desensitisation (Joseph Wolpe, 1958) rests on the principle of reciprocal inhibition: because a relaxation response is physiologically incompatible with an anxiety response, one cannot be fully anxious and fully relaxed at the same time. The client is guided up a graded fear hierarchy while maintaining relaxation, so the conditioned anxiety is progressively counter-conditioned. It has largely been superseded by in-vivo exposure.","traps":{"a":"Operant extinction involves withdrawing reinforcement of a behaviour, whereas systematic desensitisation works by counter-conditioning via incompatible relaxation, a classical-conditioning mechanism.","c":"Cognitive disputation is a cognitive-therapy technique, not the behavioural counter-conditioning that underlies Wolpe's method.","d":"The two-factor theory (Mowrer) explains how avoidance is acquired and maintained, but it is not the principle on which systematic desensitisation is built."}},{"q":"Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT) targets which six core processes (the 'hexaflex')?","concept":"Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (Steven Hayes, Kelly Wilson, Kirk Strosahl) targets six interrelated core processes, arranged as the 'hexaflex': cognitive defusion, acceptance, contact with the present moment, self-as-context, values, and committed action. Together these promote psychological flexibility, the capacity to stay present and act on values even in the presence of difficult thoughts and feelings.","traps":{"a":"Cognitive restructuring, exposure, relaxation, homework, role-play, and contingency management are traditional CBT/behavioural techniques; ACT deliberately does NOT try to restructure thoughts.","c":"Free association, dream analysis, and transference are psychoanalytic methods, entirely outside the ACT model.","d":"Mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation, and interpersonal effectiveness are the four DBT skills modules, not the ACT hexaflex."}},{"q":"Interpersonal Therapy (IPT) for depression typically organises treatment around which problem areas?","concept":"Interpersonal Therapy (Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman) is a time-limited treatment (usually 12 to 16 sessions) that links depressive symptoms to the client's current interpersonal context. It organises treatment around one of four problem areas: grief (complicated bereavement), interpersonal role disputes, role transitions, and interpersonal deficits. It does not target unconscious conflict or, primarily, cognitive distortions.","traps":{"a":"Cognitive distortions and dysfunctional schemas are the targets of cognitive therapy (Beck), not the interpersonal problem areas of IPT.","b":"Trauma reprocessing through bilateral stimulation describes EMDR, an unrelated trauma therapy.","c":"Free association and transference interpretation are psychoanalytic techniques; IPT focuses on present-day relationships, not unconscious conflict."}},{"q":"The 'empty chair' technique is most closely associated with which therapy?","concept":"The 'empty chair' technique belongs to Gestalt therapy, developed by Fritz Perls. The client speaks to an imagined person or a disowned part of the self placed in an empty chair, often switching seats to voice both sides, in order to integrate polarities and complete 'unfinished business'. It is an experiential, present-focused method.","traps":{"b":"Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy (Albert Ellis) uses the ABC model and disputation of irrational beliefs, not chair work.","c":"Person-centred therapy (Carl Rogers) relies on empathic reflection and the core conditions, not enactment techniques.","d":"Family systems therapy (Murray Bowen) uses genograms and differentiation of self, not the empty chair."}},{"q":"Eye Movement Desensitisation and Reprocessing (EMDR) was developed by:","concept":"Eye Movement Desensitisation and Reprocessing was developed by Francine Shapiro in the late 1980s. It uses an eight-phase protocol in which the client recalls distressing memories while engaging in bilateral stimulation (side-to-side eye movements, tapping, or tones). It is endorsed by the World Health Organization and the American Psychological Association for post-traumatic stress disorder, though its mechanism remains debated.","traps":{"a":"Edna Foa developed Prolonged Exposure for PTSD and co-developed ERP for OCD, not EMDR.","b":"Bessel van der Kolk is a prominent trauma researcher and author, but he did not develop EMDR.","c":"Pat Ogden founded Sensorimotor Psychotherapy, a body-oriented trauma approach, not EMDR."}},{"q":"A Type II error occurs when:","concept":"In null-hypothesis significance testing, a Type II error (beta) occurs when the null hypothesis is not rejected even though it is actually false, that is, a real effect is missed (a false negative). This contrasts with a Type I error (alpha), rejecting a true null (false positive). Statistical power equals 1 minus beta, the probability of correctly detecting a true effect, conventionally targeted at 0.80.","traps":{"a":"Rejecting a true null hypothesis is a Type I error (false positive), the opposite of a Type II error.","b":"A confidence interval being too wide relates to precision and sample size, not to the definition of a Type II error.","d":"A large sample size actually reduces the chance of a Type II error by increasing power, so it cannot define one."}},{"q":"Which test is appropriate for comparing the means of three or more independent groups on a continuous outcome with normal distribution?","concept":"One-way analysis of variance (ANOVA) is the appropriate parametric test for comparing the means of three or more independent groups on a continuous, normally distributed outcome. It partitions total variance into between-group and within-group components and tests whether group means differ more than expected by chance. If normality is violated, the Kruskal-Wallis test is the non-parametric alternative.","traps":{"a":"A paired t-test compares two related (matched or repeated) measurements, not three or more independent groups.","b":"The chi-square test analyses associations between categorical variables, not means of a continuous outcome.","d":"The Mann-Whitney U test is a non-parametric test for two independent groups, not three or more, and is not the normal-distribution parametric choice."}},{"q":"An odds ratio of 1.0 means:","concept":"The odds ratio compares the odds of an outcome in an exposed group to the odds in an unexposed group. An odds ratio of exactly 1.0 means the odds are equal in the two groups, indicating no association between exposure and outcome. Values above 1.0 suggest a positive (harmful) association and values below 1.0 suggest a protective effect; significance is judged by whether the 95% confidence interval excludes 1.","traps":{"a":"A strong positive association corresponds to an odds ratio well above 1.0, not equal to 1.0.","b":"A strong protective effect corresponds to an odds ratio well below 1.0 (approaching 0), not 1.0.","d":"Perfect correlation is a property of correlation coefficients (r = 1), a different statistic; an odds ratio of 1 actually means no association at all."}},{"q":"Cohen's d of approximately 0.5 indicates:","concept":"Cohen's d expresses the standardised difference between two means, calculated as the mean difference divided by the pooled standard deviation. By Jacob Cohen's conventional benchmarks, d around 0.2 is a small effect, around 0.5 is a medium effect, and around 0.8 is a large effect. A d of approximately 0.5 therefore indicates a medium effect.","traps":{"b":"A small effect corresponds to d around 0.2, not 0.5.","c":"A large effect corresponds to d around 0.8 or higher, well above 0.5.","d":"No effect would be d near 0; a d of 0.5 reflects a moderate, non-trivial difference between means."}},{"q":"Stratified random sampling is preferred over simple random sampling when:","concept":"Stratified random sampling divides the population into homogeneous subgroups (strata) defined by an important characteristic, then draws a random sample from within each stratum. It is preferred over simple random sampling when the researcher wants to guarantee adequate representation of key subgroups and to reduce sampling variance when those subgroups differ on the outcome of interest.","traps":{"a":"If the population is homogeneous there is little to gain from stratifying; simple random sampling suffices, so homogeneity is not the reason to stratify.","b":"Stratification is about representativeness and precision, not about minimising sample size; it does not inherently reduce the number needed.","d":"When no sampling frame exists, methods like cluster or snowball sampling are used; stratified sampling actually requires knowing subgroup membership."}},{"q":"A scale with high internal consistency but low criterion validity:","concept":"Reliability and validity are distinct. A scale with high internal consistency is reliable, meaning it measures something consistently, but if it has low criterion validity it is not measuring the intended construct. Thus a test can be reliable but not valid (consistently measuring the wrong thing). The reverse is not possible: validity presupposes reliability, since you cannot validly measure what you cannot measure consistently.","traps":{"b":"Being both reliable and valid would require high criterion validity, which the stem explicitly denies.","c":"Valid but not reliable is logically impossible, because validity requires some level of reliability as a precondition.","d":"The scale is described as internally consistent, so it is reliable; 'neither reliable nor valid' contradicts the given high internal consistency."}},{"q":"Intent-to-treat analysis means that:","concept":"Intent-to-treat analysis analyses every randomised participant in the group to which they were originally assigned, regardless of whether they completed the intervention, adhered to it, or crossed over. This preserves the balance created by randomisation and yields a conservative, real-world estimate of effectiveness. Per-protocol analysis, by contrast, includes only completers and risks selection bias.","traps":{"a":"Analysing only those who completed as planned is per-protocol analysis, which breaks randomisation, the opposite of ITT.","b":"Excluding non-completers to reduce noise reintroduces selection bias and is precisely what ITT avoids.","d":"Limiting analysis to per-protocol completers is a distinct, non-ITT approach that estimates efficacy under ideal adherence, not real-world effectiveness."}},{"q":"A 95% confidence interval for a mean difference that includes zero indicates that:","concept":"A 95% confidence interval for a mean difference that includes zero means the data are compatible with no true difference, so the null hypothesis cannot be rejected at the two-sided 0.05 significance level; the result is not statistically significant. The confidence interval and the p-value give equivalent decisions here.","traps":{"a":"Including zero does not mean the difference is exactly zero; the point estimate may be nonzero, but the range of plausible values simply cannot exclude zero.","c":"Low power can produce a wide interval that includes zero, but including zero by itself indicates non-significance, not specifically insufficient power.","d":"A large sample tends to narrow the interval, making it less likely to include zero, so it does not explain an interval that spans zero."}},{"q":"A non-parametric alternative to the independent-samples t-test is:","concept":"The Mann-Whitney U test (also called the Wilcoxon rank-sum test) is the non-parametric alternative to the independent-samples t-test, used to compare two independent groups when the assumptions of the parametric test are not met. It ranks all observations and compares rank sums between groups rather than comparing means directly.","traps":{"a":"The Wilcoxon signed-rank test is for two RELATED (paired) samples, the non-parametric analogue of the paired t-test, not the independent-samples t-test.","c":"The Friedman test is for three or more related samples, not two independent groups.","d":"The McNemar test is for paired dichotomous (categorical) data, not for comparing two independent continuous distributions."}},{"q":"Number Needed to Treat (NNT) is calculated as:","concept":"Number Needed to Treat is the number of patients who must receive the treatment for one additional beneficial outcome to occur. It is calculated as 1 divided by the Absolute Risk Reduction, where ARR is the difference in event rates between control and treatment groups. A smaller NNT indicates a more efficient treatment. The parallel harm measure is Number Needed to Harm, equal to 1 divided by the Absolute Risk Increase.","traps":{"a":"Relative risk reduction times 100 gives a percentage, not the count of patients needed to treat; NNT uses the ABSOLUTE risk reduction.","c":"1 divided by Relative Risk is not a defined outcome measure; NNT uses absolute, not relative, risk.","d":"Odds Ratio times 100 has no meaningful interpretation as NNT; the odds ratio is a measure of association, not a treatment-efficiency count."}},{"q":"The 'Bonferroni correction' is most appropriate when:","concept":"The Bonferroni correction controls the family-wise Type I error rate when many comparisons are made simultaneously, by dividing the significance threshold alpha by the number of comparisons (for example 0.05 divided by 10 equals 0.005). It is most appropriate when conducting many independent pairwise comparisons and wanting to keep the overall chance of any false positive at the nominal level. It is conservative, especially when comparisons are correlated.","traps":{"a":"A single planned comparison involves no multiplicity, so no correction is needed; Bonferroni addresses multiple tests.","b":"Reporting effect sizes concerns magnitude of effect, not control of Type I error across multiple tests.","c":"Whether a test is non-parametric is irrelevant to the need for a multiple-comparison correction; Bonferroni applies to the number of tests, not their parametric form."}},{"q":"Choose the option that best replaces the underlined portion: 'Neither the manager nor the staff <u>were</u> aware of the new policy.'","concept":"With correlative conjunctions 'neither ... nor' (and 'either ... or'), the verb follows the rule of proximity: it agrees with the subject nearer to it. Here the nearer subject is 'staff', treated as a singular collective noun taking a singular verb, so the correct form is 'was'. This is a subject-verb agreement point tested constantly in language sections.","traps":{"a":"'Were' is the seed error itself: it wrongly treats the whole 'neither the manager nor the staff' construction as plural instead of agreeing with the nearer singular subject.","b":"'Is' is singular but present tense, which clashes with the past-tense narrative frame of the sentence about awareness of a policy.","c":"'Are' is both plural and present tense, doubly wrong: it neither agrees with the singular nearer subject nor matches the past-tense context."}},{"q":"Identify the sentence with correct punctuation:","concept":"'However' used as a conjunctive adverb at the start of a clause takes a comma after it, and two independent clauses joined without a coordinating conjunction (and, but, or) must be separated by a semicolon, not a comma. Option (a) applies both rules: 'However,' then a semicolon between the two complete clauses. A comma alone between two independent clauses is the classic comma-splice error.","traps":{"b":"This omits the comma after 'However' and joins two independent clauses with only a comma, committing a comma splice.","c":"A semicolon after 'However' is wrong because a semicolon must separate two independent clauses, not a single introductory adverb from its clause.","d":"The stray comma after 'results' wrongly splits the subject from its verb 'were', and the clauses are still comma-spliced."}},{"q":"Choose the most appropriate word: 'The findings of the study are not <u>____</u> to the broader population because the sample was small and self-selected.'","concept":"After a linking verb like 'are not', a predicate adjective is needed to describe the subject 'findings'. The adjective 'generalisable' (capable of being generalised) fits: the findings are not generalisable. Recognising the part of speech required by the sentence frame is the key skill here.","traps":{"a":"'Generalisation' is a noun naming the act or product of generalising, so it cannot serve as the adjective describing 'findings' after 'are not'.","c":"'Generality' is an abstract noun (the quality of being general), again a noun where an adjective is required.","d":"'Generally' is an adverb, which would modify a verb or adjective, not function as the predicate describing the findings."}},{"q":"Which sentence uses the conditional correctly?","concept":"The third conditional expresses an unreal or hypothetical situation in the past and follows the pattern: If + past perfect (had + past participle), main clause with would have + past participle. Thus 'If I had known, I would have called you.' A very common error is putting 'would have' in the if-clause, which is nonstandard in formal English.","traps":{"a":"This inserts 'would have' into the if-clause ('If I would have known'), the classic conditional error; the if-clause should take the past perfect 'had known'.","b":"'If I knew earlier' mixes tenses and 'I would called' drops the required 'have', so neither clause matches any correct conditional pattern.","c":"'If I would knew' is ungrammatical (would cannot precede a past-tense verb) and 'I called you' lacks the 'would have' result form."}},{"q":"Choose the word closest in meaning to 'ubiquitous':","concept":"'Ubiquitous' means present or found everywhere at the same time. Its closest synonym is 'omnipresent', which literally means present in all places. Vocabulary questions reward matching the core sense (everywhere-ness), not a loosely associated feature.","traps":{"b":"'Rare' means seldom found or occurring, the near-opposite of being everywhere.","c":"'Obscure' means unclear or little-known, which concerns visibility or fame, not spatial pervasiveness.","d":"'Uniform' means consistent or unvarying in form; it shares a sense of sameness but not the 'everywhere' meaning of ubiquitous."}},{"q":"Identify the error: 'The patient, along with her two children, <u>were</u> waiting in the consulting room.'","concept":"Parenthetical phrases introduced by 'along with', 'as well as', 'in addition to', or 'together with' do not change the grammatical number of the subject. The true subject is 'the patient' (singular), so the verb should be 'was waiting'. Only a compound subject joined by 'and' would make the verb plural.","traps":{"a":"'No error' misses the agreement mistake: the singular subject 'the patient' cannot take the plural verb 'were'.","c":"Replacing 'with' with 'and' would create a genuine compound plural subject, but that rewrites the sentence rather than correcting the actual verb-agreement error.","d":"Replacing 'her' with 'their' introduces a pronoun disagreement (the children belong to the singular patient) and does not fix the verb."}},{"q":"Choose the option closest in meaning to 'salient':","concept":"'Salient' means standing out, most noticeable, or conspicuous, so its closest synonym is 'prominent'. The word derives from Latin 'salire', to leap, capturing the idea of a feature that leaps out at the observer.","traps":{"a":"'Trivial' means of little importance, the opposite of a feature that stands out.","c":"'Concealed' means hidden from view, directly contrary to being conspicuous.","d":"'Uncertain' means doubtful or not clearly established, which describes confidence, not conspicuousness."}},{"q":"Select the correctly punctuated sentence:","concept":"When quoting a direct question, the quoted sentence begins with a capital letter and the question mark goes inside the closing quotation mark because the question belongs to the quoted material. A comma sets off the reporting tag ('asked,') before the opening quote. Option (b) follows all three conventions.","traps":{"a":"The quoted question is not capitalised and the question mark is placed outside the closing quote, misattributing the question to the whole sentence rather than the quoted words.","c":"Ending the quoted direct question with a full stop instead of a question mark misrepresents the sentence as a statement.","d":"This omits the comma after 'asked' and fails to capitalise the start of the quoted question."}},{"q":"Reading comprehension: 'Although early studies suggested that lithium was uniformly effective, more recent meta-analyses have qualified this view: lithium reduces suicide and prevents relapse but is less reliable for acute mania than second-generation antipsychotics.' The passage primarily implies that:","concept":"The passage tempers early enthusiasm about lithium without dismissing it: it credits lithium with reducing suicide and preventing relapse while noting it is less reliable than second-generation antipsychotics for acute mania. The primary implication is therefore that lithium remains valuable but is not the optimal agent for every phase of bipolar disorder. Reading comprehension rewards the balanced inference the text actually supports, not an extreme restatement.","traps":{"a":"The passage explicitly names ongoing benefits (suicide reduction, relapse prevention), so claiming lithium has no role overstates the qualification.","b":"The text qualifies the early studies' conclusion, not their methodology; it never says the early studies were flawed.","c":"It says SGAs are more reliable only for acute mania, which is far from replacing lithium completely across all phases."}},{"q":"Choose the option that best replaces the underlined portion to be grammatically correct: 'Each of the participants <u>have</u> been informed about the risks.'","concept":"'Each' is a singular indefinite pronoun and always takes a singular verb, regardless of an intervening prepositional phrase like 'of the participants'. So the correct form is 'Each of the participants has been informed'. The same singular rule governs 'every', 'either', and 'neither'.","traps":{"a":"'Have' is plural and is drawn incorrectly to the nearer plural noun 'participants' rather than to the singular subject 'Each'.","c":"'Had been' shifts to the past perfect tense, changing the meaning and time frame instead of simply correcting the number agreement.","d":"'Is' is singular but does not fit the passive-perfect construction '... informed about the risks', which requires the auxiliary 'has been'."}}],"met-mock-4":[{"q":"GABA-A receptors differ from GABA-B receptors primarily in that GABA-A:","concept":"GABA is the brain's main inhibitory neurotransmitter and acts through two receptor families. GABA-A receptors are ionotropic, ligand-gated chloride channels: when GABA binds, Cl- flows in, hyperpolarizing the cell and producing a fast inhibitory postsynaptic potential (IPSP). This is why option d is correct, and it explains why benzodiazepines, barbiturates, and alcohol all act here as positive modulators. GABA-B, by contrast, is metabotropic and G-protein coupled, producing slow inhibition via K+ efflux and Ca2+ channel closure.","traps":{"a":"This describes an excitatory ionotropic receptor like the nicotinic or AMPA receptor; GABA-A is inhibitory and passes Cl-, not Na+.","b":"This actually describes the GABA-B receptor (metabotropic, G-protein coupled, acting via K+ and Ca2+ channels), not GABA-A.","c":"GABA-A is ligand-gated, opened by GABA binding, not voltage-gated and never independent of its neurotransmitter."}},{"q":"The NMDA glutamate receptor is unique among ionotropic receptors because:","concept":"The NMDA glutamate receptor is a 'coincidence detector,' which makes it unique among ionotropic receptors. At resting potential its pore is plugged by an Mg2+ ion, so glutamate binding alone cannot open it; the cell must also be depolarized (usually by AMPA receptors) to expel the Mg2+ block. Only when both conditions coincide does the channel open and admit Ca2+, the trigger for long-term potentiation and synaptic plasticity. This dual requirement is why option c is correct.","traps":{"a":"The opposite is true: NMDA opens only when the membrane is depolarized, which relieves the Mg2+ block, not at hyperpolarized potentials.","b":"Cl- permeability characterizes GABA-A and glycine receptors; NMDA is a cation channel notable for its high Ca2+ permeability.","d":"NMDA is an ionotropic ligand-gated channel; the metabotropic glutamate receptors (mGluRs) are the G-protein coupled family."}},{"q":"Bilateral medial temporal lobectomy (as in patient H.M.) produces:","concept":"Patient H.M. (Henry Molaison), described by William Scoville and Brenda Milner in 1957, had both medial temporal lobes including the hippocampi removed to treat epilepsy. The result was a dense anterograde amnesia for declarative memory (both episodic and semantic), meaning he could not form new conscious memories, while his procedural learning stayed intact (he improved on the mirror-tracing task without recalling having practiced). This dissociation established the hippocampus as critical for consolidating declarative memory, making option d correct.","traps":{"a":"H.M.'s motor coordination was intact; medial temporal structures serve memory, not motor control, which depends on the cerebellum and basal ganglia.","b":"This reverses the deficit: H.M. had profound trouble with new learning (anterograde), not primarily loss of old memories, and retrograde loss was limited.","c":"The amnesia spanned all declarative memory, both episodic and semantic; it was not selective for semantics alone."}},{"q":"The amygdala is most directly implicated in:","concept":"The amygdala, especially its central and basolateral nuclei, is the brain's hub for emotional salience, fear conditioning, and threat detection, making option a correct. Joseph LeDoux described a fast 'low road' running from the thalamus directly to the amygdala, bypassing the cortex to trigger rapid fear responses. Bilateral amygdala damage produces Kluver-Bucy syndrome (placidity, hyperorality, hypersexuality, visual agnosia).","traps":{"b":"Motor sequence learning depends on the basal ganglia and cerebellum, not the amygdala.","c":"Conscious recall of facts is a declarative memory function tied to the hippocampus and neocortex, not emotional processing.","d":"Circadian rhythm is generated by the suprachiasmatic nucleus of the hypothalamus, unrelated to amygdala function."}},{"q":"The Papez circuit includes which sequence?","concept":"James Papez (1937) proposed a closed loop as the anatomical substrate of emotion, now called the Papez circuit. The correct sequence runs hippocampus to fornix to mammillary bodies to anterior thalamic nuclei (via the mammillothalamic tract) to cingulate gyrus and back to the hippocampus, making option a correct. Damage to the mammillary bodies in Korsakoff's syndrome disrupts this circuit and impairs memory.","traps":{"b":"This is the basal ganglia (cortico-striato-pallido-thalamo-cortical) motor loop, not the limbic Papez emotion circuit.","c":"This is the visual pathway (retina to lateral geniculate nucleus to primary and secondary visual cortex), unrelated to emotion.","d":"This is a fabricated sequence; the cerebellum is not part of the Papez circuit, and the true loop passes through the mammillary bodies and anterior thalamus."}},{"q":"Wernicke's area is classically located in:","concept":"Wernicke's area sits in the posterior superior temporal gyrus (Brodmann area 22) of the dominant hemisphere, usually the left, making option c correct. It underlies language comprehension, so damage produces a fluent aphasia with poor understanding and meaningless speech. Broca's area, by contrast, lies in the inferior frontal gyrus and governs speech production.","traps":{"a":"The occipital pole houses primary visual cortex, not a language region.","b":"The inferior frontal gyrus is Broca's area (motor speech), not Wernicke's area.","d":"The postcentral gyrus is the primary somatosensory cortex, unrelated to language comprehension."}},{"q":"The key feature distinguishing Broca's aphasia from Wernicke's aphasia is that Broca's aphasia involves:","concept":"Broca's aphasia results from damage to the inferior frontal gyrus and is defined by effortful, non-fluent, telegraphic speech with relatively preserved comprehension and impaired repetition; patients are often aware and frustrated. This makes option d correct. It contrasts sharply with Wernicke's aphasia, where speech is fluent but empty and comprehension is lost.","traps":{"a":"Fluent speech with intact comprehension describes normal or anomic speech, not Broca's aphasia, which is non-fluent.","b":"Loss of comprehension with fluent, meaningless speech describes Wernicke's aphasia, the opposite condition.","c":"Selective alexia (impaired reading) with no other deficit is a specific disconnection syndrome, not the picture of Broca's aphasia."}},{"q":"Which function is most reliably lateralised to the right (non-dominant) hemisphere in right-handers?","concept":"In right-handers the left hemisphere is dominant for language (in roughly 95%), so the right hemisphere specializes in visuospatial processing and prosody, making option b correct. The right hemisphere also handles face recognition (fusiform face area) and holistic, gestalt perception, while the left handles analytic and sequential processing.","traps":{"a":"Phonological processing is a left-hemisphere language function.","c":"Grammatical syntax is lateralized to the left hemisphere, alongside other core language operations.","d":"Verbal memory is predominantly a left-hemisphere (and left medial temporal) function; the right specializes in visuospatial memory."}},{"q":"Damage to the orbitofrontal cortex is most likely to produce:","concept":"The orbitofrontal cortex regulates emotion, reward valuation, and social judgment, so its damage produces disinhibition, impulsivity, and socially inappropriate behavior, making option d correct. The classic illustration is Phineas Gage, whose personality changed dramatically after a tamping iron destroyed his orbitofrontal region. Such patients also fail the Iowa Gambling Task, reflecting impaired decision-making.","traps":{"a":"Pure aphasia follows damage to perisylvian language areas (Broca's or Wernicke's), not the orbitofrontal cortex.","b":"Cortical blindness results from bilateral occipital (visual cortex) damage.","c":"Hemiplegia follows damage to the primary motor cortex or its descending corticospinal tracts, not the orbitofrontal region."}},{"q":"Dorsolateral prefrontal cortex (DLPFC) damage most specifically impairs:","concept":"The dorsolateral prefrontal cortex (Brodmann areas 9 and 46) is the seat of working memory and executive function, so its damage specifically impairs planning, set-shifting, and response inhibition, making option b correct. Such patients perform poorly on the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test, the Stroop task, and n-back tasks. This contrasts with the orbitofrontal cortex (reward and impulse) and the anterior cingulate (error and conflict monitoring).","traps":{"a":"Color perception depends on the ventral occipitotemporal visual cortex (area V4), not the prefrontal cortex.","c":"Basic motor reflexes are mediated by the spinal cord and brainstem, not the DLPFC.","d":"Long-term declarative memory depends on the hippocampus and medial temporal lobe, not on dorsolateral prefrontal working memory circuits."}},{"q":"Slow-wave (deep) sleep is characterised on EEG by:","concept":"Slow-wave sleep (stage N3, the deepest non-REM stage) is defined on EEG by high-amplitude, low-frequency delta waves (0.5 to 4 Hz), making option b correct. This is the stage of greatest restorative and consolidation activity. Lighter stage N2 shows sleep spindles and K-complexes, while REM shows low-voltage mixed-frequency activity.","traps":{"a":"Sawtooth waves accompanied by rapid eye movements characterize REM sleep, not slow-wave sleep.","c":"Beta waves signal alert wakefulness and active concentration, the opposite of deep sleep.","d":"Sleep spindles and K-complexes are the hallmark of stage N2, not the delta-dominated N3 slow-wave sleep."}},{"q":"REM sleep behavior disorder (RBD) is characterised by:","concept":"REM sleep behavior disorder (RBD) is a failure of the normal muscle atonia (paralysis) of REM sleep, so the person physically enacts dream content by kicking, punching, or vocalizing, making option b correct. It is strongly linked to the alpha-synucleinopathies (Parkinson's disease, dementia with Lewy bodies, multiple system atrophy) and often predates motor symptoms by years. It is typically treated with melatonin or clonazepam.","traps":{"a":"RBD patients do enter REM normally; the problem is loss of atonia during REM, not absence of REM.","c":"Bedwetting during N3 is sleep enuresis, a different non-REM parasomnia.","d":"Excessive daytime sleepiness without nocturnal enactment points to narcolepsy or hypersomnia, not RBD."}},{"q":"SSRIs typically take 2-4 weeks for clinical antidepressant effect despite rapid synaptic serotonin elevation. The most-supported explanation is:","concept":"Although SSRIs raise synaptic serotonin within hours, the clinical antidepressant effect takes 2 to 4 weeks because of downstream adaptive changes, making option b correct. Initially, presynaptic 5-HT1A autoreceptors sense the extra serotonin and suppress firing; over weeks these autoreceptors desensitize, firing normalizes, and slower processes (BDNF upregulation, hippocampal neurogenesis, dendritic remodeling) build up in parallel with symptom improvement.","traps":{"a":"Liver enzyme induction alters drug metabolism and clearance but does not explain the therapeutic lag.","c":"SSRIs cross the blood-brain barrier rapidly, which is why synaptic serotonin rises almost immediately.","d":"Steady-state plasma levels are reached within days, far faster than the several-week clinical delay."}},{"q":"Lithium is classically associated with which narrow therapeutic concern?","concept":"Lithium's defining clinical concern is its narrow therapeutic index: the effective range (about 0.6 to 1.2 mEq/L) sits dangerously close to toxic levels, making option a correct. Toxicity brings tremor, ataxia, and nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, and levels climb with dehydration, NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors, and thiazide diuretics. Routine monitoring of serum lithium, renal function, and thyroid function is therefore standard.","traps":{"b":"Constipation is not lithium's dose-limiting toxicity; the concern is a narrow window where neurologic and renal toxicity emerge.","c":"Lithium is renally cleared and unsafe in renal impairment without monitoring, since impaired excretion raises levels toward toxicity.","d":"Lithium is renally cleared and does not cause acute fulminant liver failure; hepatotoxicity is not its characteristic hazard."}},{"q":"Benzodiazepines and barbiturates both potentiate GABA-A receptor activity but differ in that:","concept":"Benzodiazepines and barbiturates are both positive modulators of the GABA-A receptor but act by different mechanisms, making option a correct. Benzodiazepines increase the frequency of chloride channel opening and require GABA to be present, giving a ceiling effect that makes them relatively safe in overdose alone. Barbiturates increase the duration of channel opening and at high doses directly open the channel without GABA, so they have no ceiling and can cause fatal respiratory depression.","traps":{"b":"Barbiturates act on GABA-A, not GABA-B; baclofen is the GABA-B agonist.","c":"Benzodiazepines work at the GABA-A receptor, not at NMDA glutamate receptors.","d":"This reverses the two mechanisms: benzodiazepines increase frequency of opening while barbiturates increase duration."}},{"q":"Long-term potentiation (LTP) in the hippocampus most directly involves:","concept":"Long-term potentiation (LTP), first described by Timothy Bliss and Terje Lomo in 1973, is the leading cellular model of memory. High-frequency stimulation depolarizes the postsynaptic neuron, relieving the NMDA receptor's Mg2+ block so Ca2+ flows in; this Ca2+ activates CaMKII, which drives insertion of more AMPA receptors into the membrane, strengthening the synapse. This calcium-triggered AMPA insertion is why option b is correct.","traps":{"a":"LTP is associated with increased dendritic spine density and growth, not a decrease.","c":"LTP strengthens transmission and does not deplete glutamate stores.","d":"LTP builds synaptic connections; apoptosis (programmed cell death) is a destructive process unrelated to potentiation."}},{"q":"Which hypothalamic nucleus is most directly involved in oxytocin and ADH synthesis?","concept":"Oxytocin and antidiuretic hormone (ADH, vasopressin) are synthesized by magnocellular neurons of the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus, making option c correct. Their axons travel down to the posterior pituitary, which stores and releases these hormones into the bloodstream. Parvocellular neurons of the paraventricular nucleus additionally make CRH to drive the anterior pituitary.","traps":{"a":"The suprachiasmatic nucleus is the master circadian clock, not a site of oxytocin or ADH synthesis.","b":"The mammillary bodies function in memory (Papez circuit), not hormone synthesis.","d":"The arcuate nucleus regulates appetite and releases hormones like GnRH and dopamine, not oxytocin or ADH."}},{"q":"Which neurotransmitter is synthesised from the amino acid tryptophan?","concept":"Serotonin (5-HT) is synthesized from the amino acid tryptophan, making option d correct. The pathway runs tryptophan to 5-HTP by tryptophan hydroxylase (the rate-limiting step) and then to serotonin by aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase. Knowing precursor pathways is a common exam point, since each transmitter has a distinct starting amino acid.","traps":{"a":"Acetylcholine is made from choline and acetyl-CoA by choline acetyltransferase, not from an amino acid precursor like tryptophan.","b":"Dopamine is synthesized from tyrosine (via L-DOPA), not tryptophan.","c":"GABA is synthesized from glutamate by glutamic acid decarboxylase, not from tryptophan."}},{"q":"Erikson's first psychosocial stage (0-18 months) involves the conflict of:","concept":"Erik Erikson's first psychosocial stage, spanning roughly birth to 18 months, is Trust versus Mistrust, making option a correct. Consistent, responsive caregiving lets the infant develop basic trust in the world; neglect breeds mistrust. The remaining seven stages unfold across the lifespan, each defined by its own central conflict.","traps":{"b":"Initiative versus Guilt is Erikson's third stage (roughly ages 3 to 6), not the first.","c":"Industry versus Inferiority is the fourth stage (roughly ages 6 to 12).","d":"Autonomy versus Shame and Doubt is the second stage (roughly 18 months to 3 years), the one that follows Trust versus Mistrust."}},{"q":"In Piaget's theory, object permanence is acquired during the:","concept":"In Jean Piaget's theory, object permanence (understanding that an object still exists when it cannot be seen) is acquired during the sensorimotor stage, around 8 to 12 months, making option c correct. Its emergence is imperfect at first, shown by the A-not-B error, and is fully mastered by about 18 to 24 months. It is a signature achievement of infancy.","traps":{"a":"The concrete operational stage (roughly ages 7 to 11) is when conservation and logical operations appear, long after object permanence.","b":"The formal operational stage (from about age 11) brings abstract and hypothetical reasoning, not object permanence.","d":"Object permanence develops in infancy, not at age 4; the preoperational stage is marked by egocentrism and symbolic play."}},{"q":"Vygotsky's concept of the Zone of Proximal Development (ZPD) refers to:","concept":"Lev Vygotsky's Zone of Proximal Development (ZPD) is the gap between what a child can do independently and what the child can achieve with guidance from a more knowledgeable other, making option d correct. It implies that teaching should target this zone, and it underlies the practice of scaffolding (a term later developed by Wood, Bruner, and Ross in 1976). It highlights the social nature of learning.","traps":{"a":"The influential peer group relates to socialization theories, not Vygotsky's ZPD.","b":"The window for language acquisition is the critical period concept (Lenneberg), not the ZPD.","c":"The ZPD is a psychological and educational construct about potential learning, not a neural region."}},{"q":"Kohlberg's pre-conventional level of moral reasoning is characterised by:","concept":"Lawrence Kohlberg's pre-conventional level of moral reasoning bases judgments on external consequences, specifically avoiding punishment (Stage 1) and gaining rewards or instrumental exchange (Stage 2), making option c correct. It is the dominant mode in young children. The later conventional and post-conventional levels move toward social approval, law, and abstract ethical principles.","traps":{"a":"Conformity to peer and social norms is Stage 3 (good boy/good girl) of the conventional level, not pre-conventional.","b":"Maintaining social order and law is Stage 4 (law and order) of the conventional level.","d":"Universal ethical principles define Stage 6 of the post-conventional level, the highest and most abstract stage."}},{"q":"In Ainsworth's Strange Situation, an infant who shows little distress on separation, ignores the caregiver on reunion, and treats stranger and caregiver similarly is classified as:","concept":"In Mary Ainsworth's Strange Situation, an infant who shows little distress at separation, ignores or avoids the caregiver at reunion, and treats stranger and caregiver alike is classified as insecure-avoidant (Type A), making option b correct. This pattern is thought to reflect a caregiver who is consistently unresponsive or rejecting. It contrasts with the secure Type B infant, who protests but is readily comforted.","traps":{"a":"Disorganized (Type D), described by Mary Main, shows contradictory, freezing, or fearful behavior, often linked to maltreatment, not calm avoidance.","c":"Insecure-resistant/ambivalent (Type C) infants show intense distress and cannot be soothed, seeking then resisting contact, the opposite of the avoidant pattern.","d":"Secure (Type B) infants protest separation but actively seek and accept comfort on reunion, unlike the indifferent avoidant infant."}},{"q":"Bowlby's concept of the internal working model refers to:","concept":"John Bowlby's internal working model is the mental representation a child builds of the self, attachment figures, and relationships, formed in infancy from repeated interactions, making option c correct. It encodes whether the self is lovable, whether caregivers are available, and whether relationships are predictable, and it serves as the template guiding future attachment behavior. This is Bowlby's proposed mechanism for the continuity of attachment across the lifespan.","traps":{"a":"A cognitive map of physical space is a spatial-memory concept (Tolman), not Bowlby's relational model.","b":"The neural substrate of language acquisition is unrelated to attachment representations.","d":"Object permanence is Piaget's cognitive concept, distinct from Bowlby's emotional working model of relationships."}},{"q":"Baumrind's authoritative parenting style is characterised by:","concept":"Diana Baumrind's authoritative parenting style combines high demandingness with high responsiveness (warmth), making option b correct. These parents set clear standards and enforce them while remaining warm and communicative, and this style is associated with the best child outcomes. Maccoby and Martin later completed the 2x2 model by adding the neglectful (uninvolved) cell.","traps":{"a":"Low demandingness with high responsiveness is the permissive (indulgent) style, associated with impulsive children.","c":"Low demandingness with low responsiveness is the neglectful (uninvolved) style, linked to the poorest outcomes.","d":"High demandingness with low responsiveness is the authoritarian style, associated with withdrawn or hostile children."}},{"q":"The concept of 'goodness of fit' in temperament research (Thomas & Chess) refers to:","concept":"In the temperament research of Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess (the New York Longitudinal Study), 'goodness of fit' refers to the match between a child's temperament and the demands and expectations of the environment or caregiver, making option a correct. Their key insight was that outcomes depend not on temperament alone (easy, difficult, slow-to-warm-up) but on how well it fits the surrounding environment. A difficult child in a patient, structured home may thrive.","traps":{"b":"The IQ difference between identical twins concerns heritability research, not the temperament-environment fit.","c":"The number of siblings is a family structure variable, unrelated to goodness of fit.","d":"Genetic concordance between parent and child is a behavior-genetics measure, not the temperament-environment match Thomas and Chess described."}},{"q":"Harry Harlow's rhesus monkey experiments demonstrated that:","concept":"Harry Harlow's rhesus monkey experiments (1958) showed that contact comfort, not feeding, is the primary basis of attachment, making option b correct. Infant monkeys preferred a soft cloth surrogate 'mother' over a wire one that dispensed milk, clinging to the cloth figure especially when frightened. This directly challenged the behaviorist, feeding-based (cupboard love) account of attachment.","traps":{"a":"Rapid, time-limited imprinting in the first hours characterizes Lorenz's precocial birds, not Harlow's primate work.","c":"Harlow's work showed the opposite: maternal deprivation produced severe, lasting behavioral disturbance in the monkeys.","d":"The whole point of Harlow's findings was that operant conditioning and feeding do not fully explain attachment."}},{"q":"Lorenz's concept of imprinting is best demonstrated by:","concept":"Konrad Lorenz's concept of imprinting is best shown by greylag goslings forming a rapid, durable following response to the first moving object they encounter shortly after hatching, making option b correct. This bonding occurs only within a narrow sensitive (critical) period, roughly 13 to 16 hours in geese, and established the idea of critical periods in development. The response is fast, lasting, and relatively general to the stimulus.","traps":{"a":"Habituation is a decline in response to repeated stimuli, a form of non-associative learning, not imprinting.","c":"A 5-year-old's playmate preference reflects social development, not the hatchling imprinting Lorenz studied.","d":"Reflexive neonatal smiling is an innate reflex, not the learned attachment-during-a-critical-period seen in imprinting."}},{"q":"Theory of Mind, classically tested with the false-belief task (Sally-Anne), typically emerges around:","concept":"Theory of Mind, the ability to attribute mental states (including false beliefs) to others, is classically tested with the Sally-Anne false-belief task and typically emerges around age 4, making option a correct. In the task of Wimmer and Perner (1983), later adapted by Simon Baron-Cohen (1985), children under about 4 answer based on reality rather than Sally's mistaken belief. Children with autism spectrum disorder often show delayed or impaired performance, the basis of the Theory of Mind deficit hypothesis.","traps":{"b":"At 12 months infants lack the representational capacity to reason about others' false beliefs; ToM emerges years later.","c":"By age 8 (concrete operational) most children have long passed false-belief tasks; the milestone is around age 4.","d":"Theory of Mind appears in early childhood, not adolescence; adolescence brings more advanced perspective-taking, not first-order false belief."}},{"q":"Marcia's identity statuses are defined by two dimensions:","concept":"James Marcia (1966), building on Erikson's fifth stage, defined four identity statuses using two dimensions: crisis (active exploration of alternatives) and commitment (a firm choice), making option b correct. Crossing these yields Identity Diffusion (neither), Foreclosure (commitment without crisis), Moratorium (crisis without commitment), and Identity Achievement (crisis resolved into commitment). The typical adolescent path runs from diffusion through moratorium to achievement.","traps":{"a":"IQ and emotional intelligence are ability constructs, not the dimensions Marcia used to define identity statuses.","c":"Internal and external locus of control is Rotter's construct, unrelated to Marcia's exploration-and-commitment model.","d":"Approach and avoidance motivation is a motivational dimension, not the crisis-and-commitment framework of identity statuses."}},{"q":"Selman's stages of perspective-taking describe progressive shifts from:","concept":"Robert Selman's stages of social perspective-taking chart how a child moves from being unable to separate self from other to coordinating multiple viewpoints. It runs from Stage 0 (egocentric, roughly 3-6 years, where the child cannot distinguish their own view from another's) up through Stage 3 (mutual, third-person perspective, 10-12 years) and Stage 4 (societal perspective-taking in adolescence). The progression is egocentric to multiple, mutual perspective-taking, and it parallels Jean Piaget's account of declining egocentrism and the growth of theory of mind.","traps":{"a":"Concrete to abstract numerical reasoning describes Piaget's shift from concrete-operational to formal-operational thought, a cognitive-logic sequence, not Selman's social perspective-taking model.","c":"Inductive to deductive logic is a reasoning distinction (specific-to-general versus general-to-specific), unrelated to how a child comes to understand other people's viewpoints.","d":"Procedural to declarative memory is a memory-systems contrast (skills versus facts), which has nothing to do with Selman's stages of social cognition."}},{"q":"Erikson's psychosocial conflict of late adulthood is:","concept":"Erik Erikson's eighth and final psychosocial stage, occurring in late adulthood (roughly 65 and older), is Ego Integrity versus Despair. The person reviews their life and either accepts it as meaningful and well-lived (integrity) or is overwhelmed by regret and bitterness about missed chances (despair). Successful resolution yields the virtue of wisdom.","traps":{"a":"Identity versus Role Confusion is Erikson's fifth stage, occurring in adolescence, centred on forming a coherent sense of self.","c":"Generativity versus Stagnation is the seventh stage, middle adulthood, about contributing to and guiding the next generation.","d":"Intimacy versus Isolation is the sixth stage, young adulthood, about forming committed close relationships."}},{"q":"The DSM-5 minimum duration for a Major Depressive Episode is:","concept":"In the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition (DSM-5), a Major Depressive Episode requires at least five of nine symptoms (including depressed mood or anhedonia) present most of the day, nearly every day, for a minimum of two weeks, causing impairment and not attributable to a substance or medical condition. The two-week threshold is the defining minimum duration. Longer chronic low mood points instead to Persistent Depressive Disorder.","traps":{"a":"Six months does not match any depressive episode criterion; it resembles durations used in disorders like generalized anxiety, not an MDE.","b":"One month is too long for the MDE minimum and does not correspond to any DSM-5 depression threshold.","c":"One week is too short; a single week of low mood does not meet the two-week MDE requirement."}},{"q":"DSM-5 requires symptoms of schizophrenia to be present for at least:","concept":"In the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition (DSM-5), schizophrenia requires at least two characteristic symptoms (delusions, hallucinations, disorganised speech, grossly disorganised or catatonic behaviour, or negative symptoms) present for at least one month of active-phase symptoms, with at least one being from the first three. Additionally, continuous signs of disturbance must persist for at least six months. Durations under one month indicate brief psychotic disorder, and one to six months indicates schizophreniform disorder.","traps":{"a":"Three months is not a schizophrenia threshold; it falls within the schizophreniform range (one to six months), not the full diagnosis.","c":"Two weeks of active phase only understates the required one-month active phase and omits the six-month continuous-disturbance criterion.","d":"One year of any psychotic symptom is neither the required duration nor specific enough; a single symptom for a year does not satisfy the two-symptom, six-month structure."}},{"q":"Schneider's first-rank symptoms classically include:","concept":"Kurt Schneider (1959) identified first-rank symptoms once believed to be pathognomonic for schizophrenia. These include thought insertion, withdrawal, and broadcasting; audible thoughts (Gedankenlautwerden); voices arguing or commenting; delusional perception; somatic passivity; and made affect, will, or actions. The DSM-5 no longer treats them as pathognomonic, but they remain clinically useful markers of the schizophrenic experience.","traps":{"a":"Echolalia and stereotyped movements are catatonic or autistic-spectrum features, not Schneiderian first-rank symptoms.","b":"Olfactory hallucinations and tactile illusions point toward organic or temporal-lobe pathology, not the passivity and thought-alienation phenomena Schneider described.","d":"Pure anhedonia and avolition are negative symptoms of schizophrenia, which Schneider explicitly distinguished from his first-rank (largely positive) symptoms."}},{"q":"DSM-5 requires obsessive-compulsive disorder to take up at least:","concept":"In the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition (DSM-5), obsessive-compulsive disorder requires obsessions and/or compulsions that are time-consuming (taking more than one hour per day) OR that cause clinically significant distress or impairment. The DSM-5 loosened the older DSM-IV strict time criterion by adding the distress/impairment alternative. It also introduced an insight specifier (good/fair, poor, or absent/delusional).","traps":{"a":"Six hours per day is an arbitrary overstatement; the threshold is more than one hour, not six.","c":"Requiring only that obsessions are present ignores that compulsions alone can qualify and that the time/distress criterion must be met.","d":"Two weeks of continuous symptoms is a depressive-episode style duration criterion and is not part of OCD diagnosis."}},{"q":"Which feature most reliably distinguishes Borderline PD from Conduct Disorder?","concept":"Borderline Personality Disorder and Conduct Disorder differ most reliably in who is diagnosed and what the core disturbance is. BPD is a cluster B personality disorder diagnosed in adults, centred on instability of identity, relationships, and affect, marked impulsivity, self-harm, and intense fear of abandonment. Conduct Disorder is diagnosed before age 18 and centres on a persistent pattern of rule violations, aggression, deceit, and antisocial behaviour; childhood CD typically precedes Antisocial (not Borderline) Personality Disorder.","traps":{"a":"Saying both are identical conditions is plainly false; they differ in age of onset, core features, and developmental trajectory.","b":"CD does not always become BPD; when it progresses to a personality disorder, the typical adult outcome is Antisocial Personality Disorder.","c":"Neither disorder is exclusively biological or exclusively environmental; both arise from gene-environment interaction, making this dichotomy inaccurate."}},{"q":"Capgras delusion is the belief that:","concept":"Capgras delusion is a delusional misidentification syndrome in which the patient believes a familiar person (often a spouse or close relative) has been replaced by an identical-looking impostor. It is thought to arise from a disconnect between face recognition and the emotional familiarity response. It belongs to a family of misidentification delusions alongside Fregoli, Cotard, and Othello syndromes.","traps":{"a":"The belief that one is dead or does not exist is Cotard (nihilistic) delusion, not Capgras.","b":"The belief that one's spouse is unfaithful without evidence is Othello syndrome (delusional jealousy).","c":"The belief that strangers are familiar people in disguise is Fregoli delusion, the mirror image of Capgras."}},{"q":"The defining feature distinguishing conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder) from malingering is that conversion disorder involves:","concept":"Conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder) is defined by neurological symptoms (motor or sensory) that are genuinely experienced, produced unintentionally and unconsciously, and are not feigned for any gain. This distinguishes it sharply from malingering (deliberate faking for external reward) and factitious disorder (deliberate faking to assume the sick role). The key is the absence of conscious intent and the absence of external incentive.","traps":{"a":"Intentional production of symptoms for external gain defines malingering, the opposite of conversion disorder's unconscious mechanism.","b":"Conscious production of symptoms for the sick role defines factitious disorder, which involves deliberate deception without external reward.","c":"Faked symptoms in legal proceedings only is a narrow instance of malingering, not the unconscious, non-feigned process of conversion disorder."}},{"q":"DSM-5 requires PTSD symptoms to persist for at least:","concept":"In the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition (DSM-5), Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder requires exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury, or sexual violence, followed by symptoms persisting for at least one month across all four symptom clusters (intrusion, avoidance, negative alterations in cognition/mood, and alterations in arousal/reactivity). Symptoms under one month indicate Acute Stress Disorder. A delayed-expression specifier applies when full criteria are not met until at least six months post-event.","traps":{"a":"One week is far too short and corresponds to no PTSD threshold; symptoms this brief fall under Acute Stress Disorder.","c":"Six months is the cutoff for the delayed-expression specifier, not the minimum duration for diagnosis.","d":"Three days is the minimum for Acute Stress Disorder, not PTSD, which requires at least one month."}},{"q":"DSM-5 criteria for Binge Eating Disorder require binge episodes to occur on average at least:","concept":"In the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition (DSM-5), Binge Eating Disorder requires recurrent binge episodes occurring on average at least once per week for at least three months, marked by a sense of loss of control and at least three associated features (eating rapidly, until uncomfortably full, when not hungry, alone, or with guilt afterward). Unlike Bulimia Nervosa, there are no compensatory behaviours. The once-weekly-for-three-months frequency mirrors the bulimia frequency criterion.","traps":{"a":"Once weekly for six months overstates the duration; the requirement is three months, not six.","b":"Twice monthly for one month understates both frequency and duration; the DSM-5 requires roughly weekly episodes for three months.","d":"Once daily for a year is far more frequent and prolonged than required; daily bingeing is not the diagnostic threshold."}},{"q":"Charles Bonnet syndrome is characterised by:","concept":"Charles Bonnet syndrome consists of complex visual hallucinations (faces, figures, or patterns) occurring in cognitively intact people who have significant visual impairment, such as macular degeneration. Crucially, insight is preserved: the person knows the images are not real. It is thought to reflect cortical release or deafferentation, where visually deprived cortex generates spontaneous imagery.","traps":{"a":"Auditory hallucinations in dementia are unrelated; Charles Bonnet syndrome is specifically visual and occurs without cognitive decline.","b":"Tactile hallucinations in alcohol withdrawal describe delirium tremens (formication), a very different aetiology.","d":"Olfactory hallucinations preceding seizures describe an uncinate or temporal-lobe aura, not the visual release phenomenon of Charles Bonnet syndrome."}},{"q":"The most reliable clinical feature distinguishing delirium from dementia is:","concept":"Delirium and dementia are most reliably distinguished by course and attention. Delirium has an acute onset (hours to days), a fluctuating course, prominent inattention as the cardinal feature, altered consciousness, and an often reversible underlying cause (infection, drugs, metabolic disturbance). Dementia has an insidious onset (months to years) and a progressive course, with memory impairment prominent early and attention relatively preserved until later. The two commonly co-occur in hospitalised elderly patients.","traps":{"a":"Delirium occurs only in young patients is false; it is in fact especially common in the elderly, particularly those with underlying dementia.","b":"Memory loss being greater in delirium misidentifies the cardinal deficit; the hallmark of delirium is inattention, and prominent memory loss is more characteristic of dementia.","d":"Dementia is always reversible is incorrect; most dementias are progressive and irreversible, whereas delirium is the potentially reversible condition."}},{"q":"DSM-5 Persistent Depressive Disorder (dysthymia) requires symptoms to be present for at least:","concept":"In the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition (DSM-5), Persistent Depressive Disorder (dysthymia) requires depressed mood most of the day, more days than not, for at least two years in adults and at least one year in children and adolescents, plus at least two of six associated symptoms. The person is never symptom-free for more than two months, and there is no manic or hypomanic episode. DSM-5 merged chronic major depression and dysthymia into this single category.","traps":{"a":"Five years overstates the requirement; the adult threshold is two years, not five.","c":"Two weeks is the criterion for a Major Depressive Episode, not the chronic course of Persistent Depressive Disorder.","d":"Six months is too short for PDD, which requires two years in adults (one year in youth)."}},{"q":"Tourette's disorder requires:","concept":"In the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition (DSM-5), Tourette's disorder requires multiple motor tics plus at least one vocal tic (though they need not occur concurrently), present for at least one year, with onset before age 18 and not attributable to a substance or medical condition. Coprolalia, though famous, occurs in only about 10-15 percent of cases and is not required. Comorbid OCD and ADHD are very common.","traps":{"a":"Coprolalia as a required feature is a popular misconception; it is present in a minority of cases and is not diagnostic.","b":"Vocal tics only for over one month describes provisional or a persistent vocal tic disorder, not Tourette's, which requires both motor and vocal tics.","d":"Only motor tics for six months describes a persistent (chronic) motor tic disorder, missing Tourette's requirement of a vocal tic and its one-year duration."}},{"q":"Anorexia nervosa, restricting subtype, is defined in DSM-5 by:","concept":"In the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition (DSM-5), the restricting subtype of Anorexia Nervosa is defined by the absence of recurrent binge-eating or purging behaviours during the current three-month episode, with weight loss achieved through dietary restriction, fasting, and/or excessive exercise. The binge-eating/purging subtype, by contrast, involves recurrent such behaviours. DSM-5 removed the amenorrhea criterion and softened the strict weight threshold.","traps":{"a":"Required compensatory vomiting contradicts the restricting subtype, which is defined precisely by the absence of purging.","c":"Binge-purge cycles within three months describe the binge-eating/purging subtype, the opposite of the restricting subtype.","d":"Required weekly laxative use is again a purging behaviour and would place the case in the binge/purge subtype, not the restricting one."}},{"q":"The four DSM-5 PTSD symptom clusters are:","concept":"In the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition (DSM-5), PTSD is organised into four symptom clusters: Intrusion (flashbacks, nightmares, intrusive memories), Avoidance (of internal or external reminders), Negative alterations in cognition and mood (negative beliefs, blame, detachment, diminished interest), and Alterations in arousal and reactivity (hypervigilance, exaggerated startle, irritability, sleep disturbance). DSM-IV had three clusters; DSM-5 split the negative-cognition/mood cluster off from avoidance to create four.","traps":{"b":"Hyperarousal only, with mood changes optional, collapses four required clusters into one and wrongly makes mood alterations optional.","c":"Flashbacks, denial, anger, bargaining mixes one true PTSD feature with the Kubler-Ross stages of grief, which are not PTSD clusters.","d":"Dissociation, anxiety, depression, mania are separate symptom domains or disorders, not the four defined PTSD clusters."}},{"q":"Pseudobulbar affect (PBA) is characterised by:","concept":"Pseudobulbar affect (pathological laughing and crying) is characterised by sudden, brief, involuntary outbursts of crying or laughing that are disproportionate to, or incongruent with, the person's underlying mood. It results from disruption of corticobulbar pathways and is associated with neurological conditions such as stroke, multiple sclerosis, ALS, traumatic brain injury, and dementia. It is treated with dextromethorphan-quinidine or SSRIs and is distinct from post-stroke depression.","traps":{"a":"Anxious affect in generalized anxiety disorder is a mood/anxiety symptom, not the involuntary emotional expression of a corticobulbar lesion.","b":"Affect blunting in schizophrenia is a reduction of emotional expression, the opposite of the exaggerated, involuntary outbursts of PBA.","d":"Mood-congruent affect in major depression means the emotional display matches the mood, whereas PBA is defined by incongruence between expression and mood."}},{"q":"The key DSM-5 feature distinguishing mania from hypomania is:","concept":"In the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition (DSM-5), mania and hypomania differ mainly in severity, duration, and consequences. Mania lasts at least seven days (or any duration if hospitalisation is required), causes marked impairment, and may include psychotic features, defining Bipolar I. Hypomania lasts at least four days, causes no marked impairment, involves no psychosis, and is observable by others, and with a major depressive episode defines Bipolar II. By definition, hypomania never includes psychotic features.","traps":{"b":"Saying mania has no euphoria is false; euphoric (or irritable) mood is a core feature of a manic episode.","c":"Hypomania lasting longer than mania is backwards; hypomania has a shorter minimum duration (four days versus seven).","d":"Saying only hypomania can include psychotic features inverts the truth; psychosis occurs in mania and is explicitly excluded from hypomania."}},{"q":"Folie à deux (shared psychotic disorder) describes:","concept":"Folie a deux (shared psychotic disorder), described by Lasegue and Falret in 1877, is a condition in which a delusion develops in a previously healthy person who is in a close relationship with, and often isolated alongside, a 'primary' individual who has an established psychotic illness. The delusion is transmitted from the dominant partner to the secondary one. DSM-5 folds this into 'Other Specified Schizophrenia Spectrum and Other Psychotic Disorder.'","traps":{"a":"Group hallucinations in cult settings describe mass or collective phenomena, not the two-person delusional transmission that defines folie a deux.","b":"Two unrelated patients with identical hallucinations lacks the required close relationship and the primary-to-secondary transmission that is the essence of the syndrome.","d":"Schizophrenia transmitted via genetic inheritance describes heritability of a disorder, not the psychosocial induction of a delusion in a close contact."}},{"q":"On the WAIS-IV, the four index scores are:","concept":"The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale, Fourth Edition (WAIS-IV, 2008) replaced the older Verbal IQ and Performance IQ structure with four index scores: the Verbal Comprehension Index (VCI), Perceptual Reasoning Index (PRI), Working Memory Index (WMI), and Processing Speed Index (PSI). The Full Scale IQ is the overall composite derived from these indices. This four-index model reflects a more factor-analytically grounded structure of intelligence.","traps":{"a":"Crystallised, Fluid, Visual, Quantitative are broad Cattell-Horn-Carroll or Woodcock-Johnson style abilities, not the WAIS-IV index labels.","c":"VIQ, PIQ, Memory, Attention mixes the outdated Verbal/Performance IQ terms with domains that are not WAIS-IV indices.","d":"Verbal IQ, Performance IQ, Full Scale IQ, and Memory Quotient describe the older Wechsler and Wechsler Memory Scale structure that WAIS-IV replaced."}},{"q":"On the MMPI-2, the F (Infrequency) scale is used to detect:","concept":"On the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory-2 (MMPI-2), the F (Infrequency) scale detects random responding or the over-reporting of unusual or severe symptoms, often called 'faking bad.' It is one of several validity scales, alongside L (Lie), K (Correction), and the VRIN and TRIN inconsistency scales. An elevated F must be interpreted cautiously, ruling out random responding via VRIN/TRIN before concluding symptom exaggeration.","traps":{"a":"Religious bias is not what any MMPI-2 validity scale measures; the F scale targets infrequent symptom endorsement, not belief content.","b":"Item endorsement based on social desirability (faking good) is captured by the L and K scales, not the F scale.","d":"Defensiveness only is the domain of the L and K scales; the F scale detects the opposite tendency, over-reporting."}},{"q":"Cronbach's alpha is most commonly used to estimate:","concept":"Cronbach's alpha, introduced by Lee Cronbach in 1951, is the most commonly used index of internal consistency reliability, estimating how closely the items of a multi-item scale measure the same underlying construct. It ranges from 0 to 1, with values of roughly 0.70 acceptable, 0.80 good, and 0.90 excellent, though very high alpha can indicate item redundancy. It is distinct from inter-rater reliability (Cohen's kappa) and test-retest reliability (Pearson r over time).","traps":{"a":"Construct validity concerns whether a test measures the intended construct, which is a validity question, not the internal-consistency reliability that alpha estimates.","c":"Predictive validity concerns how well scores forecast a future criterion, again a validity issue rather than reliability.","d":"Test-retest reliability is estimated by correlating scores across two time points (Pearson r), not by Cronbach's alpha."}},{"q":"The Exner Comprehensive System is the major scoring system for:","concept":"John Exner's Comprehensive System (1974) is the major standardised scoring system for the Rorschach Inkblot Test. Exner integrated five earlier, competing Rorschach systems into a single approach with norms, coding location, determinants, content, populars, and special scores into a structural summary. It has been largely superseded by the Rorschach Performance Assessment System (R-PAS, 2011).","traps":{"a":"The MMPI is an objective self-report inventory scored by validity and clinical scales, not by the Exner system.","b":"The Thematic Apperception Test is scored by narrative/thematic methods (for example Murray's needs and press), not the Exner Comprehensive System.","d":"The Wechsler scales are intelligence tests scored by index and IQ composites, entirely unrelated to Rorschach coding."}},{"q":"Sensitivity of a screening test refers to:","concept":"Sensitivity is the true positive rate of a screening test: the proportion of people who actually have the condition whom the test correctly identifies as positive, calculated as true positives divided by (true positives plus false negatives). It contrasts with specificity, the true negative rate. A highly sensitive test is good for ruling out disease when negative (the SnNOut rule).","traps":{"a":"The probability that a positive test reflects true disease is positive predictive value, which depends on prevalence, not sensitivity.","b":"The probability of disease prior to testing is the pre-test probability or prevalence, not a test's sensitivity.","d":"The proportion of true negatives correctly identified is specificity, the complementary measure to sensitivity."}},{"q":"A stanine score of 5 corresponds to which position on the normal curve?","concept":"Stanine scores ('standard nine') are a normalised scale from 1 to 9 with a mean of 5 and a standard deviation of about 2, where each band is roughly half a standard deviation wide. A stanine of 5 is the central band, corresponding to the 40th to 60th percentile (the mean plus or minus about 0.25 SD). Stanines 1-3 are below average, 4-6 average, and 7-9 above average.","traps":{"a":"The 23rd-39th percentile corresponds to a stanine of 4 (just below average), not the central stanine of 5.","b":"Above the 95th percentile corresponds to a stanine of 9, the top band, far above the middle stanine of 5.","d":"The 1st-4th percentile corresponds to a stanine of 1, the lowest band, not the average middle band."}},{"q":"BDI-II severity bands are conventionally:","concept":"The Beck Depression Inventory-II (BDI-II, 1996) is a 21-item self-report scale with a total range of 0 to 63, conventionally banded as 0-13 minimal, 14-19 mild, 20-28 moderate, and 29-63 severe depression. Knowing these cutoffs distinguishes it from other instruments such as the PHQ-9 and the clinician-rated Hamilton Depression Rating Scale, which use different ranges.","traps":{"b":"The 0-5 / 6-10 / 11-15 / 16+ banding does not match the BDI-II's 0-63 range and fabricated small cutoffs.","c":"The 0-9 / 10-15 / 16-23 / 24-40 banding is incorrect for the BDI-II and does not reach its maximum of 63.","d":"The 0-20 / 21-40 / 41-60 banding invents three broad ranges that do not correspond to the established BDI-II severity thresholds."}},{"q":"Item Response Theory (IRT) differs from Classical Test Theory (CTT) primarily in that IRT:","concept":"Item Response Theory (IRT), developed by figures such as Georg Rasch, Frederic Lord, and Allan Birnbaum, models the probability of a correct response as a logistic function of a person's latent ability (theta) and item parameters: difficulty (b), discrimination (a), and guessing (c). The one-parameter (Rasch) model uses difficulty only, the two-parameter adds discrimination, and the three-parameter adds guessing. This item-level modelling is what enables computer-adaptive testing, unlike Classical Test Theory which focuses on whole-test scores.","traps":{"b":"Saying IRT is identical to CTT but relabelled is false; IRT models item-level probabilities and person ability, a fundamentally different framework from CTT.","c":"Claiming IRT cannot estimate item difficulty is wrong; difficulty (the b parameter) is central to every IRT model.","d":"Requiring only a single item parameter and no person parameter contradicts IRT, which always includes a person-ability parameter (theta) alongside item parameters."}},{"q":"The Bender Visual-Motor Gestalt Test (Bender-Gestalt II) primarily assesses:","concept":"The Bender Visual-Motor Gestalt Test, Second Edition (Bender-Gestalt II, Brannigan and Decker 2003) primarily assesses visual-motor integration by having the subject copy geometric designs, scored for accuracy, organisation, and distortion. It is used to screen for developmental delays in children (for example school readiness) and for neuropsychological or organic brain dysfunction in adults, though its specificity is limited. It was historically sometimes interpreted projectively.","traps":{"a":"Auditory memory is assessed by verbal memory tasks, not by a design-copying visual-motor test.","c":"Verbal abstraction is measured by tasks like similarities or proverb interpretation, not by copying geometric figures.","d":"Assessing personality via projective interpretation only misrepresents the Bender-Gestalt; its primary, validated use is visual-motor screening, with projective use being a legacy secondary practice."}},{"q":"The Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) is classified as a:","concept":"The Thematic Apperception Test (TAT), developed by Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan at Harvard in 1935, is a projective technique in which the subject constructs stories about ambiguous interpersonal scenes. Responses are interpreted for underlying needs, press, and recurring themes, grounded in Murray's Need-Press theory. As a projective measure it contrasts with objective, self-report inventories.","traps":{"a":"An objective achievement test measures acquired knowledge with right/wrong answers, unlike the open-ended, interpretive TAT.","b":"A self-report personality inventory (such as the MMPI) uses fixed items scored objectively, the opposite of the TAT's projective storytelling.","c":"A structured neuropsychological battery assesses cognitive functions with standardised tasks, not the personality dynamics the projective TAT elicits."}},{"q":"Concurrent validity is established by:","concept":"Criterion validity asks whether a test correlates with an external gold-standard criterion. It has two forms: concurrent validity, where the criterion is measured at roughly the same time as the test, and predictive validity, where the criterion is measured later. Option a describes exactly the concurrent case, so it is correct.","traps":{"b":"Correlating a test with future performance is predictive validity, the other branch of criterion validity, not concurrent validity.","c":"Expert agreement that items 'look right' is face validity, a subjective judgment about appearance, not an empirical correlation with a criterion.","d":"Comparing scores across two cultures speaks to measurement invariance or cross-cultural bias, not to whether a test tracks a same-time criterion."}},{"q":"The Halstead-Reitan Neuropsychological Battery includes which classic subtest?","concept":"The Halstead-Reitan Neuropsychological Battery, standardised by Ralph Reitan, is a fixed battery of sensitive tests that yield an overall Impairment Index for brain dysfunction. Its classic subtests include the Category Test, Tactual Performance Test, Trail Making A and B, Speech-Sounds Perception, Seashore Rhythm, and Finger Tapping. Option a lists these core Halstead-Reitan components, so it is correct.","traps":{"b":"The Beck Depression Inventory and Hamilton Depression Rating Scale are mood measures, not neuropsychological subtests of the Halstead-Reitan.","c":"The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale and Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory are general IQ and personality instruments that may accompany but do not constitute the Halstead-Reitan battery.","d":"The Bender-Gestalt is a single visuomotor screening test, not the multi-subtest Halstead-Reitan battery."}},{"q":"The Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales primarily assess:","concept":"The Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales, developed by Sara Sparrow and colleagues, use a caregiver interview or questionnaire to measure adaptive functioning across the lifespan. They assess four domains: Communication, Daily Living Skills, Socialisation, and Motor Skills, plus a Maladaptive Behaviour index. Because adaptive deficits are required alongside low IQ to diagnose intellectual disability under the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, option d is correct.","traps":{"a":"Suicidal ideation severity is captured by scales like the Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale or Beck Scale for Suicide Ideation, not by an adaptive-behaviour instrument.","b":"Personality disorders are assessed with instruments such as the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory or SCID-5-PD, not the Vineland.","c":"Cognitive ability alone is measured by IQ tests like the Wechsler scales; the Vineland deliberately measures everyday adaptive skills, not intelligence."}},{"q":"The standard error of measurement (SEM) of a test is calculated as:","concept":"The standard error of measurement indexes how much an examinee's observed score is expected to fluctuate around their true score due to unreliability. It is computed as the standard deviation of scores multiplied by the square root of one minus the reliability coefficient, so SEM = SD times the square root of (1 minus r). As reliability approaches 1, the SEM shrinks toward 0, making option c correct.","traps":{"a":"Dividing SD by reliability is not a defined psychometric formula and would grow the error as reliability falls, which is backward.","b":"Multiplying SD by (1 plus reliability) inflates rather than reduces error as reliability rises, the opposite of the intended relationship.","d":"SD times reliability confuses reliability with its complement; the SEM depends on the square root of (1 minus reliability), not reliability itself."}},{"q":"The Wechsler Memory Scale-IV (WMS-IV) yields which primary indices?","concept":"The Wechsler Memory Scale, Fourth Edition, developed by David Wechsler's successors, is the standard adult memory battery. Its primary index scores are Auditory Memory, Visual Memory, Visual Working Memory, Immediate Memory, and Delayed Memory, built from subtests like Logical Memory, Verbal Paired Associates, and Visual Reproduction. Option c names these indices, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Verbal IQ and Performance IQ are older Wechsler intelligence-scale indices, not memory-scale outputs, and were themselves dropped in recent WAIS editions.","b":"Crystallised and fluid intelligence are Cattell-Horn constructs measured by ability tests, not by the WMS-IV.","d":"Attention, concentration, and vigilance are single cognitive processes tapped in part by the scale, not the named primary index structure of the WMS-IV."}},{"q":"Socratic questioning in CBT is used primarily to:","concept":"Socratic questioning, central to Aaron T. Beck's cognitive therapy, uses open-ended guided-discovery questions to help clients examine the evidence for and against their automatic thoughts. Rather than the therapist supplying conclusions, the client is led to weigh alternatives and reach more balanced, adaptive views themselves. Option d captures this collaborative evidence-testing, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Free-associating to childhood memories is a psychoanalytic technique, not the present-focused, thought-testing method of Socratic questioning.","b":"Socratic questioning complements rather than replaces exposure therapy; the two address different targets and are often used together.","c":"Telling the client what to think is directive instruction, the exact opposite of guided discovery, which draws conclusions from the client."}},{"q":"The first-line behavioural treatment for OCD is:","concept":"The first-line behavioural treatment for obsessive-compulsive disorder is Exposure and Response Prevention, formalised by Edna Foa and Michael Kozak. The client is exposed to feared triggers while refraining from compulsions, allowing the anxiety to extinguish and breaking the obsession-anxiety-compulsion cycle. Option a is therefore correct, with SSRIs as first-line medication.","traps":{"b":"Hypnotherapy has no established evidence base as a first-line OCD treatment and is not recommended by major guidelines.","c":"Free association is a psychoanalytic method, not the structured behavioural intervention proven for OCD.","d":"Systematic desensitisation is a graded, relaxation-based procedure for phobias; ERP, which emphasises response prevention, is the specific OCD treatment."}},{"q":"Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT) was developed by Marsha Linehan primarily for:","concept":"Dialectical Behavior Therapy was developed by Marsha Linehan primarily for chronically suicidal clients and those with Borderline Personality Disorder. It synthesises behavioural change strategies with acceptance and mindfulness drawn from Zen, delivered through four skill modules: mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation, and interpersonal effectiveness. Option d names its original target population, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Childhood ADHD is treated primarily with behavioural parent training and stimulant medication, not DBT.","b":"Panic disorder is treated first-line with cognitive behavioural therapy and interoceptive exposure, not DBT.","c":"Schizophrenia is managed with antipsychotics plus psychosocial interventions; DBT was not designed for it."}},{"q":"Rogers' core conditions for client-centered therapy are:","concept":"Carl Rogers specified three core conditions he called necessary and sufficient for therapeutic change: empathy, unconditional positive regard, and congruence (genuineness). These relational attitudes, uniquely emphasised in person-centered therapy, form the foundation of the common-factors literature. Option c lists all three, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Interpretation, transference, and free association are psychoanalytic techniques, not Rogers' humanistic core conditions.","b":"Modeling, role-play, and homework are behavioural and cognitive-behavioural methods, not the person-centered relational conditions.","d":"Confrontation, paradoxical intention, and prescription are directive strategic techniques that contradict Rogers' non-directive stance."}},{"q":"Working through in psychoanalytic therapy refers to:","concept":"Working through, Freud's Durcharbeiten, is the effortful psychoanalytic process of repeatedly re-encountering the same conflict, resistance, and interpretation across different contexts such as transference, dreams, and daily life. This repetition consolidates insight so that it moves beyond intellectual understanding into genuine emotional and behavioural change. Option b describes this process, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Solving problems by direct advice is a supportive or directive approach, not the insight-integrating work of psychoanalysis.","c":"Ending therapy after a single insight is the opposite of working through, which specifically requires sustained repetition.","d":"Free association without interpretation is the raw analytic method, but working through is precisely the repeated application of interpretation to that material."}},{"q":"Beck's cognitive triad in depression consists of negative views about:","concept":"Aaron T. Beck's cognitive triad describes the three negative belief domains that characterise depression: negative views of the self, of the world or current experience, and of the future. These pervasive negative schemas generate the automatic thoughts that cognitive therapy targets. Option c lists self, world, and future, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Mood, behaviour, and somatic state are symptom clusters of depression, not the belief domains of Beck's triad.","b":"Past, present, and future is a time framing; the triad specifically concerns self, world, and future, not the past.","d":"Self, mother, and father reflects an object-relations or psychoanalytic frame, not Beck's cognitive triad."}},{"q":"The 'spirit' of Motivational Interviewing is captured by the acronym PACE, which stands for:","concept":"William Miller and Stephen Rollnick describe the underlying spirit of Motivational Interviewing with the acronym PACE: Partnership, Acceptance, Compassion, and Evocation. This collaborative, client-honouring stance draws out the person's own motivation for change rather than imposing it. Option a spells out PACE, so it is correct.","traps":{"b":"Presence, Affirmation, Confrontation, Empathy is invented; confrontation in particular runs counter to the non-confrontational MI spirit.","c":"Practice, Active listening, Change, Engagement does not match the PACE acronym and mixes in unrelated terms.","d":"Plan, Act, Check, Evaluate is a quality-improvement or PDCA cycle, not the MI spirit."}},{"q":"In Minuchin's structural family therapy, enmeshment refers to:","concept":"In Salvador Minuchin's structural family therapy, families are organised into subsystems separated by boundaries that vary in permeability. Enmeshment refers to diffuse, overly permeable boundaries marked by over-involvement, intrusion, and loss of individual autonomy. Option a describes this, so it is correct; its opposite is disengagement with rigid boundaries.","traps":{"b":"Strategic paradoxical interventions belong to strategic family therapy (Haley, the Milan group), not to the structural concept of enmeshment.","c":"Rigid boundaries with disengagement is the opposite pole of enmeshment on Minuchin's boundary continuum.","d":"A complete lack of communication describes extreme disengagement, not the over-involvement that defines enmeshment."}},{"q":"Yalom's emphasis on the 'here-and-now' in group therapy is intended to:","concept":"Irvin Yalom treats the therapy group as a social microcosm in which members' habitual relationship patterns replay in real time. Emphasising the here-and-now means using these immediate in-group interactions, then reflecting on them through a self-reflective loop, as the primary engine of change. Option d captures this, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"The here-and-now focus does not forbid discussing outside life; it uses in-group events to illuminate those outside patterns.","b":"Yalom's approach complements rather than permanently replaces individual therapy.","c":"Examining group dynamics is central to the here-and-now method, not something it bypasses."}},{"q":"Joseph Wolpe's principle of reciprocal inhibition is the basis for:","concept":"Joseph Wolpe's principle of reciprocal inhibition holds that an anxiety response can be inhibited by simultaneously eliciting an incompatible response such as deep relaxation. This principle is the basis of systematic desensitisation: train relaxation, build a fear hierarchy, then progress through it while staying relaxed. Option b describes this, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Acceptance and Commitment Therapy is rooted in Relational Frame Theory and psychological flexibility, not Wolpe's reciprocal inhibition.","c":"Paradoxical intention is a logotherapy/strategic technique (Frankl), unrelated to pairing relaxation with feared stimuli.","d":"Free association is a psychoanalytic method, not a behaviour-therapy application of reciprocal inhibition."}},{"q":"The 'hexaflex' of Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT) consists of:","concept":"The hexaflex of Acceptance and Commitment Therapy, developed by Steven Hayes, maps the six interrelated processes that produce psychological flexibility: acceptance, cognitive defusion, self-as-context, contact with the present moment, values, and committed action. Each process counters a corresponding rigidity such as experiential avoidance or cognitive fusion. Option d lists the six, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Six neurotransmitter systems is a neurobiology reference with nothing to do with the ACT hexaflex.","b":"Six DSM disorder clusters is a diagnostic grouping, not the ACT process model.","c":"Sensation, perception, attention, memory, emotion, behaviour are basic cognitive-psychology domains, not the ACT flexibility processes."}},{"q":"Interpersonal Therapy (IPT) targets four main problem areas:","concept":"Interpersonal Therapy, developed by Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman for major depression, is a time-limited treatment focused on the client's current interpersonal context. Therapist and client select one of four problem areas to target: grief, interpersonal role disputes, role transitions, and interpersonal deficits. Option d names these four, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Schemas, automatic thoughts, behaviours, and biological factors are cognitive-behavioural targets, not the four IPT problem areas.","b":"Id, ego, superego, and defences are psychoanalytic structures, not IPT foci.","c":"Cognitive, affective, motor, and somatic are symptom domains, not the interpersonal problem areas IPT works with."}},{"q":"The 'empty chair' technique is most associated with which therapeutic school?","concept":"The empty chair technique is most associated with Gestalt therapy, founded by Fritz Perls. The client dialogues with an absent person or a disowned part of the self imagined in an empty chair, often switching seats to voice each side. This surfaces unfinished business and integrates polarities such as top dog and underdog, so option c is correct.","traps":{"a":"Person-centered therapy relies on the core conditions and non-directive reflection, not staged chair dialogues.","b":"DBT uses skills training and dialectics, not the empty-chair experiment.","d":"CBT uses thought records and behavioural experiments; the empty chair is a Gestalt, later emotion-focused, technique."}},{"q":"Francine Shapiro's EMDR (Eye Movement Desensitization and Reprocessing) is recommended primarily for:","concept":"Eye Movement Desensitization and Reprocessing, developed by Francine Shapiro, is an eight-phase protocol that pairs brief recall of a distressing memory with bilateral stimulation such as guided eye movements. It is recommended primarily for post-traumatic stress disorder and trauma-related conditions and is endorsed by the American Psychological Association, NICE, and the World Health Organization. Option a is therefore correct.","traps":{"b":"Schizophrenia is treated with antipsychotics and psychosocial care; EMDR is not a primary treatment for it.","c":"Bipolar disorder is managed with mood stabilisers, not EMDR.","d":"OCD's first-line treatment is Exposure and Response Prevention, not EMDR."}},{"q":"A Type II error in hypothesis testing occurs when:","concept":"In null hypothesis significance testing, a Type II error (beta) occurs when a false null hypothesis is not rejected, meaning a real effect exists but the test fails to detect it: a false negative. Statistical power equals 1 minus beta and rises with larger samples, larger effect sizes, and lower variance. Option c describes this false negative, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Setting the p-value or alpha threshold is a design choice, not the definition of a Type II error.","b":"An excessively large sample tends to reduce Type II error, not cause it.","d":"Rejecting a true null is a false positive, which is a Type I error, not Type II."}},{"q":"ANOVA (analysis of variance) is most appropriate when comparing:","concept":"Analysis of variance compares means across groups by partitioning variance into between-group and within-group components, tested with an F ratio. One-way ANOVA is the appropriate choice when comparing three or more independent group means on a continuous outcome; a significant F is followed by post-hoc tests. Option d states this, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Two paired observations of the same subject call for a paired t-test, not ANOVA.","b":"Two categorical variables are analysed with a chi-square test, not ANOVA.","c":"Two independent group means are compared with an independent-samples t-test; ANOVA is reserved for three or more groups."}},{"q":"An odds ratio of 1.0 indicates:","concept":"An odds ratio quantifies the odds of an outcome in an exposed group relative to an unexposed group. When the odds ratio equals exactly 1.0, the odds are identical in both groups, indicating no association between exposure and outcome. Option d states this, so it is correct; the same null value logic applies to risk ratios and hazard ratios.","traps":{"a":"A protective exposure yields an odds ratio below 1.0, not equal to 1.0.","b":"An odds ratio, being observational, does not by itself establish causation, and 1.0 indicates no association at all.","c":"Doubling the odds corresponds to an odds ratio of 2.0, not 1.0."}},{"q":"Cohen's d of 0.5 conventionally represents:","concept":"Cohen's d expresses a between-group difference in standard-deviation units. By Jacob Cohen's conventional benchmarks, roughly 0.2 is a small effect, 0.5 is a medium effect, and 0.8 is a large effect. A d of 0.5 therefore represents a medium effect, making option b correct.","traps":{"a":"A trivial effect would be well below 0.2, far smaller than 0.5.","c":"A small effect corresponds to about 0.2, not 0.5.","d":"A large effect corresponds to about 0.8 or higher, not 0.5."}},{"q":"Stratified random sampling differs from simple random sampling in that it:","concept":"Stratified random sampling first divides the population into mutually exclusive subgroups (strata) defined by a relevant characteristic, then randomly samples within each stratum. This guarantees each subgroup is represented and typically yields lower variance than simple random sampling when strata are internally homogeneous. Option d describes this, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Selecting every nth subject from a list is systematic sampling, not stratified sampling.","b":"Selecting intact clusters such as whole schools is cluster sampling, which samples groups rather than sampling within strata.","c":"Using only volunteers is convenience sampling, a non-probability method, not stratified random sampling."}},{"q":"Reliability and validity differ in that:","concept":"Reliability and validity are distinct measurement properties. Reliability is the consistency or reproducibility of a measurement, while validity is whether the test actually measures the construct it claims to. A test can be reliable without being valid, but a valid test must be reliable, so option a is correct.","traps":{"b":"Reliability includes many forms such as test-retest and internal consistency, not only inter-rater agreement.","c":"Reliability and validity are conceptually different, not two names for the same thing.","d":"There is no rule that validity must be established before reliability; if anything reliability sets an upper bound on validity."}},{"q":"Intention-to-treat (ITT) analysis in an RCT means:","concept":"Intention-to-treat analysis in a randomised controlled trial analyses every randomised participant in the group to which they were originally assigned, regardless of adherence, drop-out, or crossover. This preserves the balance created by randomisation and prevents bias from differential attrition, giving a pragmatic effectiveness estimate. Option a states this, so it is correct.","traps":{"b":"Analysing only those who completed the intended treatment is per-protocol analysis, which breaks randomisation and tends to inflate efficacy.","c":"Analysing only responders is a biased selective approach, not intention-to-treat.","d":"Including only the control group ignores the intervention arm entirely and is not intention-to-treat."}},{"q":"A 95% confidence interval for a mean difference of 0.4 that ranges from −0.1 to 0.9 implies:","concept":"A confidence interval for a mean difference conveys both the effect estimate and its precision. When a 95% confidence interval for a difference includes 0 (the null value), the result is not statistically significant at alpha 0.05 because a zero difference remains plausible. The interval from minus 0.1 to 0.9 crosses 0, so option d is correct.","traps":{"a":"A result crossing 0 is not statistically significant, so it cannot be highly significant.","b":"A non-significant interval does not prove there is no effect; the study may simply be underpowered.","c":"A large sample would narrow the interval; a wide interval crossing 0 suggests limited precision, not an oversized sample."}},{"q":"The Mann-Whitney U test is the non-parametric analogue of:","concept":"The Mann-Whitney U test (equivalently the Wilcoxon rank-sum test) is the non-parametric analogue of the independent-samples t-test, used to compare two independent groups when normality is violated or data are ordinal. It ranks all observations and tests whether the distributions differ. Option d correctly identifies its parametric counterpart.","traps":{"a":"The chi-square test of independence handles categorical associations; its rank-based counterpart is not the Mann-Whitney U.","b":"The non-parametric analogue of the paired t-test is the Wilcoxon signed-rank test, not Mann-Whitney U.","c":"The non-parametric analogue of one-way ANOVA is the Kruskal-Wallis test, not Mann-Whitney U."}},{"q":"Number Needed to Treat (NNT) is calculated as:","concept":"The Number Needed to Treat is the number of patients who must receive a treatment for one additional person to benefit compared with control. It is calculated as 1 divided by the absolute risk reduction, where the absolute risk reduction is the control event rate minus the treatment event rate. Option a gives this formula, so it is correct.","traps":{"b":"Odds ratio minus 1 is not a defined NNT formula and mixes a relative measure with an absolute count.","c":"Relative risk times the control event rate does not yield the NNT.","d":"Patients enrolled divided by number of events is an event-rate-type calculation, not the reciprocal of absolute risk reduction."}},{"q":"The Bonferroni correction adjusts the alpha threshold when conducting multiple comparisons by:","concept":"The Bonferroni correction controls the family-wise error rate that inflates when many comparisons are made. It divides the alpha threshold by the number of comparisons (alpha divided by k), so each individual test must meet a stricter significance level. This conservative adjustment, which lowers power, makes option a correct.","traps":{"b":"Bonferroni adjusts the alpha threshold; it does not replace the p-value with a Z-score.","c":"Ignoring the number of comparisons is exactly what Bonferroni corrects for, not what it does.","d":"Multiplying alpha by the number of comparisons would loosen the threshold and inflate false positives, the opposite of Bonferroni."}},{"q":"Choose the correctly punctuated sentence.","concept":"When a sentence opens with a dependent (subordinate) clause, standard English punctuation places a comma after that clause and before the main independent clause. Here 'Although it was raining' is a subordinate clause introduced by 'although', so it must be followed by a comma: 'Although it was raining, we decided to walk.' The subordinating conjunction 'although' itself is not followed by a comma, and a semicolon cannot be used because a semicolon joins two independent clauses, not a dependent clause to an independent one.","traps":{"a":"This wrongly inserts a comma after 'Although' and omits the needed comma after the subordinate clause, cutting the conjunction off from the clause it introduces.","b":"A semicolon joins two independent clauses, but 'Although it was raining' is dependent, so a semicolon is grammatically wrong here.","c":"The comma is misplaced inside the main clause ('we decided, to walk'), which incorrectly separates the verb 'decided' from its infinitive object."}},{"q":"Choose the option that correctly completes the sentence: 'The data ___ inconclusive.'","concept":"'Data' is historically the plural of the Latin singular 'datum', so in formal, academic, and scientific writing it takes a plural verb: 'The data are inconclusive.' Although everyday and increasingly some journalistic usage treats 'data' as a singular mass noun ('the data is'), exam and research contexts expect the traditional plural agreement. The same pattern applies to 'media' (plural of medium) and 'criteria' (plural of criterion).","traps":{"b":"'Was' is singular past tense, treating 'data' as a singular mass noun, which formal usage rejects.","c":"'Has been' is singular present perfect and mismatches the plural subject 'data'.","d":"'Is' is the singular present verb and reflects the informal treatment of 'data' as singular, not the formal plural preferred here."}},{"q":"Identify the sentence that uses 'who' / 'whom' correctly.","concept":"'Who' is the subject form and 'whom' is the object form; the reliable test is to substitute he/him. In 'Who shall I say is calling?', the phrase 'shall I say' is a parenthetical interruption, and the real clause is 'who is calling', where the pronoun is the subject of 'is calling' (He is calling), so 'who' is correct. 'Whom' would only be right where the pronoun functions as an object.","traps":{"b":"'Whom is at the door?' wrongly uses the object form for the subject of 'is'; it should be 'Who is at the door?'","c":"'Who did you give the book to?' uses the subject form where the pronoun is the object of the preposition 'to', so strictly it should be 'whom' (colloquially accepted but not formally correct).","d":"'Whom shall I say is calling?' misreads the parenthetical 'shall I say' and wrongly makes the subject of 'is calling' an object; it should be 'who'."}},{"q":"Which sentence uses the conditional correctly?","concept":"The third conditional expresses an unreal or hypothetical situation in the past and follows the pattern: If + past perfect, would have + past participle. Thus 'If I had known, I would have come.' is correct. A common error is putting 'would have' inside the if-clause; the if-clause takes the past perfect ('had known'), and 'would have' belongs only in the main result clause.","traps":{"a":"This commits the classic error of using 'would have' in the if-clause; the condition should read 'If I had known'.","b":"This mixes tenses illogically, pairing a present-style 'would know' condition with a simple past 'came' result, matching no valid conditional pattern.","c":"This mismatches a second-conditional-style 'If I knew' with a present perfect 'have come', so the tenses do not form a coherent conditional."}},{"q":"Select the sentence with a misplaced modifier.","concept":"A misplaced or dangling modifier is a descriptive phrase positioned so that it appears to modify the wrong noun. In 'Running quickly, the marathon finish line came into view,' the participial phrase 'Running quickly' seems to describe 'the marathon finish line', which cannot run; the sentence needs a human subject, e.g., 'Running quickly, she saw the finish line come into view.' The other options place their modifiers directly beside the words they logically describe.","traps":{"a":"'He drove to the office in the morning' places 'in the morning' sensibly next to the action it modifies, so there is no misplaced modifier.","c":"'The teacher gave the test on Friday' correctly attaches 'on Friday' to the giving of the test, with no dangling phrase.","d":"'She ate the soup that was hot' places the relative clause 'that was hot' directly after 'soup', the noun it properly modifies."}},{"q":"Choose the word closest in meaning to UBIQUITOUS.","concept":"'Ubiquitous' means present, appearing, or found everywhere; pervasive or omnipresent. Its closest synonym among the options is 'universal / present everywhere'. Its antonyms are 'rare', 'scarce', and 'uncommon', and it is frequently confused with the similar-sounding 'iniquitous', which means wicked or grossly unjust.","traps":{"a":"'Quietly persistent' suggests something enduring or insistent, but ubiquitous is about being everywhere in space, not persisting over time.","b":"'Outdated' concerns being old-fashioned or obsolete and has no bearing on how widespread something is.","d":"'Rare' is the direct antonym of ubiquitous, describing scarcity rather than omnipresence."}},{"q":"Identify the sentence in passive voice.","concept":"In the passive voice the grammatical subject receives the action rather than performing it, formed with a form of 'to be' plus the past participle, and the doer (agent) appears in an optional 'by' phrase. 'The proposal was approved by the committee' is passive because 'the proposal' receives the approving and 'the committee' is the agent. The active equivalent is 'The committee approved the proposal.'","traps":{"a":"'The committee will approve the proposal' is active future tense: the committee (subject) performs the action.","b":"'Did the committee approve the proposal?' is an active interrogative sentence, with the committee as the doer, not a passive construction.","c":"'The committee approved the proposal' is active past tense, with the subject performing the action rather than receiving it."}},{"q":"Which option corrects the run-on sentence: 'She studied hard she passed the exam.'?","concept":"A run-on (fused) sentence joins two independent clauses with no punctuation or connector, as in 'She studied hard she passed the exam.' It can be fixed by a period, a semicolon (optionally with a conjunctive adverb like 'consequently' followed by a comma), or a comma plus a coordinating conjunction. 'She studied hard; consequently, she passed the exam' correctly uses a semicolon before the conjunctive adverb and a comma after it.","traps":{"a":"'She studied hard so she passed the exam' uses the coordinating conjunction 'so' but omits the comma that should precede it, so it is not the cleanest correction.","b":"'She studied, hard she passed the exam' inserts a comma in the wrong place and leaves the two clauses improperly fused.","d":"'She studied hard, she passed the exam' joins two independent clauses with only a comma, creating a comma splice, which is still an error."}},{"q":"Read the brief passage and answer:\n'Despite mounting evidence of climate disruption, public policy responses have been hampered by short electoral cycles and powerful incumbent interests, leaving long-horizon problems chronically under-addressed.'\nThe author's main point is best described as:","concept":"The passage uses a 'Despite X, public policy is hampered by Y and Z, leaving long-horizon problems under-addressed' structure, which explicitly blames short electoral cycles and entrenched incumbent interests for the inadequate response to climate change. The author's main point is therefore that short political horizons and vested interests prevent an adequate response to long-term problems. Reading comprehension requires identifying the claim the sentence actually asserts, not adding outside inferences.","traps":{"b":"'Climate change is not happening' directly contradicts the passage, which opens with 'mounting evidence of climate disruption'.","c":"'Renewable energy is more profitable than fossil fuels' introduces an economic claim the passage never makes.","d":"'Elections should be abolished' is an extreme prescriptive leap; the passage diagnoses short electoral cycles as a problem but never proposes ending elections."}},{"q":"Choose the correct option to complete: 'Neither the manager nor the employees ___ available for comment.'","concept":"With correlative conjunctions 'neither ... nor' (and 'either ... or'), the verb agrees with the subject nearer to it, called the rule of proximity. Here the closer subject is the plural 'employees', so the verb must be plural: 'Neither the manager nor the employees were available for comment.' If the order were reversed, with the singular 'manager' nearer the verb, it would take the singular 'was'.","traps":{"b":"'Has been' is singular and clashes with the nearer plural subject 'employees'.","c":"'Was' is singular and would be correct only if the nearer subject were singular, which it is not here.","d":"'Is' is singular present and ignores the proximity rule, which requires agreement with the plural 'employees'."}}],"diagnostic-1":[{"q":"A researcher rejects the null hypothesis when it is actually true. This is:","concept":"In null hypothesis significance testing there are two possible mistakes. A Type I error (alpha) is a false positive: you reject the null hypothesis when it is actually true, concluding an effect exists when it does not. A Type II error (beta) is a false negative: you retain a false null. The probability of a Type I error is set by the significance level alpha (commonly 0.05). Rejecting a true null is by definition a Type I error.","traps":{"b":"A Type II (beta) error is the opposite mistake, failing to reject a null that is actually false, not rejecting a true one.","c":"Low statistical power is (1 minus beta) being small, which raises the risk of Type II errors, not the act of rejecting a true null.","d":"A confounding error refers to a third variable distorting the observed relationship, a design/validity issue, not the decision error described here."}},{"q":"Which test is appropriate for comparing the means of three or more independent groups?","concept":"When comparing means across three or more independent groups, the correct test is a one-way analysis of variance (ANOVA), which uses the F-ratio (between-group variance over within-group variance). Running multiple t-tests instead inflates the family-wise Type I error rate, which is exactly why ANOVA exists. A single t-test can only compare two group means.","traps":{"a":"An independent samples t-test compares the means of only two independent groups, not three or more.","c":"Pearson correlation measures the linear association between two continuous variables, it does not compare group means.","d":"A chi-square test analyses associations between categorical (frequency) variables, not differences between group means."}},{"q":"The non-parametric equivalent of the independent-samples t-test is the:","concept":"Non-parametric tests are used when data violate the assumptions of parametric tests (e.g., non-normal, ordinal). The Mann-Whitney U test is the non-parametric equivalent of the independent-samples t-test: it compares two independent groups by ranking all scores together. Its paired counterpart is the Wilcoxon signed-rank test.","traps":{"a":"The Wilcoxon signed-rank test is the non-parametric equivalent of the paired (related) t-test, not the independent-samples t-test.","b":"The Kruskal-Wallis test is the non-parametric equivalent of one-way ANOVA (three or more independent groups).","d":"The Friedman test is the non-parametric equivalent of repeated-measures ANOVA (three or more related conditions)."}},{"q":"Cronbach's alpha is a measure of:","concept":"Cronbach's alpha, developed by Lee Cronbach, is an index of internal consistency reliability: it estimates how strongly the items of a scale intercorrelate, that is, whether they measure the same underlying construct. Values range from 0 to 1, with roughly 0.70 and above considered acceptable. It is a reliability index, not a validity index.","traps":{"a":"Criterion validity concerns how well a test predicts or agrees with an external criterion, a form of validity, not internal consistency.","b":"Test-retest reliability assesses stability of scores over time using two administrations, whereas alpha is computed from a single administration.","c":"Inter-rater reliability measures agreement between different raters/observers, not the internal coherence of items."}},{"q":"A correlation coefficient of −0.85 indicates:","concept":"A correlation coefficient conveys two things: its sign shows direction and its absolute value (closeness to 1) shows strength. A value of minus 0.85 has a negative sign (as one variable rises, the other falls) and a large magnitude, so it indicates a strong negative relationship. Strength is unrelated to the sign.","traps":{"b":"A weak negative relationship would be a small magnitude such as minus 0.15 or minus 0.20, not minus 0.85.","c":"No relationship corresponds to a coefficient near 0, whereas minus 0.85 is far from zero.","d":"A strong positive relationship would require a positive sign (e.g., plus 0.85); the minus sign here makes it negative."}},{"q":"Which of the following is a PROBABILITY sampling method?","concept":"Probability (random) sampling means every element has a known, non-zero chance of selection, which supports generalisation and estimation of sampling error. Stratified random sampling divides the population into strata and randomly samples within each, so it is a probability method. Quota, purposive, and snowball are non-probability methods that rely on the researcher's judgement or convenience.","traps":{"a":"Quota sampling fills preset category quotas using non-random selection, making it a non-probability method.","c":"Purposive (judgemental) sampling deliberately selects cases the researcher deems informative, so selection probabilities are unknown.","d":"Snowball sampling recruits participants through referrals from existing ones, a non-probability chain-referral technique."}},{"q":"For a one-sample t-test with a sample size of 20, the degrees of freedom are:","concept":"Degrees of freedom (df) are the number of values free to vary when estimating a parameter. For a one-sample t-test, one parameter (the mean) is estimated, so df equals n minus 1. With n equal to 20, df equals 19.","traps":{"a":"20 is the sample size n itself, not df; df subtracts one for the estimated mean.","b":"18 would be n minus 2, the df formula for an independent two-sample situation per adjustment, not a one-sample t-test.","d":"21 is n plus 1, which reverses the correct subtraction."}},{"q":"Which of the following is a PROJECTIVE test of personality?","concept":"Projective tests present ambiguous stimuli and assume the person 'projects' unconscious needs and conflicts onto them. The Thematic Apperception Test (TAT), developed by Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan, asks the person to tell stories about ambiguous picture cards, so it is projective. Objective inventories instead use structured, self-report items with fixed response formats.","traps":{"a":"The 16PF is Raymond Cattell's objective self-report personality inventory measuring 16 traits, not a projective test.","b":"The MMPI-2 is an objective true/false self-report inventory of psychopathology, not projective.","c":"The NEO-PI-R is an objective self-report questionnaire measuring the Big Five traits, not projective."}},{"q":"A T-score has a mean and standard deviation of:","concept":"Standardised scores rescale raw scores to a fixed mean and standard deviation. A T-score has a mean of 50 and a standard deviation of 10, so a T-score of 60 lies one SD above the mean. This distinguishes it from z-scores and deviation IQ scores.","traps":{"b":"Mean 100, SD 15 describes the deviation IQ metric (e.g., Wechsler scales), not a T-score.","c":"Mean 0, SD 1 describes the z-score (standard score) distribution, not a T-score.","d":"Mean 500, SD 100 describes large-scale test scaling such as the SAT/GRE metric, not a T-score."}},{"q":"The MMPI-2 contains how many clinical scales?","concept":"The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory-2 (MMPI-2) contains 10 clinical scales (e.g., Hypochondriasis, Depression, Hysteria, Psychopathic Deviate, Paranoia, Schizophrenia), alongside a set of validity scales (such as L, F, and K) that detect response distortion. The number of clinical scales is 10.","traps":{"a":"4 does not match; the validity indicators are several, and the core clinical scales number 10.","c":"16 confuses the MMPI-2 with Cattell's 16PF, which measures 16 personality factors.","d":"13 is not the count; the standard MMPI-2 clinical scale set is 10."}},{"q":"Sten scores (range 1–10) are most associated with which test?","concept":"A sten ('standard ten') score divides the distribution into ten units, ranging from 1 to 10 with a mean of 5.5 and a standard deviation of 2. This scoring metric is the signature of Raymond Cattell's 16PF personality questionnaire.","traps":{"a":"Wechsler scales report deviation IQs (mean 100, SD 15) and scaled subtest scores (mean 10), not stens.","b":"The Stanford-Binet uses a deviation IQ metric, not the 1 to 10 sten scale.","d":"The Rorschach is a projective inkblot test scored by systems such as Exner's, not by stens."}},{"q":"Administering the same test to the same people on two occasions and correlating the scores assesses:","concept":"Reliability concerns consistency of measurement. Test-retest reliability specifically assesses the temporal stability of scores: the same test is given to the same people on two occasions and the two sets of scores are correlated. A high correlation indicates scores are stable over time.","traps":{"a":"Internal consistency (e.g., Cronbach's alpha) is computed from a single administration by examining item intercorrelations, not two occasions.","b":"Construct validity concerns whether a test measures the intended theoretical construct, a validity question, not consistency over time.","c":"Content validity concerns whether items adequately sample the domain, judged by expert review, not by re-testing."}},{"q":"Loosening of associations and 'knight's move' thinking are characteristic of:","concept":"Formal thought disorder is a disturbance in the form or flow of thought inferred from disorganised speech. Loosening of associations (ideas slipping onto unrelated tracks) and 'knight's move' thinking (illogical jumps between topics) are classic signs, seen most characteristically in schizophrenia. They reflect disorganised thinking rather than mood or anxiety pathology.","traps":{"b":"Major depressive disorder is a mood disorder marked by low mood and anhedonia, not by loosening of associations.","c":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder features intrusive obsessions and compulsions with generally intact thought form.","d":"Specific phobia involves marked fear of a specific object or situation, with no formal thought disorder."}},{"q":"Compared with DSM-IV-TR, the DSM-5 (2013):","concept":"The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, Fifth Edition (DSM-5, 2013) abolished the multiaxial system that DSM-IV-TR used. Former Axes I, II, and III (clinical disorders, personality disorders/intellectual disability, and medical conditions) were merged into a single list of diagnoses, Axis IV became separate notation for psychosocial and contextual factors, and Axis V (the Global Assessment of Functioning) was dropped.","traps":{"a":"DSM-5 did not add a sixth axis; it removed the axial system entirely.","c":"Retaining the five-axis system describes DSM-IV/DSM-IV-TR, not DSM-5.","d":"DSM-5 kept its own diagnostic criteria and did not replace diagnoses with ICD codes only, though it lists corresponding ICD codes."}},{"q":"Anhedonia refers to:","concept":"Anhedonia is the markedly diminished ability to experience interest or pleasure in activities that were previously enjoyable. It is one of the two cardinal (core) symptoms of a major depressive episode, alongside depressed mood, in the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual.","traps":{"a":"Reduced need for sleep is a feature of a manic episode, not the definition of anhedonia.","b":"Excessive fear of social situations describes social anxiety disorder, not anhedonia.","d":"Loss of contact with reality describes psychosis, a different construct from the loss of pleasure."}},{"q":"First-rank symptoms of schizophrenia were described by:","concept":"The First-Rank Symptoms of schizophrenia (e.g., audible thoughts, thought insertion/withdrawal/broadcast, delusional perception, passivity phenomena) were described by Kurt Schneider in 1959 as symptoms with strong (though not pathognomonic) diagnostic weight. Other theorists shaped the concept differently: Kraepelin and Bleuler defined the disorder, and Jaspers advanced its phenomenology.","traps":{"a":"Emil Kraepelin coined 'dementia praecox' and its subtypes, not the First-Rank Symptoms.","b":"Eugen Bleuler coined the term 'schizophrenia' and described the four A's (associations, affect, ambivalence, autism), not first-rank symptoms.","c":"Karl Jaspers pioneered descriptive psychopathology and phenomenology but did not formulate the first-rank symptoms."}},{"q":"The minimum duration of symptoms required to diagnose schizophrenia under DSM-5 is:","concept":"Under the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition (DSM-5), a schizophrenia diagnosis requires continuous signs of disturbance for at least 6 months in total (which may include prodromal or residual periods), of which at least 1 month must contain active-phase symptoms. This contrasts with ICD, which requires about 1 month.","traps":{"b":"1 month is the active-phase requirement within DSM-5 and the ICD threshold, not the total duration DSM-5 demands.","c":"1 week is too short and corresponds to no standard schizophrenia criterion (it is closer to a brief psychotic episode boundary).","d":"2 years is not required; it overstates the duration criterion for schizophrenia."}},{"q":"Which cluster of DSM-5 personality disorders is described as 'dramatic, emotional, erratic'?","concept":"The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual groups personality disorders into three clusters. Cluster B, described as dramatic, emotional, and erratic, comprises Antisocial, Borderline, Histrionic, and Narcissistic personality disorders. (Cluster A is odd/eccentric; Cluster C is anxious/fearful.)","traps":{"a":"Cluster A is the 'odd or eccentric' group (Paranoid, Schizoid, Schizotypal), not dramatic/emotional.","c":"Cluster C is the 'anxious or fearful' group (Avoidant, Dependent, Obsessive-Compulsive personality disorders).","d":"There is no Cluster D in the DSM classification; only clusters A, B, and C exist."}},{"q":"Unconditional positive regard, empathy, and congruence are the core conditions described by:","concept":"Carl Rogers, founder of Person-Centred (client-centred) therapy, specified three core conditions he called necessary and sufficient for therapeutic change: unconditional positive regard, empathic understanding, and congruence (genuineness). These describe the therapist's way of being with the client rather than specific techniques.","traps":{"a":"Aaron Beck developed cognitive therapy, focused on identifying and testing automatic thoughts and schemas, not the Rogerian core conditions.","b":"Albert Ellis developed Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy centred on disputing irrational beliefs, not unconditional positive regard.","d":"Fritz Perls developed Gestalt therapy, known for techniques like the empty chair, not the three core conditions."}},{"q":"Systematic desensitization, based on reciprocal inhibition, was developed by:","concept":"Systematic desensitization was developed by Joseph Wolpe. It rests on reciprocal inhibition, the principle that a response incompatible with anxiety (typically deep muscle relaxation) can inhibit anxiety. The client is gradually exposed to a hierarchy of feared stimuli while relaxed, so the feared cues lose their power.","traps":{"a":"B. F. Skinner developed operant conditioning (reinforcement and punishment), not systematic desensitization.","b":"John Watson pioneered behaviourism and the 'Little Albert' fear-conditioning study but did not create systematic desensitization.","c":"Ivan Pavlov discovered classical conditioning, which underlies the phenomenon, but the therapy itself was Wolpe's."}},{"q":"The 'empty chair' technique is most associated with:","concept":"The 'empty chair' (and 'two-chair') technique is a hallmark of Gestalt therapy, developed by Fritz Perls. The client speaks to an imagined person or a disowned part of the self seated in an empty chair, promoting present-moment awareness and integration of conflicting feelings.","traps":{"b":"Psychoanalysis uses free association, dream analysis, and transference interpretation, not the empty-chair technique.","c":"Cognitive therapy focuses on identifying and restructuring maladaptive thoughts, not empty-chair dialogue.","d":"Behaviour therapy uses techniques like exposure and reinforcement, not the Gestalt empty chair."}},{"q":"In Ellis's REBT, the 'B' in the ABC model stands for:","concept":"In Albert Ellis's Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy (REBT), the ABC model holds that emotional consequences arise not directly from events but from beliefs about them. A is the Activating event, B is the Belief (especially irrational beliefs), and C is the Consequence (emotional or behavioural). Ellis later added D (disputing the belief) and E (the new effect).","traps":{"a":"Behaviour is not what B stands for; the model targets beliefs as the mediator between event and consequence.","c":"Baseline is a measurement concept from behavioural assessment, not a component of the ABC model.","d":"Bias is a general cognitive term, not the specific 'B' (Belief) element in Ellis's model."}},{"q":"Transference and free association are central techniques of:","concept":"Free association (saying whatever comes to mind), dream analysis, transference (redirecting feelings from past figures onto the therapist), and interpretation are the central techniques of psychoanalytic therapy founded by Sigmund Freud. They aim to make unconscious conflicts conscious.","traps":{"a":"Behaviour therapy uses conditioning-based methods such as exposure and reinforcement, not free association or transference.","b":"Person-centred therapy relies on the therapist's core conditions (empathy, congruence, unconditional positive regard), not transference work.","d":"Reality therapy, developed by William Glasser, focuses on present choices and responsibility, not free association."}},{"q":"Dialectical Behaviour Therapy (DBT) was developed by Marsha Linehan primarily to treat:","concept":"Dialectical Behaviour Therapy (DBT) was developed by Marsha Linehan primarily to treat borderline personality disorder and chronic suicidal/self-harming behaviour. It blends cognitive-behavioural change strategies with acceptance and mindfulness, organised around modules of mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation, and interpersonal effectiveness.","traps":{"a":"Schizophrenia is treated mainly with antipsychotics and psychosocial rehabilitation, not DBT as its primary indication.","b":"Autism spectrum disorder is addressed with behavioural and developmental interventions, not DBT.","c":"Specific phobias are treated with exposure-based behaviour therapy, not DBT."}},{"q":"Object permanence, according to Piaget, first develops during the:","concept":"Object permanence is the understanding that objects continue to exist even when they cannot be seen. According to Jean Piaget, it first develops during the sensorimotor stage (roughly birth to 2 years), as infants gradually learn to represent absent objects, shown by searching for a hidden toy.","traps":{"b":"The preoperational stage (about 2 to 7 years) is marked by symbolic thought and egocentrism, but object permanence is already established before it.","c":"The concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11 years) brings conservation and logical operations on concrete material, well after object permanence.","d":"The formal operational stage (about 11 years onward) introduces abstract and hypothetical reasoning, long after object permanence emerges."}},{"q":"In Erikson's theory, the psychosocial crisis of adolescence is:","concept":"Erik Erikson proposed eight psychosocial stages, each defined by a central crisis. Adolescence corresponds to the fifth stage, Identity versus Role Confusion, in which the person works to form a coherent sense of self and future direction; failure leaves confusion about one's roles and values.","traps":{"a":"Trust versus mistrust is Erikson's first stage, occurring in infancy, not adolescence.","c":"Industry versus inferiority is the fourth stage, spanning the school-age years before adolescence.","d":"Intimacy versus isolation is the sixth stage, the crisis of young adulthood, not adolescence."}},{"q":"The 'Strange Situation' procedure for assessing attachment was devised by:","concept":"The 'Strange Situation' is a structured laboratory procedure devised by Mary Ainsworth to assess infant attachment through a series of separations and reunions with the caregiver. It classified infants into secure, anxious-avoidant, and anxious-resistant (ambivalent) patterns; a disorganised category was added later by Mary Main.","traps":{"a":"John Bowlby founded attachment theory and its ethological framework but did not design the Strange Situation procedure.","b":"Harry Harlow conducted the rhesus-monkey surrogate-mother studies on contact comfort, not the Strange Situation.","d":"Konrad Lorenz studied imprinting in geese, an ethological phenomenon distinct from the Strange Situation."}},{"q":"The 'Zone of Proximal Development' is a concept proposed by:","concept":"The Zone of Proximal Development (ZPD) was proposed by Lev Vygotsky. It is the gap between what a learner can accomplish independently and what they can achieve with guidance from a more knowledgeable other. Support tailored to this zone, later termed 'scaffolding', is gradually withdrawn as competence grows.","traps":{"a":"Jean Piaget emphasised individual, stage-based cognitive construction, not the socially mediated ZPD.","b":"Lawrence Kohlberg developed a theory of moral development stages, unrelated to the ZPD.","c":"Jerome Bruner popularised the term 'scaffolding' and modes of representation but did not originate the ZPD concept."}},{"q":"The tendency for individuals to be less likely to help when others are present is the:","concept":"The bystander effect is the finding that people are less likely to help a victim when other people are present, largely because responsibility is diffused across the group and each person assumes someone else will act. It was studied experimentally by John Darley and Bibb Latané following the Kitty Genovese case.","traps":{"b":"Social facilitation is the improvement of performance on simple tasks in the presence of others, not reduced helping.","c":"The halo effect is a bias where one positive trait colours overall impressions of a person, unrelated to helping behaviour.","d":"The just-world phenomenon is the belief that people get what they deserve, which can lead to victim-blaming, not diffusion of responsibility."}},{"q":"The theory of cognitive dissonance was proposed by:","concept":"The theory of cognitive dissonance was proposed by Leon Festinger in 1957. It holds that holding two inconsistent cognitions (beliefs, attitudes, or a belief and a behaviour) creates uncomfortable tension, which motivates the person to reduce it by changing an attitude, adding consonant beliefs, or minimising the inconsistency.","traps":{"a":"Stanley Milgram is known for the obedience-to-authority experiments, not cognitive dissonance.","c":"Solomon Asch is known for the conformity line-judgement experiments, not dissonance theory.","d":"Philip Zimbardo conducted the Stanford Prison Experiment on situational roles, not cognitive dissonance."}},{"q":"Overestimating dispositional causes and underestimating situational causes of others' behaviour is the:","concept":"The fundamental attribution error, described by Lee Ross (1977), is the tendency to explain other people's behaviour by their internal traits or personality (dispositional causes) while discounting the power of the situation they are in. If someone cuts you off in traffic, you decide they are rude rather than considering they might be rushing to a hospital. It reflects a systematic bias in how observers attribute the causes of others' actions.","traps":{"a":"Self-serving bias is taking personal credit for successes but blaming external factors for failures, which protects one's own self-esteem rather than judging others' behaviour.","b":"Actor-observer bias is the broader pattern that we attribute our OWN behaviour to the situation but others' behaviour to their disposition, whereas this question is specifically about the disposition-over-situation error for others.","d":"Confirmation bias is seeking or interpreting information so it confirms one's pre-existing beliefs, a reasoning bias unrelated to attributing causes of behaviour."}},{"q":"The classic line-judgment experiments demonstrating conformity to group pressure were conducted by:","concept":"Solomon Asch's line-judgment experiments (1951) had participants match the length of a line to comparison lines while surrounded by confederates who deliberately gave the same wrong answer. About one third of responses conformed to the incorrect majority, demonstrating normative social influence, the pressure to go along with a group to fit in even when one privately knows it is wrong.","traps":{"a":"Stanley Milgram is known for the obedience-to-authority studies (shock experiments), which measured compliance with an authority figure, not conformity to peers.","b":"Philip Zimbardo ran the Stanford Prison Experiment on how situational roles shape behaviour, not perceptual conformity.","c":"Muzafer Sherif used the autokinetic effect to study the formation of group norms in an ambiguous task and the Robbers Cave study of intergroup conflict, not the unambiguous line-judgment conformity paradigm."}},{"q":"Parkinson's disease is primarily associated with a deficiency of which neurotransmitter?","concept":"Parkinson's disease results primarily from the degeneration of dopamine-producing neurons in the substantia nigra, which projects along the nigrostriatal pathway to the basal ganglia. Loss of this dopamine disrupts the control of movement, producing the classic triad of resting tremor, rigidity, and bradykinesia. This is why treatment relies on the dopamine precursor levodopa.","traps":{"b":"Serotonin deficiency is more associated with depression and anxiety disorders, not the motor degeneration of Parkinson's disease.","c":"Acetylcholine loss is centrally implicated in Alzheimer's disease; in Parkinson's the relative excess of acetylcholine activity is a downstream consequence, not the primary deficiency.","d":"GABA is the brain's main inhibitory neurotransmitter and is linked to anxiety and seizure disorders, not the dopaminergic loss defining Parkinson's."}},{"q":"The 'rest and digest' functions of the body are governed by the:","concept":"The parasympathetic nervous system is the branch of the autonomic nervous system responsible for 'rest and digest' functions: it slows the heart rate, stimulates digestion, and conserves the body's energy when it is at ease. It works in opposition to the sympathetic system, which mobilizes the body for 'fight or flight'. Both are subdivisions of the autonomic nervous system.","traps":{"a":"The sympathetic nervous system does the opposite, mobilizing the body for 'fight or flight' by raising heart rate and releasing adrenaline.","c":"The somatic nervous system controls voluntary skeletal muscle movement and carries sensory information, not the involuntary visceral 'rest and digest' functions.","d":"The central nervous system is the brain and spinal cord as a whole; 'rest and digest' is governed specifically by an autonomic (peripheral) branch, not the CNS at large."}},{"q":"Damage to Broca's area typically results in:","concept":"Broca's area lies in the inferior frontal gyrus of the (usually left) frontal lobe and is critical for speech production. Damage causes Broca's (expressive/non-fluent) aphasia: speech is slow, effortful, and agrammatic (missing small connecting words), yet the person can still largely understand what is said to them. The dissociation between poor output and preserved comprehension is the diagnostic clue.","traps":{"a":"Fluent speech with poor comprehension describes Wernicke's (receptive) aphasia from temporal-lobe damage, the near-opposite of Broca's.","b":"Complete loss of hearing is a sensory/auditory deficit, not a language-production disorder produced by frontal-lobe damage.","d":"Inability to recognize faces is prosopagnosia, caused by damage to the fusiform face area in the occipitotemporal region, unrelated to Broca's area."}},{"q":"The hippocampus is most critical for:","concept":"The hippocampus, in the medial temporal lobe, is essential for consolidating new declarative (episodic and semantic) long-term memories, converting short-term experiences into durable stored memories. The famous patient H.M., whose hippocampi were removed, could no longer form new long-term memories (anterograde amnesia) though older memories and skills survived. This shows the hippocampus builds new memories rather than storing all of them permanently.","traps":{"a":"Regulating body temperature is a function of the hypothalamus, not the hippocampus.","b":"Controlling balance and coordination is the role of the cerebellum.","c":"Producing speech is governed by Broca's area in the frontal lobe, not the hippocampus."}},{"q":"The working memory model with a phonological loop, visuospatial sketchpad, and central executive was proposed by:","concept":"Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch (1974) proposed the working memory model, replacing the passive short-term store with an active system. It comprises a central executive (an attentional controller) that directs two slave systems: the phonological loop for verbal and acoustic information and the visuospatial sketchpad for visual and spatial information; Baddeley later added the episodic buffer (2000).","traps":{"b":"Atkinson and Shiffrin (1968) proposed the multi-store (modal) model of sensory, short-term, and long-term memory, not the multi-component working memory model.","c":"Endel Tulving distinguished episodic from semantic long-term memory, a different contribution to memory theory.","d":"George Sperling studied the capacity and rapid decay of iconic (visual sensory) memory using partial-report experiments, not working memory structure."}},{"q":"When previously learned information interferes with the recall of newly learned information, it is called:","concept":"Proactive interference occurs when previously learned (older) information disrupts the recall of newly learned information; the learning moves forward in time to interfere. For example, an old phone number keeps intruding when you try to recall a new one. It contrasts with retroactive interference, where new learning disrupts the recall of old material.","traps":{"a":"Retroactive interference is the reverse: NEW learning interferes with the recall of OLD information (it works backward).","c":"Decay theory attributes forgetting to the fading of a memory trace over time through disuse, not to interference from other information.","d":"Encoding failure is forgetting because the information was never properly stored in the first place, not competition between two learned sets."}},{"q":"George Miller's classic estimate of the capacity of short-term memory is:","concept":"George Miller's 1956 paper 'The Magical Number Seven, Plus or Minus Two' estimated that short-term memory can hold about 7 plus or minus 2 (roughly 5 to 9) items at once. Crucially, the unit of capacity is the 'chunk', a meaningful grouping, so chunking can pack more raw information into each of those slots. This defines the limited capacity of short-term memory.","traps":{"a":"3 plus or minus 1 items is closer to Nelson Cowan's later, more conservative estimate of working memory capacity, not Miller's classic figure.","b":"12 plus or minus 3 items overstates short-term capacity well beyond any accepted estimate.","d":"Unlimited capacity describes long-term memory, not the sharply limited short-term store."}},{"q":"Negative reinforcement involves:","concept":"Negative reinforcement, an operant conditioning concept from B. F. Skinner, means removing or avoiding an aversive stimulus so that the behaviour that ended it becomes MORE likely to recur. Taking an aspirin to stop a headache reinforces pill-taking because the unpleasant pain is removed. 'Negative' refers to taking something away, not to punishment, and reinforcement always increases behaviour.","traps":{"a":"Presenting an aversive stimulus to decrease behaviour is positive punishment, not negative reinforcement.","b":"Removing a reward to decrease behaviour is negative punishment (for example a time-out or a fine).","c":"Presenting a reward to increase behaviour is positive reinforcement, which adds a pleasant stimulus rather than removing an aversive one."}},{"q":"The 'Little Albert' experiment, demonstrating the conditioning of a fear response, was conducted by:","concept":"John B. Watson, the founder of behaviourism, together with Rosalie Rayner conducted the 'Little Albert' experiment in 1920. They paired a white rat (a neutral stimulus) with a loud, frightening noise until the infant, Albert, came to fear the rat alone, demonstrating that emotional responses like fear can be classically conditioned. It applied Pavlovian principles to human emotion.","traps":{"b":"B. F. Skinner is associated with operant conditioning (the Skinner box and reinforcement), not the classical conditioning of Little Albert.","c":"Edward Thorndike formulated the law of effect using puzzle-box experiments with cats, a precursor to operant conditioning.","d":"Ivan Pavlov discovered classical conditioning with dogs and salivation; Watson applied Pavlov's principles to Little Albert, but Pavlov did not run the study."}},{"q":"Learning that occurs without reinforcement and is not demonstrated until there is an incentive (shown in Tolman's rat maze studies) is called:","concept":"Latent learning, demonstrated by Edward Tolman with rats in mazes, is learning that occurs without any reinforcement and remains hidden until an incentive gives the animal a reason to show it. Rats allowed to explore a maze with no reward later ran it quickly once food was introduced, revealing they had learned the layout all along. Tolman argued they had formed a cognitive map, challenging strict stimulus-response behaviourism.","traps":{"a":"Insight learning, studied by Wolfgang Kohler with chimpanzees, is the sudden 'aha' grasp of a solution, not learning that stays hidden until rewarded.","c":"Observational learning, associated with Albert Bandura, is acquiring behaviour by watching a model, not the unreinforced latent learning of Tolman's mazes.","d":"Vicarious learning refers to learning from the consequences experienced by others (a facet of Bandura's social learning), not from unrewarded personal exploration."}},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the Big Five (OCEAN) personality traits?","concept":"The Big Five, remembered by the acronym OCEAN, are the five broad dimensions of personality supported by factor analysis: Openness to experience, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness, and Neuroticism. Intelligence is a cognitive ability measured separately and is not one of these trait dimensions. The model is one of the most empirically robust frameworks in personality psychology.","traps":{"a":"Openness to experience is a genuine Big Five trait (the 'O' in OCEAN), so it cannot be the exception.","b":"Conscientiousness is a genuine Big Five trait (the 'C' in OCEAN).","d":"Neuroticism is a genuine Big Five trait (the 'N' in OCEAN), capturing emotional instability."}},{"q":"In Freud's structural model, the part of the personality that operates on the 'pleasure principle' is the:","concept":"In Sigmund Freud's structural model of personality, the id is the primitive, unconscious reservoir of drives that operates on the pleasure principle, demanding immediate gratification of instincts regardless of reality or morality. The ego mediates using the reality principle, and the superego supplies moral standards. The id is present from birth and is entirely unconscious.","traps":{"a":"The ego operates on the reality principle, negotiating between the id's demands and external constraints, not on the pleasure principle.","b":"The superego embodies morality and conscience (the internalized rules of parents and society), the opposite of the pleasure-seeking id.","c":"The preconscious is a level of awareness (material that can be brought to mind), not a structural component driven by the pleasure principle."}},{"q":"The Rehabilitation Council of India (RCI) was established as a statutory body under the RCI Act of:","concept":"The Rehabilitation Council of India was established as a statutory body under the Rehabilitation Council of India Act, 1992. The Act makes registration with the RCI mandatory in order to practise as a clinical psychologist or other rehabilitation professional, and it regulates training standards and maintains a Central Rehabilitation Register. Practising without registration is a punishable offence.","traps":{"b":"1987 is the year of the old Mental Health Act, not the RCI Act.","c":"2002 is not the founding year of the RCI; no core disability-professional statute of that year applies here.","d":"2016 is the year of the Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, a different piece of legislation from the RCI Act."}},{"q":"The concept of an 'Advance Directive' and a 'Nominated Representative' for mental healthcare in India comes from the:","concept":"The Mental Healthcare Act, 2017 introduced the rights-based provisions of an advance directive (letting a person state in advance how they wish to be treated if they lose capacity) and a nominated representative to act on their behalf. It also affirmed the right to access mental healthcare and effectively decriminalized attempted suicide. It replaced the older Mental Health Act, 1987.","traps":{"a":"The Mental Health Act, 1987 was the earlier, more custodial law that the 2017 Act repealed; it contained no advance directives or nominated representatives.","c":"The Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, 2016 addresses disability rights broadly, not the specific advance-directive and nominated-representative mechanisms of mental healthcare.","d":"The RCI Act, 1992 regulates the training and registration of rehabilitation professionals, not patients' advance directives."}},{"q":"The 'scientist-practitioner' model of clinical psychology training (Ph.D.) originated at the 1949 conference held at:","concept":"The scientist-practitioner (Boulder) model of clinical psychology training was formulated at the 1949 Boulder Conference in Boulder, Colorado. It holds that clinical psychologists should be trained both as researchers who generate science and as practitioners who apply it, and it is associated with the Ph.D. degree. A later 1973 Vail Conference produced the practitioner-scholar (Vail) model tied to the Psy.D.","traps":{"a":"Vail (1973) gave rise to the practitioner-scholar model and the Psy.D., not the 1949 scientist-practitioner model.","b":"Salt Lake City is a distractor location not associated with either founding training-model conference.","d":"Adelphi is linked to early professional (Psy.D.-style) training programs, not the 1949 Boulder scientist-practitioner conference."}},{"q":"The legal principle that a therapist has a 'duty to warn/protect' an identifiable third party from a client's threats stems from:","concept":"The duty to warn or protect an identifiable third party stems from Tarasoff v. Regents of the University of California (1976), following the killing of Tatiana Tarasoff. The court ruled that when a therapist determines a client poses a serious danger to an identifiable victim, the therapist has a duty to take reasonable steps to protect that person, creating a recognized limit to client confidentiality.","traps":{"a":"The Durham rule is an insanity-defence standard (the 'product' test), concerning criminal responsibility, not a therapist's duty to warn.","b":"The Daubert standard governs the admissibility of expert scientific testimony in court, unrelated to protecting third parties.","c":"The M'Naghten rule is the classic 'right from wrong' test for the insanity defence, again about criminal responsibility rather than confidentiality limits."}},{"q":"'Dhat syndrome', characterized by anxiety over the loss of semen, is a culture-bound syndrome reported in:","concept":"Dhat syndrome is a culture-bound syndrome reported in the Indian subcontinent (South Asia), in which men experience anxiety, fatigue, and somatic complaints that they attribute to the loss of semen (dhatu), believed to be a vital bodily essence. It illustrates how cultural beliefs shape the presentation of distress. It is recognized in cultural formulations of illness such as those in the ICD and DSM.","traps":{"b":"Malaysia is the setting for culture-bound syndromes such as amok and latah, not dhat.","c":"Latin America is associated with syndromes like susto and ataque de nervios, not semen-loss anxiety.","d":"The Arctic is linked to pibloktoq (Arctic hysteria) among Inuit populations, not dhat syndrome."}},{"q":"In India, the Act that provides for the protection of children from sexual offences is the POCSO Act of:","concept":"The Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (POCSO) Act was enacted in 2012 to protect children from sexual assault, harassment, and pornography. It is gender-neutral, defines a child as any person below 18 years of age, and provides for child-friendly procedures for reporting, recording evidence, and trial through Special Courts. It filled major gaps in earlier laws on child sexual abuse.","traps":{"a":"2005 is the year of the Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, not POCSO.","c":"2009 is the year of the Right to Education Act, unrelated to child sexual offences.","d":"2015 is the year of the Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act, a distinct statute from POCSO."}},{"q":"In the Mental Status Examination (MSE), 'insight' refers to:","concept":"In the Mental Status Examination, 'insight' refers to the patient's awareness that they are ill and their understanding of the nature and cause of that illness. It is typically graded along a spectrum from complete denial of illness to intellectual and finally true emotional insight, and it has bearing on treatment adherence and prognosis. It is distinct from orientation, cognition, and perception, which are assessed separately.","traps":{"a":"Orientation to time, place, and person is a separate MSE component (part of the cognition/sensorium assessment), not insight.","b":"Serial subtraction (for example serial sevens) tests attention and concentration, a cognitive function distinct from insight.","d":"The presence of hallucinations falls under the perception section of the MSE, not insight."}},{"q":"The first psychological clinic and the term 'clinical psychology' are credited to:","concept":"Lightner Witmer founded the first psychological clinic at the University of Pennsylvania in 1896 and coined the term 'clinical psychology'. His clinic focused on assessing and helping children with learning and behavioural difficulties, marking the applied, individual-case beginnings of the discipline. He is therefore regarded as the founder of clinical psychology.","traps":{"a":"Sigmund Freud founded psychoanalysis, a theory and therapy of the unconscious, not the field of clinical psychology or its first clinic.","b":"Wilhelm Wundt founded the first experimental psychology laboratory (Leipzig, 1879), which was for research, not clinical service.","c":"G. Stanley Hall was a pioneer of developmental and educational psychology and the first APA president, but he did not coin 'clinical psychology'."}},{"q":"The founder of psychophysics, who studied the relationship between physical stimuli and sensations, is:","concept":"Gustav Fechner is regarded as the founder of psychophysics, the study of the relationship between physical stimuli and the sensations they produce. In his Elements of Psychophysics (1860) he built on Ernst Weber's findings to formulate the Weber-Fechner law, showing that sensation grows as the logarithm of stimulus intensity. This gave psychology one of its first quantitative methods.","traps":{"b":"Wilhelm Wundt founded the first psychology laboratory and structuralism-oriented experimental psychology, not psychophysics as a field.","c":"Ernst Weber discovered the just-noticeable-difference and Weber's law, providing the foundation Fechner extended, but Fechner is credited as the founder of psychophysics.","d":"Hermann Ebbinghaus pioneered the experimental study of memory and forgetting (the forgetting curve), not psychophysics."}},{"q":"The first experimental psychology laboratory (Leipzig, 1879) was established by:","concept":"Wilhelm Wundt established the first laboratory dedicated to experimental psychology at the University of Leipzig in 1879, an event conventionally treated as the birth of psychology as an independent scientific discipline. He used methods such as introspection to study conscious experience and trained many early psychologists. This separated psychology from philosophy and physiology.","traps":{"a":"William James was a founder of American psychology and functionalism, but he did not establish the first experimental laboratory at Leipzig.","c":"Edward Titchener was Wundt's student who brought structuralism to America; he did not found the original Leipzig lab.","d":"Sigmund Freud developed psychoanalysis clinically in Vienna and had no role in founding the first experimental psychology laboratory."}},{"q":"Functionalism, emphasizing the purpose of mental processes in adaptation, is most associated with:","concept":"Functionalism is the school of psychology, most associated with William James, that emphasized the purpose or function of mental processes in helping an organism adapt to its environment, drawing heavily on Charles Darwin's evolutionary thinking. Rather than dissecting consciousness into elements, it asked what mind and behaviour are for. James's 'Principles of Psychology' (1890) was its landmark text.","traps":{"a":"Wilhelm Wundt is linked to the experimental study of the structure of consciousness, not the adaptive-function emphasis of functionalism.","b":"Edward Titchener championed structuralism, which broke conscious experience into basic elements, the rival school to functionalism.","d":"John Watson founded behaviourism, which rejected the study of mental processes altogether in favour of observable behaviour."}},{"q":"The Gestalt principle stating that objects close together are perceived as a group is the law of:","concept":"The Gestalt law of proximity states that elements which are close together in space are perceived as belonging to the same group. It is one of the Gestalt grouping principles showing that the mind organizes perception into wholes rather than isolated parts, following the idea that 'the whole is different from the sum of its parts'. Spacing alone can create the impression of distinct clusters.","traps":{"a":"The law of similarity groups elements by shared features such as colour or shape, not by their nearness.","b":"The law of continuity (good continuation) makes us perceive smooth, continuous lines rather than abrupt breaks, not grouping by closeness.","c":"The law of closure fills in gaps so an incomplete figure is seen as whole, a different grouping principle from proximity."}},{"q":"When participants change their behaviour simply because they know they are being observed/studied, this is the:","concept":"The Hawthorne effect is the tendency for people to change or improve their behaviour simply because they know they are being observed or studied, regardless of the actual experimental manipulation. It is named after the Western Electric Hawthorne Works studies, where worker productivity rose with almost any change in conditions. It is an important threat to the internal validity of research.","traps":{"b":"The placebo effect is improvement caused by the expectation of treatment when receiving an inactive substance, not by being observed.","c":"The halo effect is letting one positive trait bias overall judgment of a person, a social-perception bias unrelated to being watched.","d":"The Pygmalion effect is when higher expectations from others (for example teachers) lead to improved performance, not a reaction to observation itself."}},{"q":"At the apex of Maslow's hierarchy of needs is:","concept":"At the apex of Abraham Maslow's hierarchy of needs, in the classic five-level pyramid, is self-actualization: the drive to realize one's full potential and become everything one is capable of becoming. Lower needs (physiological, safety, belongingness, esteem) must be reasonably met before this growth need is pursued. It represents the fulfilment of one's unique talents and purpose.","traps":{"a":"Esteem needs sit just below self-actualization in the pyramid, not at its apex.","c":"Safety needs are near the base of the hierarchy, second from the bottom, far below the top.","d":"Belongingness (love) needs occupy the middle of the hierarchy, below esteem and self-actualization."}},{"q":"The extent to which a test score predicts performance on a future criterion is called:","concept":"Predictive validity is the degree to which a test score forecasts performance on a FUTURE criterion measured later in time, for example an entrance exam predicting later academic success. It is one form of criterion-related validity. Its defining feature is the time gap between administering the test and collecting the outcome it is meant to predict.","traps":{"a":"Content validity is how well a test's items sample the full domain it is meant to cover, not its ability to forecast a future outcome.","b":"Concurrent validity correlates test scores with a criterion measured at the SAME time, not a future one.","d":"Face validity is merely whether a test appears to measure what it claims on the surface, the weakest and most superficial form of validity."}},{"q":"The two-factor theory of intelligence, proposing a general factor 'g' and specific factors 's', was given by:","concept":"Charles Spearman proposed the two-factor theory of intelligence, arguing that performance on any mental task reflects a general intelligence factor 'g' common to all tasks plus a specific factor 's' unique to that particular task. He derived this using factor analysis, a statistical technique he helped pioneer, after noticing that scores on diverse cognitive tests tend to correlate positively.","traps":{"a":"L. L. Thurstone rejected a single 'g' and proposed seven primary mental abilities instead of a two-factor structure.","b":"J. P. Guilford proposed the Structure of Intellect model with up to 120 or more distinct factors, not Spearman's two factors.","c":"Howard Gardner proposed the theory of multiple intelligences (linguistic, musical, spatial, and so on), a modern pluralistic view opposed to a single general factor."}}],"bio-testing-paper":[{"q":"The 12 cranial nerves in order from I to XII begin with which sensory nerve, the only one that does NOT pass through the thalamus before reaching its primary cortex?","concept":"The olfactory nerve (Cranial Nerve I) is the only sensory system whose afferents reach the cerebral cortex without first relaying in the thalamus: olfactory receptor axons synapse in the olfactory bulb, then project directly via the olfactory tract to the piriform (primary olfactory) cortex, amygdala, and entorhinal cortex. Every other sensory modality (vision, hearing, somatosensation, taste) synapses in a thalamic nucleus before reaching its primary cortex. This direct, phylogenetically old routing is why smell has such immediate links to emotion and memory.","traps":{"b":"The trigeminal nerve (V) carries facial somatosensation and does relay through the ventral posteromedial nucleus of the thalamus, so it is not the thalamus-bypassing pathway.","c":"The optic nerve (II) relays in the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus before reaching the primary visual cortex, so vision does pass through the thalamus.","d":"The vestibulocochlear nerve (VIII) sends auditory signals through the medial geniculate nucleus of the thalamus to the auditory cortex, so hearing is thalamically relayed."}},{"q":"The pyramidal (corticospinal) tract carries voluntary motor commands. Where does it decussate?","concept":"The pyramidal (corticospinal) tract carries voluntary motor commands from the motor cortex to lower motor neurons. About 80 to 90 percent of its fibres cross the midline at the pyramidal decussation in the lower (caudal) medulla, forming the lateral corticospinal tract that then descends on the contralateral side to synapse on anterior-horn motor neurons. This is why a lesion above the medulla produces contralateral weakness. The remaining 10 to 15 percent stay ipsilateral as the anterior corticospinal tract and decussate only at their spinal segmental level.","traps":{"a":"The third ventricle is a fluid-filled cavity in the diencephalon, not a site of motor fibre crossing; no tract decussates there.","b":"Claiming no decussation contradicts the defining feature of the corticospinal system and would wrongly predict ipsilateral rather than contralateral hemiplegia after a cortical lesion.","c":"The mid-pons is where corticopontine fibres synapse en route to the cerebellum, not where the corticospinal tract crosses; the crossing is lower, in the medulla."}},{"q":"The direct pathway through the basal ganglia is most accurately described as:","concept":"The direct pathway of the basal ganglia facilitates movement. Cortex excites striatal neurons bearing D1 receptors, which inhibit the internal globus pallidus and substantia nigra pars reticulata (GPi/SNr); because GPi/SNr normally inhibit the thalamus, this inhibition disinhibits the thalamus, which then drives the cortex and promotes movement. Dopamine from the substantia nigra pars compacta reinforces this by exciting the D1 direct pathway while inhibiting the D2 indirect pathway. Loss of dopamine impairs the direct pathway (Parkinsonian hypokinesia); overactivity produces hyperkinetic chorea.","traps":{"a":"This describes the indirect pathway (D2 receptors, external GP, subthalamic nucleus) whose net effect inhibits movement, not the movement-facilitating direct pathway.","c":"Hippocampus to fornix to thalamus is the Papez memory circuit, not a basal ganglia motor loop.","d":"The cortico-cerebellar loop coordinates movement but always relays through the thalamus back to cortex, so 'no thalamic relay' is wrong and it is not the basal ganglia direct pathway."}},{"q":"MAO-A and MAO-B enzymes principally metabolise:","concept":"Monoamine oxidase (MAO) enzymes catabolise monoamine neurotransmitters. MAO-A preferentially degrades serotonin, norepinephrine, dopamine, and dietary tyramine, and predominates in the gut and liver; MAO-B preferentially breaks down dopamine and phenylethylamine and predominates in the brain. This is why monoamine oxidase inhibitors raise monoamine levels and act as antidepressants, why selective MAO-B inhibitors (selegiline, rasagiline) help Parkinson's, and why tyramine plus a non-selective MAOI can trigger a hypertensive crisis.","traps":{"b":"GABA is inactivated mainly by GABA transaminase and reuptake, not by monoamine oxidase, since it is an amino-acid transmitter, not a monoamine.","c":"Glutamate and glycine are amino-acid transmitters cleared by transporters and other enzymes, not substrates of MAO.","d":"Acetylcholine is broken down by acetylcholinesterase, an entirely different enzyme from monoamine oxidase."}},{"q":"Astrocytes perform all of the following functions EXCEPT:","concept":"Astrocytes are the principal supporting glia of the central nervous system, but they do not conduct action propagating action potentials; they lack the density of propagating voltage-gated sodium channels needed and instead signal slowly via calcium waves. Their genuine roles include maintaining the blood-brain barrier through perivascular end-feet, buffering extracellular potassium, taking up and recycling glutamate through the glutamate-glutamine cycle, providing metabolic substrates, and forming glial scars. Conducting action potentials is the job of neurons.","traps":{"a":"Recycling glutamate via the glutamate-glutamine cycle is a genuine astrocyte function, so it cannot be the exception.","b":"Maintaining the blood-brain barrier with end-feet on capillaries is a real astrocyte role, so it is not the exception.","d":"Potassium buffering and supplying metabolic substrates to neurons are established astrocyte functions, so this is not the exception."}},{"q":"Microglia function chiefly as:","concept":"Microglia are the resident macrophages and immune cells of the central nervous system, derived from yolk-sac progenitors rather than neural tissue. They perform immune surveillance, phagocytose debris and pathogens, release cytokines, and prune synapses (especially during development). Activated microglia drive neuroinflammation and are implicated in Alzheimer's disease, multiple sclerosis, and chronic pain. Myelination is handled by oligodendrocytes in the CNS and Schwann cells in the periphery.","traps":{"a":"Myelin in the CNS is made by oligodendrocytes, not microglia, so this describes the wrong glial cell.","b":"Potassium buffering around neurons is a hallmark astrocyte function, not a microglial one.","d":"Sensing voltage is a property of neuronal ion channels, not a function of microglia."}},{"q":"Mirror neurons, first described in macaque ventral premotor cortex (area F5), fire when:","concept":"Mirror neurons, first described by Rizzolatti, Gallese, di Pellegrino and colleagues in macaque ventral premotor cortex (area F5) in the 1990s, fire both when the animal performs a goal-directed action and when it observes another individual performing the same action. This dual firing has been proposed as a neural substrate for action understanding, imitation, and empathy, with a putative human mirror system in the inferior frontal gyrus, inferior parietal lobule, and superior temporal sulcus. The correct option captures this defining execution-plus-observation property.","traps":{"b":"Firing only during self-executed action describes ordinary motor neurons, missing the observation component that defines mirror neurons.","c":"Firing only during observation describes purely visual or perceptual neurons, missing the execution component of mirror neurons.","d":"Restricting activity to REM sleep is unrelated to mirror neurons, which are defined by waking action and observation."}},{"q":"The locus coeruleus is the principal source of which neurotransmitter in the CNS?","concept":"The locus coeruleus, a small pigmented nucleus in the dorsal pons near the fourth ventricle, is the principal source of norepinephrine in the central nervous system, sending widespread projections to the cortex, hippocampus, and cerebellum. It governs arousal, attention, and the stress response. Knowing the other principal sources helps: the ventral tegmental area and substantia nigra supply dopamine, the raphe nuclei supply serotonin, and the nucleus basalis of Meynert and medial septum supply acetylcholine.","traps":{"b":"Serotonin originates from the raphe nuclei along the brainstem midline, not from the locus coeruleus.","c":"Acetylcholine in the CNS comes chiefly from the nucleus basalis of Meynert and medial septum, not the locus coeruleus.","d":"Dopamine arises mainly from the ventral tegmental area and substantia nigra pars compacta, not the locus coeruleus."}},{"q":"The raphe nuclei are arranged along which axis and are the major source of:","concept":"The raphe nuclei are clusters of serotonergic neurons arranged along the midline of the brainstem and are the major source of serotonin (5-HT) projections in the brain; the rostral group projects to the forebrain while the caudal group projects to the spinal cord. They modulate mood, sleep, appetite, and pain, and the dorsal raphe is the largest of them. Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors act downstream of these projections at serotonergic synapses.","traps":{"a":"The cerebellum and acetylcholine have nothing to do with the raphe nuclei, which are brainstem serotonergic clusters.","b":"The raphe nuclei lie along the brainstem, not the spinal cord, and release serotonin rather than dopamine.","d":"Oxytocin comes from hypothalamic neurons (paraventricular and supraoptic nuclei), not from the raphe nuclei."}},{"q":"The hypothalamus connects to the anterior pituitary primarily via:","concept":"The anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis) is controlled by releasing and inhibiting hormones that the hypothalamus secretes into the hypothalamo-hypophyseal portal venous system at the median eminence; these hormones (including CRH, TRH, GnRH, GHRH, somatostatin, and dopamine) travel through blood to reach the gland. This vascular link contrasts with the posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis), which receives direct axons from the paraventricular and supraoptic nuclei that release oxytocin and antidiuretic hormone straight into the circulation.","traps":{"a":"Cerebrospinal fluid is not the route by which hypothalamic hormones reach the anterior pituitary; a dedicated portal blood system is.","b":"Direct neural axons connect the hypothalamus to the posterior pituitary, not the anterior pituitary, which is controlled hormonally.","d":"The vagus nerve is a parasympathetic nerve to the viscera and has no role in anterior pituitary control."}},{"q":"Pre-ganglionic sympathetic neurons originate from spinal cord segments:","concept":"Preganglionic sympathetic neurons arise from the thoracolumbar spinal cord, specifically segments T1 to L2, where their cell bodies sit in the intermediolateral cell column. Sympathetic preganglionic fibres are relatively short (synapsing in nearby sympathetic chain ganglia) with long postganglionic fibres. This contrasts with the parasympathetic (craniosacral) outflow from cranial nerves III, VII, IX, and X plus sacral segments S2 to S4. The adrenal medulla is a modified sympathetic ganglion releasing epinephrine into the blood.","traps":{"a":"Cervical levels contain no lateral horn for sympathetic outflow; the sympathetic origin is thoracolumbar (T1 to L2).","c":"Cranial nerves carry parasympathetic (and other) fibres, not the sympathetic preganglionic outflow.","d":"S2 to S4 is the sacral parasympathetic outflow, not the sympathetic origin."}},{"q":"The somatosensory homunculus on the postcentral gyrus shows that the largest cortical representation belongs to:","concept":"Penfield's somatosensory homunculus on the postcentral gyrus is grossly distorted: body parts with dense sensory receptors and fine discriminative ability, namely the hand, lips, and tongue, occupy disproportionately large cortical territory, while the trunk and proximal limbs occupy small areas. This cortical magnification reflects receptor density and functional importance rather than physical skin area, which is why our fingertips and mouth are so sensitive.","traps":{"a":"The trunk and back have sparse sensory innervation and therefore a small, not large, cortical representation.","b":"The knee and hip are proximal, low-acuity regions with modest cortical territory.","c":"The dorsum of the foot has low receptor density and only a small cortical area, unlike the hand and face."}},{"q":"Parvocellular vs magnocellular layers of the LGN differ in that:","concept":"The lateral geniculate nucleus has six layers with a functional division of labour. Magnocellular layers (1 and 2) contain large cells that are fast-conducting, achromatic, and specialised for motion and contrast, feeding the dorsal 'where/how' stream. Parvocellular layers (3 to 6) contain small cells that are slower, colour-sensitive (red-green), and specialised for fine detail, feeding the ventral 'what' stream. Koniocellular layers between them handle blue-yellow signals. The correct option pairs each layer set with its true properties.","traps":{"a":"This reverses the roles: magnocellular handles motion and contrast, while parvocellular handles colour and fine detail.","b":"Magnocellular layers feed the dorsal motion stream, not exclusively area V4, which is a colour and form region of the ventral stream.","c":"The two layer types carry distinctly different information (motion versus colour and detail), so they are not identical."}},{"q":"Cochlear hair cells transduce sound waves into neural signals primarily via:","concept":"Cochlear hair cells transduce sound by mechanotransduction, not by chemical or purely electrical triggering. As the basilar membrane moves, the stereocilia of inner hair cells bend; this bending pulls tip-links that mechanically open cation channels, allowing potassium to flow in from the potassium-rich endolymph, which depolarises the cell and triggers glutamate release onto spiral ganglion fibres of cranial nerve VIII. Outer hair cells amplify the signal via the motor protein prestin, and frequency is coded tonotopically (high frequencies at the base, low at the apex).","traps":{"a":"Transduction opens cation channels via mechanical bending; it is not driven by closure of voltage-gated sodium channels.","b":"GABA is not the trigger; the depolarising event is potassium influx through mechanically gated channels, and hair cells release glutamate.","d":"Olfactory chemical signalling is an entirely different modality and has nothing to do with cochlear mechanotransduction."}},{"q":"The vestibular system's semicircular canals detect:","concept":"The three semicircular canals (anterior, posterior, and lateral) of the vestibular system detect rotational, that is angular, acceleration of the head in three planes: endolymph movement deflects the cupula within the ampulla, bending hair cells. This is distinct from the otolith organs (utricle and saccule), which detect linear acceleration and head tilt via otoconia. Vestibular signals travel through cranial nerve VIII to the vestibular nuclei, driving the vestibulo-ocular reflex and postural control.","traps":{"a":"Static head position and tilt are sensed by the otolith organs (utricle and saccule), not the semicircular canals.","c":"Auditory pitch is coded by the cochlea, not by the vestibular semicircular canals.","d":"Linear acceleration is detected by the otolith organs; the semicircular canals detect angular, not linear, acceleration."}},{"q":"BDNF (brain-derived neurotrophic factor) is most strongly implicated in:","concept":"Brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF) is a neurotrophin that binds TrkB receptors and drives signalling supporting neuronal survival, dendritic growth, long-term potentiation, and hippocampal neurogenesis. It is central to the neurotrophic hypothesis of depression: BDNF is reduced in major depressive disorder, and selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors, electroconvulsive therapy, exercise, and ketamine all raise it. The Val66Met polymorphism, which affects activity-dependent BDNF secretion, is studied as a risk variant for depression and cognitive outcomes.","traps":{"b":"Firing individual action potentials is a fast electrical event mediated by ion channels, not the trophic, plasticity-related role of BDNF.","c":"BDNF promotes survival and growth rather than being an 'apoptosis only' factor, so this is the opposite of its main action.","d":"Myelin synthesis is carried out by oligodendrocytes and is not the defining function of BDNF."}},{"q":"Long-term depression (LTD) at hippocampal synapses differs from LTP in that LTD involves:","concept":"Long-term depression (LTD) is the mirror image of long-term potentiation and weakens synaptic strength. At hippocampal synapses, brief low-frequency stimulation produces a smaller but more prolonged rise in postsynaptic calcium, which favours protein phosphatases (PP1 and PP2A) over kinases, leading to removal of AMPA receptors from the membrane and a sustained decrease in synaptic strength. Together LTP and LTD allow bidirectional, tunable plasticity; a distinct cerebellar LTD at climbing-fibre to Purkinje-cell synapses underlies motor learning.","traps":{"a":"LTD weakens a synapse but does not kill the cell; it is a reversible change in synaptic strength, not permanent cell death.","b":"Glutamate release does have a postsynaptic effect in LTD, namely a modest calcium rise that triggers AMPA receptor removal.","d":"High-frequency stimulation with NMDA blockade would prevent plasticity, whereas LTD is induced by low-frequency stimulation that still admits calcium through NMDA receptors."}},{"q":"The P300 event-related potential is most associated with:","concept":"The P300 (or P3) is a positive event-related potential appearing about 300 milliseconds after an infrequent target stimulus in the oddball paradigm, reflecting attention allocation, stimulus categorisation, and context updating. Its amplitude is reduced and its latency prolonged in schizophrenia and Alzheimer's disease, and it is studied as an endophenotype. Other components differ: N400 indexes semantic incongruence, P600 indexes syntactic violations, and mismatch negativity reflects pre-attentive auditory change detection.","traps":{"a":"Motor command generation relates to the readiness potential (Bereitschaftspotential), not the P300.","b":"REM sleep onset is not indexed by the P300, which is a waking attentional response to oddball stimuli.","c":"The initial visual cortex response is an early sensory component (such as P1 or C1), far earlier and different from the cognitive P300."}},{"q":"Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome is most directly caused by:","concept":"Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome results directly from thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency, classically in chronic alcohol use. Wernicke's encephalopathy is the acute phase with the triad of confusion, ophthalmoplegia, and ataxia; Korsakoff's is the chronic phase with anterograde amnesia, confabulation, and apathy, reflecting damage to the mammillary bodies and thalamus. Treatment is intravenous thiamine given before glucose, because administering glucose first can precipitate or worsen the syndrome.","traps":{"a":"Hypoxia causes diffuse ischaemic brain injury, not the specific thiamine-dependent mammillary and thalamic pathology of this syndrome.","c":"Lead poisoning causes peripheral neuropathy and encephalopathy but is not the cause of Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome.","d":"Vitamin B12 deficiency causes subacute combined degeneration and megaloblastic anaemia, a different deficiency from the thiamine loss that causes this syndrome."}},{"q":"Typical (first-generation) antipsychotics primarily exert clinical effects via blockade of which receptors, and which side-effect cluster results?","concept":"Typical (first-generation) antipsychotics such as haloperidol, chlorpromazine, and fluphenazine exert their antipsychotic effect mainly by blocking dopamine D2 receptors. Blockade in the mesolimbic pathway reduces positive symptoms, but blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway produces extrapyramidal symptoms (acute dystonia, akathisia, parkinsonism, and tardive dyskinesia), and blockade in the tuberoinfundibular pathway raises prolactin. Atypicals add serotonin 5-HT2A blockade (the Meltzer hypothesis), reducing extrapyramidal symptoms but increasing metabolic side effects.","traps":{"a":"5-HT2A blockade and metabolic syndrome characterise atypical antipsychotics, not the D2-driven typicals in question.","b":"NMDA glutamate involvement relates to models of psychosis and drugs like ketamine, not the mechanism of typical antipsychotics.","c":"GABA-A modulation with sedation describes benzodiazepines, not the dopamine-blocking action of typical antipsychotics."}},{"q":"The Yerkes-Dodson law describes the relationship between:","concept":"The Yerkes-Dodson law (Robert Yerkes and John Dillingham Dodson, 1908) describes the relationship between arousal and task performance as an inverted-U: performance improves as arousal rises to a moderate optimum and then declines with further arousal. Crucially, the optimum is lower for difficult or novel tasks and higher for simple, well-learned ones, which is why a little anxiety can help but too much impairs. Related ideas include the Easterbrook cue-utilisation hypothesis of attentional narrowing under arousal.","traps":{"b":"The relationship between IQ and emotional intelligence is a separate individual-differences question, not the Yerkes-Dodson arousal law.","c":"Salary and job satisfaction is an organisational-psychology topic unrelated to the arousal-performance curve.","d":"Punishment severity and learning rate concerns reinforcement and conditioning, not the inverted-U of arousal and performance."}},{"q":"The 'two-streams' (dorsal/ventral) visual processing hypothesis (Ungerleider & Mishkin, 1982) proposes:","concept":"The two-streams hypothesis of Leslie Ungerleider and Mortimer Mishkin (1982) holds that after the primary visual cortex, visual information splits into two pathways: a dorsal stream running to the parietal lobe that handles 'where' and 'how' (spatial location and action guidance), and a ventral stream running to the inferior temporal lobe that handles 'what' (object recognition, including faces and places). Milner and Goodale later reframed this as an action versus perception distinction, supported by the double dissociation between optic ataxia (dorsal damage) and visual agnosia (ventral damage).","traps":{"a":"This reverses the mapping: the dorsal stream handles spatial and motion-related 'where/how' processing, while colour is a ventral-stream property.","b":"Both streams are visual; neither is auditory, so this mischaracterises the pathways entirely.","d":"The streams diverge after V1 rather than converging in it, so 'both converge in V1' is incorrect."}},{"q":"Tonic-clonic seizures involve:","concept":"A generalised tonic-clonic seizure (grand mal) involves loss of consciousness with widespread cortical hyperexcitation, unfolding as a tonic phase of generalised muscle stiffening (with apnoea and cyanosis, lasting about 10 to 30 seconds), followed by a clonic phase of rhythmic jerking (about 1 to 2 minutes), then post-ictal confusion. The EEG shows high-amplitude generalised spike-and-wave activity. Treatment options include valproate, levetiracetam, and lamotrigine.","traps":{"a":"A brief staring spell without loss of tone describes a typical absence seizure, not a tonic-clonic one.","c":"Motor symptoms confined to one limb with preserved awareness describe a simple partial (focal) seizure, not a generalised tonic-clonic event.","d":"A sleep-only seizure with no EEG correlate does not fit tonic-clonic seizures, which have a florid generalised spike-and-wave EEG signature."}},{"q":"Parkinson's disease pathology principally involves degeneration of:","concept":"Parkinson's disease is defined pathologically by degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta, with alpha-synuclein-containing Lewy body inclusions. Loss of nigrostriatal dopamine impairs facilitation of the basal ganglia direct pathway, producing bradykinesia, rigidity, a resting tremor (3 to 5 Hz), and postural instability. Treatment is symptomatic: L-DOPA combined with carbidopa (a peripheral DOPA decarboxylase inhibitor), dopamine agonists, and MAO-B inhibitors.","traps":{"a":"Degeneration of spinal motor neurons characterises amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, not Parkinson's disease.","b":"Loss of hippocampal pyramidal cells relates to amnestic and Alzheimer-type pathology, not the nigral degeneration of Parkinson's.","c":"Cholinergic basal forebrain neuron loss is a feature of Alzheimer's disease, not the dopaminergic loss of Parkinson's."}},{"q":"The amygdala receives a 'low road' input from the thalamus that bypasses the cortex (LeDoux). This pathway:","concept":"Joseph LeDoux's dual-route model of fear describes a fast subcortical 'low road' in which the sensory thalamus projects directly to the amygdala, bypassing the cortex, to permit rapid but crude detection of potential threat and quick defensive responses before conscious recognition. A slower 'high road' runs from the sensory thalamus through the cortex to the amygdala, providing more accurate identification. The low road's value is speed for survival-critical reactions, even at the cost of precision.","traps":{"a":"The low road provides only crude, fast threat signals, not the full detailed content of conscious vision, which depends on cortical processing.","c":"Voluntary motor output is generated by motor cortical and basal ganglia circuits, not by the thalamo-amygdala threat pathway.","d":"Slower, more accurate object identification is the function of the cortical 'high road', not the fast 'low road'."}},{"q":"Wakefulness is associated on EEG with which dominant frequencies?","concept":"On the EEG, alert wakefulness is dominated by beta activity (13 to 30 Hz), reflecting focused, active cognition, while relaxed wakefulness with the eyes closed shows posterior alpha rhythm (8 to 13 Hz) that blocks (attenuates) when the eyes open. As one moves through drowsiness and sleep the frequencies slow: theta (4 to 8 Hz) in N1 drowsiness, sleep spindles and K-complexes in N2, and high-amplitude delta (0.5 to 4 Hz) in N3 deep sleep. The correct option pairs both waking states with their true bands.","traps":{"b":"Theta alone characterises drowsiness and light N1 sleep, not the fully awake states.","c":"Sleep spindles are a signature of N2 sleep, not of wakefulness.","d":"Delta predominance marks deep N3 sleep, the opposite of alert wakefulness."}},{"q":"Cannabinoid CB1 receptors are most densely concentrated in:","concept":"Cannabinoid CB1 receptors are the most abundant G-protein-coupled receptors in the brain, densely expressed in the hippocampus (memory), basal ganglia and cerebellum (motor control), cortex, hypothalamus, and amygdala. Acting through Gi/o proteins, they reduce presynaptic neurotransmitter release, and their endogenous ligands are anandamide and 2-arachidonoylglycerol. This central distribution explains cannabis's effects on memory, movement, and reward. By contrast, CB2 receptors are found mainly on immune cells and microglia.","traps":{"a":"CB1 receptors are widespread in the brain, not confined to spinal motor neurons, so limiting them to the cord is wrong.","c":"Immune cells express mainly CB2, not CB1, so 'peripheral immune cells exclusively' misattributes the receptor.","d":"The adrenal cortex is not the dense CB1 site; CB1 concentrates in brain regions like the hippocampus and basal ganglia."}},{"q":"The hippocampal formation is critical for:","concept":"The hippocampal formation is critical for encoding and consolidating declarative memory, especially episodic and spatial memory (its place cells earned John O'Keefe a share of the 2014 Nobel Prize). Importantly, it is not the permanent storage site of remote memories: over time, according to the standard consolidation model, memories become dependent on distributed neocortical stores, though multiple-trace theory argues the hippocampus stays involved for detailed episodic recall. Procedural and motor memory instead depend on the striatum and cerebellum.","traps":{"b":"Procedural and motor memory rely on the striatum and cerebellum, not the hippocampus, which handles declarative memory.","c":"Olfactory transduction occurs in the olfactory epithelium and bulb, not the hippocampus.","d":"Voluntary motor execution is a function of the motor cortex and basal ganglia, not the hippocampal formation."}},{"q":"Multiple sclerosis is characterised by:","concept":"Multiple sclerosis is an autoimmune, T-cell-mediated demyelinating disease of central nervous system white matter, in which oligodendrocytes and their myelin are the target, producing lesions disseminated in time and space (the basis of the McDonald criteria). Typical lesions are periventricular, juxtacortical, infratentorial, and spinal, causing optic neuritis, paraesthesias, weakness, and ataxia; MRI shows T2 hyperintense plaques. Early on there is demyelination with relative axonal preservation, and treatment uses disease-modifying therapies such as interferon-beta, glatiramer, and monoclonal antibodies.","traps":{"a":"Alpha-synuclein deposits in the substantia nigra define Parkinson's disease, not the demyelinating pathology of multiple sclerosis.","c":"Cortical beta-amyloid plaques characterise Alzheimer's disease, not the white-matter demyelination of multiple sclerosis.","d":"Lower motor neuron degeneration describes disorders like amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, not the myelin-targeted autoimmunity of multiple sclerosis."}},{"q":"The Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (WCST) is most sensitive to dysfunction of which region?","concept":"The Wisconsin Card Sorting Test requires the subject to sort cards by an unstated rule (colour, shape, or number) and to shift sets when feedback signals the rule has changed. It is most sensitive to dorsolateral prefrontal cortex dysfunction, which produces perseverative errors (continuing to sort by the old rule), and it is widely used in schizophrenia, frontal lobe injury, and ADHD. For contrast, the Stroop taps the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex and anterior cingulate, the Iowa Gambling Task taps the orbitofrontal cortex, and the Tower of London taps planning.","traps":{"b":"The primary motor cortex governs movement execution, not the set-shifting and rule-updating measured by the card sorting test.","c":"The hippocampus supports declarative memory, whereas the card sorting test probes executive set-shifting.","d":"The cerebellum coordinates movement and some cognition, but the card sorting test's perseverative-error sensitivity localises to the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex."}},{"q":"The WAIS-IV Verbal Comprehension Index (VCI) consists of which core subtests?","concept":"On the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale, Fourth Edition (WAIS-IV), the Verbal Comprehension Index taps acquired verbal knowledge and reasoning, and its three core subtests are Similarities, Vocabulary, and Information (Comprehension is supplementary). Each of the four indexes draws on its own cluster of subtests, so the correct grouping is defined by which construct the index measures. VCI reflects crystallised verbal ability, distinct from perceptual reasoning, working memory, and processing speed.","traps":{"a":"Block Design, Matrix Reasoning, and Visual Puzzles are the core subtests of the Perceptual Reasoning Index (PRI), which measures nonverbal fluid reasoning, not verbal comprehension.","b":"Coding, Symbol Search, and Cancellation belong to the Processing Speed Index (PSI), tapping speed of simple visual scanning rather than verbal knowledge.","c":"Digit Span, Arithmetic, and Letter-Number Sequencing make up the Working Memory Index (WMI), which measures mental manipulation and attention, not verbal comprehension."}},{"q":"Raven's Progressive Matrices is considered a 'culture-fair' test because it measures:","concept":"Raven's Progressive Matrices, devised by John C. Raven in 1936, presents abstract visual patterns with a missing piece that the examinee completes by inferring the underlying rule. Because the task uses no words and minimal cultural content, it is regarded as a 'culture-fair' measure of fluid (nonverbal) reasoning, or Gf in Cattell-Horn-Carroll theory. It is one of the purest markers of general reasoning ability, though education and culture still exert some real-world influence.","traps":{"a":"Reading comprehension is a verbal, language-loaded skill; the whole point of Raven's is to avoid such content so it can be culture-fair.","c":"Crystallised verbal intelligence (Gc) reflects acquired knowledge and vocabulary, the opposite of the nonverbal fluid reasoning Raven's is designed to isolate.","d":"Memory alone is not what the matrices assess; the task requires active rule induction and pattern completion, not simple recall."}},{"q":"On the Stanford-Binet 5 (SB5), the five factors are:","concept":"The Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scales, Fifth Edition (SB5, Gale H. Roid, 2003), is organised around five Cattell-Horn-Carroll factors, each assessed in both a verbal and a nonverbal domain, giving ten subtests. The five factors are Fluid Reasoning, Knowledge, Quantitative Reasoning, Visual-Spatial Processing, and Working Memory. This yields a Full Scale IQ plus Verbal IQ, Nonverbal IQ, and factor index scores.","traps":{"a":"Sensory, Motor, Memory, Attention, and Language describe a neuropsychological domain breakdown, not the CHC factor structure of the SB5.","b":"The Verbal IQ / Performance IQ split belongs to older Wechsler editions (e.g. WAIS-III); the SB5 crosses verbal and nonverbal domains onto five factors instead.","d":"The Big Five personality factors measure personality, not intelligence, and have nothing to do with the SB5 cognitive structure."}},{"q":"The MMPI-2 clinical scale 'Pd' (Psychopathic Deviate, Scale 4) classically elevates in:","concept":"On the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory, Second Edition (MMPI-2), Scale 4, Psychopathic Deviate (Pd), was originally developed to identify social maladjustment and rebelliousness. High scorers characteristically show antisocial behaviour, conflict with family and authority, impulsivity, and a sense of alienation. It is not a mood or anxiety scale; it captures a behavioural-dyscontrol and rule-defiance profile.","traps":{"a":"Mania is indexed by Scale 9 (Ma, Hypomania), not by Scale 4; elevation on Pd is not exclusive to mania.","b":"Depression is captured by Scale 2 (D); Pd is not a depression-specific scale even though depressive features can co-occur.","d":"Obsessive-compulsive and anxiety features load on Scale 7 (Pt, Psychasthenia), not on the Psychopathic Deviate scale."}},{"q":"The MoCA (Montreal Cognitive Assessment) differs from the MMSE in that MoCA:","concept":"The Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA; Ziad Nasreddine, 2005) is a 30-point, roughly 10-minute cognitive screen designed to catch mild cognitive impairment that the Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE) often misses. It samples executive function (trail-making, alternating sequence), clock-drawing, abstraction, and delayed recall, giving it greater sensitivity to frontal-executive and early deficits. A common screening cut-off is a score of 25 or below out of 30, with an education adjustment.","traps":{"a":"The MoCA tests memory (delayed recall) plus multiple other domains; claiming it omits memory is simply false.","c":"The MoCA is a brief screen usable by trained clinicians, nurses, and general practitioners, not something restricted to neurologists.","d":"The MoCA and MMSE take comparable time (about 10 minutes each); the real distinction is sensitivity and executive-function coverage, not a 5-versus-30-minute gap."}},{"q":"The PANSS (Positive and Negative Syndrome Scale) is used to assess:","concept":"The Positive and Negative Syndrome Scale (PANSS; Stanley Kay, Abraham Fiszbein, and Lewis Opler, 1987) is a 30-item, clinician-rated instrument for grading symptom severity in schizophrenia. Items divide into a 7-item Positive subscale, a 7-item Negative subscale, and a 16-item General Psychopathology subscale, each rated 1 to 7. It is the standard outcome measure in schizophrenia trials, distinct from scales targeting mood, personality, or cognition.","traps":{"a":"Personality disorders are assessed with instruments like the SCID-5-PD or PCL-R, not the PANSS, which is schizophrenia-specific.","b":"Depression severity is rated by scales such as the Hamilton or Beck inventories; the PANSS measures psychotic and general psychopathology, not depression per se.","c":"Cognitive impairment is screened by tools like the MoCA or MMSE; the PANSS rates symptom severity, not cognitive status."}},{"q":"The Y-BOCS (Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale) rates:","concept":"The Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale (Y-BOCS; Wayne Goodman et al., 1989) is the gold-standard clinician-rated measure of OCD severity. It has 10 items, five rating obsessions and five rating compulsions, each scored 0 to 4 across the dimensions of time occupied, interference, distress, resistance, and control, for a total of 0 to 40. Crucially it rates severity independent of symptom content, which a preceding symptom checklist catalogues.","traps":{"a":"Suicidal ideation severity is measured by scales like the Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale, not the Y-BOCS, which is OCD-specific.","b":"Depression severity is captured by instruments such as the Beck Depression Inventory; the Y-BOCS does not rate mood.","c":"Hallucination frequency is a psychotic symptom rated on scales like the PANSS or SAPS, unrelated to the obsessive-compulsive focus of the Y-BOCS."}},{"q":"The PCL-R (Psychopathy Checklist–Revised) was developed by:","concept":"The Psychopathy Checklist-Revised (PCL-R) was developed by Robert D. Hare in 1991, building on Hervey Cleckley's clinical description of psychopathy in 'The Mask of Sanity' (1941). It comprises 20 items scored 0 to 2 by a trained clinician using a semi-structured interview plus file review, giving a maximum of 40, with 30 the conventional North American cut-off for psychopathy. It is a central tool in forensic risk assessment.","traps":{"a":"Hervey Cleckley (1941) provided the foundational clinical concept of psychopathy but did not create the PCL-R; Hare operationalised it decades later.","b":"Aaron T. Beck developed cognitive therapy and its depression and anxiety inventories, not a psychopathy checklist.","d":"Hans Eysenck built dimensional personality theory (the PEN model) and its questionnaires, not the PCL-R."}},{"q":"Cohen's kappa (κ) is used to estimate:","concept":"Cohen's kappa is a chance-corrected index of agreement between two raters classifying cases into nominal or categorical categories, computed as kappa = (Po minus Pe) divided by (1 minus Pe), where Po is observed and Pe is expected-by-chance agreement. It answers how reliably two raters assign the same category beyond what coincidence would produce. Landis and Koch benchmarks label values above 0.61 as substantial and above 0.81 as almost perfect.","traps":{"b":"Predictive validity concerns how well a test forecasts a future criterion, a validity question, not the inter-rater agreement kappa quantifies.","c":"Effect size for ANOVA is captured by statistics like eta-squared or Cohen's f, not by kappa.","d":"Internal consistency (how well items hang together) is measured by Cronbach's alpha or KR-20, not by a chance-corrected agreement coefficient."}},{"q":"Spearman-Brown prophecy formula predicts:","concept":"The Spearman-Brown prophecy formula predicts how a test's reliability changes when its length is altered by a factor k, via r_new = (k times r_old) divided by (1 plus (k minus 1) times r_old). Adding parallel items raises reliability with diminishing returns. Its classic use is correcting split-half reliability, estimating the full test's reliability from a single half, and planning how many items are needed to hit a target reliability.","traps":{"a":"The validity of a new test concerns its correlation with a criterion or construct, which the Spearman-Brown formula does not address; it deals with reliability and length.","b":"Effect size in ANCOVA is a separate statistical quantity; the formula makes no prediction about experimental effect sizes.","c":"The factor structure of the WAIS is a matter for factor analysis, not for a reliability-by-length formula."}},{"q":"Kuder-Richardson 20 (KR-20) differs from Cronbach's alpha in that KR-20:","concept":"Kuder-Richardson 20 (KR-20) is an internal-consistency reliability coefficient that applies specifically to dichotomously scored items, those marked right/wrong or yes/no. It is in fact the special case of Cronbach's alpha for two-category items; when items are polytomous (e.g. Likert scales) Cronbach's alpha generalises the same idea. KR-21 is a simplified variant assuming equal item difficulty.","traps":{"b":"KR-20 does estimate internal consistency; saying it cannot is the opposite of its purpose.","c":"KR-20 and Cronbach's alpha are not identical in scope: KR-20 handles only dichotomous items, whereas alpha extends to multi-point scoring.","d":"KR-20 is used for right/wrong dichotomous items (often ability tests), not exclusively for personality items."}},{"q":"Confirmatory Factor Analysis (CFA) differs from Exploratory Factor Analysis (EFA) primarily in that CFA:","concept":"Confirmatory Factor Analysis (CFA) tests a factor structure that the researcher specifies in advance, fixing which items load on which latent factors and then evaluating model fit with indices such as CFI, TLI, RMSEA, and SRMR. Exploratory Factor Analysis (EFA), by contrast, is data-driven: it discovers how many factors exist and how items cluster without an a priori model. CFA is the measurement component of structural equation modelling.","traps":{"a":"CFA does not always use oblique rotation; rotation is an EFA concern, and CFA instead constrains loadings within a specified model.","c":"CFA is not limited to dichotomous data; it handles continuous and ordinal indicators with appropriate estimators.","d":"Having no a priori model describes EFA, not CFA; CFA is defined precisely by testing a pre-specified model."}},{"q":"A Likert-type scale differs from a Thurstone scale in that Likert items are:","concept":"A Likert-type scale, introduced by Rensis Likert in 1932, asks respondents to rate their agreement with each statement on a symmetric continuum (for example 1 to 5 or 1 to 7), and the endorsed ratings are summed into a total score assuming roughly equal item weights. The key contrast with a Thurstone scale is that Thurstone items are pre-scaled by judges before administration, whereas Likert items are weighted equally and scaled by the respondents themselves.","traps":{"a":"Likert items are not dichotomous; they use a graded multi-point agree-disagree continuum, which is what distinguishes them.","c":"Pre-scaling of items by judges before administration is the defining feature of the Thurstone equal-appearing-intervals method, not the Likert method.","d":"Drawing responses from a sentence-completion stimulus describes a projective technique, not a Likert rating scale."}},{"q":"Q-sort methodology was developed by William Stephenson and Carl Rogers to:","concept":"Q-sort methodology, developed by William Stephenson and adopted by Carl Rogers, has a respondent sort a set of descriptive statements (such as self-descriptors) into a forced quasi-normal distribution from most to least characteristic. This captures idiographic, within-person structure rather than comparing one person against others. Rogers famously used it to measure the congruence between self-concept and ideal self before and after therapy.","traps":{"a":"Q-sort was not designed to replace the MMPI; it is a distinct idiographic sorting method, not a substitute clinical inventory.","c":"Infant reflex testing belongs to neurodevelopmental examination, entirely unrelated to Q-sort card sorting.","d":"Q-sort is not a diagnostic instrument for schizophrenia; it measures self-perception and within-person variation."}},{"q":"The Hawthorne effect refers to:","concept":"The Hawthorne effect, named after the Western Electric Hawthorne plant studies (Roethlisberger and Dickson, 1924 to 1932), is the tendency of people to change their behaviour, typically improving performance, simply because they know they are being observed or studied, regardless of the actual intervention. It is a form of reactivity or observer effect. Recognising it matters because apparent treatment gains may reflect being watched rather than the manipulation itself.","traps":{"a":"Demand characteristics arising from instructions are cues about the hypothesis (Orne's concept), distinct from the mere effect of being observed.","b":"Experimenters unconsciously biasing the data describes experimenter expectancy or the Rosenthal/Pygmalion effect, not the Hawthorne effect.","d":"The placebo effect is improvement from an inert treatment believed to be active, a different mechanism from reactivity to observation."}},{"q":"Demand characteristics, as described by Martin Orne, are:","concept":"Demand characteristics, described by Martin Orne in 1962, are the cues within an experimental setting, including instructions, the setting itself, rumours, and procedures, from which participants infer what the experimenter expects and then adjust their behaviour accordingly. They threaten internal validity by turning the participant into a hypothesis-guesser. Controls include blinding, unobtrusive measures, deception with debriefing, and post-experimental inquiry.","traps":{"a":"Demand characteristics are cues in the situation, not properties of the experimental hypothesis itself.","b":"Statistical demands of a test refer to assumptions or requirements of an analysis, not to situational cues that shape participant behaviour.","c":"The amount of effort an item requires is an item-difficulty or workload notion, unrelated to Orne's concept of situational expectancy cues."}},{"q":"A ceiling effect on a test means:","concept":"A ceiling effect occurs when so many examinees score at or near the maximum that the test can no longer discriminate among the highest performers, compressing variance at the top. Its mirror image, the floor effect, bunches low performers at the minimum. Both restrict range, lower reliability and validity for the affected end, and can mask genuine differences or intervention effects; the remedy is adding items of appropriate difficulty.","traps":{"b":"Items being too difficult overall would drive scores down toward the minimum (a floor effect), which is the opposite of a ceiling effect.","c":"Reliability cannot exceed 1, and a ceiling effect actually reduces reliability at the top rather than being about impossible reliability values.","d":"A normal distribution of scores implies good spread; a ceiling effect specifically produces a skewed pile-up at the maximum, not normality."}},{"q":"Convergent validity is demonstrated when:","concept":"Convergent validity is evidence for construct validity shown when a test correlates highly with other measures of the same construct, for example a depression scale correlating strongly with another depression measure. It is one half of construct validation (Cronbach and Meehl, 1955), paired with discriminant validity (low correlation with unrelated constructs). Campbell and Fiske's multitrait-multimethod matrix (1959) evaluates both at once.","traps":{"b":"Two raters agreeing on a score is inter-rater reliability, not convergent validity across measures of a construct.","c":"A test predicting future criteria is predictive (criterion) validity, a separate form of validity from convergent evidence.","d":"Failing to correlate with measures of unrelated constructs defines discriminant validity, the complement of convergent validity, not convergent validity itself."}},{"q":"Criterion contamination refers to:","concept":"Criterion contamination occurs when the predictor, or knowledge of the predictor's scores, influences the criterion measure, so that the observed predictor-criterion correlation is spuriously inflated. A classic case is a supervisor who knows employees' test scores and then rates their job performance partly on that knowledge. Remedies include blinding raters and using independent criteria; it differs from criterion deficiency, where the criterion omits relevant content.","traps":{"a":"Random measurement error in the criterion is unreliability, which attenuates (lowers) correlations, the opposite of the inflation criterion contamination causes.","b":"Replacing the criterion mid-study is a design change, not the overlap-driven artefactual inflation that defines contamination.","d":"A criterion with too few items concerns criterion reliability or breadth, not the predictor-into-criterion leakage that constitutes contamination."}},{"q":"A z-score of +1.5 on a normally distributed test corresponds to approximately which percentile?","concept":"On the standard normal distribution a z-score expresses how many standard deviations a score lies above or below the mean, and it maps directly onto a percentile via the cumulative normal curve. A z of +1.5 corresponds to roughly the 93rd percentile (about 93.3 percent of the area lies below it). Useful anchors are z = 0 at the 50th, z = +1 at the 84th, and z = +2 at about the 98th percentile.","traps":{"a":"The 99th percentile corresponds to about z = +2.33, well above +1.5.","b":"The 84th percentile corresponds to z = +1.0, not +1.5.","c":"The 50th percentile is z = 0 (the mean), far below a z of +1.5."}},{"q":"T-scores (as used on the MMPI, NEO-PI-R) have a mean and SD of:","concept":"T-scores are a standardised score scale with a mean of 50 and a standard deviation of 10, used on instruments such as the MMPI, the NEO-PI-R, and the ASEBA. Converting to a common metric lets scores across scales be compared, with values above 65 (1.5 SD) typically flagged as clinically elevated on the MMPI. This differs from the Wechsler IQ metric (mean 100, SD 15) and the z-score metric (mean 0, SD 1).","traps":{"a":"Mean 100, SD 15 is the deviation IQ scale (Wechsler, Stanford-Binet), not the T-score metric.","c":"Mean 500, SD 100 is the old SAT/GRE scaling, not the T-score.","d":"Mean 0, SD 1 is the z-score, the raw standard-normal metric from which T-scores are derived, not the T-score itself."}},{"q":"On the MMPI-2-RF, the RC (Restructured Clinical) scales differ from the original clinical scales in that RC scales:","concept":"On the MMPI-2-RF the Restructured Clinical (RC) scales, developed by Auke Tellegen and colleagues (2003), were built by first extracting the pervasive 'demoralisation' factor into its own scale (RCd) so that the remaining RC scales each capture a more distinctive core dimension of psychopathology with cleaner discriminant validity. This addressed the heavy intercorrelation and construct overlap of the original clinical scales. The RC scales serve as the core of the MMPI-2-RF (2008) and MMPI-3 (2020).","traps":{"a":"The RC scales were not dropped in the MMPI-2-RF; they are its core scales.","b":"The RC scales are not defined by using only true/false items (the MMPI item format is already true/false); their innovation is removing demoralisation variance.","d":"The RC scales are deliberately not identical to the original clinical scales; they were restructured to reduce overlap and improve discriminant validity."}},{"q":"The NEO-PI-R measures personality on which model?","concept":"The NEO-PI-R (Paul Costa and Robert McCrae, 1992) is a 240-item self-report questionnaire operationalising the Five-Factor Model, or Big Five: Openness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness, and Neuroticism (OCEAN), with six facets measured under each domain. The five factors recur across languages and cultures, consistent with the lexical hypothesis. A 60-item short form, the NEO-FFI, measures the domains without facets.","traps":{"a":"The Cattell 16PF measures 16 primary factors from Cattell's model, not the five-factor structure the NEO-PI-R is built on.","b":"The MBTI's 16-type typology derives from Jungian dichotomies, a categorical model distinct from the continuous Big Five.","d":"Eysenck's PEN model has three dimensions (Psychoticism, Extraversion, Neuroticism), not the five factors of the NEO-PI-R."}},{"q":"Cattell's 16PF (Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire) is most associated with:","concept":"Raymond Cattell's Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF) is a factor-analytically derived inventory that reduces the natural-language descriptors of personality to 16 primary 'source' traits (such as Warmth, Reasoning, and Emotional Stability). These primary traits in turn combine into five second-order global factors that broadly approximate the Big Five. Cattell's lexical, factor-analytic approach makes it a landmark trait instrument.","traps":{"a":"The 16PF is a general normal-personality measure, not restricted to diagnosing personality disorders.","b":"Q-sort methodology is Stephenson's forced-distribution sorting technique, unrelated to Cattell's factor-based questionnaire.","d":"Projective interpretation applies to instruments like the Rorschach or TAT; the 16PF is an objective, self-report questionnaire."}},{"q":"Generalizability theory (G theory, Cronbach et al. 1972) extends Classical Test Theory by:","concept":"Generalizability theory (Lee Cronbach and colleagues, 1972) extends Classical Test Theory by decomposing observed-score variance into multiple 'facets' of measurement, such as raters, items, and occasions, using analysis of variance to estimate each source of error separately. Rather than lumping everything into one undifferentiated error term, a G-study quantifies variance components and a follow-up D-study projects how reliability would change under different administration designs. It complements but does not replace Item Response Theory.","traps":{"b":"Treating measurement error as a single undifferentiated term is precisely the Classical Test Theory limitation that G theory was created to overcome.","c":"G theory does not eliminate the need for reliability estimation; it provides a richer, multi-faceted way of estimating it.","d":"Modelling item response curves as a function of latent ability is the province of Item Response Theory, not generalizability theory."}},{"q":"The Item Characteristic Curve (ICC) in IRT plots:","concept":"In Item Response Theory the Item Characteristic Curve (ICC) plots the probability of a correct (or keyed) response on the y-axis against the examinee's latent ability, theta, on the x-axis, producing an S-shaped logistic curve. Item parameters shape it: b (difficulty) locates the curve along the theta axis, a (discrimination) sets its steepness, and c (guessing) sets the lower asymptote. The 1PL/Rasch model uses only b, the 2PL adds a, and the 3PL adds c.","traps":{"b":"Validity coefficients across studies are the material of validity generalisation or meta-analysis, not what an ICC plots.","c":"Internal consistency by item is a reliability concern (item-total statistics), not the ability-versus-probability relationship an ICC displays.","d":"Test reliability against sample size is a different relationship entirely; the ICC's x-axis is latent ability, not sample size."}},{"q":"Test-retest reliability is most appropriate to estimate when:","concept":"Test-retest reliability is estimated by administering the same test twice and correlating the scores, so it is only appropriate when the construct being measured is expected to remain stable across the retest interval, as with intelligence or trait personality. For state-like constructs such as current mood or anxiety, genuine change over time would lower the correlation and be misread as unreliability. Short intervals also risk memory carryover, while long ones confound true change with error.","traps":{"a":"A current mood state is exactly the kind of unstable construct for which test-retest reliability is inappropriate, since real change would depress the correlation.","b":"Having no opportunity to administer twice makes test-retest impossible to compute, not appropriate; it points toward alternative reliability methods.","c":"Having only one rater speaks to whether inter-rater reliability applies, not to when test-retest reliability is appropriate."}},{"q":"The Standard Error of Measurement (SEM = SD × √(1 − r)) for a test with SD = 15 and reliability = 0.91 is approximately:","concept":"The Standard Error of Measurement quantifies the expected spread of an individual's observed scores around their true score, computed as SEM = SD times the square root of (1 minus reliability). With SD = 15 and reliability = 0.91, SEM = 15 times the square root of 0.09 = 15 times 0.3 = 4.5. Higher reliability and lower SD both shrink the SEM, tightening the confidence interval around a score.","traps":{"a":"15.0 would result only if reliability were 0 (all variance error); with reliability 0.91 the SEM is far smaller.","b":"10.0 does not follow from the formula; the square root of 0.09 is 0.3, giving 4.5, not a value near two-thirds of the SD.","c":"1.5 underestimates the SEM; it would require a reliability of 0.99, not 0.91."}},{"q":"Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) analysis plots:","concept":"Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) analysis plots sensitivity (the true positive rate) on the y-axis against 1 minus specificity (the false positive rate) on the x-axis across every possible cut-off value of a test. The Area Under the Curve (AUC) summarises overall discrimination as the probability that a randomly chosen positive case scores higher than a randomly chosen negative, with 0.5 meaning chance and values above 0.9 outstanding. ROC curves guide selection of clinical cut-off scores.","traps":{"a":"Predictive validity plotted against reliability is not what ROC displays; ROC plots true positive against false positive rates.","c":"Item difficulty versus discrimination is an item-analysis plot from IRT or classical analysis, not an ROC curve.","d":"Score versus age describes a developmental norming or growth curve, unrelated to the sensitivity-specificity trade-off of ROC."}},{"q":"The Vineland-3, K-SADS, ADI-R, and ADOS-2 are best classified as:","concept":"The Vineland-3, K-SADS, ADI-R, and ADOS-2 are all structured or semi-structured interview and observation instruments used in developmental and psychiatric assessment rather than self-report questionnaires or lab paradigms. The Vineland-3 is a caregiver interview for adaptive behaviour, the K-SADS is a semi-structured interview for childhood psychiatric disorders, the ADI-R is a caregiver autism interview, and the ADOS-2 is a direct interaction-based autism observation, with ADI-R plus ADOS-2 forming the gold-standard autism diagnostic combination.","traps":{"a":"EEG paradigms are electrophysiological recordings; none of these four instruments involves brain-wave measurement.","b":"Personality projective techniques (like the Rorschach) present ambiguous stimuli for interpretation, unlike these structured diagnostic interviews and observations.","c":"Self-report cognitive tests are completed directly by the examinee; these instruments instead rely on caregiver interview or clinician observation of the child."}}],"nimhans-mock-1":[{"q":"In a cohort study investigating the association between a new psychological intervention and relapse rates in schizophrenia, the risk ratio is calculated as 1.0. This finding indicates that:","concept":"The risk ratio (relative risk) divides the incidence of the outcome in the exposed group by the incidence in the unexposed group. When RR equals exactly 1.0, the two incidences are identical, meaning the exposure (the intervention) is not associated with any change in relapse risk. By convention RR greater than 1 indicates increased risk (harm) and RR less than 1 indicates reduced risk (protection).","traps":{"a":"An RR of 2.0, not 1.0, would mean the exposed group's incidence is double that of the unexposed group.","b":"A significant protective effect would require RR below 1.0 with a confidence interval that does not cross 1, which is not the case here.","d":"This describes the interpretation of an odds ratio of 1.0 framed by exposure odds, whereas the question specifically asks about a risk ratio."}},{"q":"A researcher conducts a systematic review on the efficacy of CBT for insomnia. To ensure transparency and replicability in the methodology, the researcher should adhere to which of the following guidelines?","concept":"PRISMA (Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses) is the reporting guideline designed specifically to make systematic reviews transparent and replicable, covering search strategy, study selection, and the flow of records. Each major study design has its own reporting standard, so matching the guideline to the design is the key exam skill here.","traps":{"a":"The CONSORT Statement is the reporting standard for randomized controlled trials, not for systematic reviews.","c":"The STROBE checklist governs the reporting of observational studies (cohort, case-control, cross-sectional).","d":"CARE guidelines are for writing up individual case reports, not synthesized reviews of many studies."}},{"q":"To investigate a rare psychological outcome (e.g., a specific dissociative disorder) and its potential past exposures (e.g., childhood trauma), which of the following epidemiological study designs would be the MOST feasible and appropriate?","concept":"A case-control study starts by identifying people who already have the outcome (cases) and comparable people without it (controls), then looks backward at prior exposures. Because it recruits on the basis of the outcome, it is the most efficient and feasible design for rare outcomes, where waiting for cases to appear in a cohort would be impractical.","traps":{"a":"A prospective cohort would require following an enormous sample forward for years to accumulate enough cases of a rare disorder, making it impractical.","b":"A cross-sectional study measures exposure and outcome at a single time point, so it cannot establish temporal order and captures too few rare cases.","d":"A randomized controlled trial deliberately assigns an intervention and cannot ethically or practically assign a harmful past exposure like childhood trauma."}},{"q":"A researcher randomly selects 5 districts from each state, then 10 schools per district, then 30 students per school. This sampling technique is known as:","concept":"Multi-stage cluster sampling proceeds through a hierarchy of naturally occurring groups (clusters), selecting randomly at each level: first districts, then schools within chosen districts, then students within chosen schools. It is used when a full sampling frame of individuals is unavailable but the population is organized into nested units.","traps":{"a":"Stratified random sampling divides the whole population into strata and samples from every stratum, rather than randomly discarding entire clusters at each stage.","b":"Quota sampling is a non-probability method that fills preset category targets by convenience, with no random selection at any level.","d":"Purposive sampling deliberately handpicks participants judged to be informative, unlike the random multi-level selection described here."}},{"q":"Which sampling method relies on the researcher's judgment to select participants who are 'typical' or 'representative,' and does NOT allow for the calculation of sampling error?","concept":"Quota sampling is a non-probability technique in which the researcher uses personal judgment to select participants believed to be typical or representative until preset category quotas are filled. Because selection is not random and each unit's probability of inclusion is unknown, sampling error cannot be estimated.","traps":{"a":"Simple random sampling gives every individual a known equal chance of selection, so sampling error is calculable.","b":"Stratified random sampling is a probability method that randomly samples within defined strata and permits sampling error estimation.","d":"Cluster sampling randomly selects intact groups and is a probability method, allowing sampling error to be quantified."}},{"q":"On a Scree plot, eigenvalues drop sharply from 3.5 to 1.8, then plateau at 0.9, 0.7, 0.5. By the Kaiser-Guttman rule (eigenvalue > 1) and the elbow, how many factors should be retained?","concept":"The Kaiser-Guttman rule retains only factors whose eigenvalue exceeds 1.0, because such factors explain more variance than a single standardized variable. Here only the eigenvalues 3.5 and 1.8 exceed 1.0, so two factors are retained, and the scree plot's elbow (the point where the curve flattens after 1.8) agrees.","traps":{"a":"Retaining all five would ignore the eigenvalue-greater-than-1 criterion, keeping factors (0.9, 0.7, 0.5) that explain less variance than one original variable.","b":"Retaining three would incorrectly include the 0.9 eigenvalue, which falls below the Kaiser threshold of 1.0.","d":"Retaining only one drops the 1.8 eigenvalue, which clearly exceeds 1.0 and lies above the scree elbow."}},{"q":"When comparing two independent samples and the data does not meet assumptions for parametric testing, which non-parametric test is most commonly used?","concept":"The Mann-Whitney U test is the standard non-parametric alternative to the independent-samples t-test, comparing two unrelated groups by ranking all observations rather than assuming normality. It is chosen whenever two independent samples must be compared and parametric assumptions (normality, interval data) are not met.","traps":{"a":"The Wilcoxon signed-rank test is for two related or paired samples, not independent groups.","c":"The Kruskal-Wallis test extends the logic to three or more independent groups, more than the two specified here.","d":"The Friedman test handles three or more repeated (within-subjects) measures, not two independent samples."}},{"q":"Cohen's Kappa is a statistical measure used to assess which of the following?","concept":"Cohen's Kappa quantifies the agreement between two raters classifying items into categories, correcting for the agreement that would be expected purely by chance. It is the standard index of inter-rater reliability for categorical judgments.","traps":{"a":"Test-retest reliability is assessed by correlating the same measure across two time points, typically with Pearson's r, not Kappa.","b":"Internal consistency (how well items on a scale hang together) is measured by Cronbach's alpha.","d":"Criterion validity concerns how well a test predicts an external standard, which is a validity question, not rater agreement."}},{"q":"When two predictors in a regression analysis have a high correlation with each other, this statistical problem is known as:","concept":"Multicollinearity occurs when two or more predictor variables in a regression are highly correlated with each other, which inflates the standard errors of the coefficients and makes the individual effects unstable and hard to interpret. It is diagnosed with the Variance Inflation Factor (VIF above 10) or tolerance below 0.1.","traps":{"a":"Heteroscedasticity refers to non-constant variance of the residuals across levels of the predictor, not correlation between predictors.","c":"Autocorrelation is the correlation of residuals with each other across observations (common in time-series data), not correlation among predictors.","d":"Homogeneity of variance is an assumption of equal variances across groups, relevant to ANOVA and t-tests, not a predictor-correlation problem."}},{"q":"In statistical hypothesis testing, rejecting a true null hypothesis represents which type of error?","concept":"A Type I error is rejecting the null hypothesis when it is actually true, that is, a false positive; its probability is the significance level alpha. This contrasts with a Type II error, which is failing to reject a false null hypothesis (a false negative).","traps":{"b":"A Type II error is failing to reject a null hypothesis that is actually false, the opposite mistake to the one described.","c":"Sampling error is the natural variability between a sample statistic and the true population parameter, not a hypothesis-testing decision error.","d":"Standard error is a measure of the precision of a sample estimate, not a category of decision error."}},{"q":"Which statistical measure tells how far individual data points are dispersed from the mean expressed in standard deviation units?","concept":"A Z-score (standard score) expresses how far a raw score lies from the mean in units of standard deviation, calculated as (X minus the mean) divided by the standard deviation. Because it is scaled in standard deviation units, it allows scores from different distributions to be compared directly.","traps":{"a":"Variance is the average of squared deviations from the mean and is expressed in squared units, not standard deviation units per data point.","c":"The coefficient of variation is the ratio of the standard deviation to the mean, used to compare relative dispersion across datasets, not to locate a single point.","d":"Standard error describes the variability of a sample statistic (like the mean) across repeated samples, not the position of an individual score."}},{"q":"When the assumption of normal distribution is met and you want to compare the means of two independent groups, which statistical test is most appropriate?","concept":"When data are normally distributed and the goal is to compare the means of two independent (unrelated) groups, the independent-samples t-test is the appropriate parametric test. If the assumption of equal variances is violated, Welch's t-test is used instead.","traps":{"a":"The Mann-Whitney U test is the non-parametric alternative, used when normality cannot be assumed, so it is unnecessary here.","c":"The Chi-square test compares frequencies of categorical variables, not means of continuous data.","d":"The Wilcoxon signed-rank test is non-parametric and for paired data, not independent groups under normality."}},{"q":"Interaction effects between two or more independent variables can be detected and analyzed using which statistical technique?","concept":"Factorial ANOVA involves two or more independent variables (factors) and can test not only their separate main effects but also their interaction, that is, whether the effect of one IV on the dependent variable depends on the level of another. Detecting interaction effects requires this factorial structure.","traps":{"a":"Correlation analysis measures the strength and direction of a linear association between variables and cannot partition main effects from interactions.","b":"The independent-samples t-test handles only one independent variable with two levels, so it cannot test interactions.","d":"The Chi-square test examines associations between categorical variables and does not model interaction effects on a continuous outcome."}},{"q":"If a researcher wants to subtract out the influence of number of children from the relationship between marital stress and length of marriage, which statistical method is most appropriate?","concept":"Partial correlation measures the association between two variables (marital stress and length of marriage) while statistically holding constant, or subtracting out, the influence of a third variable (number of children). This isolates the direct relationship after removing the confound.","traps":{"a":"Biserial correlation estimates the relationship between a continuous variable and an artificially dichotomized one, not the control of a confounder.","b":"Multiple correlation relates one dependent variable to several predictors jointly, rather than removing the effect of a single control variable.","c":"Linear correlation simply measures the bivariate association without controlling for any third variable."}},{"q":"A researcher wants to calculate the degrees of freedom (df) for a 3 × 4 contingency table in a Chi-square test. The df is:","concept":"For a chi-square test on a contingency table, degrees of freedom equal (number of rows minus 1) multiplied by (number of columns minus 1). For a 3 by 4 table this is 2 multiplied by 3, giving 6.","traps":{"a":"12 is simply the total number of cells (3 times 4), not the degrees of freedom.","b":"7 does not follow the (r minus 1)(c minus 1) formula and likely comes from adding rather than the correct calculation.","d":"11 is the total number of cells minus 1, which is the wrong rule for a two-way contingency table."}},{"q":"In Phenylketonuria (PKU), the body fails to convert phenylalanine into which substance due to a deficiency of phenylalanine hydroxylase?","concept":"Phenylketonuria is an autosomal recessive disorder in which a deficiency of the enzyme phenylalanine hydroxylase prevents the conversion of phenylalanine into tyrosine. Phenylalanine therefore accumulates to toxic levels while tyrosine falls, and untreated cases develop intellectual disability, which newborn screening and a low-phenylalanine diet prevent.","traps":{"a":"Urea is the nitrogen waste product of the urea cycle and is unrelated to the phenylalanine hydroxylase reaction.","c":"Tryptophan is a separate essential amino acid and the precursor of serotonin, not the product blocked in PKU.","d":"Histidine is another amino acid and is not the substance that phenylalanine is converted into."}},{"q":"What is the alternative name for adrenaline, the hormone released by the adrenal medulla during the fight-or-flight response?","concept":"Adrenaline and epinephrine are two names for the same catecholamine hormone released by the adrenal medulla during sympathetic fight-or-flight activation. Epinephrine is the term standardized in medical and pharmacological usage.","traps":{"a":"Norepinephrine (noradrenaline) is a closely related but distinct catecholamine and is not simply the alternative name for adrenaline.","c":"Dopamine is a precursor catecholamine and neurotransmitter, not another name for adrenaline.","d":"Cortisol is a glucocorticoid released by the adrenal cortex, not the medulla, and is chemically unrelated to adrenaline."}},{"q":"Which of the following hormones is NOT secreted by the anterior pituitary gland?","concept":"The anterior pituitary secretes growth hormone, adrenocorticotropic hormone, thyroid stimulating hormone, follicle stimulating hormone, luteinizing hormone, and prolactin. Melatonin is not among them; it is produced by the pineal gland to regulate the sleep-wake cycle.","traps":{"a":"Growth hormone is a genuine anterior pituitary hormone, so it is not the exception.","b":"Adrenocorticotropic hormone is secreted by the anterior pituitary to drive the adrenal cortex, so it is not the exception.","d":"Thyroid stimulating hormone is an anterior pituitary product acting on the thyroid, so it is not the exception."}},{"q":"Which brain structure serves as the primary control center for integrating the endocrine, visceral, and autonomic nervous systems?","concept":"The hypothalamus is the master integrating center that links the nervous and endocrine systems, controlling the pituitary gland and governing homeostatic functions such as temperature, hunger, thirst, autonomic outflow, and the sleep-wake cycle. This coordinating role over endocrine, visceral, and autonomic systems makes it the answer.","traps":{"a":"The cerebellum coordinates movement, balance, and motor learning, not endocrine and autonomic integration.","c":"The thalamus is chiefly a sensory relay station directing signals to the cortex, not the endocrine control center.","d":"The brainstem controls vital reflexes like breathing and heart rate but does not serve as the master endocrine-autonomic integrator."}},{"q":"In neuroimaging, which technique directly measures the electrical activity of the brain with high temporal resolution, akin to ECG in cardiology?","concept":"Electroencephalography (EEG) records the summed electrical activity of cortical neurons through scalp electrodes with millisecond temporal resolution, directly analogous to how the ECG records the heart's electrical activity. Its strength is temporal precision, though its spatial resolution is poor.","traps":{"a":"fMRI measures the blood-oxygen-level-dependent signal indirectly and offers high spatial but poor temporal resolution, and it is not electrical.","b":"PET tracks metabolic or receptor activity using radioactive tracers, not direct electrical activity, and has slow temporal resolution.","d":"MEG measures the magnetic fields generated by neural currents rather than the electrical potentials themselves, and is far less commonly used."}},{"q":"The lateral ventricles, the largest of the ventricles in the brain, are filled with:","concept":"All four ventricles of the brain, including the large paired lateral ventricles, are filled with cerebrospinal fluid produced by the choroid plexus. CSF cushions the brain, removes waste, and supports nutrient exchange, flowing from the lateral ventricles to the third and fourth ventricles.","traps":{"a":"Blood circulates in vessels and sinuses, not within the ventricular cavities.","c":"There is no fluid called 'synaptic fluid'; the ventricles contain CSF.","d":"Myelin is the lipid sheath insulating axons, not a fluid that fills ventricles."}},{"q":"Patient H.M. underwent surgical removal of the medial temporal lobes (incl. hippocampus) for epilepsy. His most profound deficit was in:","concept":"After bilateral removal of his medial temporal lobes, including the hippocampus, patient Henry Molaison (H.M.) was unable to form new declarative (conscious) memories, a condition called anterograde amnesia. This case established the hippocampus as essential for consolidating new long-term memories, while sparing working memory and most older memories.","traps":{"a":"H.M.'s procedural (skill) memory was preserved, as shown by his improvement on motor tasks, so it was not his profound deficit.","b":"Semantic memory acquired before surgery remained largely intact; his core problem was forming new memories, not accessing old facts.","d":"His retrograde amnesia was comparatively limited; the hallmark and most profound deficit was the anterograde loss."}},{"q":"Despite his severe amnesia, H.M. learned and improved on a mirror-tracing task across days, with no conscious recollection of practising it. This demonstrates the preservation of:","concept":"Despite being unable to consciously recall practising, H.M. steadily improved on the mirror-tracing task, demonstrating intact procedural (skill) memory. Procedural memory depends on the basal ganglia and cerebellum rather than the hippocampus, and this dissociation is key evidence for separate memory systems.","traps":{"a":"Episodic memory (recollection of specific events) was severely impaired in H.M., as he could not remember the practice sessions.","b":"Declarative memory, the conscious memory of facts and events, was exactly the system he had lost.","d":"Semantic memory, general knowledge of facts, is a declarative system and was not what the mirror-tracing task tapped."}},{"q":"Loss of memories for events that happened BEFORE a brain injury, such as a concussion or stroke, is termed:","concept":"Retrograde amnesia is the loss of memories for events that occurred before the brain injury, whereas anterograde amnesia is the inability to form new memories after it. According to Ribot's law, more recent memories are typically lost first while remote memories are relatively spared.","traps":{"a":"Anterograde amnesia is the inability to lay down new memories after the injury, the opposite temporal direction to what is described.","c":"Source amnesia is forgetting where or how information was learned while retaining the information itself, not a time-based loss around an injury.","d":"Dissociative amnesia is a psychogenic memory loss for personal information triggered by trauma or stress, not caused by a physical brain injury."}},{"q":"The primary brain region responsible for the comprehension of spoken and written language is:","concept":"Wernicke's area, in the left superior temporal gyrus, is responsible for the comprehension of spoken and written language. Damage produces fluent but meaningless speech (word salad) with impaired understanding, and its connection to Broca's area via the arcuate fasciculus supports the flow from comprehension to production.","traps":{"a":"Broca's area in the left inferior frontal gyrus governs speech production, not comprehension.","c":"The primary auditory cortex (Heschl's gyrus) detects and processes sound, but does not extract linguistic meaning.","d":"The angular gyrus contributes to reading, writing, and integrating information, but it is not the primary language-comprehension center."}},{"q":"Damage to Broca's area typically results in:","concept":"Broca's aphasia is an expressive (non-fluent) aphasia resulting from damage to Broca's area, marked by effortful, telegraphic, agrammatic speech while comprehension remains relatively intact. Because they understand their errors, patients are typically aware and frustrated.","traps":{"a":"Fluent speech with poor comprehension describes Wernicke's (receptive) aphasia, not Broca's aphasia.","c":"Anomia (word-finding or naming difficulty) is a broader symptom seen across many aphasias and is not the defining feature of Broca's aphasia.","d":"Prosopagnosia, the inability to recognize faces, results from damage to the fusiform face area, unrelated to Broca's area."}},{"q":"[Repeated 2023, 2024] The reticular activating system (RAS), located in the brainstem, is crucial for the regulation of:","concept":"The ascending reticular activating system in the brainstem regulates cortical arousal, wakefulness, and attention, and controls transitions between sleep and waking. Severe damage to it can produce coma, underscoring its role in maintaining consciousness.","traps":{"a":"Fine motor coordination is a function of the cerebellum and basal ganglia, not the reticular activating system.","c":"Emotional memory is associated with the amygdala and hippocampus, not the RAS.","d":"Balance and posture are governed by the cerebellum and vestibular system, not the arousal-regulating RAS."}},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with chronic schizophrenia on long-term antipsychotics presents with repetitive, involuntary lip-smacking, tongue movements, and grimacing. This is most likely:","concept":"Tardive dyskinesia is a late-onset, often irreversible movement disorder caused by chronic dopamine D2 receptor blockade from long-term antipsychotic use, presenting with repetitive involuntary oro-facial movements such as lip-smacking, tongue protrusion, and grimacing. Risk is higher with typical (first-generation) antipsychotics, and it is monitored using the Abnormal Involuntary Movement Scale.","traps":{"a":"Akathisia is a subjective inner restlessness with an urge to move, not the repetitive oro-facial dyskinesia described.","b":"Acute dystonia is a sudden, early sustained muscle contraction (such as torticollis or oculogyric crisis) occurring soon after starting medication, not a late-onset movement.","d":"Drug-induced parkinsonism presents with tremor, rigidity, and bradykinesia, not the choreoathetoid oro-facial movements of tardive dyskinesia."}},{"q":"What is the primary function of the myelin sheath surrounding nerve axons?","concept":"The myelin sheath insulates axons and enables saltatory conduction, in which the action potential jumps between the Nodes of Ranvier, dramatically increasing the speed of nerve impulse conduction (roughly 10 to 100 times). Demyelinating diseases such as multiple sclerosis slow this transmission and cause neurological deficits.","traps":{"a":"Structural support to neurons is provided mainly by glial cells and the cytoskeleton, not the primary role of myelin.","c":"Neurotransmitters are stored in synaptic vesicles at axon terminals, not within the myelin sheath.","d":"Action potentials are generated at the axon hillock and Nodes of Ranvier by ion channels; myelin speeds conduction rather than generating the impulse."}},{"q":"A patient presents with severe memory deficits, confabulation, and nystagmus, with a history of chronic alcohol use. This clinical picture is classic for:","concept":"The triad of severe anterograde memory deficits, confabulation, and eye signs such as nystagmus in a chronic alcohol user is classic for Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, caused by thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency. Wernicke's encephalopathy presents acutely with confusion, ataxia, and ophthalmoplegia, and if untreated progresses to the amnestic, confabulating Korsakoff psychosis; treatment is intravenous thiamine given before glucose.","traps":{"a":"Alzheimer's disease is a gradual degenerative dementia of older age without the nystagmus or alcohol-linked thiamine deficiency seen here.","c":"Vascular dementia produces stepwise cognitive decline linked to cerebrovascular events, not the confabulation-plus-nystagmus picture of thiamine deficiency.","d":"Frontotemporal dementia presents with early personality change and language decline, not the acute ocular signs and confabulation described."}},{"q":"[Repeated 2023, 2024] 'Folie à deux,' or Shared Psychotic Disorder, is characterized by:","concept":"Folie a deux (Shared Psychotic Disorder) occurs when a dominant, psychotic 'inducer' transmits a fixed delusional belief to a passive, closely associated 'recipient' who normally would not be psychotic. The relationship (often shared isolation and dependency) is the vehicle: separating the pair frequently resolves the recipient's symptoms. The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition removed it as a standalone entity (folded under delusional disorder / other specified psychotic disorder), but the term remains clinically useful.","traps":{"a":"Two independent simultaneous psychoses describes coincident illness, not the defining transmission of one person's delusion to another within a relationship.","c":"A delusion shared by a whole cultural group is closer to a culturally sanctioned belief or mass phenomenon, not the dyadic inducer-recipient pattern of folie a deux.","d":"A brief stress-triggered psychosis in two people describes shared acute stress reactions, missing the core mechanism of belief transmission from a dominant inducer."}},{"q":"The specific phobia of pain is known as:","concept":"The specific phobia of pain is algophobia, from the Greek 'algos' meaning pain. Specific phobias are named by their Greek or Latin root plus '-phobia', so knowing the root reliably identifies the fear. Algophobia is a marked, persistent fear of experiencing or anticipating physical pain.","traps":{"a":"Nosophobia is the fear of having or developing a specific disease (from 'nosos', disease), not the fear of pain.","c":"Cynophobia is the fear of dogs (from 'kyon/kynos', dog), an animal-type specific phobia.","d":"Hematophobia is the fear of blood (from 'haima', blood), part of the blood-injection-injury phobia cluster."}},{"q":"A client reports persistent admiration-seeking but presents with excessive emotionality, attention-seeking, and seductive behavior, rather than grandiosity. This is most characteristic of:","concept":"Histrionic personality disorder (Cluster B) is defined by pervasive excessive emotionality and attention-seeking: theatrical, seductive or provocative behavior, shallow and rapidly shifting affect, suggestibility, and discomfort when not the center of attention. The key discriminator here is that the person seeks admiration through dramatic emotional display rather than through beliefs of superiority. That absence of grandiosity and entitlement rules out narcissism.","traps":{"a":"Narcissistic personality disorder also craves admiration but does so through grandiosity, entitlement, and a need for status, not theatrical seductiveness and shallow emotionality.","c":"Borderline personality disorder centers on identity disturbance, abandonment fears, affective instability, and impulsivity, not primarily attention-seeking through seductive display.","d":"Dependent personality disorder involves clinging, submissive behavior and needing others to take responsibility, not dramatic attention-seeking."}},{"q":"A client reports persistent and frightening experiences of their surroundings seeming unreal, distorted, or dreamlike. This symptom is best described as:","concept":"Derealization is a dissociative experience in which the external world feels unreal, distorted, dreamlike, foggy, or lifeless, though the person retains intact reality testing (they know it only feels that way). It is distinguished from depersonalization, which targets the self rather than the surroundings. Both can co-occur in depersonalization/derealization disorder, panic attacks, and post-traumatic stress disorder.","traps":{"b":"Depersonalization is feeling detached from or unreal within one's own self, thoughts, or body, whereas the described symptom concerns the surroundings.","c":"Deja vu is a fleeting sense that a novel situation has been experienced before, not a persistent dreamlike unreality of the environment.","d":"A hallucination is a false sensory perception without an external stimulus; here perception is accurate but feels unreal, so reality testing is preserved."}},{"q":"A patient presents with a fixed delusional belief that their spouse is being unfaithful, despite no evidence. This specific form of delusional jealousy is known as:","concept":"Othello syndrome (morbid or delusional jealousy) is a fixed, false belief that one's partner is being unfaithful, held with delusional conviction despite absent or contradictory evidence. It is named after Shakespeare's jealous Othello and can drive dangerous stalking or violence. It is classified among the delusional misidentification and content-specific delusional syndromes.","traps":{"a":"Capgras syndrome is the delusion that a familiar person has been replaced by an identical impostor, not a belief about infidelity.","c":"Fregoli syndrome is the delusion that different strangers are actually one persecutor in disguise, a misidentification of identity rather than jealousy.","d":"De Clerambault's syndrome (erotomania) is the delusion that another person, often of higher status, is secretly in love with the patient, the opposite theme to jealous suspicion."}},{"q":"A 67-year-old man with mild cognitive impairment insists that his wife of 40 years is an identical 'duplicate' impostor, despite acknowledging she looks exactly like her. Most likely diagnosis:","concept":"Capgras syndrome is the delusional belief that a familiar person (here the wife) has been replaced by an identical-looking impostor or 'duplicate', even while the patient admits the resemblance is perfect. It is thought to arise from a disconnection between intact face recognition (ventral visual stream) and the emotional response that normally accompanies recognizing a loved one, so the face looks right but 'feels' wrong. It commonly appears in dementia, schizophrenia, and other neurological conditions.","traps":{"a":"Fregoli syndrome is the reverse error: believing strangers are one familiar person in disguise, not that a familiar person is an impostor.","c":"Cotard syndrome is a nihilistic delusion of being dead or nonexistent, unrelated to misidentifying another person.","d":"Reduplicative paramnesia is the delusion that a place has been duplicated or relocated, concerning locations rather than identifying a person as an impostor."}},{"q":"Cotard syndrome, a rare neuropsychiatric condition, is primarily characterized by which type of delusional belief?","concept":"Cotard syndrome (walking corpse or nihilistic delusion) is characterized by the fixed belief that one is dead, does not exist, is decaying, or has lost internal organs or one's soul. It reflects an extreme nihilistic delusional content and is most often seen in severe psychotic depression, but also in schizophrenia and neurodegenerative disease. The defining feature is denial of one's own existence or life.","traps":{"a":"Grandiose delusions of special powers are the opposite in content and typify mania or grandiose-type delusional disorder, not Cotard.","b":"Persecutory delusions of being followed define paranoid presentations, not the nihilistic self-denial of Cotard.","d":"Somatic delusions of harboring a serious illness point to illness-themed delusional disorder, distinct from the belief that one is already dead or nonexistent."}},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman presents with sudden right-arm paralysis after an argument; neuro exam is normal and she appears strikingly unconcerned ('It's not really bothering me'). This presentation is most characteristic of:","concept":"Conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder) presents with neurological symptoms such as sudden paralysis, blindness, or seizures that are incompatible with recognized neurological disease and are often precipitated by psychological stress (here an argument). A classic accompanying sign is la belle indifference, a striking lack of concern about a dramatic deficit. The symptoms are not consciously produced, distinguishing it from feigning.","traps":{"b":"Borderline personality disorder involves affective instability and interpersonal chaos, not the sudden loss of a neurological function with normal exam findings.","c":"Major depressive disorder is a mood syndrome; it would not produce isolated limb paralysis with intact neurology and indifferent affect.","d":"Factitious disorder involves deliberate fabrication or induction of symptoms to assume the sick role, whereas conversion symptoms are unconsciously produced."}},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman exhibits distinct personalities with their own names, memories, and behaviour, with amnesia between episodes; she finds clothes she does not remember buying. Most consistent diagnosis:","concept":"Dissociative identity disorder is defined by two or more distinct identity states (each with its own name, memories, and behavior) accompanied by recurrent gaps in autobiographical memory that exceed ordinary forgetting, such as finding purchases one does not remember making. It is strongly linked to severe, chronic early childhood trauma. The presence of multiple identities plus inter-identity amnesia is the diagnostic hallmark.","traps":{"a":"Dissociative amnesia is an inability to recall important personal information without distinct alternate identities, so it cannot explain the separate named personalities.","c":"Depersonalization/derealization disorder involves feelings of unreality of self or surroundings without amnesia or distinct identity states.","d":"Dissociative fugue involves sudden purposeful travel with amnesia for one's past, a single episode rather than recurring distinct identities."}},{"q":"Persistent preoccupation with perceived defects or flaws in physical appearance that are not observable to others is characteristic of which disorder?","concept":"Body dysmorphic disorder is a preoccupation with one or more perceived defects or flaws in physical appearance that are unobservable or appear only slight to others, coupled with repetitive behaviors such as mirror checking, grooming, or reassurance seeking. It sits within the obsessive-compulsive and related disorders chapter of the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition and carries a notably high suicide risk. The focus on appearance flaws distinguishes it from illness- or symptom-focused disorders.","traps":{"a":"Somatic symptom disorder centers on distressing physical symptoms and excessive health-related thoughts and behaviors, not on perceived appearance defects.","c":"Illness anxiety disorder is preoccupation with having or acquiring a serious illness, not with a flaw in how one looks.","d":"Conversion disorder involves neurological-type functional deficits, unrelated to preoccupation with appearance."}},{"q":"Which of the following best describes the concept of 'ego-dystonic' as it applies to obsessions in OCD?","concept":"Ego-dystonic means an experience is felt as alien, intrusive, unwanted, and inconsistent with one's sense of self, which is exactly how obsessions in obsessive-compulsive disorder are experienced: the person recognizes the thoughts as their own but resists and is distressed by them. This contrasts with ego-syntonic experiences, which feel congruent with the self (as in many personality disorder traits). The distress and rejection of the thought define ego-dystonia.","traps":{"a":"Experiencing obsessions as a core part of identity describes ego-syntonic content, the opposite of the ego-dystonic quality of OCD obsessions.","c":"Deriving pleasure from the thoughts contradicts the distressing, unwanted nature of obsessions.","d":"Being completely unaware that the obsessions are unreasonable describes poor or absent insight, which is a separate specifier, not the meaning of ego-dystonic."}},{"q":"[Repeated 2023, 2024] 'Dementia praecox,' which translates to 'premature dementia,' is historically most directly associated with which contemporary diagnostic category?","concept":"Dementia praecox ('premature dementia') is the historical forerunner of schizophrenia. Emil Kraepelin coined the term to describe an early-onset, deteriorating psychotic illness, and Eugen Bleuler later renamed it schizophrenia in 1908, emphasizing the splitting of psychic functions and his four A's (loosening of associations, affect disturbance, ambivalence, autism). The term maps onto the modern category of schizophrenia, not a dementia.","traps":{"a":"Alzheimer's disease is a neurodegenerative dementia of late life; despite the word 'dementia' in the old term, dementia praecox is a psychotic, not neurodegenerative, illness.","b":"Bipolar I disorder derives historically from Kraepelin's separate category of manic-depressive insanity, which he explicitly distinguished from dementia praecox.","d":"Frontotemporal dementia is a genuine neurodegenerative dementia and is unrelated to the historical concept of dementia praecox."}},{"q":"According to the ICD-10, what is the minimum duration of symptoms required for a definitive diagnosis of Schizophrenia?","concept":"Under the International Classification of Diseases, Tenth Revision, a definitive diagnosis of schizophrenia requires the characteristic symptoms to be present for most of the time during at least one month. This one-month threshold is a common exam contrast with the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition, which requires at least six months of total disturbance including at least one month of active-phase symptoms. The question specifies ICD-10, so the answer is one month.","traps":{"a":"One week corresponds roughly to brief psychotic disorder or acute presentations, not the ICD-10 threshold for schizophrenia.","c":"Three months matches ICD durations for some other conditions but is not the schizophrenia requirement.","d":"Six months is the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition criterion, not the ICD-10 one that the question asks about."}},{"q":"Which of the following is considered a negative symptom of schizophrenia, characterized by reduced quantity and quality of verbal expression?","concept":"Alogia (poverty of speech) is a negative symptom of schizophrenia, reflecting a reduction in the quantity and content of spontaneous verbal output. Negative symptoms represent a loss or diminution of normal function and are often summarized as the five A's: alogia, anhedonia, avolition, affective flattening, and asociality. This contrasts with positive symptoms, which are additions to normal experience.","traps":{"a":"Auditory hallucinations are a positive symptom, an added abnormal perception rather than a reduction of function.","c":"Persecutory delusions are positive symptoms, representing added false beliefs.","d":"Disorganized behavior is generally classed among positive or disorganization symptoms, not a negative symptom of reduced speech."}},{"q":"A patient with Korsakoff's psychosis fabricates detailed and believable stories about their past to fill memory gaps, without intent to deceive. This is:","concept":"Confabulation is the production of false or distorted memories without any conscious intent to deceive, typically to fill gaps left by amnesia; the patient genuinely believes the fabricated account. It is a hallmark of Korsakoff's syndrome and is also seen with frontal lobe damage and anterior communicating artery aneurysm. The absence of deceptive intent distinguishes it from lying and malingering.","traps":{"b":"Confusion is a global disturbance of clear thinking and orientation, not the specific filling of memory gaps with fabricated stories.","c":"Delirium is an acute, fluctuating disturbance of attention and awareness, a broader state rather than the specific memory phenomenon described.","d":"Malingering is the deliberate fabrication of symptoms for external gain, which requires the conscious intent to deceive that confabulation lacks."}},{"q":"According to Thomas and Chess's temperament model, a child who is generally negative in mood, slow to adapt, and has irregular biological rhythms would be classified as:","concept":"In Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess's New York Longitudinal Study temperament model, the 'difficult' child is characterized by predominantly negative mood, slow adaptability to change, withdrawal from new stimuli, high intensity of reactions, and irregular biological rhythms (sleep, feeding). Their framework also describes 'easy' and 'slow-to-warm-up' patterns, with 'goodness of fit' between temperament and environment predicting adjustment. The cluster of negativity, poor adaptability, and irregular rhythms defines the difficult temperament.","traps":{"a":"The easy temperament shows positive mood, regular rhythms, and ready adaptability, the opposite of the described profile.","c":"The slow-to-warm-up child is mild and initially withdrawn but has more regular rhythms and adapts gradually, lacking the pervasive negativity and irregularity of the difficult type.","d":"Insecure attachment is a relational classification from attachment theory, not a temperament category in the Thomas and Chess model."}},{"q":"[Repeated 2023, 2024] The 'Marshmallow Test,' pioneered by Walter Mischel, primarily measures a child's capacity for:","concept":"Walter Mischel's marshmallow test presented preschoolers with an immediate small reward and the option to wait alone for a larger one, measuring the capacity for delay of gratification, that is, forgoing an immediate reward for a greater future one. Longer wait times predicted later self-regulation and achievement outcomes, though later replications with socioeconomic controls found smaller effects. The construct assessed is specifically delay of gratification.","traps":{"a":"Sustained attention concerns maintaining focus over time, which is not what the wait-for-a-bigger-reward paradigm was designed to measure.","b":"Resistance to temptation is a loose description, but the precise psychological construct Mischel named and measured is delay of gratification.","d":"Impulse control in social settings narrows the construct to interpersonal contexts, whereas the task measured general self-imposed delay for a future reward."}},{"q":"A child sees two identical balls of clay; one is rolled into a sausage. When asked if the amount is the same, the child says 'yes.' Per Piaget, this child is most likely in which stage?","concept":"Conservation, the understanding that quantity stays the same despite changes in shape or arrangement, emerges in Jean Piaget's concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11 years). A child who correctly says the rolled-out clay still has the same amount has decentered and can mentally reverse the transformation, hallmarks of concrete operations. Preoperational children typically fail this by centering on one dimension such as length.","traps":{"a":"The sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2 years) is dominated by object permanence and motor exploration, far too early for verbal conservation reasoning.","b":"A preoperational child would usually be misled by the change in shape and deny that the amount is conserved, so success here points beyond this stage.","d":"The formal operational stage adds abstract and hypothetical reasoning; conservation of mass is mastered earlier, in concrete operations."}},{"q":"A 5-year-old sorts cards by colour but cannot then re-sort the same deck by suit. This inflexibility is an example of:","concept":"Centration is Jean Piaget's term for the preoperational tendency to focus on a single salient feature of a situation while ignoring other relevant dimensions. The child who can sort only by color and cannot flexibly re-sort by suit is fixed on one attribute, unable to shift, which is centration. Overcoming it (decentration) is part of the transition into concrete operations.","traps":{"a":"Egocentrism is the inability to take another person's perspective, not the fixation on one feature of a task.","b":"Animism is attributing life and intentions to inanimate objects, unrelated to sorting inflexibility.","d":"Transductive reasoning is drawing links between specific events without true logical cause, not the focusing-on-one-dimension error described."}},{"q":"According to Erikson, the primary crisis to be resolved in adolescence (12-18 years) is:","concept":"In Erik Erikson's psychosocial stages, adolescence (roughly 12 to 18 years) centers on the crisis of identity versus role confusion, in which the young person works to form a coherent, integrated sense of self. Successful resolution yields the virtue of fidelity. James Marcia later operationalized this into four identity statuses (diffusion, foreclosure, moratorium, achievement).","traps":{"a":"Industry versus inferiority is the school-age stage (about 6 to 11 years), concerning competence and productivity, not the adolescent identity task.","c":"Intimacy versus isolation is the young-adult stage focused on forming committed relationships, which follows adolescence.","d":"Generativity versus stagnation is the middle-adulthood crisis about contributing to the next generation, much later than adolescence."}},{"q":"A middle-aged adult finds meaning by mentoring younger colleagues and contributing to the next generation. According to Erikson, this individual is successfully navigating:","concept":"Mentoring younger colleagues and contributing to the next generation is the essence of generativity in Erik Erikson's middle-adulthood stage (about 40 to 65 years), the crisis of generativity versus stagnation. Successful resolution produces the virtue of care, whereas failure leads to self-absorption and stagnation. Guiding and investing in others is the defining marker of generativity.","traps":{"a":"Intimacy versus isolation is the earlier young-adult stage about forming close partnerships, not about mentoring the next generation.","c":"Ego integrity versus despair is the late-life stage of reviewing and accepting one's life, which comes after middle adulthood.","d":"Identity versus role confusion is the adolescent stage of self-definition, far earlier than the described middle-aged mentoring."}},{"q":"According to Erikson, the primary virtue that emerges from successfully resolving 'Trust vs. Mistrust' in infancy is:","concept":"In Erik Erikson's first psychosocial stage, trust versus mistrust in infancy, consistent and responsive caregiving lets the infant develop basic trust, and the virtue that emerges is hope. Each Erikson stage yields a specific virtue when resolved well, and hope is the earliest of these. Reliable care teaches the infant that the world can be depended upon.","traps":{"a":"Will is the virtue of the autonomy versus shame and doubt stage in toddlerhood, not of infancy.","b":"Purpose arises from the initiative versus guilt stage in the preschool years.","d":"Competence is the virtue of the industry versus inferiority stage in the school-age years."}},{"q":"According to Piaget, abstract reasoning and hypothetical thinking abilities are primarily developed during which developmental stage?","concept":"Abstract reasoning, hypothetical and hypothetico-deductive thinking, and propositional logic are the defining achievements of Jean Piaget's formal operational stage, beginning around 11 years and into adolescence. At this stage a person can reason about possibilities and abstractions rather than only concrete, present realities. This capacity for 'what if' systematic reasoning is what distinguishes formal from concrete operations.","traps":{"a":"The sensorimotor stage involves early sensory and motor learning and object permanence, with no abstract thought.","b":"The preoperational stage features symbolic play and language but is limited by egocentrism and centration, lacking logical reasoning.","c":"The concrete operational stage allows logical reasoning only about tangible, concrete material, not abstract or hypothetical propositions."}},{"q":"Which parenting style is characterized by emotional support, clear boundaries, and accountability for children's behaviour?","concept":"In Diana Baumrind's typology, the authoritative parenting style combines high warmth and emotional support with clear, firm boundaries and expectations of accountability. It consistently predicts the best child outcomes in competence, self-regulation, and social adjustment. The balance of responsiveness plus reasonable demandingness is its defining signature.","traps":{"a":"Authoritarian parenting is high in control but low in warmth, demanding obedience without emotional support, so it lacks the warmth described.","c":"Permissive parenting is high in warmth but low in control, indulgent and lacking the clear boundaries and accountability described.","d":"Neglectful (uninvolved) parenting is low in both warmth and control, the opposite of the supportive, boundaried style described."}},{"q":"Mary Ainsworth is best known for her work on which aspect of developmental psychology?","concept":"Mary Ainsworth is best known for her contributions to attachment theory, particularly the Strange Situation procedure, which classified infant-caregiver attachment into secure, insecure-avoidant, and insecure-resistant patterns. Her work operationalized and extended John Bowlby's attachment ideas, and a disorganized category was later added by Mary Main and Judith Solomon. Attachment is the domain that defines her legacy.","traps":{"a":"Cognitive development is associated with Jean Piaget, not Ainsworth.","b":"Moral development is associated with Lawrence Kohlberg (and Jean Piaget's earlier work), not Ainsworth.","d":"Language acquisition is linked to theorists such as Noam Chomsky, not to Ainsworth's attachment research."}},{"q":"A 10-year-old illiterate child from a rural area is brought for intellectual assessment. Which test would be MOST appropriate to minimize cultural and educational bias?","concept":"Bhatia's Battery of Performance Tests of Intelligence is the most appropriate choice for an illiterate rural child because its five performance subtests require no reading or writing and it carries Indian norms, minimizing cultural and educational bias. Performance-based, nonverbal assessment avoids penalizing a child for lack of schooling or literacy. This makes it the standard Indian tool for low-literacy assessment.","traps":{"b":"The Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children, Fourth Edition, is heavily verbal and Western-normed, disadvantaging an illiterate child from a rural Indian background.","c":"The Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale, Fifth Edition, is also verbally loaded and Western-normed, poorly suited to minimizing cultural and educational bias here.","d":"Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children, though Indian-adapted, contains substantial verbal subtests requiring language competence that an illiterate child may lack."}},{"q":"A test developer administers a new anxiety scale to a group, then re-administers the same scale two weeks later, correlating the two sets of scores. This establishes:","concept":"Administering the same test to the same group on two occasions and correlating the two sets of scores establishes test-retest reliability, an index of the stability or consistency of scores over time (usually a Pearson correlation). It answers whether the measure yields the same result when repeated. The two-week interval and re-administration of the identical scale are the giveaways.","traps":{"a":"Split-half reliability is estimated from a single administration by correlating two halves of the test, a measure of internal consistency, not stability over time.","b":"Inter-rater reliability concerns agreement between different scorers or observers, not repeated administration to the same people.","d":"Parallel forms reliability uses two equivalent versions of the test rather than the same test re-administered."}},{"q":"A clinician wants a client to reveal characteristic ways of thinking and feeling in close relationships by telling stories about ambiguous pictures. The MOST appropriate test is:","concept":"Henry Murray's Thematic Apperception Test presents ambiguous interpersonal pictures and asks the person to tell stories about them; the narratives reveal characteristic needs, drives, conflicts, and relationship themes through projection. It is the projective test of choice when the goal is to elicit interpersonal and motivational content. The storytelling-to-ambiguous-pictures format defines the TAT.","traps":{"a":"The Rorschach Inkblot Test uses inkblots and focuses on perception and form quality rather than eliciting interpersonal narratives.","c":"The Sentence Completion Test uses stems to complete rather than storytelling about pictures, giving less rich narrative material about relationships.","d":"The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory is an objective, self-report questionnaire, not a projective storytelling task."}},{"q":"On the WAIS-IV, a scaled score of 10 on a subtest indicates performance that is:","concept":"On the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale, Fourth Edition, individual subtest scaled scores have a mean of 10 and a standard deviation of 3, so a scaled score of 10 is exactly average, at the 50th percentile. This is distinct from the index and full-scale composite scores, which use a mean of 100 and standard deviation of 15. A score of 10 sits right at the center of the distribution.","traps":{"a":"The 16th percentile corresponds to a scaled score of about 7 (one standard deviation below the mean), not 10.","c":"The 84th percentile corresponds to a scaled score of about 13 (one standard deviation above the mean), not 10.","d":"The 2nd percentile corresponds to about two standard deviations below the mean (a scaled score near 4), far below the average score of 10."}},{"q":"In the WAIS-IV, how many CORE subtests are there in the Verbal Comprehension Index and the Perceptual Reasoning Index respectively?","concept":"On the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale, Fourth Edition, the Verbal Comprehension Index has three core subtests (Similarities, Vocabulary, Information) and the Perceptual Reasoning Index has three core subtests (Block Design, Matrix Reasoning, Visual Puzzles), giving three and three. The Working Memory Index and Processing Speed Index each add two more core subtests. So the correct pairing is three verbal comprehension and three perceptual reasoning.","traps":{"a":"Three and four misstates the perceptual reasoning core count, which is three, not four.","c":"Four and three overstates the verbal comprehension core, which has three core subtests, not four.","d":"Two and four is wrong on both indices, understating verbal comprehension and overstating perceptual reasoning."}},{"q":"Cattell's 16PF (16 Personality Factor Questionnaire) is primarily designed to measure:","concept":"The 16PF (16 Personality Factor Questionnaire), built by Raymond Cattell, uses factor analysis to reduce the surface language of personality into 16 underlying source traits (for example warmth, reasoning, dominance, vigilance). It is a self-report inventory of normal-range personality, organized hierarchically so the 16 primary traits load onto 5 global factors that broadly map to the Big Five. Its purpose is describing personality structure, not measuring cognitive ability, so the correct answer is that it measures personality traits.","traps":{"a":"Fluid intelligence is Cattell's own construct (reasoning with novel problems independent of learning), but that belongs to his theory of intelligence, not to the 16PF personality inventory.","b":"Crystallized intelligence, also a Cattell concept, refers to acquired knowledge and vocabulary and is assessed by ability tests, not by a personality questionnaire.","d":"General intelligence (Spearman's g-factor) is captured by IQ batteries such as the Wechsler scales, whereas the 16PF deliberately measures traits rather than overall cognitive capacity."}},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a recognized Rorschach inkblot test scoring system?","concept":"The Rorschach Inkblot Test spawned several competing scoring systems in the twentieth century: Samuel Beck, Bruno Klopfer, Zygmunt Piotrowski, Marguerite Hertz, and the Rapaport-Schafer approach, later consolidated by John Exner into the Comprehensive System and, more recently, R-PAS. Henry Murray is associated with the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT), a different projective built from ambiguous interpersonal pictures, not inkblots. Therefore the Murray system is not a recognized Rorschach scoring system.","traps":{"a":"The Beck system is a genuine early Rorschach scoring approach developed by Samuel Beck, so it is a recognized system, not the exception.","b":"The Exner Comprehensive System is the most widely used empirically grounded Rorschach scoring method, so it does not fit a NOT question.","d":"The Klopfer system is a legitimate Rorschach scoring tradition emphasizing experiential and qualitative aspects, so it is a valid system rather than the odd one out."}},{"q":"The original Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale used which method for calculating IQ scores?","concept":"The original Stanford-Binet, adapted by Lewis Terman from the Binet-Simon scale, expressed intelligence as the Ratio IQ using William Stern's formula: mental age divided by chronological age, multiplied by 100. This works in childhood but breaks down in adulthood because chronological age keeps climbing while mental age plateaus, deflating scores. David Wechsler later replaced it with the Deviation IQ (mean 100, standard deviation 15), now the standard, so the original method was the Ratio IQ.","traps":{"a":"Deviation IQ with standard scores is Wechsler's later innovation that fixed the ratio method's flaws, not the technique the original Stanford-Binet used.","c":"A percentile ranking system reports relative standing but was not the computational method used to derive the original Stanford-Binet IQ score.","d":"T-score transformation (mean 50, standard deviation 10) is a scaling used on some personality and clinical measures, not the intelligence-quotient formula of the early Stanford-Binet."}},{"q":"The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI) is best described as a(n):","concept":"The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI) is the prototypical objective personality test: it uses a large fixed set of true/false items, standardized scoring, and built-in validity scales (L, F, K) to detect response distortion. Its scales were derived by empirical (criterion) keying, contrasting clinical groups with normals. Because responses are structured and scored to a fixed key rather than interpreted from ambiguous stimuli, it is objective, not projective.","traps":{"a":"Projective personality tests (Rorschach, TAT) rely on ambiguous stimuli and open interpretation, which is the opposite of the MMPI's fixed items and scoring.","c":"An intelligence test measures cognitive ability; the MMPI measures psychopathology and personality, so this misreads its purpose.","d":"A neuropsychological screening battery assesses brain-behavior functions such as memory and attention, not the self-reported personality and symptom domains the MMPI covers."}},{"q":"A 35-year-old presents with persistent sadness, anhedonia, weight loss, insomnia, worthlessness, and concentration difficulties for 2 months. Which assessment would be MOST appropriate to further evaluate her symptoms?","concept":"The vignette describes a two-month depressive picture (persistent sadness, anhedonia, weight loss, insomnia, worthlessness, poor concentration). When you already suspect depression and want to quantify its severity, the most appropriate instrument is a targeted, validated depression measure. The Beck Depression Inventory (BDI), Aaron T. Beck's 21-item self-report, does exactly this, directly grading symptom severity, so it is the best fit.","traps":{"b":"The Rorschach Inkblot Test is a broad projective measure of personality and thought process, far less efficient or specific than a symptom scale for grading a clear depressive presentation.","c":"The MMPI is a wide-band personality and psychopathology inventory; it is overly broad and time-consuming when the goal is simply to quantify depression severity.","d":"The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale measures IQ and is irrelevant to evaluating mood symptoms in this case."}},{"q":"Which therapeutic modality was pioneered by Maxwell Jones and is characterized by democratic involvement of staff and patients in decision-making and daily activities?","concept":"The therapeutic community was pioneered by Maxwell Jones at the Henderson Hospital and described in his 1953 work. Its defining features are a flattened hierarchy in which staff and patients share decision-making, plus the principles of communalism, permissiveness, democratization, and reality confrontation (later summarized by Rapoport). Because the question stresses democratic involvement of staff and patients, the answer is the therapeutic community.","traps":{"a":"Encounter groups grew from the humanistic and Carl Rogers tradition, focused on intense interpersonal experience and personal growth, not on Jones's democratic ward structure.","c":"Group analysis is S. H. Foulkes's psychoanalytically informed group method, not a whole-ward democratic community model.","d":"Milieu therapy uses the treatment environment therapeutically and overlaps conceptually, but it is a broader later concept, whereas the specific model Maxwell Jones founded is the therapeutic community."}},{"q":"A client with a phobia of dogs is asked to relax and then imagine a graded series of fear-provoking interactions with a dog. This technique is called:","concept":"Pairing relaxation with a graded, imagined hierarchy of feared situations is Joseph Wolpe's systematic desensitization. It works through reciprocal inhibition: a relaxation response incompatible with anxiety is conditioned to progressively stronger fear cues, so anxiety is displaced step by step up the hierarchy. The graded, relaxed, imaginal quality is what distinguishes it, making it the correct answer.","traps":{"a":"Flooding exposes the client to the full-intensity feared stimulus at once, without a graded hierarchy or relaxation, which is the opposite of the described technique.","c":"Implosive therapy (Stampfl) uses intense imagined exposure to exaggerated, often psychodynamic fear themes without graduation or relaxation.","d":"Modeling teaches new responses by having the client observe someone else cope with the feared stimulus, not by relaxing through a personal imagined hierarchy."}},{"q":"Viktor Frankl's concept of the 'will to meaning,' the fundamental motivational force in humans to find purpose, is central to which therapeutic school?","concept":"The 'will to meaning,' the idea that the primary human motivation is to find purpose, is the cornerstone of Viktor Frankl's logotherapy, set out in Man's Search for Meaning. Frankl proposed three pathways to meaning: creative work or deeds, experiences and love, and the attitude one takes toward unavoidable suffering. So the correct school is logotherapy.","traps":{"a":"Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy (Albert Ellis) targets irrational beliefs through the ABC model, not the search for existential meaning.","c":"Existential-Humanistic therapy shares themes with Frankl but is a broader tradition (Yalom, May); the specific term 'will to meaning' is Frankl's logotherapy.","d":"Acceptance and Commitment Therapy uses values-based action and psychological flexibility within a behavioral framework, distinct from Frankl's concept of the will to meaning."}},{"q":"A therapist helps a client identify and challenge irrational beliefs such as 'I absolutely must be approved by everyone' (musterbation). This therapist is most likely practising:","concept":"Challenging absolutist demands like 'I must be approved by everyone,' which Albert Ellis called musturbation, is the signature of Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy (REBT). Ellis's ABC model holds that an Activating event does not cause the Consequence directly; irrational Beliefs (musts, shoulds, awfulizing, low frustration tolerance) do, and the therapist actively disputes them. The focus on rigid 'must' demands identifies REBT.","traps":{"a":"Beck's Cognitive Therapy targets automatic thoughts and cognitive distortions through collaborative empiricism and gentle Socratic questioning, not the forceful disputation of absolutist 'musts' that is Ellis's trademark.","c":"Dialectical Behavior Therapy (Marsha Linehan) balances acceptance and change with skills training for emotion dysregulation, not disputation of irrational beliefs.","d":"Reality Therapy (William Glasser) focuses on present choices and responsibility to meet basic needs, not on challenging irrational 'must' beliefs."}},{"q":"[Repeated 2023, 2024, 2025] The Premack Principle states that:","concept":"The Premack Principle, formulated by David Premack in 1965, states that a more probable (higher-frequency) behavior can serve as a reinforcer for a less probable (lower-frequency) behavior. This is 'Grandma's rule': eat your vegetables (low probability) and then you get dessert (high probability). So access to the preferred activity reinforces the less-preferred one, making option a correct.","traps":{"b":"This reverses the principle; a low-probability behavior cannot reinforce a high-probability one, since it is the preferred (high-probability) activity that does the reinforcing.","c":"Reinforcement after every correct response describes a continuous reinforcement schedule, a separate operant concept unrelated to the Premack relationship between behaviors.","d":"The claim that punishment beats reinforcement is a general (and incorrect) behavioral assertion, not a statement of the Premack Principle."}},{"q":"[Repeated 2023, 2024] A therapist uses 'time-out' to manage a child's aggressive outbursts. Time-out is technically classified as:","concept":"In operant terms, the label depends on whether something is added or removed and whether behavior goes up or down. Time-out (time-out from positive reinforcement) removes access to a desirable stimulus such as attention or activity, and it is used to decrease behavior. Removing a pleasant stimulus to reduce behavior is negative punishment, so option c is correct.","traps":{"a":"Positive punishment adds an aversive stimulus (for example a reprimand) to decrease behavior, whereas time-out takes something away rather than adding it.","b":"Negative reinforcement removes an aversive stimulus to increase behavior, the opposite of time-out's goal of reducing behavior.","d":"Positive reinforcement adds a desirable stimulus to increase behavior, which is the reverse of removing reinforcement to suppress aggression."}},{"q":"Which therapeutic approach focuses on the 'real relationship' and the therapist's unconditional positive regard, rather than the working alliance per se?","concept":"Carl Rogers's Person-Centered Therapy holds that the therapeutic relationship itself is the engine of change. Its three core conditions are unconditional positive regard, congruence (genuineness), and accurate empathic understanding. Because the emphasis is on the genuine, prizing relationship and unconditional positive regard rather than on a task-focused working alliance, the answer is Person-Centered Therapy.","traps":{"a":"Psychoanalysis works through transference interpretation and insight into unconscious conflict, not through Rogers's unconditional positive regard.","b":"Cognitive-Behavioral Therapy centers on modifying maladaptive thoughts and behaviors, using the relationship as a vehicle rather than the primary change agent.","d":"Interpersonal Therapy is a structured, time-limited treatment focused on current relationship problems (grief, role transitions, disputes), not on unconditional positive regard as the core mechanism."}},{"q":"In Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT), the 'core mindfulness' skills are primarily derived from which tradition?","concept":"Marsha Linehan built Dialectical Behavior Therapy by integrating standard cognitive-behavioral change strategies with acceptance practices drawn from Zen Buddhism. The core mindfulness skills (observe, describe, participate; nonjudgmentally, one-mindfully, effectively) come directly from that Zen tradition. DBT's four skill modules are mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation, and interpersonal effectiveness, so the source of core mindfulness is Zen Buddhism.","traps":{"a":"Stoicism influenced cognitive therapy's view that beliefs shape distress, but it is not the source of DBT's mindfulness skills.","c":"Psychoanalysis contributed concepts of the unconscious and defenses, unrelated to DBT's mindfulness practices.","d":"Existentialism informs meaning-focused therapies like logotherapy, not the Zen-derived mindfulness training in DBT."}},{"q":"Borderline Personality Disorder is most characteristically associated with which primary defense mechanism?","concept":"Borderline Personality Disorder is characterized in object-relations theory (Otto Kernberg, building on Melanie Klein) by splitting, the failure to integrate good and bad images of self and others. People are experienced as all-good or all-bad, and these views flip rapidly, driving the idealization-devaluation cycle in relationships. So splitting is the defense most characteristic of BPD.","traps":{"a":"Repression, keeping unacceptable material out of awareness, is a broad neurotic-level defense central to psychoanalysis generally, not the hallmark of BPD.","c":"Sublimation, channeling drives into socially valued activity, is a mature adaptive defense, not the primitive defense typical of borderline organization.","d":"Rationalization, offering plausible justifications for behavior, is a neurotic-level defense and not the signature mechanism of BPD."}},{"q":"Affirmative therapy is specifically designed to support which population of clients?","concept":"Affirmative therapy is specifically designed to support LGBTQ+ clients. It validates non-heteronormative sexual orientations and gender identities, addresses minority stress arising from stigma and discrimination, and explicitly rejects conversion or reparative therapy. Because its defining aim is affirming these identities, the correct population is LGBTQ+ individuals.","traps":{"a":"Individuals with anxiety disorders are treated with modalities like CBT or exposure; affirmative therapy is defined by the population it serves, not by an anxiety focus.","b":"Elderly clients with cognitive decline are served by geriatric and cognitive interventions, not by the identity-affirming framework of affirmative therapy.","d":"Children with developmental delays receive developmental and behavioral services, which is unrelated to the LGBTQ+ focus of affirmative therapy."}},{"q":"[Repeated 2023, 2024] The bystander effect, where the presence of others inhibits helping, is primarily explained by two factors:","concept":"The bystander effect, that people are less likely to help when others are present, was explained by Bibb Latane and John Darley through two main mechanisms. Diffusion of responsibility means each onlooker assumes someone else will act, so personal obligation is diluted. Pluralistic ignorance means each person reads others' apparent calm as a sign that nothing is wrong. Together these give option b.","traps":{"a":"Social loafing (reduced effort in groups) and deindividuation (loss of self-awareness in crowds) are real group phenomena but are not the standard explanation for bystander non-intervention.","c":"Social comparison and normative influence concern how we evaluate ourselves and conform to be liked, not the specific dynamics of emergency helping.","d":"Group polarization and groupthink describe how group discussion shifts and distorts decisions, which is unrelated to why individual bystanders fail to help."}},{"q":"The classic 'bystander apathy' effect was demonstrated in the laboratory following the real-life murder of:","concept":"The laboratory study of bystander apathy was prompted by the 1964 murder of Kitty Genovese in Queens, New York, reportedly witnessed by many neighbors who did not intervene. Although the original claim of 38 unresponsive witnesses has since been historically disputed, the case still motivated Latane and Darley's foundational research on bystander intervention. So the answer is Kitty Genovese.","traps":{"a":"Martin Luther King Jr. was assassinated in 1968, a politically motivated killing unconnected to bystander-effect research.","c":"Emmett Till's 1955 murder was a landmark in civil-rights history, not the case that inspired bystander-intervention experiments.","d":"Malala Yousafzai survived a 2012 attack and is an education activist, entirely unrelated to the origins of bystander research."}},{"q":"[Repeated 2023, 2024] The 'just-world hypothesis' explains victim-blaming as a result of:","concept":"The just-world hypothesis, developed by Melvin Lerner, is the belief that the world is orderly and fair so that people get what they deserve and deserve what they get. When an innocent victim's suffering threatens this comforting belief, observers often restore it by derogating or blaming the victim rather than accepting that bad things happen randomly. So victim-blaming stems from the need to believe the world is fair, option b.","traps":{"a":"Attributing victim-blaming to inherent sadism misreads the motive; the theory says it comes from a need for a predictable, fair world, not a wish to see suffering.","c":"Attributing behavior to dispositional factors describes the fundamental attribution error, a related but distinct bias, not the just-world motive.","d":"Comparing oneself with a less fortunate other is downward social comparison, a self-esteem mechanism, not the fairness belief behind just-world reasoning."}},{"q":"The phenomenon where qualified individuals (especially women / minorities) are blocked from advancing to higher career positions by an invisible barrier is called:","concept":"The glass ceiling is the invisible, often informal barrier that keeps qualified people, especially women and minorities, from rising into top organizational positions despite their competence. The term was popularized in a 1986 Wall Street Journal article by Carol Hymowitz and Timothy Schellhardt. Because the question describes an unseen barrier to advancement, the answer is the glass ceiling effect.","traps":{"a":"The Matthew effect is the accumulation of advantage in which the already successful gain more, not a barrier blocking advancement.","c":"The halo effect is a bias where one positive impression colors judgments of other traits, unrelated to career-advancement barriers.","d":"The Pygmalion effect is the self-fulfilling prophecy by which higher expectations boost performance, the opposite of a barrier holding people back."}},{"q":"Learned helplessness, in social learning theory, refers to:","concept":"Learned helplessness, demonstrated by Martin Seligman and Steven Maier (1967), is the passivity that develops after exposure to uncontrollable aversive events. Dogs given inescapable shocks later failed to escape shocks they could have avoided, having learned that responding was futile. The reformulated model (Lyn Abramson and colleagues) linked it to a depressogenic attributional style (internal, stable, global). So the correct description is repeated unavoidable aversive events leading to later passivity, option c.","traps":{"a":"Performing a behavior to escape an aversive stimulus is escape (negative reinforcement) learning, effectively the opposite of the helpless failure to escape.","b":"Observing a model rewarded for aggression describes Bandura's observational learning, not the effect of uncontrollable aversive events.","d":"Failing to receive reinforcement for a previously learned behavior describes extinction, which is not the same as helplessness produced by uncontrollability."}},{"q":"[Repeated 2023, 2024] The 'Type D' personality, identified as a risk factor for poor cardiovascular prognosis, is characterized by:","concept":"The Type D ('distressed') personality, identified by Johan Denollet, combines two stable traits: high negative affectivity (a tendency to experience worry, gloom, and irritability) and high social inhibition (suppressing the expression of these emotions in social settings). This combination roughly doubles the risk of cardiac morbidity and mortality independent of disease severity. So the correct answer is negative affectivity plus social inhibition, option b.","traps":{"a":"Hostility and competitiveness describe the Type A behavior pattern (Friedman and Rosenman), a different cardiovascular risk profile, not Type D.","c":"An optimistic, resilient outlook is protective, the opposite of the distressed Type D constellation.","d":"Perfectionism and rigidity are personality features, but they do not define the specific two-factor Type D pattern of negative affect and social inhibition."}},{"q":"The 'halo effect' is a cognitive bias in which:","concept":"The halo effect, first documented by Edward Thorndike in 1920, is a cognitive bias in which a global impression of a person spills over into ratings of their specific, unrelated traits. For example, someone judged attractive is often assumed to be more competent or kinder. Because the described bias is an overall impression coloring specific trait judgments, the answer is option a.","traps":{"b":"Taking credit for success while blaming external factors for failure is the self-serving bias, an attributional bias, not the halo effect.","c":"Overestimating how much others share one's opinions is the false consensus effect, a different social-perception error.","d":"Letting an initial impression anchor later judgments describes anchoring or the primacy effect, related but distinct from the trait-spillover of the halo effect."}},{"q":"The 'looking-glass self,' introduced by Charles Horton Cooley, posits that our self-concept is primarily formed by:","concept":"Charles Horton Cooley's looking-glass self (1902) proposes that our self-concept is socially constructed from how we believe others see us. It has three steps: we imagine how we appear to another person, we imagine that person's judgment of that appearance, and we react with a self-feeling such as pride or shame. So self-concept is formed by imagining how we appear to others and how they judge us, option c.","traps":{"a":"Innate unconscious drives shaping the self is a psychoanalytic (Freudian) view, not Cooley's socially reflected self.","b":"Comparing ourselves with similar others is Leon Festinger's social comparison theory, a different account of self-evaluation.","d":"Resolving psychosocial crises at each stage is Erik Erikson's developmental theory, not Cooley's looking-glass self."}},{"q":"In social psychology, the concept of reactance refers to which of the following phenomena?","concept":"Reactance, described in Jack Brehm's reactance theory, is the motivational arousal that arises when a person perceives that a specific freedom is threatened or eliminated. The restricted option becomes more attractive, and the person is driven to reassert the lost freedom, which explains backlash against bans and paradoxical failures of persuasion. So reactance is increased motivation to perform a behavior when freedom to do it is threatened, option b.","traps":{"a":"Complying with authority figures is obedience (studied by Stanley Milgram), essentially the opposite of the defiance seen in reactance.","c":"Changing one's attitude to match the majority is conformity, a distinct social-influence process, not reactance.","d":"Evaluating one's abilities by comparison with others is social comparison, unrelated to freedom-threat and reactance."}},{"q":"The sociological concept of anomie, as introduced by Émile Durkheim, refers to which of the following?","concept":"Anomie, introduced by Emile Durkheim, refers to a state of normlessness in which social norms become unclear, weak, or inapplicable, often during rapid social change. In this condition the usual regulation of behavior breaks down, increasing deviance and, in Durkheim's analysis, suicide. Robert Merton later extended the idea into strain theory (a gap between cultural goals and legitimate means). So anomie is a state of normlessness, option a.","traps":{"b":"The progression from traditional to modern societies is a broad theme of social evolution and modernization, not the meaning of anomie.","c":"Adapting to the values of a dominant culture is acculturation or assimilation, a different sociological process.","d":"Internalizing societal norms and values describes socialization, which is essentially the opposite of the normlessness that anomie names."}},{"q":"In Baddeley's model of working memory, the subsystem responsible for integrating information from the phonological loop, visuospatial sketchpad, and long-term memory into a single coherent episode is the:","concept":"In Alan Baddeley's model of working memory, the episodic buffer (added in 2000) is the component that integrates information from the phonological loop, the visuospatial sketchpad, and long-term memory into a single, coherent, multimodal episode. It is a limited-capacity temporary store controlled by the central executive. Because the question asks about binding across subsystems, the answer is the episodic buffer.","traps":{"a":"The central executive is the attentional control system that allocates resources and coordinates the subsystems, but it is not itself the storage buffer that binds them into an episode.","c":"The visuospatial sketchpad holds visual and spatial information only, so it is one of the slave systems being integrated, not the integrator.","d":"The phonological loop stores and rehearses verbal and acoustic information, again a slave system feeding into the buffer rather than the binding store."}},{"q":"The correct sequence of the four stages of creative thinking, as proposed by Graham Wallas, is:","concept":"Graham Wallas, in The Art of Thought (1926), described four stages of the creative process in this order: Preparation (gathering information and defining the problem), Incubation (unconscious processing while attention is elsewhere), Illumination (the sudden 'aha' insight), and Verification (testing and refining the idea). So the correct sequence is Preparation, Incubation, Illumination, Verification, option c.","traps":{"a":"This places Illumination before Incubation, which is impossible since insight follows the unconscious incubation period.","b":"This starts with Incubation before Preparation, but one cannot incubate before gathering and defining the problem in the preparation stage.","d":"This begins with Illumination and ends with Incubation, completely reversing the logical flow of the creative process."}},{"q":"According to Albert Bandura, which of the following is NOT a necessary component process for observational learning to occur?","concept":"Albert Bandura's model of observational (social) learning specifies four necessary sub-processes: Attention to the model, Retention of what was observed, Reproduction (motor reproduction) of the behavior, and Motivation to perform it. Crucially, direct external reinforcement is not required; vicarious reinforcement (seeing the model rewarded) can suffice. This distinguished Bandura from Skinner, so the component that is NOT necessary is reinforcement, option c.","traps":{"a":"Attention is one of Bandura's four essential processes; without attending to the model, learning cannot begin, so it is required.","b":"Motivation is a genuine component; it determines whether the learned behavior is actually performed, so it is necessary rather than the exception.","d":"Reproduction (motor reproduction) is required because the learner must be able to enact the observed behavior, so it belongs to the model."}},{"q":"The backward digit span test, where participants must repeat a sequence of numbers in reverse order, is primarily used to assess which type of memory system?","concept":"Digit span comes in two forms. Forward digit span, simply holding and repeating a sequence, taps short-term storage (the phonological loop). Backward digit span requires holding the numbers while mentally reversing their order, which demands active manipulation of stored information. That manipulation engages the central executive, making backward digit span a measure of working memory, option b.","traps":{"a":"Long-term memory stores information over minutes to years; the brief, immediate reversal task of backward digit span does not tap it.","c":"Sensory memory is a very brief, high-capacity trace (iconic or echoic) that fades in under a second, not the manipulated span being tested.","d":"Procedural memory is implicit memory for skills and habits, unrelated to the conscious manipulation of a digit sequence."}},{"q":"In psychoanalytic theory, what term describes the mental representation of psychic energy being invested or attached to a particular object, person, or idea?","concept":"In Sigmund Freud's psychoanalytic theory, cathexis (German Besetzung) is the investment or attachment of psychic energy (libido) onto a mental representation of an object, person, or idea. The withdrawal of that energy is decathexis. Because the question asks for the term describing psychic energy invested in an object, the answer is cathexis.","traps":{"a":"Libido is the psychic energy itself (the drive energy), not the process of attaching that energy to a particular object.","c":"Fixation is the arrest of development at an earlier psychosexual stage due to over- or under-gratification, not the investment of energy in an object.","d":"Sublimation is a defense mechanism that redirects unacceptable drives into socially acceptable outlets, not the concept of energy attachment."}},{"q":"'Crystallized intelligence' is best defined as:","concept":"In the Cattell-Horn-Carroll model, Raymond Cattell distinguished two broad intelligences: crystallized intelligence (Gc) is the store of accumulated factual knowledge, vocabulary, and verbal skills built up through education and life experience, and it tends to rise or stay stable across the lifespan. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the capacity to reason and solve novel problems independent of prior learning, and it peaks in early adulthood then declines. Option c captures Gc because it names acquired knowledge and verbal skill derived from experience, which is the defining feature of crystallized intelligence.","traps":{"a":"Solving novel problems is the definition of fluid intelligence (Gf), the reasoning ability that is independent of accumulated knowledge, so it describes the opposite construct.","b":"Speed of neural processing refers to mental or processing speed (Gs), a distinct CHC ability sometimes underlying Gf, not the stored knowledge that defines crystallized intelligence.","d":"The capacity of working memory is short-term/working memory (Gsm), a separate cognitive resource for holding and manipulating information, not the experiential knowledge base of Gc."}},{"q":"Which personality inventory is directly based on Carl Jung's theory of psychological types?","concept":"The Myers-Briggs Type Indicator was developed by Katharine Cook Briggs and Isabel Briggs Myers directly from Carl Gustav Jung's theory of psychological types. It sorts people on four dichotomies (Extraversion/Introversion, Sensing/Intuition, Thinking/Feeling, Judging/Perceiving) to yield sixteen types, operationalizing Jung's attitudes and functions. Option b is correct because among these inventories only the MBTI was built explicitly on Jungian typology.","traps":{"a":"The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory is an empirically keyed clinical psychopathology inventory, not a measure derived from Jung's type theory.","c":"The 16PF was developed by Raymond Cattell using factor analysis of trait descriptors, an empirical/statistical origin rather than Jungian typology.","d":"The NEO-PI-R by Costa and McCrae measures the Big Five (Five-Factor Model) traits, a lexical/dimensional framework unrelated to Jung's types."}},{"q":"According to Aaron Beck's cognitive theory of depression, the pattern of negative thoughts about self, world, and future is known as:","concept":"Aaron Temkin Beck's cognitive theory of depression proposes that depressed individuals hold pervasive negative views across three domains: the self ('I am worthless'), the world/current experience ('everything is against me'), and the future ('nothing will improve'). Beck named this pattern the negative cognitive triad, and it is fueled by underlying schemas and surfaces as automatic thoughts. Option b is correct because the triad specifically refers to negative cognition about self, world, and future.","traps":{"a":"Cognitive distortions are the faulty processing styles (for example overgeneralization, catastrophizing) that bias thinking, not the three-domain content pattern named by the triad.","c":"Automatic thoughts are the involuntary, situation-specific negative thoughts that pop up in the moment; they are a product of the triad, not the triad itself.","d":"Dysfunctional assumptions are the deeper conditional rules or intermediate beliefs ('If I fail, I am worthless') beneath automatic thoughts, distinct from the self-world-future triad."}},{"q":"The phonological loop, a component of Baddeley's model of working memory, is primarily responsible for:","concept":"In Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch's working memory model, the phonological loop is the subsystem that handles speech-based and verbal information. It has two parts: a phonological store that briefly holds sound-based traces (about two seconds) and an articulatory rehearsal process that refreshes them by subvocal repetition. Option b is correct because storing and rehearsing auditory/verbal material is precisely the loop's function, supported by the word-length effect, articulatory suppression, and phonological similarity effect.","traps":{"a":"Visual and spatial processing is the job of the visuospatial sketchpad, the separate slave system for imagery and spatial location, not the phonological loop.","c":"Coordinating and integrating information from different sources describes the central executive (and the later episodic buffer), the attentional controller rather than the verbal store.","d":"Managing long-term memory retrieval is not the loop's role; the episodic buffer, added later, links working memory to long-term memory."}},{"q":"Weber's Law is best described by which of the following statements?","concept":"Weber's Law, from Ernst Heinrich Weber, states that the just-noticeable difference (JND) between two stimuli is a constant proportion of the original stimulus intensity, expressed as the Weber fraction delta-I over I equals k. So a heavier baseline weight or brighter baseline light requires a proportionally larger change to be detected. Option b is correct because it states this proportional (constant-fraction) relationship; Gustav Fechner later extended it into a logarithmic law relating sensation to intensity.","traps":{"a":"A fixed absolute JND for any stimulus is exactly what Weber's Law denies; the JND grows with stimulus magnitude rather than staying a constant amount.","c":"Detection depending solely on exposure duration is a temporal-summation idea, not Weber's Law, which concerns the ratio of intensity change to baseline intensity.","d":"Claiming perception is unaffected by intensity changes contradicts the whole premise of difference thresholds and Weber's proportional relationship."}},{"q":"Wolpe's systematic desensitisation pairs graded exposure to a feared stimulus with deep muscle relaxation. It is based on the principle of:","concept":"Joseph Wolpe's systematic desensitisation is grounded in the principle of reciprocal inhibition: a response that is physiologically incompatible with anxiety, deep muscle relaxation, is paired with a graded hierarchy of feared stimuli so that relaxation displaces and inhibits the anxiety response. Because relaxation and anxiety cannot occur simultaneously, repeated pairing weakens the conditioned fear. Option b is correct because reciprocal inhibition is the explicit mechanism Wolpe proposed.","traps":{"a":"Operant extinction involves withholding reinforcement to reduce a behavior; systematic desensitisation works by counter-conditioning with an incompatible response, not by removing a reinforcer.","c":"Aversive conditioning pairs an unwanted behavior with an unpleasant stimulus to suppress it, the opposite aim of desensitisation, which reduces anxiety through relaxation.","d":"Flooding is a competing exposure technique using intense, ungraded exposure without relaxation, so it relies on habituation/extinction rather than reciprocal inhibition."}},{"q":"The presence of two or more distinct personality states together with recurrent gaps in autobiographical recall is the core feature of:","concept":"Dissociative identity disorder (DID) is defined by the presence of two or more distinct personality (identity) states, each with its own pattern of experiencing, together with recurrent gaps in the recall of everyday events and personal information that exceed ordinary forgetting. In both the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual (DSM-5) and ICD-11, the combination of distinct identities plus dissociative amnesia is the core. Option c is correct because only DID pairs distinct personality states with autobiographical memory gaps.","traps":{"a":"Depersonalisation/derealisation disorder involves feelings of detachment from oneself or unreality of surroundings while reality testing stays intact, without distinct identities or amnesia.","b":"Dissociative amnesia features the memory gaps but lacks the two or more distinct personality states that define DID, so it captures only half the described picture.","d":"Schizophrenia is a psychotic disorder marked by hallucinations, delusions, and disorganization, not a dissociative disorder with alternating identities and autobiographical amnesia."}},{"q":"The extrapyramidal side effects (dystonia, parkinsonism) of typical antipsychotics arise mainly from dopamine D2 blockade in which pathway?","concept":"Typical (first-generation) antipsychotics block dopamine D2 receptors throughout the brain. Extrapyramidal side effects such as acute dystonia, drug-induced parkinsonism, akathisia, and tardive dyskinesia arise from D2 blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway, which normally regulates motor control. Option d is correct because the nigrostriatal (substantia nigra to striatum) dopamine route governs movement, so its blockade produces EPS.","traps":{"a":"The mesolimbic pathway's D2 blockade reduces the positive symptoms of psychosis (hallucinations, delusions); it is the therapeutic target, not the source of movement side effects.","b":"The mesocortical pathway, when blocked, tends to worsen negative and cognitive symptoms; it does not cause the dystonia and parkinsonism of EPS.","c":"Tuberoinfundibular D2 blockade removes dopamine's inhibition of prolactin, causing hyperprolactinaemia (galactorrhoea, menstrual changes), not extrapyramidal motor effects."}},{"q":"The Vineland Adaptive Behaviour Scales are primarily used to assess:","concept":"The Vineland Adaptive Behaviour Scales, developed from Edgar Doll's work, assess adaptive functioning: the everyday practical skills a person actually performs across communication, daily living skills, socialisation, and motor skills. They are administered via informant interview and are central to diagnosing intellectual disability, where deficits in adaptive behaviour must accompany low IQ. Option b is correct because the Vineland measures adaptive functioning in daily living rather than any single cognitive ability.","traps":{"a":"Intelligence quotient is measured by IQ tests like the Wechsler or Stanford-Binet scales; the Vineland complements these but does not itself yield an IQ.","c":"Personality structure is assessed by inventories or projective tests (for example the MMPI or Rorschach), not by an adaptive behaviour scale.","d":"Neuropsychological memory deficits are evaluated with memory batteries (for example the Wechsler Memory Scale); the Vineland targets real-world adaptive skills, not memory."}},{"q":"Which sampling method gives every member of the population a known, non-zero probability of being selected?","concept":"Probability (random) sampling is defined by the fact that every member of the population has a known, non-zero chance of being selected, which is what allows the sample to be representative and sampling error to be estimated. Its forms include simple random, stratified, cluster, and systematic sampling. Option c is correct because only probability sampling guarantees a known non-zero selection probability for each unit.","traps":{"a":"Quota sampling fills preset category targets using the researcher's judgment, so selection probabilities are unknown; it is a non-probability method.","b":"Snowball sampling recruits participants through referrals from existing subjects, giving unknown and unequal selection chances, typical of hard-to-reach populations.","d":"Purposive sampling deliberately selects specific units based on the researcher's judgment of relevance, so members have no known probability of inclusion."}}]});
